%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: paintkit.ps — pressure-sensitive ribbon strokes for artkit
%%Creator: pscat — issue #41, the painterly-brush series' foundation
%%EndComments
%
% Every procedure here is defined `} bind def` (issue #307). Ghostscript
% looks up each executable name in a procedure body at run time, and
% those lookups are slow once userdict holds the libraries' several
% hundred names; `bind` resolves the operators once, when the library
% loads. The cost: an operator redefined *after* this file loads (a
% caller's own `/show`, `/rand`, `/fill`) no longer reaches these
% procedures, and one redefined *before* it loads is left alone, since
% `bind` only replaces names that are operators at that moment. Names
% that are not operators (every library procedure, and tunables such as
% drykit's `dkbudget`) stay late-bound, so overriding those works as
% before. tests/libbind.rs fails for any procedure added without `bind`.
% @requires: (lib/artkit.ps) run

% A fifth sibling to graph.ps/dataviz.ps/etching.ps/pagekit.ps, but --
% like pagekit -- it depends on lib/artkit.ps, specifically walkpath
% (issue #40, the centerline sampler). Load artkit first:
%
%   (lib/artkit.ps) run  (lib/paintkit.ps) run
%
% What's inside: `pkribbon`, that treats the current path as a
% centerline and fills a variable-width ribbon along it -- the minimum
% useful abstraction for a PostScript path as the motion of a physical
% tool rather than a uniform geometric stroke -- and `pknib` (issue
% #42), an angled-nib calligraphy preset built on top of it. See
% `pknib`'s own header, near the end of this file, for its docs.
%
% `pkdry` (issue #43) is a dry-bristle preset also built on `pkribbon`:
% a bounded family of thin offset bristles scattered across the
% centerline, each broken into ink/no-ink runs by a seeded two-state
% Markov chain, so the mark ranges from a mostly loaded brush to
% visibly broken coverage without any raster work. See its own header,
% after `pknib`'s, for full docs.
%
% `pkoil` (issue #45) is a stylized oil-paint preset — layered pressure
% ribbons and bristle traces suggesting loaded impasto without any 3D
% height map or fluid simulation. See its own header, after `pkspray`'s,
% for full docs.
%
% `pkliner` (issue #116) is the fine-detail end of the same family: a
% long soft loaded tip for branches, trunks, grass stems and other thin
% organic marks, and the complement of `pknib`'s rigid nib. It is the one
% preset here that displaces the *centerline* rather than varying width
% or coverage around it. See its own header, after `pkbroad`'s, for full
% docs.
%
% `pkdab` (issue #117) is the foliage brush: clustered irregular dabs
% pounced along a spine, painted as a darker underlayer, a body and
% lighter highlight dabs from one set of geometry, for bushes and leafy
% masses. Like `pkspray` it is not built on `pkribbon`. See its own
% header, at the end of this file, for full docs.
%
% The brush *motions* (issue #184) are not brushes: path builders that make
% the input a preset takes (criss-cross, scumble, tap, pull, zig-zag). After
% them, `pkbrushedge` and its motion `pkfeather` (issue #347) brush the ruled
% edge of a filled mass.
%
% `pkspray` (issue #44) is not built on `pkribbon` -- spray is discrete
% particle deposition, not an offset band -- but it shares the same
% walkpath-driven shape: seeded particles scattered around each sampled
% centerline stop under the caller's own srand, with a radial falloff,
% overspray mist past the nozzle edge, and optional start/end trigger-
% dwell bursts. See its own header, after `pkdry`'s, for full docs.
%
%   opts pkribbon -
%
% `opts` is a dict of optional keys (a sparse or empty dict still
% renders -- a plain constant-width band). See the `@param` tags
% directly above `pkribbon`'s own definition below for the full
% parameter list with defaults. A few caveats the terse tags don't
% carry: /Pressure must actually be a procedure, checked with `xcheck`;
% /StartCap and /EndCap reject any value other than /round, /flat, or
% /pointed rather than silently falling through to /flat, though a cap
% whose computed half-width is exactly 0 (taper or a zero-pressure
% endpoint) degrades to /pointed automatically rather than emitting a
% zero-radius arc (safe in both pscat and Ghostscript, but pointless);
% and a /Jitter amount past Width/2 can push an edge point across the
% centerline to the far side -- `fill`'s nonzero winding absorbs the
% resulting local self-crossing without error, but past that point
% "jitter" is closer to "noise" -- keep it well under Width/2 for a
% ribbon that still reads as one continuous band.
%
% Color is deliberately not a key: `pkribbon` fills with whatever
% color the caller already set (`setrgbcolor` et al.), same as any
% other artkit shape helper.
%
% Same contract as walkpath: the caller's current path survives,
% flattened, since `pkribbon`'s own drawing happens inside its own
% `gsave`/`newpath`/`grestore`, never touching the path it just walked.
%
% Multiple subpaths in the source path each become an independent
% ribbon. A subpath actually closed with `closepath` -- tracked
% directly from the path itself (`pkscanclosed`), not inferred from
% its endpoints merely coinciding, since an open subpath can
% legitimately end exactly where it started too -- is filled as two
% concentric closed loops with no caps at all -- /StartCap//EndCap
% only apply to open paths. A degenerate single-point subpath, or a
% closed subpath too short to leave a genuinely distinct interior
% sample, falls
% back to a single filled dot (diameter Width*Pressure at that point)
% rather than erroring. An entirely empty source path is a no-op.
%
% Built with plain `fill` (nonzero winding), not `eofill`: a
% self-intersecting offset -- unavoidable wherever the centerline's
% curvature radius drops below half the ribbon's width, e.g. a tight
% Bezier at a generous /Width -- still paints the whole ribbon rather
% than punching a hole in it. That's a real, documented limitation of
% simple offset-curve stroking, not a bug. (A closed polygon's sharp
% corners might look like the same problem -- no miter/bevel joint
% between consecutive segments -- but empirically don't show it even
% at a ribbon width several times the polygon's own size: nonzero
% fill's overlap tolerance absorbs the unmitered per-vertex offset
% cleanly for the shapes this library actually renders.)
%
% Shared key names (issue #141). A key means one thing across every
% preset in this file: /Spread is always a 0..1 fraction of /Width that
% bristles or ridges occupy, and /Load is always a 0..1 resume-contact
% rate per /Width of travel. Four keys that used to break this rule were
% renamed, and each old name still works as a deprecated alias, with the
% new name winning when both are given: pkwet /Spread (points) -> /Offset,
% pktrowel /Load (deposit width) -> /Cover, pkdab /Spread (points) ->
% /Scatter, pkcrisscross /Spread (degrees) -> /Lean. Every `@param` tag
% starts with its unit in brackets -- [points], [degrees], [count],
% [0..1], [fraction of /Width], [per /Width], ... -- so `pscat
% --capabilities` shows a unit for each parameter;
% tests/capabilities.rs checks the vocabulary.
%
% Scratch prefixes: pk- (pkribbon/dispatch), pkbr- (pkbuildrun),
% pko- (pkopenrun), pkl- (pkloop), pke- (pkedge), pkh- (pkhalfwat),
% pktf- (pktaperf), pkd- (pkdot), pkg- (pkgetdef), pksc- (pkscanclosed).
% pn- (pknib/dispatch, pnangleat, pnpressure) -- issue #42, see below.
% pb- (pkdry/dispatch, pbdashrun) -- issue #43, see below. Deliberately
% distinct from pk-/pn-: pkdry calls pkribbon itself once per bristle
% dash, and pkribbon's own body freely redefines every pk*-prefixed
% name listed above on each call -- a pkdry-owned name sharing that
% prefix would get silently clobbered by its own nested pkribbon call.
% pz- (pkspray/dispatch, pzpdot, pzburstcluster) -- issue #44, see
% below; also deliberately distinct, same reasoning as pkdry's pb-.
% po- (pkoil/dispatch, porad, poroll) -- issue #45, see below; also
% deliberately distinct, same reasoning — pkoil calls pkribbon once per
% bristle dash and pkribbon's own body redefines every pk*-prefixed name.
% pq- (pkliner/dispatch, pqaff, pqoff, pqnrm, pqside, pqemit) -- issue
% #116, see below.
% pd- (pkdab/dispatch, pddab) -- issue #117, see below. Not `pdf`, which
% is a scratch name elsewhere in the library.
% mh-/mx-/mc-/ms-/mt-/mp-/mz- (the brush motion generators: pkcrisscross,
% pkscumblein, pkscumble, pktap, pkpull, pkzigzag) -- issue #184, see the
% section at the end of this file. They call no preset.
% mf- (pkfeather) and pe- (pkbrushedge) -- issue #347, the "Brushed edges"
% section after the motions. pe- is distinct from every preset prefix
% because pkbrushedge calls the caller's /Brush, usually a preset.
% pj- (pkcanvas, the canvas surface's sampler and pjcatch) -- issue #182,
% see the last section of this file. Distinct from every pk* name because the
% presets that will call pjcatch (pkdry, pkfan) call pkribbon.

% - [bool0 bool1 ...]   (one flag per subpath, in path order: was it
% closed with an explicit closepath?)
% A subpath whose open endpoint merely happens to coincide with its
% start (a `lineto` back to the exact starting coordinate, a full-circle
% `arc`, no `closepath`) is not the same thing as a subpath actually
% closed with `closepath` -- comparing walkpath's start/end stop
% coordinates can't tell the two apart, so the ribbon has to ask the
% path itself. `pathforall`'s close proc fires only for a real
% `closepath` segment, never for coordinates merely matching, so this
% is authoritative where a coordinate comparison is only a heuristic
% (Codex review, round 4). Same two-pass shape as artkit's own
% wkmeasure: emit the previous subpath's flag on each new moveto
% (skipped on the very first), then once more after the loop for the
% last subpath.
%
% This is also the segment-count bound for the presets that scan before
% they walk (issue #299). pkribbon, pknib and pkfeather call it ahead of
% their first walkpath, so walkpath's own bound (artkit's wkmaxsegs,
% issue #110) cannot fire first. Moveto, lineto and closepath each count
% one, as in wkmeasure, and a path over wkmaxsegs raises
% `walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit` the moment the count
% passes it, so the refusal costs 50,000 visits and not the whole path.
% The scan reads the path before it is flattened, so its count is never
% more than wkmeasure's: no path walkpath accepts is refused here. The
% error fires with this procedure's `[` mark and the pathforall operands
% on the stack, so it clears back to the mark first.
% @internal
/pkscanclosed {
    /pkscclosed false def /pkscfirst true def /pkscn 0 def
    [
        { pop pop
          /pkscn pkscn 1 add def
          pkscn wkmaxsegs gt { cleartomark walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
          pkscfirst { /pkscfirst false def } { pkscclosed } ifelse
          /pkscclosed false def }
        { pop pop
          /pkscn pkscn 1 add def
          pkscn wkmaxsegs gt { cleartomark walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if }
        { pop pop pop pop pop pop }
        { /pkscn pkscn 1 add def
          pkscn wkmaxsegs gt { cleartomark walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
          /pkscclosed true def }
        pathforall
        pkscfirst not { pkscclosed } if
    ]
} bind def

% opts key default pkgetdef  value -
% The default is wrapped in a 1-element array rather than bound
% straight to a name -- a name bound directly to a procedure (e.g. a
% /Pressure default) auto-executes on bare reference, same as calling
% any other proc by name in this file, so returning it as `pkgdef`
% would silently invoke the default instead of yielding it. Reading it
% back with `get` sidesteps that regardless of the default's type.
% @internal
/pkgetdef {                                      % dict key default -
    /pkgdef exch 1 array astore def
    /pkgkey exch def
    /pkgdict exch def
    pkgdict pkgkey known
        { pkgdict pkgkey get }
        { pkgdef 0 get }
    ifelse
} bind def

% --- pressure presets: t -> mult -------------------------------------
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: pkribbon's default /Pressure preset: constant width (t -> 1.0).
% @example: t pkflat mult
/pkflat  { pop 1.0 } bind def                          % constant
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A pkribbon /Pressure preset: linear taper across the whole stroke (t -> t).
% @example: t pktaper mult
/pktaper { } bind def                                  % linear (t is the mult)
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A pkribbon /Pressure preset: non-linear 0->1->0 hump, widest at the middle of the stroke.
% @example: t pkbell mult
/pkbell  { 180 mul sin } bind def                      % non-linear 0->1->0 hump

% t -> mult   (StartTaper/EndTaper ramp; reads pkstarttaper/pkendtaper)
% Both ramps are computed unconditionally and combined with the
% minimum, not picked by a mutually exclusive branch on which side of
% the midpoint t falls -- if /StartTaper and /EndTaper sum past 1
% their ramp regions overlap, and a branch that applies only one would
% jump discontinuously right at the branch boundary (Codex review,
% round 1). Each ramp already evaluates to 1.0 (no constraint) outside
% its own region, so the minimum is exactly "whichever ramp constrains
% more here," continuous everywhere including the overlap.
% @internal
/pktaperf {
    /pktft exch def
    /pktfs
        pktft pkstarttaper lt pkstarttaper 0 gt and
            { pktft pkstarttaper div }
            { 1.0 }
        ifelse
    def
    /pktfe
        pktft 1 pkendtaper sub gt pkendtaper 0 gt and
            { 1 pktft sub pkendtaper div }
            { 1.0 }
        ifelse
    def
    pktfs pktfe lt { pktfs } { pktfe } ifelse
} bind def

% t -> halfwidth   (reads pkwidth/pkpressure/pktaperf's globals)
% @internal
/pkhalfwat {
    /pkht exch def
    pkwidth
    pkht pkpressure
    pkht pktaperf
    mul mul 2 div
} bind def

% amount -> offset in -amount..amount, deterministic under srand
% @internal
/pkjit { frnd 2 mul 1 sub mul } bind def

% x y ang halfw side -> ex ey   (side: -1 right / +1 left of travel;
% applies /Jitter along the perpendicular, skipped entirely at 0)
% @internal
/pkedge {
    /pkeside exch def /pkehw exch def /pkeang exch def
    /pkey exch def /pkex exch def
    /pkeoff pkjitter 0 eq { pkehw } { pkehw pkjitter pkjit add } ifelse def
    /pkepa pkeang 90 pkeside mul add def
    pkex pkepa cos pkeoff mul add
    pkey pkepa sin pkeoff mul add
} bind def

% [x y ang t sp atend] pkdot -   (degenerate single-point fallback)
% Diameter is Width*Pressure only -- not pkhalfwat's full
% Width*Pressure*taper. /StartTaper//EndTaper ramp width in from the
% *ends of a path*; a lone point has no path to ramp along, and
% applying them anyway could zero out the dot entirely (e.g. any
% nonzero /StartTaper at t=0) for a caller who never asked for that.
% @internal
/pkdot {
    dup 0 get /pkdx exch def
    dup 1 get /pkdy exch def
    3 get /pkdt exch def
    /pkdhw pkwidth pkdt pkpressure mul 2 div def
    pkdhw 0 gt { pkdx pkdy pkdhw 0 360 arc closepath } if
} bind def

% start end side reverse pkloop -   (one closed offset loop, no caps)
% `reverse` must be opposite between the two loops of a ring (right
% and left side of the same closed centerline): offsetting doesn't
% change the underlying curve's own traversal direction, so building
% both loops start-to-end gives them the *same* winding sign under
% nonzero fill and they add instead of one punching a hole in the
% other -- confirmed empirically (a closed square rendered solid, no
% ring) before this fix (Codex review, round 1).
% @internal
/pkloop {
    /pklrev exch def /pklside exch def /pklend exch def /pklstart exch def
    /pklfrom pklrev { pklend } { pklstart } ifelse def
    /pklto pklrev { pklstart } { pklend } ifelse def
    /pklstep pklrev { -1 } { 1 } ifelse def
    pkraw pklfrom get
    dup 0 get /pklx exch def dup 1 get /pkly exch def
    dup 2 get /pklang exch def 3 get /pklt exch def
    pklx pkly pklang pklt pkhalfwat pklside pkedge moveto
    pklfrom pklstep add pklstep pklto {
        /pkli exch def
        pkraw pkli get
        dup 0 get /pklx exch def dup 1 get /pkly exch def
        dup 2 get /pklang exch def 3 get /pklt exch def
        pklx pkly pklang pklt pkhalfwat pklside pkedge lineto
    } for
    closepath
} bind def

% start end pkopenrun -   (one open ribbon polygon with caps)
% A cap degrades to /pointed automatically only when its half-width is
% truly <= 0, not some small-but-scale-fragile epsilon: a zero-radius
% `arc` is well-defined and safe in both pscat and Ghostscript
% (verified directly), so there's nothing to guard against by treating
% a merely-small-in-*user-space* width as zero -- that would wrongly
% collapse a real cap to a point under a large CTM, where a user-space
% width like 0.002 can still be many device pixels wide (Codex review,
% round 1).
% @internal
/pkopenrun {
    /pkoend exch def /pkostart exch def

    pkraw pkostart get
    dup 0 get /pkox0 exch def dup 1 get /pkoy0 exch def
    dup 2 get /pkoang0 exch def 3 get /pkot0 exch def
    /pkohw0 pkot0 pkhalfwat def
    /pkostartpointed
        pkstartcap /pointed eq pkohw0 0 le or
    def

    pkraw pkoend get
    dup 0 get /pkoxe exch def dup 1 get /pkoye exch def
    dup 2 get /pkoange exch def 3 get /pkotend exch def
    /pkohwe pkotend pkhalfwat def
    /pkoendpointed
        pkendcap /pointed eq pkohwe 0 le or
    def
    /pkorightlast pkoendpointed { pkoend 1 sub } { pkoend } ifelse def

    % Both ends pointed with no interior stops between them (walkpath
    % emits only start+end for a subpath shorter than /Pitch): the
    % straight start-tip-to-end-tip line this would otherwise build has
    % zero area under `fill`, silently rendering nothing for a valid
    % short pointed (or fully-tapered) stroke. Synthesize one interior
    % sample at the run's midpoint -- chord direction (exact regardless
    % of the underlying curve, since there's nothing between the two
    % known points to look at) and averaged progress -- so it renders
    % as a lens instead (Codex review, round 3).
    pkostartpointed pkoendpointed and pkorightlast pkostart le and {
        % The chord itself can be zero-length here -- an open subpath
        % that returns exactly to its own start (an unclosed full-circle
        % arc, a lineto back to the exact starting point) and is also
        % shorter than /Pitch has pkox0==pkoxe/pkoy0==pkoye, and `atan`
        % on (0,0) is undefined in both pscat and Ghostscript (Codex
        % review, round 6). No chord direction exists to synthesize a
        % midpoint from in that case; fall back to a dot, same as any
        % other genuinely degenerate short run.
        /pkomdx pkoxe pkox0 sub def
        /pkomdy pkoye pkoy0 sub def
        pkomdx 0 eq pkomdy 0 eq and
        {
            pkraw pkostart get pkdot
        } {
            /pkomidx pkox0 pkoxe add 2 div def
            /pkomidy pkoy0 pkoye add 2 div def
            /pkomidang pkomdy pkomdx atan def
            /pkomidt pkot0 pkotend add 2 div def
            pkox0 pkoy0 moveto
            pkomidx pkomidy pkomidang pkomidt pkhalfwat -1 pkedge lineto
            pkoxe pkoye lineto
            pkomidx pkomidy pkomidang pkomidt pkhalfwat 1 pkedge lineto
            closepath
        } ifelse
    } {
        pkostartpointed
            { pkox0 pkoy0 moveto }
            { pkox0 pkoy0 pkoang0 pkohw0 -1 pkedge moveto }
        ifelse

        pkostart 1 add 1 pkorightlast {
            /pkoi exch def
            pkraw pkoi get
            dup 0 get /pkoix exch def dup 1 get /pkoiy exch def
            dup 2 get /pkoiang exch def 3 get /pkoit exch def
            pkoix pkoiy pkoiang pkoit pkhalfwat -1 pkedge lineto
        } for

        pkoendpointed {
            pkoxe pkoye lineto
        } {
            pkendcap /round eq {
                pkoxe pkoye pkohwe pkoange 90 sub pkoange 90 add arc
            } {
                pkoxe pkoye pkoange pkohwe 1 pkedge lineto
            } ifelse
        } ifelse

        /pkoleftfirst pkostartpointed { pkostart 1 add } { pkostart } ifelse def
        pkoend 1 sub -1 pkoleftfirst {
            /pkoi exch def
            pkraw pkoi get
            dup 0 get /pkoix exch def dup 1 get /pkoiy exch def
            dup 2 get /pkoiang exch def 3 get /pkoit exch def
            pkoix pkoiy pkoiang pkoit pkhalfwat 1 pkedge lineto
        } for

        pkostartpointed { } {
            pkstartcap /round eq {
                pkox0 pkoy0 pkohw0 pkoang0 90 add pkoang0 270 add arc
            } if
        } ifelse
        closepath
    } ifelse
} bind def

% start end closed pkbuildrun -   (one subpath's worth of walkpath stops;
% closed comes from pkscanclosed, not inferred from coordinates)
% @internal
/pkbuildrun {
    /pkbrclosed exch def /pkbrend exch def /pkbrstart exch def
    % Coalesce a trailing sp==0 duplicate (the guaranteed end stop
    % landing exactly on the last regular pitch-multiple stop, per
    % walkpath's own documented behavior) up front, before anything
    % below decides whether a genuine interior sample exists -- a
    % stroke whose length happens to be an exact multiple of /Pitch
    % would otherwise look like it has one real sample past the start
    % when both remaining candidates actually sit at the same position
    % (Codex review, round 4). Always safe: the only way to reach a
    % 2-stop run (pkbrend == pkbrstart+1) is a subpath shorter than one
    % pitch step, whose second stop's sp is the subpath's positive
    % length, not 0, so this never collapses a run down past pkbrend's
    % immediate predecessor.
    pkbrend pkbrstart gt pkraw pkbrend get 4 get 0 eq and {
        /pkbrend pkbrend 1 sub def
    } if
    pkbrstart pkbrend eq pkraw pkbrstart get 5 get 3 eq and {
        pkraw pkbrstart get pkdot
    } {
        pkraw pkbrstart get 0 get /pkbrx0 exch def
        pkraw pkbrstart get 1 get /pkbry0 exch def
        pkbrclosed {
            % Even after the dedup above, a closed run whose *total*
            % length is under one pitch step never gets a genuine
            % intermediate sample -- walkpath emits only its start and
            % the guaranteed end, and for a closed subpath the end
            % *always* returns to the start's own coordinates (that's
            % what closed means), so pkbrend's position is never a
            % useful signal here -- comparing it against pkbrstart is
            % comparing a closed loop's end to itself, always equal by
            % construction, regardless of whether real content exists
            % in between. What actually matters is whether at least one
            % *index* strictly between pkbrstart and pkbrend exists
            % (pkbrend >= pkbrstart+2) -- a real regular stop from
            % walkpath's own arc-length stepping, which can only differ
            % from the start position if genuine path length separates
            % them.
            /pkbrhasinterior pkbrend pkbrstart 1 add gt def
            pkbrhasinterior {
                pkbrstart pkbrend -1 false pkloop
                pkbrstart pkbrend 1 true pkloop
            } {
                pkraw pkbrstart get pkdot
            } ifelse
        } {
            pkbrstart pkbrend pkopenrun
        } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def

% opts pkribbon -
%
% Self-tests: `pscat --selftest lib/paintkit.ps`. Each block below
% pins a defect cross-model review actually found on PR #76, so a
% guard can't be dropped or weakened without something going red --
% see docs/dev/SELFTEST.md for the convention and the defect map.
%
%%SelfTest: pkribbon-rejects-a-non-callable-pressure
%   % PR #76 round 2: a bare number as /Pressure is never
%   % auto-executed, so it silently leaks an extra operand into every
%   % downstream computation instead of raising.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /Pressure 5 >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-pressure-must-be-a-procedure
%   (a bare number as /Pressure) mustguard
%
%   % Round 3: `xcheck` alone isn't enough -- an executable
%   % non-procedure passes it and corrupts the stack exactly the same
%   % way, so the guard has to check `type` too.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /Pressure 2 cvx >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-pressure-must-be-a-procedure
%   (an executable non-procedure as /Pressure) mustguard
%
%   % ...while the documented form still has to work.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /Pressure { pkflat } >> pkribbon }
%   (a procedure literal as /Pressure) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkribbon-rejects-executable-value-options
%   % Round 7: /Width and friends are documented as plain values, so an
%   % executable one is malformed input -- and a bare reference to it
%   % would auto-run before any check saw it.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /Width { 10 } >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-width-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (an executable /Width) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /Pitch { 3 } >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (an executable /Pitch) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /StartCap { /round } >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-startcap-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (an executable /StartCap) mustguard
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkribbon-rejects-undocumented-cap-values
%   % Round 2: anything outside the three documented values used to
%   % fall through to /flat silently.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /StartCap /square >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-startcap-must-be-round-flat-or-pointed
%   (/square as a start cap) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /EndCap /square >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-endcap-must-be-round-flat-or-pointed
%   (/square as an end cap) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /StartCap /pointed /EndCap /flat >> pkribbon }
%   (the documented cap values) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkribbon-rejects-out-of-range-tapers
%   % Round 5: outside 0..1 doesn't crash, it just degrades silently --
%   % a contract violation the caller never hears about.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /StartTaper 2 >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-starttaper-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a /StartTaper above 1) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /EndTaper -0.5 >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-endtaper-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a negative /EndTaper) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /StartTaper 0.25 /EndTaper 1 >> pkribbon }
%   (tapers at the ends of the documented range) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkribbon-rejects-non-positive-width-and-pitch
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /Width 0 >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-width-must-be-positive
%   (a zero /Width) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto << /Pitch -1 >> pkribbon }
%   /pkribbon-pitch-must-be-positive
%   (a negative /Pitch) mustguard
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkribbon-survives-a-degenerate-pointed-subpath
%   % Round 6: an open subpath shorter than /Pitch that returns exactly
%   % to its own start has no chord direction, and `atan` on (0,0) is
%   % undefined in both pscat and Ghostscript. This must degrade to a
%   % dot, not raise.
%   { newpath 20 20 moveto 20 20 lineto
%     << /Width 6 /StartCap /pointed /EndCap /pointed >> pkribbon }
%   (a zero-length pointed subpath) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pre-walkpath-scans-share-walkpaths-segment-bound
%   % Issue #299: pkribbon, pknib and pkfeather scan the path before
%   % walkpath, via pkscanclosed, which carries the same wkmaxsegs bound.
%   % A moveto, (wkmaxsegs - 2) zero-length linetos and one real lineto are
%   % exactly wkmaxsegs visits; one more zero-length lineto is one over.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto wkmaxsegs 2 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat 40 0 rlineto
%     << /Pitch 50 >> pkribbon }
%   (pkribbon at exactly wkmaxsegs segments) mustpass
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto wkmaxsegs 2 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat 40 0 rlineto
%     << /Pitch 50 >> pknib }
%   (pknib at exactly wkmaxsegs segments) mustpass
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto wkmaxsegs 1 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat 40 0 rlineto
%     << >> pkribbon }
%   /walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (pkribbon one segment over wkmaxsegs) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto wkmaxsegs 1 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat 40 0 rlineto
%     << >> pknib }
%   /walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (pknib one segment over wkmaxsegs) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto wkmaxsegs 1 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat 40 0 rlineto
%     << >> pkfeather }
%   /walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (pkfeather one segment over wkmaxsegs) mustguard
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pre-walkpath-scans-refuse-before-walkpath-runs
%   % The block above passes on a tree without the scan bound too, because
%   % walkpath's own pass refuses the same path. Here walkpath is replaced by
%   % a stub (each block runs in a fresh interpreter), so only a refusal from
%   % the scan itself passes.
%   /tgot false def
%   /walkpath { pop pop /tgot true def } def
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto wkmaxsegs 1 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat 40 0 rlineto
%     << >> pkribbon }
%   /walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (pkribbon is refused by its scan) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto wkmaxsegs 1 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat 40 0 rlineto
%     << >> pknib }
%   /walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (pknib is refused by its scan) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto wkmaxsegs 1 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat 40 0 rlineto
%     << >> pkfeather }
%   /walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (pkfeather is refused by its scan) mustguard
%   tgot not (walkpath was never reached) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Treats the current path as a centerline and fills a variable-width ribbon along it, built on walkpath. Color comes from whatever the caller already set.
% @example: newpath ... << /Width 10 /Pressure { pktaper } >> pkribbon
% @param: /Width [points] Base width; must be > 0 (default 6)
% @param: /Pitch [points] walkpath sampling pitch; must be > 0 (default Width*0.5, capped at 6)
% @param: /Pressure [proc] {t -> mult} proc over normalized path progress; must be a procedure (pkflat/pktaper/pkbell presets ship) (default { pkflat })
% @param: /StartTaper [fraction of path progress] 0..1 fraction of progress to ramp width in from the start, independent of Pressure (default 0)
% @param: /EndTaper [fraction of path progress] 0..1 fraction of progress to ramp width out at the end, independent of Pressure (default 0)
% @param: /StartCap [name] /round, /flat, or /pointed; any other value is rejected (default /round)
% @param: /EndCap [name] /round, /flat, or /pointed; any other value is rejected (default /round)
% @param: /Jitter [points] Seeded edge displacement amount, deterministic under the caller's own N srand (default 0)
/pkribbon {
    /pkopts exch def
    % Value options (/Width here, and /Pitch/StartTaper/EndTaper/Jitter/
    % StartCap/EndCap below) must not themselves be executable: binding
    % one straight to its own name the way pkgetdef's result normally
    % is makes every later *bare* reference to that name auto-execute
    % it instead of yielding the value, since a name bound to something
    % executable always runs on reference -- the same trap /Pressure's
    % own guard exists for (see below), but these fields are documented
    % as plain values, not callbacks, so an executable one (e.g.
    % `<< /Width { 10 } >>`) is malformed input, not something to
    % support (Codex review, round 7). Checked with `load`, same
    % reason as /Pressure: a bare `pkwidth xcheck` would itself
    % auto-execute pkwidth before xcheck ever saw it.
    /pkwidth pkopts /Width 6 pkgetdef def
    /pkwidth load xcheck { pkribbon-width-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pkwidth 0 le { pkribbon-width-must-be-positive } if
    /pkpitch
        pkopts /Pitch
        pkwidth 0.5 mul dup 6 gt { pop 6 } if
        pkgetdef
    def
    /pkpitch load xcheck { pkribbon-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pkpitch 0 le { pkribbon-pitch-must-be-positive } if
    % /Pressure must actually be a procedure: pkhalfwat calls it as
    % `t pkpressure`, expecting it to consume t and leave one number.
    % A non-procedure value (e.g. a bare number) is never auto-executed
    % -- it just gets pushed instead of run -- leaking extra operands
    % into every downstream computation instead of raising a clean
    % error (Codex review, round 2). Checked with `/pkpressure load
    % xcheck`, not a bare `pkpressure xcheck`: pkpressure is *already*
    % bound to a procedure in the common case, and a bare reference to
    % a name bound to an executable auto-runs it -- the exact trap
    % `pkgetdef`'s own header comment documents -- which would call the
    % default pressure proc with nothing to operate on before `xcheck`
    % ever saw it. `load` fetches the bound value without executing it,
    % regardless of its type. `xcheck` alone isn't sufficient either
    % (Codex review, round 3): it only tests the executable attribute,
    % and an executable non-procedure (e.g. `2 cvx`) passes it despite
    % `pkht pkpressure` just pushing the number instead of running
    % anything, same corruption as an unvalidated plain number -- so
    % `type` must also be an actually-callable kind: `arraytype` for a
    % user proc, `operatortype` for a bound built-in, or (Codex review,
    % round 5) `packedarraytype` -- under a Level 2 interpreter with
    % packing enabled (`true setpacking`; confirmed against real
    % Ghostscript, though pscat itself doesn't actually pack), a plain
    % procedure literal like the /Pressure default itself, `{ pkflat }`,
    % can legitimately have this type instead of `arraytype`, and
    % rejecting it would make `pkribbon` fail even on its own default.
    /pkpressure pkopts /Pressure { pkflat } pkgetdef def
    /pkpressure load xcheck
    /pkpressure load type
    dup /arraytype eq
    exch dup /operatortype eq
    exch /packedarraytype eq
    or or
    and
    not { pkribbon-pressure-must-be-a-procedure } if
    % /StartTaper//EndTaper: the documented range is 0..1 -- outside it
    % doesn't crash (pktaperf's guard conditions just degrade: negative
    % silently disables the ramp, above 1 keeps the whole stroke short
    % of full width) but that's a real contract violation, not merely a
    % style choice, so it's validated the same as every other documented
    % constraint here (Codex review, round 5).
    /pkstarttaper pkopts /StartTaper 0 pkgetdef def
    /pkstarttaper load xcheck { pkribbon-starttaper-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pkstarttaper 0 lt pkstarttaper 1 gt or
        { pkribbon-starttaper-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pkendtaper pkopts /EndTaper 0 pkgetdef def
    /pkendtaper load xcheck { pkribbon-endtaper-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pkendtaper 0 lt pkendtaper 1 gt or
        { pkribbon-endtaper-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    % /StartCap//EndCap: anything other than the three documented
    % values used to fall through to /flat silently (Codex review,
    % round 2) -- validated explicitly instead. Checked for executable-
    % ness first, same reason as /Width above: an executable value
    % (even one that happens to push a valid cap name when run) isn't
    % itself one of the three documented literal values.
    /pkstartcap pkopts /StartCap /round pkgetdef def
    /pkstartcap load xcheck { pkribbon-startcap-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pkstartcap /round eq pkstartcap /flat eq or pkstartcap /pointed eq or not
        { pkribbon-startcap-must-be-round-flat-or-pointed } if
    /pkendcap pkopts /EndCap /round pkgetdef def
    /pkendcap load xcheck { pkribbon-endcap-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pkendcap /round eq pkendcap /flat eq or pkendcap /pointed eq or not
        { pkribbon-endcap-must-be-round-flat-or-pointed } if
    /pkjitter pkopts /Jitter 0 pkgetdef def
    /pkjitter load xcheck { pkribbon-jitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if

    % Scan first, name second: the bound (issue #299) can raise inside the
    % scan, and a name pushed ahead of it would be left on the stack.
    pkscanclosed /pkclosedflags exch def

    % Two walkpath passes over the same (surviving, per its own
    % contract) flattened path -- count, then fill a preallocated
    % array -- same shape as artkit's own wkmeasure/wkseg split.
    % Tried a one-pass `[ pitch { 6 array astore } walkpath ]` first:
    % it looks like it should work (walkpath's own state lives in
    % named vars, not the stack), and it does for a single-segment
    % path, but it breaks on a second subpath. walkpath's own moveto
    % handler calls `wkend` (emitting the previous subpath's guaranteed
    % end stop through wkproc) *before* consuming the x/y pathforall
    % just pushed it for the new subpath -- so a wkproc that leaves an
    % array sitting on the stack instead of consuming it steals that
    % x/y out from under it. Confirmed empirically against plain
    % walkpath, no paintkit involved. A proc that fully consumes its 6
    % inputs every call (store-by-index, net stack effect zero) sidesteps
    % it regardless of how deep it's nested in someone else's callback.
    /pkcount 0 def
    pkpitch { pop pop pop pop pop pop /pkcount pkcount 1 add def } walkpath
    /pkraw pkcount array def
    /pkci 0 def
    pkpitch {
        6 array astore pkraw pkci 3 -1 roll put
        /pkci pkci 1 add def
    } walkpath
    /pkn pkraw length def

    pkn 0 gt {
        gsave
        newpath
        /pki 0 def
        /pkrunstart 0 def
        /pksubidx 0 def
        {
            pki pkn ge { exit } if
            pkraw pki get 5 get /pkae exch def
            pkae 1 and 1 eq { /pkrunstart pki def } if
            pkae 2 and 2 eq {
                pkrunstart pki pkclosedflags pksubidx get pkbuildrun
                /pksubidx pksubidx 1 add def
            } if
            /pki pki 1 add def
        } loop
        fill
        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% --- angled-nib calligraphy brush (issue #42) -------------------------
% opts pknib -
% A chisel/broad-edge calligraphic-nib preset built on `pkribbon`: mark
% width is driven by the angle between the path's local direction of
% travel and a fixed nib angle (`/Angle`), the same physical model as a
% real flat-nib pen -- widest where travel runs perpendicular to the
% nib, narrowing toward a hairline where travel runs parallel to it
% (`width ~ |sin(travel - Angle)|`). Pressure, taper, caps, and seeded
% jitter all still come straight from `pkribbon` (the brush core) and
% compose multiplicatively with the nib-angle response.
%
%   opts pknib -
%
% The current path must be exactly one *open* subpath -- a nib stroke
% has a start and an end by definition, the same as lifting a real pen
% off the page; a closed loop has no ends to cap or taper, and more
% than one subpath has no single well-defined travel direction for
% `/Pressure` to reset against at each one's own t=0. Both are rejected
% with a clear error rather than guessing which subpath is "the"
% stroke or silently treating a closed loop as open. Draw multiple
% strokes by calling `pknib` once per stroke -- the natural mental
% model for a pen that's picked up and set down again. An empty path
% is a no-op, same as `pkribbon`.
%
% Approximation, stated plainly: this varies *width* with travel angle
% but still offsets edges perpendicular to travel (via `pkribbon`'s own
% `pkedge`) and caps perpendicular to travel too -- a real broad nib
% instead shears its edges along the fixed nib axis and leaves a flat
% end-cut *at* the nib angle, regardless of travel direction. That
% distinction shows up mainly at corners and cut ends, not along a
% stroke's body. The issue's own framing -- "derive mark width from the
% relationship between path direction and a configurable nib angle" --
% asks for the width response, which this delivers exactly; the sheared
% -edge/angled-cut refinement is a real, deliberately out-of-scope next
% step for a later revision, not an oversight.
%
% See the `@param` tags directly above `pknib`'s own definition below
% for the full parameter list with defaults. Two caveats the terse
% tags don't carry: /MinWidth is a floor on the nib-angle response
% itself (not a floor on the final rendered width) -- the multiplier
% `|sin(travel - Angle)|` never drops below it before /Pressure and the
% tapers multiply through, modeling a real nib's own finite edge
% thickness rather than a mathematically perfect zero-width hairline;
% and /Pressure multiplies with the nib-angle response rather than
% replacing it, the same kind of composition /StartTaper//EndTaper
% already have with /Pressure inside `pkribbon` itself.
%
% Implementation: a preliminary `walkpath` pass, at the same pitch
% `pkribbon` would use, samples (t, travel angle) once per stop; a
% synthesized `/Pressure` proc installed on a shallow copy of `opts`
% looks up the *nearest* sample's angle for a given t -- no
% interpolation, deliberately: a real chisel nib's width genuinely
% jumps at a sharp corner, so smoothing across one would be less
% faithful, not more, and it sidesteps degrees-wraparound interpolation
% entirely -- and folds in the nib-angle multiplier before calling
% through to the caller's own /Pressure. `pkribbon` itself never
% inspects /Angle//MinWidth -- unknown dict keys are already ignored
% elsewhere in this file (`pkgetdef` only reads keys it's told to), so
% the shallow copy doesn't need to strip them. `pkribbon` re-walks the
% same (already-flattened, so identically flattened both times) path
% itself when it actually fills, at the same default pitch when the
% caller didn't override /Pitch -- its own stops land at the same t
% values this pass already sampled.
% Each pnangles entry is [t x y ang] -- x/y are needed only for the
% two-sample chord fallback below, not the ordinary nearest-sample path.
% @internal
/pnangleat {                                      % t -> travel angle (degrees)
    /pnqt exch def
    pnangles 0 get
    dup 0 get pnqt sub abs /pnbestd exch def
    3 get /pnbestang exch def
    1 1 pnangles length 1 sub {
        /pnqi exch def
        pnangles pnqi get
        dup 0 get pnqt sub abs /pnqd exch def
        3 get /pnqang exch def
        pnqd pnbestd lt {
            /pnbestd pnqd def
            /pnbestang pnqang def
        } if
    } for
    % A run with exactly two raw samples (start+end, no interior stop
    % -- a subpath shorter than one pitch step) has nothing at t values
    % strictly between them. pkopenrun's own short-run synthesis (both
    % ends pointed or zero-width) fills that gap with one interior
    % bulge point, evaluated at the *chord's* own direction, not either
    % endpoint's travel angle -- so a query for that synthesized point
    % previously fell back to whichever endpoint's t happened to be
    % numerically closer, an arbitrary and sometimes very wrong answer
    % (a short symmetric arch with a horizontal midpoint tangent could
    % render at nearly full width instead of the requested near-
    % hairline; Codex review, PR #77, round 2). pkribbon only ever
    % evaluates /Pressure at a raw stop's own t or at that one
    % synthesized t, so a query that doesn't closely match either of
    % our two raw samples can only be the synthesized point -- switch
    % to the chord direction then, computed the same way pkopenrun
    % computes it. A coincident-endpoint chord (an open path with
    % positive arc length that still returns to its own start -- same
    % edge case pkopenrun's own round-6 fix guards) has no direction to
    % synthesize either; fall back to the nearest-sample angle already
    % computed above rather than call `atan` on a zero chord.
    pnangles length 2 eq pnbestd 1.0e-6 gt and {
        pnangles 0 get /pnq0 exch def
        pnangles 1 get /pnq1 exch def
        pnq1 1 get pnq0 1 get sub /pnqdx exch def
        pnq1 2 get pnq0 2 get sub /pnqdy exch def
        pnqdx 0 eq pnqdy 0 eq and
            { pnbestang }
            { pnqdy pnqdx atan }
        ifelse
    } {
        pnbestang
    } ifelse
} bind def

% t -> mult   (installed as pkribbon's own /Pressure; reads
% pnangle/pnminwidth/pnuserpressure/pnangles, set fresh by pknib)
% A single-sample pnangles table (pnn/pnangles length 1) means the
% source subpath was a degenerate single point -- walkpath's own
% contract guarantees any subpath with real length gets at least two
% stops, so this is an unambiguous signal, not a heuristic. A lone
% point has no direction of travel (walkpath reports a synthetic
% ang=0 for it, not a real one), so the nib-angle response has nothing
% meaningful to attenuate against; using it anyway made the degenerate-
% point dot fallback either vanish (Angle 0, MinWidth 0) or resize
% arbitrarily with Angle, silently breaking pkribbon's own documented
% "degenerate paths fall back to a dot" contract this preset is
% supposed to preserve (Codex review, PR #77). Skip the nib multiplier
% entirely in that case -- full response, same as if travel were
% perfectly perpendicular to the nib -- so the dot's size tracks only
% /Pressure, exactly like pkribbon's own undiminished pkdot.
% @internal
/pnpressure {
    /pnpt exch def
    pnangles length 1 eq
        { 1.0 }
        {
            pnpt pnangleat pnangle sub sin abs
            dup pnminwidth lt { pop pnminwidth } if
        }
    ifelse
    pnpt pnuserpressure mul
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: An angled-nib calligraphy preset built on pkribbon: mark width is driven by the angle between local path direction and a fixed nib angle, widest perpendicular to it and narrowing toward MinWidth parallel to it. The current path must be exactly one open subpath -- call once per stroke.
% @example: newpath ... << /Width 16 /Angle 30 >> pknib
% @param: /Angle [degrees] Nib angle in degrees; perpendicular to it is widest, parallel narrows toward MinWidth (default 45)
% @param: /MinWidth [fraction of /Width] 0..1 floor on the nib-angle response itself, before Pressure and the tapers multiply through (default 0.08)
% @param: /Width [points] Base width, forwarded to pkribbon (default 6)
% @param: /Pitch [points] walkpath sampling pitch, forwarded to pkribbon (default Width*0.5, capped at 6)
% @param: /Pressure [proc] {t -> mult} proc, multiplies with the nib-angle response rather than replacing it (default { pkflat })
% @param: /StartTaper [fraction of path progress] 0..1 fraction of progress to ramp width in from the start, forwarded to pkribbon (default 0)
% @param: /EndTaper [fraction of path progress] 0..1 fraction of progress to ramp width out at the end, forwarded to pkribbon (default 0)
% @param: /StartCap [name] /round, /flat, or /pointed, forwarded to pkribbon (default /round)
% @param: /EndCap [name] /round, /flat, or /pointed, forwarded to pkribbon (default /round)
% @param: /Jitter [points] Seeded edge displacement amount, forwarded to pkribbon (default 0)
/pknib {
    /pnopts exch def

    % One flag per subpath, from pkscanclosed, which is also the
    % segment-count bound (issue #299): a pathforall of its own here
    % would scan a pathological path in full before walkpath's bound
    % could fire.
    pkscanclosed /pnflags exch def
    /pnmoveto pnflags length def /pnclosed false def
    pnflags { { /pnclosed true def } if } forall
    pnmoveto 1 gt { pknib-path-must-be-a-single-subpath } if
    pnclosed { pknib-path-must-not-be-closed } if

    % Same Width/Pitch validation pkribbon itself does, and -- like
    % pkribbon -- run unconditionally, not only when the path is
    % non-empty: pkribbon validates its whole opts dict before ever
    % looking at the path, so a malformed dict (wrong types, a bare
    % non-dict operand) errors the same way regardless of what's on
    % the path. Gating validation behind `pnmoveto 0 gt` let a
    % malformed opts dict through silently whenever the path happened
    % to be empty -- `newpath 42 pknib` never even read `pnopts` as a
    % dict, unlike pkribbon's own `typecheck` for the same call (Codex
    % review, PR #77). Only the sampling/rendering work below is
    % actually conditional on a non-empty path.
    /pnwidth pnopts /Width 6 pkgetdef def
    /pnwidth load xcheck { pknib-width-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pnwidth 0 le { pknib-width-must-be-positive } if
    /pnpitch
        pnopts /Pitch
        pnwidth 0.5 mul dup 6 gt { pop 6 } if
        pkgetdef
    def
    /pnpitch load xcheck { pknib-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pnpitch 0 le { pknib-pitch-must-be-positive } if

    /pnangle pnopts /Angle 45 pkgetdef def
    /pnangle load xcheck { pknib-angle-must-not-be-a-procedure } if

    /pnminwidth pnopts /MinWidth 0.08 pkgetdef def
    /pnminwidth load xcheck { pknib-minwidth-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pnminwidth 0 lt pnminwidth 1 gt or { pknib-minwidth-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    % Same /Pressure validation as pkribbon's own (Codex rounds 2/3/5
    % there): must actually be callable, not just executable, since
    % pnpressure above calls it by bare reference expecting it to
    % consume t and leave one number.
    /pnuserpressure pnopts /Pressure { pkflat } pkgetdef def
    /pnuserpressure load xcheck
    /pnuserpressure load type
    dup /arraytype eq
    exch dup /operatortype eq
    exch /packedarraytype eq
    or or
    and
    not { pknib-pressure-must-be-a-procedure } if

    % Unconditional, like the validation above: pkribbon validates
    % *every* forwarded field (including /StartTaper//EndTaper//
    % StartCap//EndCap//Jitter, which pknib itself never checks) before
    % looking at whether the path is empty, and its own `pkn 0 gt`
    % guard already handles the actual no-op correctly -- gating this
    % block behind `pnmoveto 0 gt` let those forwarded-only fields
    % through unvalidated on an empty path (Codex review, PR #77,
    % round 2). Sampling an empty path is harmless (walkpath's own
    % callback never fires, same as pkribbon's own width-computation
    % pass over an empty path), so nothing here needs a separate guard.
    /pnn 0 def
    pnpitch { pop pop pop pop pop pop /pnn pnn 1 add def } walkpath
    /pnangles pnn array def
    /pnj 0 def
    pnpitch {
        /pnaatend exch def /pnasp exch def /pnat exch def
        /pnaang exch def /pnay exch def /pnax exch def
        pnangles pnj pnat pnax pnay pnaang 4 array astore put
        /pnj pnj 1 add def
    } walkpath

    /pnribbonopts pnopts length 1 add dict def
    pnopts pnribbonopts copy pop
    pnribbonopts /Pressure /pnpressure load put
    pnribbonopts pkribbon
} bind def

% --- dry-bristle brush (issue #43) ------------------------------------
% opts pkdry -
% A bounded family of thin offset bristles scattered across the
% centerline, each broken into its own ink/no-ink runs by a seeded
% two-state Markov chain -- the physical model of a brush that's
% running low on paint: some bristles keep full contact, others skip
% and catch unevenly, the breakup tracking the path itself rather than
% reading as unrelated page noise. Ranges from a mostly loaded stroke
% (high /Load, low /Dropout) to visibly broken dry-brush texture (low
% /Load, high /Dropout) with no raster work at all: every dash is a
% small `pkribbon` call, so caps, taper, and a single isolated fleck
% (walkpath's own degenerate single-point fallback -- see pkribbon's
% own dot doc) all come for free.
%
% See the `@param` tags directly above `pkdry`'s own definition below
% for the full parameter list with defaults. Caveats the terse tags
% don't carry: /Width bounds the bristle *scatter*, not one mark's own
% thickness (see /BristleWidth for that); /Bristles is a hard safety
% cap (1..100) chosen so even the cap combined with a long path at the
% default /Pitch stays in the single-digit seconds (see Performance
% below) -- it alone doesn't bound total ink deposits, see the
% deposit-budget check further down for that. /Load and /Dropout are
% both expressed as a rate per one /Width's worth of travel along the
% path, *not* per raw walkpath sample -- scaled down to a per-sample
% transition probability by (Pitch/Width), clamped to 0..1, so the same
% numbers read as the same dryness regardless of /Pitch (loaded: high
% Load, low Dropout; very dry: low Load, high Dropout). A subpath's
% very first sample rolls initial contact with the raw (unscaled) Load
% probability, not the Pitch-scaled per-step rate -- "does this bristle
% start loaded" is a one-time boundary condition, not a rate along the
% path, and it's the *only* roll a degenerate single-point subpath
% (see below) ever gets. /Jitter shares the same seeded random stream
% as the Markov chain above -- changing it from 0 reshuffles every
% downstream on/off roll too, not just the edges, so two renders that
% differ only in /Jitter will generally have a *different breakup
% pattern*, not the same pattern with rougher edges; still fully
% deterministic under the caller's own `N srand` either way.
% /ColorJitter: each bristle applies one `shade` draw, k = 1 +/-
% ColorJitter, around whatever color is active when pkdry is called
% (`currentrgbcolor`) -- consistent with the rest of this file, color
% is deliberately not a settable key here either. 0 disables the
% variation; the caller's own current color is restored before pkdry
% returns.
%
% Determinism: same doctrine as pkribbon -- no library-owned seed,
% reproducible under the caller's own `N srand`.
%
% A degenerate single-point subpath (a bare moveto) has no direction
% of travel to offset perpendicular to (walkpath reports a synthetic
% ang=0, not a real one, same case pknib's own pnpressure guards
% against) -- fanning bristles perpendicular to that arbitrary angle
% would draw a straight line of dots, not a dab. Instead each bristle
% scatters isotropically around the point (both x and y independently
% jittered by its own offset magnitude) so a lone point reads as a
% pressed-down dab cluster, not a line.
%
% Multiple subpaths in the source path each get their own independent
% bristle scatter and Markov runs, same generality as pkribbon. Unlike
% pkribbon, pkdry does not special-case a *truly* closed subpath into
% a two-loop ring -- every subpath, closed or not, is walked once as a
% linear sequence of raw stops for dash purposes, and the Markov chain
% does not wrap across a closed subpath's own seam. A deliberate
% simplification, not an oversight: dashes are discrete marks, not a
% continuous offset band, so there's no ring geometry to get right,
% and not wrapping keeps the seam no more special-cased than any other
% sample.
%
% Performance: the dominant cost is the per-sample Markov walk itself
% (O(Bristles * PathLength/Pitch) loop iterations, each with a handful
% of small `pkribbon` calls layered on top), not raster work. The
% `Bristles` * (raw stop count) product is checked against a fixed
% budget (150000) before any drawing starts
% (`pkdry-deposit-count-exceeds-safety-limit`) -- the documented safety
% limit against an accidentally unbounded bristle or deposit count.
% Measured on a ~700-unit curved stroke (the demo's flourish, Width
% 24): the default (18 bristles, default Pitch) renders in ~1.5s; the
% /Bristles safety cap (100) at the same default Pitch takes ~8s.
% Custom, finer /Pitch values raise the sample count and cost
% proportionally -- see docs/archive/NOTES.md's issue #43 entry for the full
% figures.
%
% Cusp guard (issue #79): the stop budget above doesn't see the cost of
% each dash's nested pkribbon call, which re-walks its offset polyline
% at /Pitch -- at a near-180 degree tangent reversal adjacent stops'
% offsets can be O(Width) apart. The counting pass therefore also sums
% a geometric bound on every adjacent-offset distance (see the comment
% there) and raises `pkdry-cusp-resample-count-exceeds-safety-limit`
% past a 60000-sample allowance, before any drawing. Straight and smooth
% strokes add ~0, so pkdry's defaults and large-Width smooth strokes
% are unaffected.
%
%%SelfTest: pkdry-touch-guards
%   % The no-canvas cases first: nothing in this block has set a surface yet.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 0.5 >> pkdry }
%   /pkdry-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch 0.5 before any pkcanvas) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 1 >> pkdry }
%   /pkdry-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch 1 before any pkcanvas: any /Touch needs a canvas) mustguard
%
%   << >> pkcanvas
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 1.5 >> pkdry }
%   /pkdry-touch-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Touch 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch { } >> pkdry }
%   /pkdry-touch-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Touch { }) mustguard
%
%   % A coarse /Pitch keeps the deposit budget out of the way.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto
%     << /Touch 0.5 /Pitch 1000 /Bristles 2 >> pkdry }
%   /pkdry-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a 1e5pt stroke at /Touch 0.5) mustguard
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 0.5 >> pkdry grestore }
%   (/Touch 0.5 with a canvas) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto << /Touch 0 >> pkdry grestore }
%   (/Touch 0 on a dab) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkdry-cusp-guard-rejects-reversals-but-not-straight-strokes
%   % Issue #79: a sharp cusp at a huge Width/Pitch ratio is rejected
%   % in the counting pass; the same Width on a straight stroke is not.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 1 0 lineto 1 1 lineto
%     << /Width 100000 /Bristles 2 /Pitch 1 /Load 1 /Dropout 0 >> pkdry }
%   /pkdry-cusp-resample-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a right-angle cusp at Width/Pitch = 100000) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100 0 lineto
%     << /Width 100000 /Bristles 2 /Pitch 1 >> pkdry }
%   (a huge Width on a straight stroke) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
% Scratch prefix pb- (pkdry/pbdashrun) -- deliberately distinct from
% pk-/pn-, see the file header.
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A dry-bristle brush built on pkribbon: a bounded family of thin offset bristles scattered across the centerline, each broken into ink/no-ink runs by a seeded two-state Markov chain, ranging from a mostly loaded stroke to visibly broken dry-brush texture with no raster work.
% @example: newpath ... << /Width 16 /Load 0.9 /Dropout 0.1 >> pkdry
% @param: /Width [points] Envelope width the bristles scatter across; must be > 0 (default 6)
% @param: /Bristles [count] Bristle count; must be a whole number, hard safety cap 1..100 (default 18)
% @param: /Spread [fraction of /Width] 0..1 fraction of Width the bristles scatter across, centered on the centerline (default 0.85)
% @param: /BristleWidth [points] Base width of each individual bristle's own mark; must be > 0 (default Width*0.12)
% @param: /WidthJitter [fraction of /BristleWidth] 0..1 fraction of BristleWidth each bristle's own width randomly varies by (default 0.4)
% @param: /Load [per /Width] 0..1 resume-contact rate per one Width of travel along the path (default 0.6)
% @param: /Dropout [per /Width] 0..1 lose-contact rate per one Width of travel along the path (default 0.4)
% @param: /Jitter [points] Edge roughness, forwarded verbatim as each dash's own pkribbon Jitter; shares the Markov chain's random stream (default 0)
% @param: /Pitch [points] walkpath sampling pitch for the shared centerline pass; must be > 0 (default BristleWidth*0.6, capped at 6)
% @param: /ColorJitter [0..1] magnitude of small per-bristle color variation around the color active when pkdry is called (default 0.06)
% @param: /Touch [0..1] How hard the brush presses into the shared canvas set by pkcanvas, which must be called first: below 1 the bristles land only on the weave's knuckles that stand proud enough for that pressure to reach, lower is lighter and 0 deposits nothing; 1 is full contact, identical to leaving it unset. Composes with /Load and /Dropout and takes no rand draws (default unset)
/pkdry {
    /pbopts exch def

    /pbwidth pbopts /Width 6 pkgetdef def
    /pbwidth load xcheck { pkdry-width-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pbwidth 0 le { pkdry-width-must-be-positive } if

    /pbbristles pbopts /Bristles 18 pkgetdef def
    /pbbristles load xcheck { pkdry-bristles-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    % Must be a whole number, not just in range: /Bristles feeds the
    % deposit-budget calculation below (Bristles * raw stop count) as
    % well as the `1 1 pbbristles for` loop bound, and a fractional
    % value like 1.5 would silently truncate through `for`'s own
    % semantics (drawing exactly one bristle) rather than erroring on
    % malformed input, undermining both the documented count and the
    % safety-budget math that assumes an integer (Codex review).
    pbbristles 1 lt pbbristles 100 gt or pbbristles truncate pbbristles ne or
        { pkdry-bristles-must-be-1-to-100 } if

    /pbspread pbopts /Spread 0.85 pkgetdef def
    /pbspread load xcheck { pkdry-spread-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pbspread 0 lt pbspread 1 gt or { pkdry-spread-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pbbristlewidth pbopts /BristleWidth pbwidth 0.12 mul pkgetdef def
    /pbbristlewidth load xcheck
        { pkdry-bristlewidth-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pbbristlewidth 0 le { pkdry-bristlewidth-must-be-positive } if

    /pbwidthjitter pbopts /WidthJitter 0.4 pkgetdef def
    /pbwidthjitter load xcheck
        { pkdry-widthjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pbwidthjitter 0 lt pbwidthjitter 1 gt or
        { pkdry-widthjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pbload pbopts /Load 0.6 pkgetdef def
    /pbload load xcheck { pkdry-load-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pbload 0 lt pbload 1 gt or { pkdry-load-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pbdropout pbopts /Dropout 0.4 pkgetdef def
    /pbdropout load xcheck { pkdry-dropout-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pbdropout 0 lt pbdropout 1 gt or { pkdry-dropout-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pbjitter pbopts /Jitter 0 pkgetdef def
    /pbjitter load xcheck { pkdry-jitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if

    /pbpitch
        pbopts /Pitch
        pbbristlewidth 0.6 mul dup 6 gt { pop 6 } if
        pkgetdef
    def
    /pbpitch load xcheck { pkdry-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pbpitch 0 le { pkdry-pitch-must-be-positive } if

    /pbcolorjitter pbopts /ColorJitter 0.06 pkgetdef def
    /pbcolorjitter load xcheck
        { pkdry-colorjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pbcolorjitter 0 lt pbcolorjitter 1 gt or
        { pkdry-colorjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    % /Touch (issue #222, CANVAS_SURFACE.md 2.2): any /Touch, even 1, needs
    % a canvas. Only a value below 1 builds a catch, so unset and 1 take
    % exactly the path they took before /Touch existed.
    /pbtouch pbopts /Touch 1 pkgetdef def
    /pbtouch load xcheck { pkdry-touch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pbtouch 0 lt pbtouch 1 gt or { pkdry-touch-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    pbopts /Touch known {
        userdict /pjCTM known not { pkdry-touch-needs-a-canvas } if
    } if
    /pbtouched pbtouch 1 lt def

    % Two walkpath passes over the shared centerline, same shape as
    % pkribbon's own pkraw collection (count, then fill) -- reused
    % across every bristle rather than re-walking the source path once
    % per bristle.
    %
    % The documented safety limit -- /Bristles alone (capped at 100
    % above) doesn't bound total deposits, since a long path at a fine
    % /Pitch multiplies out regardless -- is checked *inside* this
    % counting pass, on every stop, not only once after it finishes:
    % checking only afterward would still let a pathologically small
    % /Pitch on a long path run the counting walk itself to completion
    % first (potentially billions of interpreted iterations) before
    % ever rejecting anything, defeating the "before any drawing
    % starts" guarantee (Codex review). 150000 is calibrated against
    % the measured per-iteration cost in the header's Performance note
    % -- comfortably above the /Bristles cap (100) at the default
    % /Pitch on a long (~1500-unit) stroke, while still rejecting a
    % custom /Pitch fine enough to multiply that out by another order
    % of magnitude.
    %
    % Cusp guard (issue #79), same counting pass and so also before any
    % drawing: a dash's nested pkribbon re-walks its offset polyline at
    % /Pitch, and between two adjacent stops whose tangents differ by
    % d degrees the offset points can sit up to 2*maxoff*sin(d/2)
    % apart (maxoff = the widest a bristle's offset can get: its slot
    % in the envelope plus pkjit's 0.15*halfspread, so 1.15*halfspread).
    % That bound is geometry only -- it doesn't depend on the random
    % draws -- and smooth or straight stretches contribute almost
    % nothing, so only a real near-reversal of direction costs.
    % Accumulated in nested samples over Bristles and checked against a
    % dedicated 60000 allowance on top of the 150000 stop budget.
    /pbmaxoff pbwidth 0.5 mul pbspread mul 1.15 mul def
    /pbcextra 0 def
    /pbcprev 0 def
    /pbcount 0 def
    pbpitch {
        /pbcae exch def pop pop /pbcang exch def pop pop
        /pbcount pbcount 1 add def
        pbbristles pbcount mul 150000 gt
            { pkdry-deposit-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
        pbcae 1 and 1 ne {
            /pbcd pbcang pbcprev sub abs def
            pbcd 180 gt { /pbcd 360 pbcd sub def } if
            /pbcextra
                pbcextra pbmaxoff 2 mul pbcd 2 div sin mul pbpitch div add
            def
            pbbristles pbcextra mul 60000 gt
                { pkdry-cusp-resample-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
        } if
        /pbcprev pbcang def
    } walkpath
    /pbn pbcount def

    /pbraw pbn array def
    /pbci 0 def
    pbpitch {
        6 array astore pbraw pbci 3 -1 roll put
        /pbci pbci 1 add def
    } walkpath

    % The catch's box (CANVAS_SURFACE.md 3.2): every stop grown by the
    % farthest any emitter puts paint from it, checked against the cell
    % budget before anything is drawn. A bristle's offset is at most
    % 1.15 * halfspread (its slot plus pkjit's 0.15 * halfspread). Emitters:
    %   dashes    that offset across the travel; each dash is a pkribbon of
    %             half-width at most BristleWidth*(1 + WidthJitter)/2, whose
    %             edges pkribbon's /Jitter moves by up to |Jitter|, and
    %             whose round caps and lone-stop dots stay inside that
    %             half-width of a vertex;
    %   scatter   a degenerate subpath offsets x and y independently by up
    %             to the same magnitude, so up to sqrt(2) times as far.
    % The larger, scatter's, is used for every stop.
    pbtouched pbn 0 gt and {
        /pbreach pbwidth 0.5 mul pbspread mul 1.15 mul 2 sqrt mul
            pbbristlewidth 1 pbwidthjitter add mul 0.5 mul add
            pbjitter abs add def
        [ pbraw { pop pbreach } pjstopbox ] /pbtouchbox exch def
        pbtouchbox aload pop pjcells pjbudget gt
            { pkdry-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit } if
    } if

    pbn 0 gt {
        gsave
        pbtouched { pbtouchbox aload pop pbtouch pjcatch } if
        /pbbasecolor [ currentrgbcolor ] def
        /pbhalfspread pbwidth 0.5 mul pbspread mul def
        1 1 pbbristles {
            /pbbi exch def
            /pbevenfrac pbbi 0.5 sub pbbristles div def

            /pbsi 0 def
            /pbrunstart 0 def
            {
                pbsi pbn ge { exit } if
                pbraw pbsi get 5 get /pbae exch def
                pbae 1 and 1 eq { /pbrunstart pbsi def } if
                pbae 2 and 2 eq {
                    % Offset/width/color are drawn fresh for each
                    % subpath, not once for the whole bristle: reusing
                    % a single draw across every subpath made the same
                    % bristle index render at an identical position,
                    % width, and color on every subpath, contradicting
                    % pkribbon's own "each subpath becomes an
                    % independent [mark]" contract that pkdry's header
                    % explicitly claims too (Codex review). /pbevenfrac
                    % (the bristle's systematic slot within the
                    % envelope) stays fixed across subpaths -- only the
                    % jitter/width/color *draws* are re-rolled.
                    /pboff
                        pbevenfrac 0.5 sub 2 mul pbhalfspread mul
                        pbhalfspread 0.15 mul pkjit
                        add
                    def
                    /pbw
                        pbbristlewidth pbwidthjitter pbbristlewidth mul pkjit
                        add
                    def
                    pbw pbbristlewidth 0.05 mul lt
                        { /pbw pbbristlewidth 0.05 mul def } if
                    /pbk pbcolorjitter pkjit 1 add def
                    pbbasecolor pbk shade setrgbcolor

                    pboff pbw pbrunstart pbsi pbdashrun
                } if
                /pbsi pbsi 1 add def
            } loop
        } for
        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% rate -> bool   (true with probability rate, clamped at both ends: a
% rate >= 1 is *always* true and a rate <= 0 is *always* false, not
% left to a bare `frnd rate lt`. artkit's `frnd` (rand's high bits
% divided down) can land on exactly 1.0, which would otherwise make
% even `frnd 1 lt` false and silently break a documented "rate 1 means
% certain" contract -- confirmed: seed 5659 with a single bristle drew
% nothing at /Load 1 before this fix (Codex review). Used for every
% probabilistic decision pbdashrun makes.
% @internal
/pbroll {
    dup 1 ge
        { pop true }
        { dup 0 le { pop false } { frnd exch lt } ifelse }
    ifelse
} bind def

% pboff pbw pbrunstart pbrunend pbdashrun -   (one bristle's dashes
% across one subpath's raw-stop range [pbrunstart pbrunend], inclusive
% -- see pkdry's own header for the model)
% A two-state Markov chain over consecutive raw stops decides ink
% contact per sample: each transition's own probability is
% pkdry's pbload/pbdropout scaled by (that stop's own `sp` field /
% pbwidth) -- `sp` is the *actual* arc-length distance from the
% previous stop (walkpath's own doc: not always == Pitch -- 0 for a
% subpath's first stop, and whatever's left over for the guaranteed
% final stop), not the nominal `pbpitch`. Using the nominal pitch
% instead of each stop's real `sp` overstated the transition
% probability for the guaranteed short final stop specifically (Codex
% review) -- the "per one Width of travel" contract has to hold
% per-stop, not just on average, since a stop's actual travel can be
% shorter than a full pitch step. Each contiguous on-run becomes its
% own small polyline, flushed through a nested `pkribbon` call as soon as
% the run ends (an off transition, or the range's last sample) --
% pkribbon's own single-point-subpath handling turns a lone on-sample
% into a dot automatically, exactly the isolated-fleck fallback a real
% skipping bristle should have. Each flush passes `/Pitch pbpitch`
% explicitly rather than letting `pkribbon` default it from `pbdrw`
% (the dash's own, often tiny, width): `pkribbon`'s default Pitch is
% `Width*0.5`, so a very small `/BristleWidth` would otherwise make
% every dash internally re-walk its own short polyline at an
% arbitrarily fine resolution, unaccounted for by pkdry's own
% deposit-budget check above (which only counts the coarser outer
% `pbraw` stops) -- an unbounded amount of extra work a valid, in-range
% `/BristleWidth`/`/Pitch` combination could still trigger (Codex
% review). Reusing the same `pbpitch` already validated and budgeted
% at the outer level keeps a dash's own internal resampling the same
% order of magnitude as the stops it was built from.
% @internal
/pbdashrun {
    /pbdrend exch def /pbdrstart exch def
    /pbdrw exch def /pbdroff exch def

    % A single-index range (pbdrstart == pbdrend) can only be walkpath's
    % own degenerate single-point case -- any real, even very short,
    % subpath gets at least two raw stops by walkpath's own documented
    % contract. Offset isotropically then, not perpendicular to the
    % synthetic ang=0 (see pkdry's own header).
    /pbdrsingle pbdrstart pbdrend eq def

    % The very first sample's state uses raw (unscaled) pbload, not a
    % travel-scaled rate: "does this bristle start loaded" is a
    % one-time boundary condition, not a rate along the path, so it
    % shouldn't shrink with a finer /Pitch the way a genuine per-step
    % transition probability should. This matters most for a
    % single-sample run (pbdrsingle): with no second sample to ever
    % roll a transition, this is the *only* roll that decides whether
    % that bristle marks at all -- using a scaled rate there made
    % /Load 1 /Dropout 0 still leave most bristles unmarked for an
    % isolated dab, since a Pitch/Width-scaled rate is typically well
    % under 1 even at pbload 1.
    /pbdrstate pbload pbroll def
    /pbdrwasoff true def

    /pbdri pbdrstart def
    {
        pbdri pbdrend gt { exit } if
        pbraw pbdri get
        dup 0 get /pbdrpx exch def
        dup 1 get /pbdrpy exch def
        dup 2 get /pbdrpang exch def
        4 get /pbdrsp exch def

        pbdrsingle
            {
                /pbdrex pbdrpx pbdroff pkjit add def
                /pbdrey pbdrpy pbdroff pkjit add def
            }
            {
                /pbdrex pbdrpx pbdrpang 90 add cos pbdroff mul add def
                /pbdrey pbdrpy pbdrpang 90 add sin pbdroff mul add def
            }
        ifelse

        % Each transition's own probability is scaled by *this stop's*
        % actual sp (arc-length since the previous stop), not the
        % nominal pbpitch -- see this proc's own header.
        pbdri pbdrstart gt {
            pbdrstate
                {
                    pbdropout pbdrsp pbwidth div mul pbroll
                        { /pbdrstate false def } if
                }
                {
                    pbload pbdrsp pbwidth div mul pbroll
                        { /pbdrstate true def } if
                }
            ifelse
        } if

        pbdrstate {
            pbdrwasoff
                { newpath pbdrex pbdrey moveto }
                { pbdrex pbdrey lineto }
            ifelse
        } {
            pbdrwasoff not {
                << /Width pbdrw /Jitter pbjitter /Pitch pbpitch >> pkribbon
            } if
        } ifelse
        /pbdrwasoff pbdrstate not def
        /pbdri pbdri 1 add def
    } loop

    pbdrstate {
        << /Width pbdrw /Jitter pbjitter /Pitch pbpitch >> pkribbon
    } if
} bind def

% --- spray-paint brush (issue #44) ------------------------------------
% opts pkspray -
% Seeded particle deposition around the sampled centerline: at each
% walkpath stop a small cloud of opaque dots is scattered around that
% point, denser near the centerline and thinning outward, with an
% optional sparse mist escaping past the nozzle edge -- the physical
% model of an aerosol can moved along a gesture. Not built on
% `pkribbon` (discrete dots, not an offset band) but the same
% walkpath-driven shape as every preset here.
%
%   opts pkspray -
%
% Emission is a *cumulative accumulator*, not a fixed count per stop:
% each stop adds `Density * sp / (2*Nozzle)` to a running total (sp is
% the actual arc-length since the previous stop, walkpath's own
% contract), deposits its integer part, and keeps the fraction for the
% next stop. Total particles therefore tracks arc length --
% approximately `Density * (path length / nozzle diameter)` --
% regardless of /Pitch, so the same options read as the same density
% at any sampling pitch (the portability contract pkdry's per-Width
% rate scaling gives its Markov rates). The accumulator resets at each
% subpath's first stop: subpaths are independent marks, same doctrine
% as pkribbon/pkdry, and this makes each subpath's own total track its
% own length exactly rather than borrowing from its neighbors. The
% guaranteed-final-stop duplicate (walkpath's sp==0 coincidence case)
% then naturally deposits nothing extra.
%
% Speck sets the grain of a cloud, and the core's solidity follows from
% how many particles land per unit area. For vapour (cloud, mist, haze)
% use /Speck about 1 to 1.2: at a small /Nozzle (about 8) and a high
% /Density (150 to 400) the core overlaps into one solid mass while the
% falloff stays granular, and a later `pkwet` grades that fine fringe
% into the layer beneath. Density alone does not get there at a larger
% speck: at /Speck 2 to 3 the same counts give a solid bar with lumpy,
% coarse edges, which reads as a paint stroke, not vapour. At /Speck 2
% to 3 with low or moderate /Density (around 60 per nozzle of travel
% for a nozzle of 22) the particles stay discrete: visible spatter, not
% a cloud, and no `pkwet` pass turns it into one. At /Speck 1 to
% 1.2 the core fuses once /Density is high enough for the nozzle (150
% gives a loose core, 400 a solid one). Coverage roughly tracks
% Density * Speck^2 / Nozzle^2, so a larger /Nozzle needs proportionally
% more /Density for the same fused core.
% examples/paintkit_spray_demo.ps row 1 shows the same stroke both ways.
%
% See the `@param` tags directly above `pkspray`'s own definition below
% for the full parameter list with defaults. Caveats the terse tags
% don't carry: /Falloff is implemented as a radial draw r = Nozzle *
% (minimum of m fresh frnd draws), m = 1 + truncate(2*Falloff) --
% min-of-m-uniforms has radial density proportional to
% (1 - r/Nozzle)^(m-1) -- m=1 spreads particles uniformly *in radius*
% (areal density still rises mildly toward the center), m=2 falls off
% linearly, m=3 quadratically; three discrete levels, deliberately not
% interpolated. /Overspray places an escaped particle uniformly in the
% band [R, R*(1+Overspray)] -- R is the particle's own base radius: the
% nozzle for ordinary particles, the bloomed Nozzle*(1+Burst*0.5) for
% burst/dab particles -- drawn at half size, with the escape roll
% clamped at both ends the way pkdry's own probability rolls are
% (`pzroll`): frnd can return exactly 1.0, so a bare `frnd rate lt`
% would never escape at /Overspray 1. /Speckle variation is clamped to
% never go below 10% of Speck (same clamp shape as pkdry's
% /WidthJitter). /StartBurst//EndBurst count is
% truncate(Burst * Density * 2) (a dwell of about two nozzle-diameters'
% worth of emission), scattered with the same radial profile over
% radius Nozzle*(1 + Burst*0.5) -- a dwelling nozzle blooms.
%
% A degenerate single-point subpath (walkpath's atend=3 case) gets one
% dab of truncate(Density*2) particles around the point -- without it,
% a zero-length sp would deposit nothing and a pressed-down spray dot
% would vanish entirely (the same failure mode pkdry's dab fallback
% guards against). A single point is also both its subpath's first and
% last stop, so requested /StartBurst//EndBurst clusters land there
% too, on top of the dab.
%
% Stencil/clipping needs no support here: every particle is a plain
% fill inside whatever clip the caller set up (`clip` after a
% `charpath`, an arbitrary path, anything), exactly like every other
% mark in this file.
%
% Determinism: same doctrine as the rest of the file -- no library-
% owned seed, fully reproducible under the caller's own `N srand`.
% Per-particle draw order is pinned and deliberate: overspray roll,
% then radial position (m interior draws, or one band draw when
% oversprayed), then angle, then size jitter. Changing /Falloff changes
% the number of radial draws and therefore reshuffles the whole random
% stream downstream -- two renders differing only in /Falloff are not
% the same pattern with different spread, they're different patterns
% (same caveat pkdry documents for /Jitter). Still fully deterministic
% under any fixed seed either way.
%
% Color is deliberately not a key: all particles fill with whatever
% color the caller already set, same as every other preset here. The
% caller's current path survives (flattened); drawing happens inside
% its own gsave/newpath/grestore, batched into one path and filled
% once for the whole call.
%
% Multiple subpaths each get their own independent scatter, burst(s),
% and accumulator share. Closed subpaths need no ring special-casing:
% their stops are walked once linearly like any other subpath's, and
% the end burst lands where the nozzle lifts.
%
% Safety limit: total expected deposits are estimated during the cheap
% counting pass (accumulated per-stop emissions + 1 per stop + both
% bursts' counts per subpath + the degenerate-dab count for atend==3
% stops) and rejected past 150000
% (`pkspray-deposit-count-exceeds-safety-limit`) before any drawing or
% even the second walkpath pass starts. The per-stop +1 is not a
% truncation-loss allowance -- the fractional carry means deposits
% telescope to the floor of the accumulated emissions, with nothing
% lost per stop -- it is what guarantees the running estimate grows by
% at least 1 every callback, so a pathological fine /Pitch crosses the
% budget within ~150k callbacks instead of walking an unbounded path
% to completion first (the same placement argument pkdry's own check
% makes). The dab term is not
% optional: a page of bare movetos reports sp=0 for every one of them
% (zero accumulated emissions), so without it an uncapped /Density
% could slip arbitrarily many dab particles past the estimate.
%
% Scratch prefix pz- (pkspray/pzpdot/pzburstcluster/pzroll) --
% deliberately distinct from pk-/pn-/pb-, see the file header.
% @internal
/pzroll {                                          % rate -> bool
% Clamped probability roll: true with probability rate, certain at
% rate >= 1, impossible at rate <= 0. Mirrors pkdry's pbroll -- artkit's
% frnd can land on exactly 1.0, which a bare `frnd rate lt` would turn
% into "never" even for a documented-certain rate of 1 (pkdry's own
% header records the confirmed seed-5659 failure that fix came from).
    dup 1 ge
        { pop true }
        { dup 0 le { pop false } { frnd exch lt } ifelse }
    ifelse
} bind def

% cx cy baseradius pzpdot -   (append one particle dot to the current
% path; consumes this particle's randomness in the pinned order the
% pkspray header documents. Reads the pzm/pzoverspray/pznozzle/
% pzspeck/pzspeckle globals pkspray sets fresh on every call.)
% @internal
/pzpdot {
    /pzdbR exch def /pzdy exch def /pzdx exch def
    pzoverspray pzroll {
        % Escaped mist: uniformly in the outer band, half size. The
        % band scales off the particle's own base radius -- the nozzle
        % for ordinary particles, the bloomed burst radius for
        % trigger-dwell/dab particles (Codex review, PR #83).
        frnd pzdbR pzoverspray mul mul pzdbR add
        /pzdr exch def
        /pzdsz pzspeck 0.5 mul def
    } {
        % Interior: minimum of m fresh draws, scaled by the base
        % radius. Init above 1 (frnd's ceiling) so the first draw
        % always wins the min.
        /pzrmin 2 def
        1 1 pzm {
            pop frnd dup pzrmin lt { /pzrmin exch def } { pop } ifelse
        } for
        /pzdr pzdbR pzrmin mul def
        /pzdsz pzspeck def
    } ifelse
    % Angle and size jitter draw regardless of branch, in pinned order.
    /pzda frnd 360 mul def
    /pzdsz pzdsz pzspeckle pzspeck mul pkjit add def
    pzdsz pzspeck 0.1 mul lt { /pzdsz pzspeck 0.1 mul def } if
    /pzdpx pzdx pzdr pzda cos mul add def
    /pzdpz pzdy pzdr pzda sin mul add def
    % Jump to the circle's own start point before arcing: `arc` from a
    % current point would connect the previous particle to this one
    % with a stray line across the whole batched path.
    pzdpx pzdsz 2 div add pzdpz moveto
    pzdpx pzdpz pzdsz 2 div 0 360 arc
} bind def

% x y baseradius count pzburstcluster -   (count particles pooled
% around one point: trigger-dwell bursts and the degenerate dab)
% @internal
/pzburstcluster {
    /pzbc exch def /pzbbR exch def /pzby exch def /pzbx exch def
    1 1 pzbc { pop pzbx pzby pzbbR pzpdot } for
} bind def

% opts pkspray -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A spray-paint brush: seeded opaque particles deposited around each sampled centerline stop, thinning outward under a radial falloff, with an optional overspray mist past the nozzle edge and optional start/end trigger-dwell bursts. For vapour (cloud, mist, haze) use /Speck about 1 to 1.2 with a small /Nozzle (about 8), /Density 150 to 400 and /Falloff near 1: the core fuses solid and only the fringe stays granular (then pkwet it into the sky). At /Speck 2 to 3 high density gives coarse lumpy bars and low density gives discrete spatter; neither reads as vapour.
% @example: newpath ... << /Nozzle 12 /Density 40 >> pkspray
% @param: /Nozzle [points] Spray radius around the centerline; must be > 0 (default 8)
% @param: /Density [count per 2*/Nozzle] Mean particles deposited per one nozzle-diameter of travel; must be >= 0 (0 renders nothing); uncapped, bounded by the deposit-budget safety limit instead (default 30)
% @param: /Falloff [0..1] how quickly density thins outward; three discrete levels (min-of-m-uniforms radial draws, m = 1 + truncate(2*Falloff)) (default 0.5)
% @param: /Overspray [0..1] probability each particle escapes past the nozzle edge as half-size mist in the band [Nozzle, Nozzle*(1+Overspray)] (default 0.12)
% @param: /Speck [points] Mean particle diameter in points; must be > 0. About 1 to 1.2 with a small /Nozzle and high /Density fuses into vapour (cloud, mist); 2 to 3 gives coarse lumpy bars at high /Density and discrete spatter at low (default 1.4)
% @param: /Speckle [fraction of /Speck] 0..1 per-particle diameter variation, clamped to never go below 10% of Speck (default 0.35)
% @param: /Pitch [points] walkpath sampling pitch for the shared centerline pass; must be > 0 (default Nozzle*0.5, capped at 6)
% @param: /StartBurst [0..1] extra particles pooled at each subpath's first stop (trigger dwell) (default 0)
% @param: /EndBurst [0..1] extra particles pooled at each subpath's last stop (trigger dwell) (default 0)
/pkspray {
    /pzopts exch def

    % Validation runs unconditionally, before any walking, even for an
    % empty path -- pkribbon validates its whole dict before looking at
    % the path and pknib learned the hard way not to gate that on
    % non-emptiness (Codex review, PR #77). No proc-valued options
    % exist here, so every field gets only the executable-value guard:
    % binding a procedure straight to its name would auto-execute it on
    % every later bare reference (the trap pkgetdef's own header
    % documents), so `/key load xcheck` rejects them outright the way
    % pkribbon rejects an executable /Width (Codex review, round 7).
    /pznozzle pzopts /Nozzle 8 pkgetdef def
    /pznozzle load xcheck { pkspray-nozzle-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pznozzle 0 le { pkspray-nozzle-must-be-positive } if

    /pzdensity pzopts /Density 30 pkgetdef def
    /pzdensity load xcheck { pkspray-density-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pzdensity 0 lt { pkspray-density-must-be-non-negative } if

    /pzfalloff pzopts /Falloff 0.5 pkgetdef def
    /pzfalloff load xcheck { pkspray-falloff-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pzfalloff 0 lt pzfalloff 1 gt or { pkspray-falloff-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pzoverspray pzopts /Overspray 0.12 pkgetdef def
    /pzoverspray load xcheck
        { pkspray-overspray-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pzoverspray 0 lt pzoverspray 1 gt or
        { pkspray-overspray-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pzspeck pzopts /Speck 1.4 pkgetdef def
    /pzspeck load xcheck { pkspray-speck-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pzspeck 0 le { pkspray-speck-must-be-positive } if

    /pzspeckle pzopts /Speckle 0.35 pkgetdef def
    /pzspeckle load xcheck { pkspray-speckle-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pzspeckle 0 lt pzspeckle 1 gt or
        { pkspray-speckle-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pzpitch
        pzopts /Pitch
        pznozzle 0.5 mul dup 6 gt { pop 6 } if
        pkgetdef
    def
    /pzpitch load xcheck { pkspray-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pzpitch 0 le { pkspray-pitch-must-be-positive } if

    /pzstartburst pzopts /StartBurst 0 pkgetdef def
    /pzstartburst load xcheck
        { pkspray-startburst-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pzstartburst 0 lt pzstartburst 1 gt or
        { pkspray-startburst-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pzendburst pzopts /EndBurst 0 pkgetdef def
    /pzendburst load xcheck { pkspray-endburst-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pzendburst 0 lt pzendburst 1 gt or
        { pkspray-endburst-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    % Radial draw count for /Falloff: three discrete levels 1..3, see
    % the header.
    /pzm 1 pzfalloff 2 mul truncate add def

    % Counting pass: number the stops, run the deposit-budget check
    % inside the callback (see the header for why inside), and consume
    % all six operands every call -- walkpath's moveto handler emits a
    % subpath's closing stop *before* consuming the next subpath's x/y,
    % so anything left on the stack corrupts it (artkit's own warning;
    % store atend/sp by name first, then drop the rest).
    /pzcount 0 def /pzest 0 def
    pzpitch {
        /pzae exch def /pzasp exch def pop pop pop pop
        /pzest
            pzest
            pzdensity pzasp mul pznozzle 2 mul div
            add 1 add
        def
        % Burst estimates key off the same bitmask bits the drawing
        % pass does, with no atend==3 carve-out either side: a
        % degenerate single point is both its subpath's first and last
        % stop (3 has both bits set), so both bursts apply there and
        % both terms belong in the estimate (Codex review, PR #83).
        pzae 1 and 1 eq {
            /pzest pzest pzstartburst pzdensity 2 mul mul add def
        } if
        pzae 2 and 2 eq {
            /pzest pzest pzendburst pzdensity 2 mul mul add def
        } if
        % Degenerate single-point dab: sp arrives 0, so the accumulated-
        % emission term never sees it -- the dab's full count goes into
        % the estimate explicitly or an uncapped /Density slips past
        % the budget (review finding on this very implementation plan).
        pzae 3 eq {
            /pzest pzest pzdensity 2 mul add def
        } if
        pzest 150000 gt
            { pkspray-deposit-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
        /pzcount pzcount 1 add def
    } walkpath

    % Collection pass into a preallocated array -- count, then fill,
    % the only shape that survives walkpath's cross-subpath stack
    % discipline (same as pkribbon's pkraw).
    /pzraw pzcount array def
    /pzci 0 def
    pzpitch {
        6 array astore pzraw pzci 3 -1 roll put
        /pzci pzci 1 add def
    } walkpath

    pzcount 0 gt {
        gsave
        newpath
        /pzacc 0 def
        0 1 pzcount 1 sub {
            /pzi exch def
            pzraw pzi get
            dup 0 get /pzx exch def
            dup 1 get /pzy exch def
            dup 4 get /pzsp exch def
            5 get /pzae exch def
            pzae 3 eq {
                % Degenerate single point: one pressed-down dab. A
                % single point is also both its subpath's first and
                % last stop, so requested endpoint bursts apply here
                % too (Codex review, PR #83).
                pzx pzy pznozzle pzdensity 2 mul truncate
                pzburstcluster
                pzstartburst 0 gt {
                    pzx pzy pznozzle pzstartburst 0.5 mul 1 add mul
                    pzstartburst pzdensity mul 2 mul truncate
                    pzburstcluster
                } if
                pzendburst 0 gt {
                    pzx pzy pznozzle pzendburst 0.5 mul 1 add mul
                    pzendburst pzdensity mul 2 mul truncate
                    pzburstcluster
                } if
            } {
                pzae 1 and 1 eq {
                    % Subpath start: reset this subpath's accumulator
                    % (subpaths are independent marks) and fire the
                    % start burst if any.
                    /pzacc 0 def
                    pzstartburst 0 gt {
                        pzx pzy pznozzle pzstartburst 0.5 mul 1 add mul
                        pzstartburst pzdensity mul 2 mul truncate
                        pzburstcluster
                    } if
                } if
                /pzacc pzacc pzdensity pzsp mul pznozzle 2 mul div add def
                /pzn pzacc truncate def
                /pzacc pzacc pzn sub def
                1 1 pzn { pop pzx pzy pznozzle pzpdot } for
                pzae 2 and 2 eq {
                    pzendburst 0 gt {
                        pzx pzy
                        pznozzle pzendburst 0.5 mul 1 add mul
                        pzendburst pzdensity mul 2 mul truncate
                        pzburstcluster
                    } if
                } if
            } ifelse
        } for
        fill
        grestore
    } if
} bind def
% --- stylized oil-paint brush (issue #45) -----------------------------
% opts pkoil -
% A stylized oil-paint impasto preset built from layered pressure ribbons
% and bristle traces: a solid base ribbon suggesting a loaded brush, a
% bounded family of thin offset bristle ridges that catch and skip
% slightly (Markov, like pkdry but with high load), and restrained
% highlight/shadow edge lifts that read as paint thickness without any
% 3D height map, fluid mixing, or blending. Pure vector fill, portable
% to pscat and Ghostscript.
%
% See the `@param` tags directly above `pkoil`'s own definition below
% for the full parameter list with defaults. Caveats the terse tags
% don't carry: /Ridges is a hard cap (1..40) so even at default Pitch
% on a long stroke the work stays bounded (see Safety below); /Load and
% /Dropout are both per one Width of travel, scaled by (sp/Width) per
% step like pkdry, same portability contract; /EdgePickup is
% implemented as subtle darkening of the outermost ridges' shade, not a
% separate pass.
%
% Determinism: no library seed, fully reproducible under the caller's
% own `N srand`, same doctrine as pkribbon/pkdry/pkspray.
%
% Multiple subpaths each get independent ridge scatter and Markov runs.
% A single-point subpath scatters ridges isotropically around the point
% so a dab reads as a pressed impasto cluster, not a line.
%
% /Pressure shapes the whole composite mark, not just the base: each
% ridge/highlight/shadow dash scales both its lateral offset and its
% width by Pressure(t), with t the stop's progress through its own
% *subpath* (walkpath's t), so a zero-pressure end collapses every layer
% onto the centerline. Dashes remap their local ribbon t back into that
% subpath progress rather than restarting at 0. A single-point dab has
% no progress, so its ridges ignore /Pressure (full strength, the
% pre-#98 pressed-cluster look); only the base dot follows Pressure(0).
%
% Layering order (documented, deterministic):
%  1. Base load ribbon at current color (full Width)
%  2. Bristle ridges (Ridges thin ribbons, each broken by Markov)
%  3. Highlight ridge on the + side, shadow ridge on the - side
% Later layers are drawn on top of earlier ones.
%
% Safety: Ridges * stopCount is checked against 120000 before any
% drawing, same placement argument as pkdry's 150000 (checked inside
% the counting walk, not after). The per-ridge dash calls reuse
% poPitch for their own nested pkribbon resampling, so the budget
% accounts for outer stops only, not multiplied nested cost — same
% scope as pkdry's outer limit (pkdry's nested cost is bounded
% separately by its issue #79 cusp guard; pkoil has no equivalent).
%
% Scratch prefix po- (pkoil/poroll/porad) — deliberately distinct from
% pk-/pn-/pb-/pz-, since pkoil calls pkribbon and would clobber pk*
% names.
%
% @internal
/poroll {
    dup 1 ge { pop true } { dup 0 le { pop false } { frnd exch lt } ifelse } ifelse
} bind def

% Pressure proc handed to each nested pkribbon call. The nested ribbon
% sees only its own run's polyline, so its t restarts at 0 for every
% dash; remap it into the run's slice [porT0, porT1] of the *subpath's*
% progress (walkpath's t) before consulting the caller's /Pressure, or
% every short dash would get a fresh 0..1 pressure domain.
% t -> mult
% @internal
/porpress { porsingle { pop 1 } { porT1 porT0 sub mul porT0 add poPressure } ifelse } bind def

% -
% @internal
/porbr { << /Width porw /Jitter poJitter /Pitch poPitch /Pressure /porpress load >> pkribbon } bind def

% pooff porw porunstart porunend porad -
% Offsets are scaled by the caller's /Pressure at each stop, and the
% nested ribbon's width by the same profile (via porpress), so a ridge
% never protrudes past the envelope the base ribbon is drawing.
% @internal
/porad {
    /porend exch def /porstart exch def /porw exch def /poroff exch def
    /porsingle porstart porend eq def
    /porstate poLoad poroll def
    /porwasoff true def
    /poradi porstart def
    {
        poradi porend gt { exit } if
        poraw poradi get dup 0 get /porpx exch def dup 1 get /porpy exch def dup 2 get /porpang exch def dup 3 get /porpt exch def 4 get /porsp exch def
        /pormul porsingle { 1 } { porpt poPressure dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if } ifelse def
        porsingle {
            /porex porpx poroff pormul mul pkjit add def
            /porey porpy poroff pormul mul pkjit add def
        } {
            /porex porpx porpang 90 add cos poroff mul pormul mul add def
            /porey porpy porpang 90 add sin poroff mul pormul mul add def
        } ifelse
        poradi porstart gt {
            porstate {
                poDropout porsp poWidth div mul poroll { /porstate false def } if
            } {
                poLoad porsp poWidth div mul poroll { /porstate true def } if
            } ifelse
        } if
        porstate {
            porwasoff { /porT0 porpt def newpath porex porey moveto } { porex porey lineto } ifelse
            /porT1 porpt def
        } {
            porwasoff not { porbr } if
        } ifelse
        /porwasoff porstate not def
        /poradi poradi 1 add def
    } loop
    porstate { porbr } if
} bind def

%%SelfTest: pkoil-hue-keys
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor 5 >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-highlightcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/HighlightColor 5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor { 0.5 0.5 0.5 } >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-highlightcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/HighlightColor as a procedure) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor [ 0.5 0.5 ] >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-highlightcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/HighlightColor with two components) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor [ 0.5 0.5 1.5 ] >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-highlightcolor-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/HighlightColor with a component of 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor [ 0.5 (x) 0.5 ] >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-highlightcolor-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/HighlightColor with a string component) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColor 5 >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-shadowcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/ShadowColor 5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColor { 0.5 0.5 0.5 } >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-shadowcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/ShadowColor as a procedure) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColor [ 0.5 0.5 ] >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-shadowcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/ShadowColor with two components) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColor [ 0.5 0.5 1.5 ] >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-shadowcolor-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/ShadowColor with a component of 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColor [ 0.5 (x) 0.5 ] >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-shadowcolor-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/ShadowColor with a string component) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColour [ 1 1 0 ] >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/HighlightColour) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColour [ 0 0 1 ] >> pkoil }
%   /pkoil-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/ShadowColour) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor [ 1 1 0 ] /ShadowColor [ 0 0.2 0.6 ] >> pkoil }
%   (both mix targets) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor [ 1 0 0 ] /Highlight 0 >> pkoil }
%   (a mix target with its layer off) mustpass
%
%%EndSelfTest
%
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A stylized oil-paint impasto preset built on pkribbon: layered pressure ribbons and bristle ridges suggesting a loaded brush, with restrained highlight/shadow edge lifts that read as paint thickness without any 3D height map or blending. Deterministic under the caller's own srand; bounded by Ridges * stops; respects clip like every other preset.
% @example: newpath ... << /Width 16 /Ridges 12 /Load 0.9 >> pkoil
% @param: /Width [points] Envelope width the ridges scatter across; must be > 0 (default 14)
% @param: /Pitch [points] walkpath sampling pitch for the shared centerline pass; must be > 0 (default Width*0.35, capped at 4)
% @param: /Ridges [count] Bristle ridge count; must be a whole number, hard safety cap 1..40 (default 12)
% @param: /RidgeSpread [fraction of /Width] 0..1 fraction of Width the ridges scatter across, centered on the centerline (default 0.75)
% @param: /RidgeWidth [points] Base width of each ridge's own mark; must be > 0 (default Width*0.14)
% @param: /WidthJitter [fraction of /RidgeWidth] 0..1 fraction of RidgeWidth each ridge's own width randomly varies by (default 0.3)
% @param: /Load [per /Width] 0..1 resume-contact rate per one Width of travel; high Load stays inked (default 0.85)
% @param: /Dropout [per /Width] 0..1 lose-contact rate per one Width of travel (default 0.18)
% @param: /ColorJitter [0..1] per-ridge color variation magnitude around the caller's current color (default 0.08)
% @param: /EdgePickup [0..1] darker pickup at the envelope edges (default 0.15)
% @param: /Highlight [0..1] restrained highlight strength on one side (default 0.12)
% @param: /Shadow [0..1] restrained shadow strength on the opposite side (default 0.10)
% @param: /Pressure [proc] {t -> mult} proc over normalized path progress; shapes the base ribbon, bristle ridges, highlight and shadow alike (default { pkflat })
% @param: /Jitter [points] Seeded edge displacement amount, forwarded to each dash's pkribbon Jitter (default 0)
% @param: /HighlightColor [rgb array] the color the highlight ridge mixes toward, instead of white; /Highlight still sets how far; a bare pigment name such as /titaniumwhite stands for that pigment, and a pigmix spec goes through pigcolor (default unset: toward white)
% @param: /ShadowColor [rgb array] the color the shadow ridge mixes toward, instead of black; /Shadow still sets how far; a bare pigment name such as /titaniumwhite stands for that pigment, and a pigmix spec goes through pigcolor (default unset: toward black)
/pkoil {
    /poopts exch def
    /poWidth poopts /Width 14 pkgetdef def
    /poWidth load xcheck { pkoil-width-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poWidth 0 le { pkoil-width-must-be-positive } if
    /poPitch poopts /Pitch poWidth 0.35 mul dup 4 gt { pop 4 } if pkgetdef def
    /poPitch load xcheck { pkoil-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poPitch 0 le { pkoil-pitch-must-be-positive } if
    /poRidges poopts /Ridges 12 pkgetdef def
    /poRidges load xcheck { pkoil-ridges-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poRidges 1 lt poRidges 40 gt or poRidges truncate poRidges ne or { pkoil-ridges-must-be-1-to-40 } if
    /poRidgeSpread poopts /RidgeSpread 0.75 pkgetdef def
    /poRidgeSpread load xcheck { pkoil-ridgespread-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poRidgeSpread 0 lt poRidgeSpread 1 gt or { pkoil-ridgespread-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /poRidgeWidth poopts /RidgeWidth poWidth 0.14 mul pkgetdef def
    /poRidgeWidth load xcheck { pkoil-ridgewidth-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poRidgeWidth 0 le { pkoil-ridgewidth-must-be-positive } if
    /poWidthJitter poopts /WidthJitter 0.3 pkgetdef def
    /poWidthJitter load xcheck { pkoil-widthjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poWidthJitter 0 lt poWidthJitter 1 gt or { pkoil-widthjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /poLoad poopts /Load 0.85 pkgetdef def
    /poLoad load xcheck { pkoil-load-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poLoad 0 lt poLoad 1 gt or { pkoil-load-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /poDropout poopts /Dropout 0.18 pkgetdef def
    /poDropout load xcheck { pkoil-dropout-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poDropout 0 lt poDropout 1 gt or { pkoil-dropout-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /poColorJitter poopts /ColorJitter 0.08 pkgetdef def
    /poColorJitter load xcheck { pkoil-colorjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poColorJitter 0 lt poColorJitter 1 gt or { pkoil-colorjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /poEdgePickup poopts /EdgePickup 0.15 pkgetdef def
    /poEdgePickup load xcheck { pkoil-edgepickup-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poEdgePickup 0 lt poEdgePickup 1 gt or { pkoil-edgepickup-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /poHighlight poopts /Highlight 0.12 pkgetdef def
    /poHighlight load xcheck { pkoil-highlight-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poHighlight 0 lt poHighlight 1 gt or { pkoil-highlight-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /poShadow poopts /Shadow 0.10 pkgetdef def
    /poShadow load xcheck { pkoil-shadow-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    poShadow 0 lt poShadow 1 gt or { pkoil-shadow-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /poJitter poopts /Jitter 0 pkgetdef def
    /poJitter load xcheck { pkoil-jitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /poPressure poopts /Pressure { pkflat } pkgetdef def
    /poPressure load xcheck /poPressure load type dup /arraytype eq exch dup /operatortype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or or and not { pkoil-pressure-must-be-a-procedure } if
    % The two colours the highlight and shadow ridges mix toward: null when the
    % key is unset, so the branch below is `... null ne` and an unset key keeps
    % the `shade` toward white or black. `puspell` rejects /HighlightColour and
    % friends (pkgetdef would ignore them). Graded once for the running `pkwet`
    % pass (`puwet` is affine and the identity outside a pull); kept under po-
    % names because the nested pkribbon calls clobber pk*.
    poopts (pkoil) puspell
    poopts /HighlightColor (pkoil-highlightcolor) puhue
        dup null ne { aload pop puwet 3 array astore } if /poHighlightColor exch def
    poopts /ShadowColor (pkoil-shadowcolor) puhue
        dup null ne { aload pop puwet 3 array astore } if /poShadowColor exch def
    % counting pass with safety budget
    /pocount 0 def
    poPitch { pop pop pop pop pop pop /pocount pocount 1 add def poRidges pocount mul 120000 gt { pkoil-deposit-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if } walkpath
    /pon pocount def
    /poraw pon array def
    /poci 0 def
    poPitch { 6 array astore poraw poci 3 -1 roll put /poci poci 1 add def } walkpath
    pon 0 gt {
        gsave
        /pobasecolor [ currentrgbcolor ] def
        /pohalfspread poWidth 0.5 mul poRidgeSpread mul def
        % 1. base load ribbon at full Width
        pobasecolor aload pop setrgbcolor
        << /Width poWidth /Pitch poPitch /Jitter poJitter >> dup /Pressure /poPressure load put pkribbon
        % 2. bristle ridges
        pobasecolor aload pop setrgbcolor
        1 1 poRidges {
            /pori exch def
            /poevenfrac pori 0.5 sub poRidges div def
            /posi 0 def /porunstart 0 def
            {
                posi pon ge { exit } if
                poraw posi get 5 get /poae exch def
                poae 1 and 1 eq { /porunstart posi def } if
                poae 2 and 2 eq {
                    /pooff poevenfrac 0.5 sub 2 mul pohalfspread mul pohalfspread 0.15 mul pkjit add def
                    /porw poRidgeWidth poWidthJitter poRidgeWidth mul pkjit add def
                    porw poRidgeWidth 0.05 mul lt { /porw poRidgeWidth 0.05 mul def } if
                    /poshade 1 pori 1 eq pori poRidges eq or { poEdgePickup 0.5 mul neg } { 0 } ifelse poColorJitter pkjit add add def
                    pobasecolor poshade shade setrgbcolor
                    pooff porw porunstart posi porad
                    pobasecolor aload pop setrgbcolor
                } if
                /posi posi 1 add def
            } loop
        } for
        % 3. highlight on + side
        poHighlight 0 gt {
            /pohoff pohalfspread 0.85 mul def
            /pohpow poRidgeWidth 0.6 mul def
            poHighlightColor null ne {
                pobasecolor poHighlightColor poHighlight mix3
            } {
                pobasecolor poHighlight 1 add shade
            } ifelse
            setrgbcolor
            /posi 0 def /porunstart 0 def
            {
                posi pon ge { exit } if
                poraw posi get 5 get /poae exch def
                poae 1 and 1 eq { /porunstart posi def } if
                poae 2 and 2 eq { pohoff pohpow porunstart posi porad } if
                /posi posi 1 add def
            } loop
            pobasecolor aload pop setrgbcolor
        } if
        % 4. shadow on - side
        poShadow 0 gt {
            /posoff pohalfspread -0.85 mul def
            /pospow poRidgeWidth 0.6 mul def
            poShadowColor null ne {
                pobasecolor poShadowColor poShadow mix3
            } {
                pobasecolor poShadow neg 1 add shade
            } ifelse
            setrgbcolor
            /posi 0 def /porunstart 0 def
            {
                posi pon ge { exit } if
                poraw posi get 5 get /poae exch def
                poae 1 and 1 eq { /porunstart posi def } if
                poae 2 and 2 eq { posoff pospow porunstart posi porad } if
                /posi posi 1 add def
            } loop
            pobasecolor aload pop setrgbcolor
        } if
        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% --- trowel / palette knife (issue #111) ------------------------------
% opts pktrowel -
%
% A rigid flat blade dragging viscous paint, not a bristle brush. This
% is the one preset in this file that is *not* built on `pkribbon`, and
% the reason is structural rather than stylistic: every other preset
% here offsets a band around the path *normal*, which is what a tuft of
% bristles does. A palette knife holds its own angle independently of
% where it is going, so the deposit is swept along the **blade
% direction** -- `pkribbon` cannot express that, and a preset that
% merely widened it would be the "just a wide brush" outcome issue #111
% explicitly rules out. As a side benefit `pktrowel` calls no `pk*`
% procedure that redefines its own scratch names, so the clobbering
% hazard documented in this file's header for pkdry/pkspray/pkoil does
% not arise here at all.
%
% The blade at each stop is a segment of half-extent
%
%     h(t) = Width/2 * Pressure(t) * (0.35 + 0.65*Cover)
%
% laid at `pathAngle + 90 + Angle`, so /Angle 0 is a blade held square
% across the direction of travel (a straight scrape) and larger angles
% skew it. The blade is divided into /Lanes bands across its length;
% each lane keeps its own two-state Markov contact chain along the
% path, and each *run* of consecutive in-contact stops is emitted as
% one filled strip -- forward along the lane's inner offset, back along
% its outer -- not one quad per step.
%
% There is deliberately no solid base pass. `pkoil` lays a full-width
% ribbon and then puts ridges on top of it; a trowel does the opposite,
% depositing broken masses straight onto whatever is underneath. That
% absence is what makes the underlayer read through, and it is the
% concrete visual difference between the two tools.
%
% The four "less paint" controls are separated onto genuinely different
% axes, so each one can be seen (and tested) on its own:
%   /Cover       how much paint is on the blade -> the *width* of the
%                deposit (a nearly empty knife lays a narrow band; a
%                loaded one lays the full blade). Named /Load before
%                issue #141; /Load still works as a deprecated alias,
%                and /Cover wins when both are given. Renamed because
%                /Load everywhere else in this file is a contact rate
%                per /Width of travel, not a width
%   /Coverage    the Markov chain's steady state -> how much of the
%                contact area is inked, i.e. holes *within* the band
%   /Viscosity   the Markov chain's *rate* -> run persistence. High
%                viscosity holds paint together into long chunky
%                masses; low viscosity chatters
%   /Scrape      static, not stochastic-along-the-path: narrows every
%                lane toward its own centre (opening lengthwise streaks
%                that reveal the underlayer) and kills whole lanes
%                outright, the way a nicked blade leaves bare tracks
% /EdgeBuildup adds the two darker ridges a blade piles up at its ends,
% drawn last so they sit on top; /Drag extends each run backwards and
% forwards along the direction of travel by Drag*h, the smear paint
% leaves past where the blade actually lifted.
%
% /Angle is restricted to -80..80 degrees. The footprint's thickness
% goes as cos(Angle), so a blade held nearly parallel to its own travel
% collapses to a sliver -- physically correct, but past this range the
% mark stops reading as a trowel at all and an unguarded 90 would be a
% silent no-op.
%
% Determinism: no library seed, fully reproducible under the caller's
% own `N srand`, same doctrine as pkribbon/pkdry/pkspray/pkoil. The
% random draws are structurally fixed at three per (lane, stop) plus
% four per lane, taken whether or not they end up being used -- a lane
% killed by /Scrape still walks its stops and still draws, and `ptroll`
% draws before it clamps rather than after. That is what lets one
% control be turned without silently re-rolling every other control's
% texture, and it is why /Scrape's lane-kill test compares against
% `Scrape*0.35` rather than being skipped at Scrape 0.
%
% Multiple subpaths each get their own independent contact chain (reset
% at every subpath start). A degenerate single-point subpath is a
% *pressed* knife: contact is forced on, and the single-stop run is
% given a minimum along-travel extent of Pitch/2 so it deposits a
% blade-shaped patch rather than the zero-area polygon a
% forward-and-back strip through one point would otherwise be. That
% same minimum is what keeps low-/Viscosity chatter -- whose runs are
% frequently one stop long -- visible instead of blank.
%
% Safety: Lanes is capped 1..40 and Lanes * stopCount is checked
% against 120000 inside the counting walk, the same bound and the same
% placement argument as pkoil's. No new unbounded traversal is
% introduced (issue #110).
%
% Scratch prefix pt- (pktrowel/ptroll/ptpt/ptemit/ptridge) --
% deliberately distinct from pk-/pn-/pb-/pz-/po-/pw-/pv-, per this
% file's header.
%
% rate ptroll -> bool
% Deliberately *not* a copy of poroll, which is otherwise the same
% predicate: poroll short-circuits its clamps and draws no `frnd` at
% all at rate <= 0 or rate >= 1. That is harmless for pkoil, which
% makes no promise about how much randomness it consumes, but it would
% quietly break this file's only fixed-stream guarantee -- at
% /Coverage 1 both the seed draw and the on->off check clamp out, so
% nudging Coverage from 0.98 to 1.0 would consume two fewer draws per
% (lane, stop) and re-roll every lane's jitter and kill pattern, the
% exact coupling the guarantee exists to prevent. Draw first, clamp
% second: the cost is one wasted `frnd` at the extremes, and the
% benefit is that the draw count depends on (Lanes, stops) alone.
% @internal
/ptroll {
    frnd exch
    dup 1 ge { pop pop true } { dup 0 le { pop pop false } { lt } ifelse } ifelse
} bind def

% i u e ptpt  ->  x y
% Blade-space to device space: lateral offset `u` along the blade
% direction at stop `i`, plus `e` along the direction of travel there.
% @internal
/ptpt {
    /ptpe exch def /ptpu exch def
    ptraw exch get
    dup 0 get /ptpx exch def
    dup 1 get /ptpy exch def
    2 get /ptpa exch def
    /ptpb ptpa 90 add ptAngle add def
    ptpx ptpb cos ptpu mul add ptpa cos ptpe mul add
    ptpy ptpb sin ptpu mul add ptpa sin ptpe mul add
} bind def

% i ptcolor -   (set the loaded color for a run that starts at stop i; only
% called with /Colors set)
% The head position ptcu and the shade ptcsh are set by the caller: a lane's
% centre and its jitter, or an edge ridge's +-1 and its darkening. The flip is
% the run's first stop's, because runs never cross a subpath.
% @internal
/ptcolor {
    /ptcli exch def
    ptColorRec ptcu
    ptColorRec 2 get { ptflips ptcli get } { false } ifelse
    puat puwet 3 array astore ptcsh shade setrgbcolor
} bind def

% - ptemit -   (fills the ptk accumulated stops as one strip, resets ptk)
% Each end gets two extra vertices, carrying half a pitch of contact
% plus the /Drag smear.
%
% The half-pitch is not cosmetic. A stop is a *sample* of a continuous
% contact interval, so a run of k in-contact stops represents k pitches
% of contact -- but a strip drawn from the first stop to the last spans
% only (k-1) of them. Every run would then lose one pitch of length,
% which costs a chattery stroke far more than a chunky one and makes
% realized coverage fall as /Viscosity does: measured at /Lanes 1,
% /Coverage 0.75 came out at 0.61 for viscous paint but 0.47 for
% chattery, when both should be 0.75. Extending each end by half a
% pitch makes a run paint the k pitches it stands for, whatever k is,
% which is what actually decouples /Coverage from /Viscosity. (It also
% subsumes the old special case for single-stop runs, which needed the
% same extension for the different reason that a forward-and-back strip
% through one point has zero area.)
%
% The /Drag component is computed from *each end's own* stop, not the
% run's first. Deriving both from one end looks equivalent and isn't:
% under a varying /Pressure the two ends can be wildly different widths,
% and `/Pressure { pktaper }` starts at nearly zero, so a fully loaded
% exit end would barely smear at all however high /Drag was set (Codex
% review).
% @internal
/ptemit {
    ptk 0 gt {
        ptColorRec null ne { ptri 0 get ptcolor } if
        /ptlast ptk 1 sub def
        /ptext0 ptDrag ptraw ptri 0 get get 6 get mul ptPitch 0.5 mul add def
        /ptext1 ptDrag ptraw ptri ptlast get get 6 get mul ptPitch 0.5 mul add def
        newpath
        ptri 0 get ptua 0 get ptext0 neg ptpt moveto
        0 1 ptlast { /ptei exch def
            ptri ptei get ptua ptei get 0 ptpt lineto } for
        ptri ptlast get ptua ptlast get ptext1 ptpt lineto
        ptri ptlast get ptub ptlast get ptext1 ptpt lineto
        ptlast -1 0 { /ptei exch def
            ptri ptei get ptub ptei get 0 ptpt lineto } for
        ptri 0 get ptub 0 get ptext0 neg ptpt lineto
        closepath fill
    } if
    /ptk 0 def
} bind def

% - ptgemit -   (ptemit, but only when there is a ridge to draw)
% The ridge pass runs, and draws its randomness, even at /EdgeBuildup 0
% -- only the fill is skipped. Making the whole pass conditional
% instead would leave the caller's random stream in a different place
% depending on whether edge buildup was asked for, which is the same
% coupling `ptroll` draws-before-it-clamps to avoid: a second pktrowel
% call, or any seeded art placed after this one, would silently move.
% @internal
/ptgemit { ptEdgeBuildup 0 gt { ptemit } { /ptk 0 def } ifelse } bind def

% na nb ptridge -   (a continuous strip between two normalized blade
% offsets, one per subpath -- the edge buildup, which never breaks)
% @internal
/ptridge {
    /ptrgb exch def /ptrga exch def
    /ptrt0 frnd ptTrim mul def
    /ptrt1 1 frnd ptTrim mul sub def
    /ptk 0 def
    0 1 ptn 1 sub {
        /ptgi exch def
        /ptgrec ptraw ptgi get def
        ptgrec 3 get dup ptrt0 ge exch ptrt1 le and
        ptgrec 5 get 3 and 3 eq or {
            /ptgh ptgrec 6 get def
            ptri ptk ptgi put
            ptua ptk ptrga ptgh mul ptJitter 0.5 mul pkjit add put
            ptub ptk ptrgb ptgh mul ptJitter 0.5 mul pkjit add put
            /ptk ptk 1 add def
        } { ptgemit } ifelse
        ptgrec 5 get 2 and 2 eq { ptgemit } if
    } for
    ptgemit
} bind def

%%SelfTest: pktrowel-touch-guards
%   % The no-canvas cases first: nothing in this block has set a surface yet.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 0.5 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch 0.5 before any pkcanvas) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 1 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch 1 before any pkcanvas: any /Touch needs a canvas) mustguard
%
%   << >> pkcanvas
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 1.5 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-touch-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Touch 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch { } >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-touch-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Touch { }) mustguard
%
%   % A coarse /Pitch and one lane keep the deposit budget out of the way.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto
%     << /Touch 0.5 /Pitch 1000 /Lanes 1 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a 1e5pt stroke at /Touch 0.5) mustguard
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 0.5 >> pktrowel grestore }
%   (/Touch 0.5 with a canvas) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Touch 0.5 /Colors [ [ 1 0 0 ] [ 0 0 1 ] ] /Light 90 >> pktrowel grestore }
%   (a touched stroke with a colour load) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto << /Touch 0 >> pktrowel grestore }
%   (/Touch 0 on a pressed knife) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest

%%SelfTest: pktrowel-cover-and-its-deprecated-load-alias
%   % /Load is the deprecated name for /Cover (issue #141) and is still
%   % read, through the same guard, when /Cover is absent.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Cover 1.4 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-cover-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Cover 1.4) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Load 1.4 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-cover-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Load 1.4, the deprecated alias) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Load { 1 } >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-cover-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Load { 1 }, the deprecated alias) mustguard
%
%   % When both are given /Cover wins, and /Load is not even validated.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Cover 0.5 /Load 1.4 >> pktrowel }
%   (/Cover 0.5 with an out-of-range /Load) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Cover 1.4 /Load 0.5 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-cover-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Cover 1.4 with an in-range /Load) mustguard
%%EndSelfTest

%%SelfTest: pktrowel-colors-rejects-malformed-loads
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors 5 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (a number as /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors { [ 0 0 0 ] } >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (a procedure as /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ ] >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (no colours) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ 9 { [ 0 0 0 ] } repeat ] >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (nine colours) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 ] ] >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays
%   (a 2-element entry) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 1.5 ] ] >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colors-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a component of 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColorMix 1.5 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colormix-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/ColorMix 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pktrowel }
%   (1 colour) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] >> pktrowel }
%   (2 colours) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ 8 { [ 0.2 0.4 1 ] } repeat ] /ColorMix 0 /Light -45 >> pktrowel }
%   (8 colours, the cap) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pktrowel-colors-spelling-and-companions
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colours [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/Colours) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColourMix 0.5 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/ColourMix) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /HighlightColour [ 1 1 1 ] >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/HighlightColour) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /ColorMix 0.5 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-colormix-needs-colors
%   (/ColorMix without /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Light 90 >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-light-needs-colors
%   (/Light without /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /Light { 90 } >> pktrowel }
%   /pktrowel-light-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (a procedure as /Light) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /Light 90 >> pktrowel }
%   (/Light with /Colors) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A trowel / palette-knife stroke: a rigid flat blade dragging viscous paint, swept along the blade direction rather than the path normal, depositing broken masses with lengthwise scrape streaks, a dragged smear, and built-up ridges at the blade edges. With /Colors it runs up to eight loaded colours across the blade, blended by /ColorMix and lit from /Light. Not built on pkribbon; lays no solid base pass, so the underlayer reads through. Deterministic under the caller's own srand; bounded by Lanes * stops.
% @example: newpath 40 120 moveto 260 150 lineto << /Width 72 /Angle 18 /Coverage 0.82 >> pktrowel
% @param: /Width [points] Blade length; must be > 0 (default 24)
% @param: /Pitch [points] walkpath sampling pitch for the shared centerline pass; must be > 0 (default Width*0.35, capped at 4)
% @param: /Lanes [count] Bands the blade is divided into across its length; must be a whole number, hard safety cap 1..40 (default 14)
% @param: /Cover [0..1] paint on the blade, scaling the deposit's width from 35% of the blade to all of it. Formerly /Load, still accepted as a deprecated alias; /Cover wins if both are given (default 0.85)
% @param: /Pressure [proc] {t -> mult} proc over normalized path progress, multiplying the blade's half-extent; must be a procedure (default { pkflat })
% @param: /Angle [degrees] Blade angle in degrees relative to square-across-travel, -80..80; footprint thickness goes as cos(Angle) (default 0)
% @param: /Drag [fraction of the blade's half-extent] 0..1 along-travel smear past where the blade lifted, as a fraction of the blade's half-extent (default 0.35)
% @param: /Viscosity [0..1] contact-run persistence; high holds paint into long chunky masses, low chatters (default 0.6)
% @param: /Coverage [0..1] steady-state fraction of the contact area actually depositing (default 0.82)
% @param: /Scrape [0..1] lengthwise thinning: narrows every lane toward its centre and kills whole lanes, revealing the underlayer (default 0.15)
% @param: /EdgeBuildup [0..1] darker ridges of paint piled at the two blade edges, drawn last (default 0.35)
% @param: /Jitter [points] Seeded lateral displacement of the lane boundaries, deterministic under the caller's own N srand (default 0)
% @param: /ColorJitter [0..1] per-lane color variation magnitude around the caller's current color (default 0.06)
% @param: /Colors [array of rgb arrays] 1 to 8 loaded colors, listed from the blade's left end (looking along the travel) to its right, evenly spaced across it; the edge-buildup ridges take the colors of their own edges; when set, the current color is not used for the mark, though /ColorJitter still varies it. An entry may be a bare pigment name such as /sapgreen (see PigmentNames), which stands for that pigment; a pigmix spec goes through pigcolors (default unset)
% @param: /ColorMix [0..1] with /Colors, how far neighbouring colors have blended on the blade: 0 is hard bands, 1 a smooth gradient (default 0.3)
% @param: /Light [degrees] with /Colors, the direction the light comes from, counterclockwise from +x in user space: each subpath puts the first color on the side facing the light, whichever way it travels (default unset: the first color stays on the left of the travel)
% @param: /Touch [0..1] How hard the blade presses into the shared canvas set by pkcanvas, which must be called first: below 1 the mark lands only on the weave's knuckles that stand proud enough for that pressure to reach, lower is lighter and 0 deposits nothing; 1 is full contact, identical to leaving it unset. Takes no rand draws (default unset)
/pktrowel {
    /ptopts exch def
    /ptWidth ptopts /Width 24 pkgetdef def
    /ptWidth load xcheck { pktrowel-width-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptWidth 0 le { pktrowel-width-must-be-positive } if
    /ptPitch ptopts /Pitch ptWidth 0.35 mul dup 4 gt { pop 4 } if pkgetdef def
    /ptPitch load xcheck { pktrowel-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptPitch 0 le { pktrowel-pitch-must-be-positive } if
    /ptLanes ptopts /Lanes 14 pkgetdef def
    /ptLanes load xcheck { pktrowel-lanes-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptLanes 1 lt ptLanes 40 gt or ptLanes truncate ptLanes ne or { pktrowel-lanes-must-be-1-to-40 } if
    % /Cover, or the deprecated /Load alias when /Cover is absent (issue
    % #141). The inner pkgetdef resolves the alias, so /Cover wins.
    /ptLoad ptopts /Cover ptopts /Load 0.85 pkgetdef pkgetdef def
    /ptLoad load xcheck { pktrowel-cover-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptLoad 0 lt ptLoad 1 gt or { pktrowel-cover-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /ptAngle ptopts /Angle 0 pkgetdef def
    /ptAngle load xcheck { pktrowel-angle-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptAngle -80 lt ptAngle 80 gt or { pktrowel-angle-must-be-minus80-to-80 } if
    /ptDrag ptopts /Drag 0.35 pkgetdef def
    /ptDrag load xcheck { pktrowel-drag-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptDrag 0 lt ptDrag 1 gt or { pktrowel-drag-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /ptViscosity ptopts /Viscosity 0.6 pkgetdef def
    /ptViscosity load xcheck { pktrowel-viscosity-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptViscosity 0 lt ptViscosity 1 gt or { pktrowel-viscosity-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /ptCoverage ptopts /Coverage 0.82 pkgetdef def
    /ptCoverage load xcheck { pktrowel-coverage-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptCoverage 0 lt ptCoverage 1 gt or { pktrowel-coverage-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /ptScrape ptopts /Scrape 0.15 pkgetdef def
    /ptScrape load xcheck { pktrowel-scrape-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptScrape 0 lt ptScrape 1 gt or { pktrowel-scrape-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /ptEdgeBuildup ptopts /EdgeBuildup 0.35 pkgetdef def
    /ptEdgeBuildup load xcheck { pktrowel-edgebuildup-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptEdgeBuildup 0 lt ptEdgeBuildup 1 gt or { pktrowel-edgebuildup-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /ptJitter ptopts /Jitter 0 pkgetdef def
    /ptJitter load xcheck { pktrowel-jitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /ptColorJitter ptopts /ColorJitter 0.06 pkgetdef def
    /ptColorJitter load xcheck { pktrowel-colorjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptColorJitter 0 lt ptColorJitter 1 gt or { pktrowel-colorjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    % /Touch (issue #222, CANVAS_SURFACE.md 2.2): any /Touch, even 1, needs
    % a canvas. Only a value below 1 builds a catch, so unset and 1 take
    % exactly the path they took before /Touch existed.
    /ptTouch ptopts /Touch 1 pkgetdef def
    /ptTouch load xcheck { pktrowel-touch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    ptTouch 0 lt ptTouch 1 gt or { pktrowel-touch-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    ptopts /Touch known {
        userdict /pjCTM known not { pktrowel-touch-needs-a-canvas } if
    } if
    /ptTouched ptTouch 1 lt def
    % The load: null when /Colors is unset, so every new branch below is
    % `ptColorRec null ne` and an unset key never reaches it.
    ptopts (pktrowel) puparse /ptColorRec exch def
    ptopts (pktrowel) pulightneedscolors
    /ptPressure ptopts /Pressure { pkflat } pkgetdef def
    /ptPressure load xcheck /ptPressure load type dup /arraytype eq exch dup /operatortype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or or and not { pktrowel-pressure-must-be-a-procedure } if
    % Counting pass with the safety budget checked inside the walk, so
    % an unbounded path is rejected before any allocation (pkoil's
    % placement argument).
    /ptcount 0 def
    ptPitch { pop pop pop pop pop pop /ptcount ptcount 1 add def
              ptLanes ptcount mul 120000 gt { pktrowel-deposit-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if } walkpath
    /ptn ptcount def
    /ptloadf 0.35 0.65 ptLoad mul add def
    /ptraw ptn array def
    /ptci 0 def
    % /Pressure is resolved here, once per stop, and carried in slot 6
    % -- not re-evaluated inside the lane loop. The caller's proc is
    % arbitrary code and may legitimately consume randomness ({ frnd }
    % is a valid pressure profile), so calling it Lanes times per stop
    % would couple lane texture to the pressure profile and break
    % same-seed-same-picture.
    ptPitch {
        /ptae exch def /ptsp exch def /ptt exch def
        /ptang exch def /pty exch def /ptx exch def
        ptraw ptci
            [ ptx pty ptang ptt ptsp ptae
              ptWidth 0.5 mul ptt ptPressure mul ptloadf mul ]
        put
        /ptci ptci 1 add def
    } walkpath
    % The catch's box (CANVAS_SURFACE.md 3.2): every stop grown by the
    % farthest any emitter puts paint from it, checked against the cell
    % budget before anything is drawn. Every emitter is one ptemit strip:
    %   lanes     lateral u = n*h + jitter, |n| <= 1, jitter <= Jitter;
    %   ridges    the same strip at |n| <= 1 with half the jitter;
    %   ends      along the travel by Drag*h + Pitch/2 (each end's own h);
    %   a pressed knife (a single-point subpath) is the same strip.
    % u runs along the blade and e along the travel, so a vertex is at most
    % |u| + |e| <= |h|*(1 + Drag) + Pitch/2 + |Jitter| from its stop, where
    % h is that stop's resolved half-extent in slot 6. The caller's
    % /Pressure is never re-run, and it is not capped at 1.
    ptTouched ptn 0 gt and {
        [ ptraw
          { 6 get abs ptDrag 1 add mul ptPitch 0.5 mul add ptJitter abs add }
          pjstopbox ] /ptTouchBox exch def
        ptTouchBox aload pop pjcells pjbudget gt
            { pktrowel-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit } if
    } if
    ptn 0 gt {
        gsave
        ptTouched { ptTouchBox aload pop ptTouch pjcatch } if
        /ptbasecolor [ currentrgbcolor ] def
        % Transition rate per one Width of travel: 0.5 at full
        % viscosity (long masses) up to 6.0 at zero (fine chatter).
        /ptrate 0.5 5.5 1 ptViscosity sub mul add def
        % The two transition probabilities are Coverage*step and
        % (1-Coverage)*step, and ptroll clamps each to 1 independently.
        % Once step is large enough for the bigger of the two to clamp,
        % their *ratio* changes and Coverage stops being the chain's
        % steady state -- at Width 10 / Viscosity 0 / Coverage 0.8 the
        % realized coverage is 0.70, so turning /Viscosity would also
        % move ink coverage, exactly the coupling these controls are
        % separated to avoid (Codex review). Capping the step at
        % 1/max(Coverage, 1-Coverage) keeps the larger probability at
        % most 1 without clamping, so the ratio -- and therefore the
        % steady state -- is preserved at every viscosity.
        /ptcbig ptCoverage def
        1 ptCoverage sub ptcbig gt { /ptcbig 1 ptCoverage sub def } if
        /ptstepmax 1 ptcbig div def
        % Maximum entry/exit trim per lane, as a fraction of each
        % subpath's own progress. Intrinsic to the tool rather than a
        % parameter: a blade with machined ends is not a blade, and
        % there is no artistic reason to ask for one.
        /ptTrim 0.06 def
        % One flip per stop, so a whole subpath mirrors together. No draws.
        ptColorRec null ne {
            ptColorRec 2 get {
                /ptflips ptraw ptn ptPitch ptAngle ptColorRec 3 get puflips def
            } if
        } if
        /ptri ptn array def
        /ptua ptn array def
        /ptub ptn array def
        /ptk 0 def
        1 1 ptLanes {
            /ptL exch def
            % Lane [na, nb] in normalized blade coordinates (-1..1),
            % then narrowed toward its own centre by /Scrape.
            /ptna ptL 1 sub ptLanes div 2 mul 1 sub def
            /ptnb ptL ptLanes div 2 mul 1 sub def
            /ptnc ptna ptnb add 0.5 mul def
            % The 1.18 baseline is an 18% *overlap* between neighbouring
            % lanes, not slack: two polygons sharing an exact edge leave
            % an anti-aliased hairline seam where they abut, so at
            % /Scrape 0 an exactly-tiling blade rendered as a striped
            % rectangle rather than a solid mass. Lanes have to bleed
            % into each other for "no scrape" to mean no gaps, which
            % also makes /Scrape the only control that opens one.
            /ptshrink 1.18 ptScrape 0.85 mul sub def
            /ptna ptnc ptna ptnc sub ptshrink mul add def
            /ptnb ptnc ptnb ptnc sub ptshrink mul add def
            % Interior boundaries keep the overlap; the outermost ones
            % are clamped back onto the blade. Without this the overlap
            % pushes lane 1 and lane Lanes past +-1 by 0.18/Lanes of the
            % half-extent -- negligible at 14 lanes but 18% at /Lanes 1,
            % which would make /Width stop bounding the footprint and
            % leave the EdgeBuildup ridges at +-1 sitting inside the
            % mass instead of at its edge.
            ptna -1 lt { /ptna -1 def } if
            ptna 1 gt { /ptna 1 def } if
            ptnb -1 lt { /ptnb -1 def } if
            ptnb 1 gt { /ptnb 1 def } if
            % All four draws are unconditional: a lane killed at Scrape
            % 0.5 must consume exactly what a live lane at Scrape 0
            % does, or turning /Scrape re-rolls every other lane's
            % texture.
            /ptdead frnd ptScrape 0.35 mul lt def
            /ptcj ptColorJitter pkjit def
            % Per-lane entry/exit trim: a blade does not touch down or
            % lift off all at once, and without this every lane starts
            % and stops at the same stop, giving the mark two perfectly
            % straight machined ends.
            /ptt0 frnd ptTrim mul def
            /ptt1 1 frnd ptTrim mul sub def
            ptbasecolor 1 ptcj add shade setrgbcolor
            % With a load, ptemit sets the color at each fill, from the lane's
            % head position and this jitter.
            ptColorRec null ne { /ptcu ptnc def /ptcsh 1 ptcj add def } if
            /ptk 0 def
            /ptstate false def
            0 1 ptn 1 sub {
                /pti exch def
                /ptrec ptraw pti get def
                /ptae ptrec 5 get def
                ptae 1 and 1 eq {
                    % Subpath start: the chain is seeded from
                    % /Coverage, its steady state -- not from /Cover,
                    % which is a width control here and would bias the
                    % first run.
                    /ptstate ptCoverage ptroll def
                    ptae 2 and 2 eq { /ptstate true def } if
                } {
                    /ptstep ptrec 4 get ptWidth div ptrate mul def
                    ptstep ptstepmax gt { /ptstep ptstepmax def } if
                    ptstate {
                        1 ptCoverage sub ptstep mul ptroll { /ptstate false def } if
                    } {
                        ptCoverage ptstep mul ptroll { /ptstate true def } if
                    } ifelse
                } ifelse
                % Drawn every stop for every lane whatever the contact
                % state, so the stream length is fixed by (Lanes, stops)
                % alone.
                /ptj1 ptJitter pkjit def
                /ptj2 ptJitter pkjit def
                % A degenerate single-point subpath (atend 3) is a
                % pressed knife: it ignores the trim, or the one stop it
                % has would be trimmed away and the dab would vanish.
                /ptin ptrec 3 get dup ptt0 ge exch ptt1 le and
                      ptae 3 and 3 eq or def
                ptstate ptdead not and ptin and {
                    /pth ptrec 6 get def
                    ptri ptk pti put
                    ptua ptk ptna pth mul ptj1 add put
                    ptub ptk ptnb pth mul ptj2 add put
                    /ptk ptk 1 add def
                } {
                    ptemit
                } ifelse
                ptae 2 and 2 eq { ptemit } if
            } for
            ptemit
        } for
        % Edge buildup last, so the ridges sit on top of the masses.
        % The pass itself is unconditional (ptgemit decides whether to
        % fill) so that /EdgeBuildup 0 consumes the same randomness as
        % any other value -- see ptgemit's own note.
        /ptrwn ptEdgeBuildup 0.28 mul def
        ptEdgeBuildup 0 gt {
            ptbasecolor 1 ptEdgeBuildup 0.5 mul sub shade setrgbcolor
        } if
        % Each ridge takes the color at its own edge (+1 is the left),
        % darkened by the same shade as today.
        ptColorRec null ne { /ptcu 1 def /ptcsh 1 ptEdgeBuildup 0.5 mul sub def } if
        1 ptrwn sub 1 ptridge
        ptColorRec null ne { /ptcu -1 def } if
        -1 ptrwn 1 sub ptridge
        ptbasecolor aload pop setrgbcolor
        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% --- palette-knife plane (issue #180) ---------------------------------
% opts pktrowelplane -
%
% A palette knife's other main job, after cutting a highlight edge, is
% laying a *plane*: covering a mountain face or a rock with overlapping
% blade pulls so it reads as one flat facet that carries the blade's own
% texture. `pktrowel` only walks a centerline, and faking a plane with it
% failed twice in gallery/alpine_lake.ps (#118): a pull along a shallow
% ridge draws a bar across the range, and pulls stepped down the ridge
% make stripes. What worked was a hand-built triangle, a clip and
% vertical pulls at a fixed spacing; this is that pattern as a procedure.
%
% The current path is the area. It is left exactly as the caller had it
% (the whole procedure runs inside one gsave).
%
%   newpath x0 y0 moveto x1 y1 lineto x2 y2 lineto closepath
%   << /Width 14 /Direction -80 /Colors [ [ .3 .3 .4 ] [ .5 .5 .6 ] ] >> pktrowelplane
%
% How it works. The path is flattened and projected on the pull direction
% to find its extent (not `pathbbox`, whose box under a rotated matrix is
% a loose one that differs between interpreters). The CTM is then rotated
% so the pull direction is +x, and one `pktrowel` stroke is run along a
% straight row for each lateral step across the area, every row overrunning
% the area at both ends so that the blade's entry/exit trim, its /Drag and
% its skew all fall outside it. Every key `pktrowel` knows is passed
% straight through, so the plane takes its blade, texture and colour keys
% (/Width, /Angle, /Cover, /Coverage, /Viscosity, /Scrape, /EdgeBuildup,
% /Drag, /Colors, /Light, /Touch, ...) with their meanings and defaults
% unchanged. /Angle is the blade's angle to the pulls, as in `pktrowel`.
%
% Edge: crisp, and it comes from CLIPPING, not from blade geometry. The
% path is made the clip before the first pull, so every pull ends dead
% against the facet's boundary -- the same crisp ridge #118's clip gave.
% (A blade cannot be steered along an arbitrary boundary, and an unclipped
% plane would be a rectangle.) The clip nests inside whatever clip the
% caller already has.
%
% Spacing and overlap. A blade of width W at angle A carrying /Cover C
% deposits a band
%
%     F = W * cos(A) * (0.35 + 0.65*C)
%
% across the direction of travel (the same width `pktrowel` uses), and
% rows are placed F * (1 - Overlap) apart, so /Overlap is the fraction of
% each pull that the next one covers. Unset, it follows the blade's
% coverage: 0.15 + 0.6 * (1 - Coverage), so a patchy blade (low /Coverage)
% overlaps more and the pulls still close up into one plane while a full
% blade (/Coverage 1) overlaps only enough to hide the seam.
%
% A /Pressure that tapers each pull narrows its band below F, and rows spaced
% for F would leave bare gaps between them. So when /Pressure is given, the
% plane reads it at 17 points of the stretch of each row that lies inside the
% area, takes the smallest |value| P, and spaces the rows for F * P instead:
% /Overlap then means the fraction of the *thinnest* part of each pull that
% the next one covers. P is capped at 1 (a /Pressure above 1 just overlaps
% more; it does not spread the rows) and floored at 0.25 (a pull that tapers
% to nothing costs at most four times the rows, and keeps a thin end, as a
% real knife's does). The proc is run a few times for this, but the random
% stream is put back, so a /Pressure that draws random numbers does not move the
% plane's own draws. The default /RowJitter and /AngleJitter follow the
% narrowed band. A /Pressure of 1, or one that never dips below 1, spaces the
% rows exactly as if it were not given. A dip narrower than 1/16 of the row
% can be missed by the sampling.
%
% Texture. /RowJitter shifts each row sideways by up to that many points
% and /AngleJitter tilts it by up to that many degrees, so the rows are
% not machine-parallel. Two neighbouring rows can move toward each other
% or apart, so the default shift is 0.4 of the overlap (F * Overlap * 0.4,
% at most 0.8 of it between neighbours): a larger one opens a bare seam
% between rows (seen in the first render, as a full-height white line). A
% /RowJitter above half the overlap can do so, and so can an /AngleJitter
% on a long row. Each pull also gets `pktrowel`'s own lane texture,
% scrape and per-lane colour jitter.
%
% Determinism: the caller's own `N srand`, same doctrine as `pktrowel`.
% The stream is two draws per row, then the row's `pktrowel` draws. So it is
% fixed by what decides the number of rows, their length and the stops in
% each: the area, /Direction, /Width, /Angle, /Cover, /Overlap (and /Coverage
% when /Overlap is unset), /Drag and /Pitch (the overrun), /Lanes, and the
% depth of a tapering /Pressure (which narrows the rows). Turning
% any of those re-rolls the plane; turning a pure texture key (/Scrape,
% /Viscosity, /EdgeBuildup, /ColorJitter, /Colors, ...) does not. That is
% weaker than `pktrowel`'s own one-control-at-a-time promise.
%
% Safety: at most 4000 rows, checked before anything is drawn; each row is
% also bounded by `pktrowel`'s own deposit budget. The area outline is bounded
% too (issue #401): it is flattened and scanned for its extent without going
% through walkpath, so the scan counts moveto, lineto and closepath visits as
% walkpath's own pass does and a path over wkmaxsegs (50,000) is refused with
% `walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit`, the same name and limit as
% every walkpath preset, before any row is drawn. A refusal leaves the caller's
% path and graphics state as they were.
%
% Scratch prefix tpl- (unused elsewhere), so the many `pt*` names the
% `pktrowel` call overwrites on every row cannot clobber the loop's state.

% x y tplpt -   (widens the s/t extent by one path point)
% s is the coordinate along the pull direction, t across it (left of travel).
% @internal
/tplpt {
    /tply exch def /tplpx exch def
    tplpx tplcd mul tply tplsd mul add
    dup tplsmin lt { dup /tplsmin exch def } if
    dup tplsmax gt { /tplsmax exch def } { pop } ifelse
    tplpx tplsd mul neg tply tplcd mul add
    dup tpltmin lt { dup /tpltmin exch def } if
    dup tpltmax gt { /tpltmax exch def } { pop } ifelse
    /tplany true def
    /tpln tpln 1 add def
    tpln wkmaxsegs gt { /tplover true def exit } if
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A palette-knife plane: fills the current path's area with overlapping, parallel pktrowel pulls in one direction, so the area reads as a single flat facet carrying the blade's texture (a mountain face, a rock). The edge is crisp because the path is the clip. Takes every pktrowel key (Width, Angle, Cover, Coverage, Viscosity, Scrape, EdgeBuildup, Drag, Colors, ColorMix, Light, Touch, Pressure, ...) unchanged, plus Direction, Overlap, RowJitter and AngleJitter. Row spacing follows the blade footprint, its overlap and the thinnest a tapering Pressure gets inside the area, so a taper does not open gaps between rows; deterministic under the caller's own srand; at most 4000 rows; an outline over wkmaxsegs (50,000) segments is refused with walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit. Leaves the caller's path as it was.
% @example: newpath 40 40 moveto 200 40 lineto 120 180 lineto closepath << /Width 16 /Direction -80 /Angle 12 >> pktrowelplane
% @param: /Width [points] Blade length, passed to pktrowel; must be > 0 (default 24)
% @param: /Direction [degrees] Direction the pulls travel, counterclockwise from +x in user space; -90 pulls straight down (default -90)
% @param: /Overlap [fraction of a pull's band] How much of each pull the next one covers, 0 up to but not including 0.95; unset, 0.15 + 0.6*(1 - Coverage) (default unset)
% @param: /RowJitter [points] Largest sideways shift of a row from its even position, deterministic under the caller's srand; more than half the overlap can open a seam (default 0.4 * the overlap width)
% @param: /AngleJitter [degrees] Largest tilt of a row from the pull direction, deterministic under the caller's srand, 0 to 20 (default: the tilt that moves a row's far end 0.15 of the overlap, at most 1.5)
% @param: /Angle [degrees] Blade angle to the pulls as in pktrowel, -80..80; footprint thickness goes as cos(Angle) (default 0)
% @param: /Cover [0..1] Paint on the blade as in pktrowel; scales the band each pull deposits and so the row spacing (default 0.85)
% @param: /Coverage [0..1] Fraction of each pull's contact area that deposits, as in pktrowel; also sets the default /Overlap (default 0.82)
% @param: /Pressure [proc] {t -> mult} proc over each pull's own progress, as in pktrowel; the rows are spaced for the thinnest it gets inside the area (1 down to 0.25), so a taper does not leave gaps; a proc that draws random numbers leaves the stream as it found it (default unset)
% @param: /Scrape [0..1] Lengthwise thinning of each pull, as in pktrowel (default 0.15)
% @param: /EdgeBuildup [0..1] Darker ridges at the two blade edges of each pull, as in pktrowel (default 0.35)
% @param: /Colors [array of rgb arrays] 1 to 8 loaded colors across the blade, as in pktrowel (default unset)
% @param: /Touch [0..1] How hard the blade presses into the canvas set by pkcanvas, as in pktrowel (default unset)
/pktrowelplane {
    /tplopts exch def
    /tplWidth tplopts /Width 24 pkgetdef def
    /tplWidth load xcheck { pktrowelplane-width-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    tplWidth 0 le { pktrowelplane-width-must-be-positive } if
    /tplDir tplopts /Direction -90 pkgetdef def
    /tplDir load xcheck { pktrowelplane-direction-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /tplLanes tplopts /Lanes 14 pkgetdef def
    /tplAngle tplopts /Angle 0 pkgetdef def
    /tplAngle load xcheck { pktrowelplane-angle-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    tplAngle -80 lt tplAngle 80 gt or { pktrowelplane-angle-must-be-minus80-to-80 } if
    /tplCover tplopts /Cover tplopts /Load 0.85 pkgetdef pkgetdef def
    /tplCover load xcheck { pktrowelplane-cover-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    tplCover 0 lt tplCover 1 gt or { pktrowelplane-cover-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /tplCoverage tplopts /Coverage 0.82 pkgetdef def
    /tplCoverage load xcheck { pktrowelplane-coverage-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    tplCoverage 0 lt tplCoverage 1 gt or { pktrowelplane-coverage-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /tplDrag tplopts /Drag 0.35 pkgetdef def
    /tplDrag load xcheck { pktrowelplane-drag-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    tplDrag 0 lt tplDrag 1 gt or { pktrowelplane-drag-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /tplPitch tplopts /Pitch tplWidth 0.35 mul dup 4 gt { pop 4 } if pkgetdef def
    /tplPitch load xcheck { pktrowelplane-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    tplPitch 0 le { pktrowelplane-pitch-must-be-positive } if
    /tplOverlap tplopts /Overlap 0.15 0.6 1 tplCoverage sub mul add pkgetdef def
    /tplOverlap load xcheck { pktrowelplane-overlap-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    tplOverlap 0 lt tplOverlap 0.95 ge or { pktrowelplane-overlap-must-be-0-to-under-0.95 } if
    /tplAJit tplopts /AngleJitter 0 pkgetdef def
    /tplAJitSet tplopts /AngleJitter known def
    /tplAJit load xcheck { pktrowelplane-anglejitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    tplAJit 0 lt tplAJit 20 gt or { pktrowelplane-anglejitter-must-be-0-to-20 } if
    % the band one pull deposits across its direction of travel
    /tplF tplWidth tplAngle cos abs mul 0.35 0.65 tplCover mul add mul def
    % the default /RowJitter needs the effective band (below), so it is set there
    /tplRJitSet tplopts /RowJitter known def
    /tplRJit tplopts /RowJitter 0 pkgetdef def
    /tplRJit load xcheck { pktrowelplane-rowjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    tplRJit 0 lt { pktrowelplane-rowjitter-must-not-be-negative } if
    % Every key `pktrowel` checks is checked here, on an empty path: it runs all
    % its guards, draws nothing and takes no random draw. So a bad /Lanes,
    % /Colors, /Touch (no canvas) or /Pressure raises before the clip or the
    % rotation below is in force. If it does, the gsave is undone and the same
    % call is made again on the caller's own path: every guard sits before
    % `pktrowel` draws or changes state, so it raises the same error again,
    % from a clean graphics state and with the error reported as `pktrowel`'s.
    { gsave newpath tplopts pktrowel grestore } stopped { grestore tplopts pktrowel } if
    /tplcd tplDir cos def /tplsd tplDir sin def
    /tplsmin 1e30 def /tplsmax -1e30 def /tpltmin 1e30 def /tpltmax -1e30 def
    /tplany false def
    % Segment-count bound (issue #401): this scan walks the flattened outline
    % without ever calling walkpath, so #110's wkmaxsegs does not reach it. It
    % counts moveto, lineto and closepath visits, the three wkmeasure counts, and
    % past wkmaxsegs it stops the scan with `exit`. The raise waits until the
    % grestore below has put the caller's path and graphics state back, so a
    % refusal leaves both as it found them. `exit` is a plain `exit` out of
    % pathforall (which loops), not an error.
    /tpln 0 def /tplover false def
    gsave
        flattenpath
        { tplpt } { tplpt }
        { } { /tpln tpln 1 add def tpln wkmaxsegs gt { /tplover true def exit } if }
        pathforall
    grestore
    tplover { walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
    tplany {
        % rows run from a third of a band outside the near edge to the far one
        /tplt0 tpltmin tplF 0.3 mul sub def
        % How far a row must overrun the area so the blade's entry/exit trim
        % (up to 6% of the row), its drag and its skew all land outside it.
        /tplspan tplsmax tplsmin sub def
        /tplm tplspan 0.07 mul tplWidth 0.5 mul tplAngle sin abs tplDrag add mul add
              tplPitch add 0.85 div def
        /tplsc tplsmin tplsmax add 0.5 mul def
        /tplhl tplspan 0.5 mul tplm add def
        % The band a row really lays is F*P, where P is the thinnest the caller's
        % /Pressure gets inside the area. Only a /Pressure that dips below 1 changes
        % anything: everything else keeps F and so keeps today's output exactly.
        /tplFe tplF def
        tplopts /Pressure known {
            % The proc is read at 17 points of the row's own t that the area
            % occupies (the overrun at both ends lies outside it, and a taper to 0
            % there must not set the spacing). It may draw random numbers, so the
            % stream is put back if it moved. P is never above 1 (a heavier
            % pressure just overlaps more) and never below 0.25, so a pull that
            % tapers to nothing costs at most four times the rows and still leaves
            % a thin end, as a real knife does.
            /tplPressure tplopts /Pressure get def
            /tplrr rrand def
            /tplplo tplm 2 tplhl mul div def
            1 /tplP exch def
            0 1 16 {
                16 div 1 2 tplplo mul sub mul tplplo add tplPressure abs
                dup tplP lt { /tplP exch def } { pop } ifelse
            } for
            rrand tplrr ne { tplrr srand } if
            tplP 0.25 max /tplP exch def
            tplP 1 lt { /tplFe tplF tplP mul def } if
        } if
        /tplStep tplFe 1 tplOverlap sub mul def
        tplRJitSet not { /tplRJit tplFe tplOverlap mul 0.4 mul def } if
        % compared as a real: `cvi` of a huge extent would raise rangecheck
        % instead of the guard
        /tplrows tpltmax tplF 0.3 mul add tplt0 sub tplStep div ceiling 1 add def
        tplrows 4000 gt { pktrowelplane-row-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
        /tplrows tplrows cvi def
        % Unset, the tilt is what moves a row's far end by 0.15 of the overlap,
        % at most 1.5 degrees, so the tilt cannot open a seam on a long row.
        tplAJitSet not {
            /tplAJit 0.15 tplFe tplOverlap mul mul tplhl div 1 min
                dup dup mul 1 exch sub sqrt atan 1.5 min def
        } if
        % /Light is a direction in the caller's user space; the rows are drawn
        % in a space rotated by /Direction, where the same light is /Light -
        % /Direction. The caller's own dict is left alone.
        /tplrowopts tplopts def
        tplopts /Light known {
            /tplrowopts tplopts dup length 1 add dict copy def
            tplrowopts /Light tplopts /Light get tplDir sub put
        } if
        % A row can still raise a guard the empty-path call could not (the deposit
        % budget, or /Touch's area limit, both of which depend on the row's
        % length). The rows run under the clip and the rotation, so an error
        % must not leave them behind: it is caught, the gsave undone, and the
        % error raised again from here. Every `pktrowel` guard is an undefined
        % name, so naming it again is the same error; anything else is raised
        % through errordict, whose handler takes the offending command off the
        % operand stack (Ghostscript and pscat alike), so it is pushed first.
        % A caller's /Pressure may also just `stop`, which
        % raises no error: newerror tells the two apart, and a plain stop is
        % passed on as a plain stop.
        $error /newerror false put
        {
        gsave
            clip
            newpath
            tplDir rotate
            0 1 tplrows 1 sub {
                /tpli exch def
                % both draws are unconditional: a fixed stream
                /tplrt tplt0 tpli tplStep mul add tplRJit pkjit add def
                /tplra tplAJit pkjit def
                newpath
                tplsc tplhl tplra cos mul sub  tplrt tplhl tplra sin mul sub moveto
                tplsc tplhl tplra cos mul add  tplrt tplhl tplra sin mul add lineto
                tplrowopts pktrowel
            } for
        grestore
        } stopped {
            grestore
            $error /newerror get {
                $error /errorname get /undefined eq
                    { $error /command get cvx exec }
                    { $error /command get errordict $error /errorname get 2 copy known { get exec } { pop pop pop stop } ifelse }
                ifelse
            } { stop } ifelse
        } if
    } if
} bind def

%%SelfTest: pktrowelplane-bounds-its-area-outline
%   % Issue #401: pktrowelplane flattens and scans the area outline itself,
%   % never through walkpath, so it counts moveto, lineto and closepath visits
%   % against wkmaxsegs. A closed triangle is moveto + lineto + lineto +
%   % closepath = 4 visits, so (wkmaxsegs - 4) extra zero-length linetos make
%   % exactly wkmaxsegs; one more is over. Zero-length linetos survive
%   % flattenpath, so the count is real.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto wkmaxsegs 4 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat
%     25 40 lineto closepath << /Width 40 >> pktrowelplane }
%   (a closed outline of exactly wkmaxsegs segments still fills) mustpass
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto wkmaxsegs 3 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat
%     25 40 lineto closepath << /Width 40 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a closed outline one segment over wkmaxsegs) mustguard
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto wkmaxsegs { 0 0 rlineto } repeat
%     << /Width 40 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (an open outline one segment over wkmaxsegs) mustguard
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pktrowelplane-refuses-a-huge-outline-before-any-row
%   % Without the bound the call never refuses, so the guard is pinned by
%   % where it fires. pktrowel is replaced by a counter (each block runs in
%   % a fresh interpreter): the one call every run makes is the empty-path
%   % validation call, so a refusal before any row means exactly one call.
%   /tcalls 0 def
%   /pktrowel { pop /tcalls tcalls 1 add def } def
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto wkmaxsegs 3 sub { 0 0 rlineto } repeat
%     25 40 lineto closepath << /Width 40 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (the over-limit outline is refused) mustguard
%   tcalls 1 eq (no row was laid before the refusal) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pktrowelplane-refusal-leaves-the-caller-as-it-was
%   % The raise comes after the scan's grestore, not from inside it: the
%   % caller's graphics state and curved path are untouched by a refusal.
%   0.25 setgray
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 20 0 30 20 50 20 curveto wkmaxsegs { 0 0 rlineto } repeat
%   closepath
%   { << /Width 40 >> pktrowelplane } stopped /tstop exch def
%   tstop $error /command get /walkpath-segment-count-exceeds-safety-limit eq and
%   (the refusal is the bound's undefined name) mustbe
%   currentgray 0.25 sub abs 0.001 lt (the gray was not changed) mustbe
%   /tcurves 0 def
%   { pop pop } { pop pop } { pop pop pop pop pop pop /tcurves 1 def } { }
%   pathforall
%   tcurves 1 eq (the curve in the caller's path was not flattened) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pktrowelplane-guards
%   % a triangle path, set up per case (the procedure reads the current path)
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /Width 0 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-width-must-be-positive
%   (/Width 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /Width { 5 } >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-width-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Width { 5 }) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /Direction { 5 } >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-direction-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Direction { 5 }) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /Angle 90 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-angle-must-be-minus80-to-80
%   (/Angle 90) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /Cover 1.5 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-cover-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Cover 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /Coverage -1 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-coverage-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Coverage -1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /Overlap 1 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-overlap-must-be-0-to-under-0.95
%   (/Overlap 1: zero spacing, unbounded rows) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /Overlap -0.1 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-overlap-must-be-0-to-under-0.95
%   (/Overlap -0.1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /RowJitter -1 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-rowjitter-must-not-be-negative
%   (/RowJitter -1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /AngleJitter 30 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-anglejitter-must-be-0-to-20
%   (/AngleJitter 30) mustguard
%
%   % rows are counted before anything is drawn: a 1000pt area under a 0.1pt blade
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 1000 0 lineto 1000 1000 lineto closepath
%     << /Width 0.1 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-row-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a tiny blade on a big area) mustguard
%
%   % a key only pktrowel checks still raises before the clip and rotation
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /Lanes 50 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowel-lanes-must-be-1-to-40
%   (/Lanes 50, caught by pktrowel's own guard) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath << /Touch 0.5 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowel-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch with no canvas) mustguard
%
%   % a row past pktrowel's deposit budget raises with the state still unwound
%   % (this block also fails on a gsave left open)
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto 100000 10 lineto 0 10 lineto closepath
%     << /Width 12 /Direction 0 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowel-deposit-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a 100000pt row) mustguard
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath
%     << /Width 10 >> pktrowelplane grestore }
%   (a triangle) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath << /Width 10 >> pktrowelplane grestore }
%   (an empty path is nothing to fill) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath 5 5 moveto << /Width 10 >> pktrowelplane grestore }
%   (a bare moveto, no area) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath
%     << /Width 10 /Direction 35 /Colors [ [ 1 0 0 ] [ 0 0 1 ] ] /Light 90 >> pktrowelplane
%     grestore }
%   (a loaded, directed plane) mustpass
%
%   % a huge extent still raises the row guard, not rangecheck
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 1e12 0 lineto 1e12 1e12 lineto closepath
%     << /Width 10 >> pktrowelplane }
%   /pktrowelplane-row-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (an area far past cvi's range) mustguard
%
%   % the caller's path survives
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 25 40 lineto closepath
%     << /Width 10 >> pktrowelplane
%     pathbbox pop exch pop exch pop 50 eq grestore } stopped { false } if
%   (the path is left as it was) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest

% --- fan brush (issue #114) -------------------------------------------
% opts pkfan -
%
% A spread bundle of bristles: foliage, evergreen branches, grasses,
% cloud feathering, soft texture.
%
% What makes it a fan rather than a wide brush -- and specifically what
% distinguishes it from `pkdry`, its nearest relative here -- is one
% term. pkdry holds each bristle at a *fixed* lateral offset, so its
% bristles run as parallel tracks. A fan brush's bristles leave a
% flattened ferrule and splay apart as the stroke travels, so each
% bristle's offset *opens* along the path:
%
%   offset(bristle, t) = ubase * (Width/2) * Spread * open(t)
%   open(t)            = ferrule + (1 - ferrule) * t^0.6
%   ferrule            = 1 - Splay*0.75
%
% /Splay 0 leaves `open` flat at 1 and reproduces parallel bristles;
% /Splay 1 narrows the start to a quarter of the tip's spread. It does
% *not* narrow it to zero, and that floor is deliberate: a fan brush's
% ferrule is flat and wide, its bristles never converge to a point, and
% a version without the floor was rendered first -- every bristle
% coincided near t=0 and the mark's first third came out as one opaque
% blob with a fan glued to the end of it.
%
% The exponent likewise came from looking: linear, t^0.6 and t^1.6 were
% rendered side by side, and t^0.6 -- opening quickly and then easing --
% is the one that reads as bristles springing apart rather than as a
% smeared wedge.
%
% The two mark families the tool has to cover fall out of the contact
% chain, the same seeded two-state Markov pkdry uses: many thin
% bristles at high /Load give the soft feathered mark, while fewer
% bristles at low /Load and high /Dropout give the separated bristle
% mark.
%
% /Ragged trims each bristle to its own length so the tips feather.
% Without it every bristle stops at the same arc length and the mark
% ends in a straight edge, which reads as a comb rather than foliage.
%
% /Flick tapers a bristle toward its tip. It is applied only to the run
% that reaches the bristle's own trimmed end, never to every dash: a
% bristle broken into four dashes by the contact chain -- exactly the
% separated-bristle case above -- would otherwise become four tapered
% commas instead of one flicked bristle.
%
% Progress `t` is per *subpath* (walkpath's contract), so each subpath
% opens its own fan. That is the right behavior for foliage, where one
% call places several flicks; it also means a closed subpath's fan is
% discontinuous at the seam, since t jumps from 1 back to 0 there.
%
% A degenerate single-point subpath is a *pressed* fan: the bristles
% radiate about the point instead of following a direction that does
% not exist. Without that special case the dab collapses -- at t=0 every
% bristle sits at `ferrule` times its offset, and at a high /Splay that
% is nearly the centerline, so all of them land on top of each other.
%
% They radiate through a 140-degree arc opening toward +y, *not* through
% a full circle: a fan brush pressed to the page leaves a fan, which is
% the whole point of the tool, and a shrub or a grass clump opens
% upward. (An earlier draft of this comment said "isotropically",
% copying pkoil's language for its own degenerate case -- that was
% wrong about this preset, and Codex review caught the doc contradicting
% the code rather than the other way round.) To aim a pressed fan
% somewhere else, rotate the space around it:
%
%   gsave  x y translate  -30 rotate  newpath 0 0 moveto
%   << /Width 40 >> pkfan  grestore
%
% Determinism: the caller's own `N srand`, same doctrine as its
% siblings. Note this preset deliberately makes *no* fixed-draw-count
% claim of the kind pktrowel documents: it calls pkribbon once per
% inked run, and pkribbon consumes randomness of its own, so /Load and
% /Dropout necessarily change total consumption by changing how many
% runs there are. pkdry has the same property.
%
% Safety: /Bristles is capped 1..60 and Bristles * stopCount is checked
% against 150000 inside the counting walk, before any allocation --
% pkdry's bound and pkdry's placement argument.
%
% Loaded colours (issue #183, slice L4). `/Colors`, `/ColorMix` and `/Light`
% load the fan with more than one colour, spread across the bristles: a
% bristle's head position is its `pfub`, so the first colour sits on the left
% of the travel (the same side the splay's positive offsets go). The jitter
% draw is still taken once per bristle, where it always was, but with a load
% it is stored (`pfcj`) rather than applied there, and the colour is set per
% run (`pfrun`) and per pressed ray (`pfray`), just before their `pkribbon`
% call. That is what lets `/Light` flip the load per subpath: a bristle's runs
% span every subpath of the path, so one colour per bristle could not follow
% two subpaths travelling opposite ways. A pressed fan's rays radiate through
% 140 degrees and `/Colors` paints across that arc, first colour on the left
% of the fan's axis; a pressed point is never flipped. Unset, every branch is
% `pfColorRec null ne` and the render is byte-identical to before.
%
% Scratch prefix pf- (pkfan/pfrun/pfopen), distinct from pk-/pn-/pb-/
% pz-/po-/pt-/pw-/pv- because pkfan calls pkribbon once per dash and
% pkribbon's body redefines every pk*-prefixed name.

% How many times a pressed ray may be resampled by pkribbon. A ray is a
% straight segment, so this only has to be enough to carry /Flick's
% taper smoothly.
% @internal
/pfraysamples 16 def

% What one degenerate stop is charged against the safety budget. It is
% deliberately larger than pfraysamples: a degenerate stop costs a whole
% *nested pkribbon call* per bristle -- option parsing, its own two
% walkpath passes, its own allocation -- on top of the samples, and that
% call overhead is what dominates. Calibrated by measurement rather than
% by counting samples: 100 degenerate subpaths at 60 bristles is 6000
% rays and takes ~1.3s in release (~24s in the debug build tests run
% under), which is not something an unattended render should do
% silently, so the ceiling has to reject it -- 100*60*32 exceeds 150000.
% A realistic foliage cluster of a dozen dabs at 13 bristles charges
% under 5000 and is nowhere near it.
% @internal
/pfraycharge 32 def

% rate pfroll -> bool
% Its own copy rather than a call to pkdry's or pkoil's, per this
% file's prefix discipline -- pkfan must not depend on a sibling
% preset's scratch namespace staying put.
% @internal
/pfroll {
    dup 1 ge { pop true } { dup 0 le { pop false } { frnd exch lt } ifelse } ifelse
} bind def

% t pfopen -> multiplier   (reads pfferrule)
% @internal
/pfopen {
    dup 0 le { pop 0 } { 0.6 exp } ifelse
    1 pfferrule sub mul pfferrule add
} bind def

% i pfcolor -   (set the loaded color of the current bristle for a run that
% starts at stop i; only called with /Colors set)
% The head position is the bristle's pfub (+1 is the left of the travel, the
% first colour) and the shade is the bristle's own stored /ColorJitter draw
% pfcj, so every run of a bristle -- across every subpath -- is jittered
% alike. The flip is the run's first stop's: runs never cross a subpath, and a
% pressed ray is a degenerate subpath of its own (never flipped).
% @internal
/pfcolor {
    /pfcli exch def
    pfColorRec pfub
    pfColorRec 2 get { pfflips pfcli get } { false } ifelse
    puat puwet 3 array astore 1 pfcj add shade setrgbcolor
} bind def

% runstart runend islast pfrun -
% Builds one contiguous inked run of the current bristle as a
% centerline and hands it to pkribbon.
% @internal
/pfrun {
    /pflast exch def /pfrend exch def /pfrstart exch def
    /pflen 0 def
    newpath
    pfrstart 1 pfrend {
        /pfri exch def
        pfraw pfri get
        dup 0 get /pfrx exch def
        dup 1 get /pfry exch def
        dup 2 get /pfra exch def
        3 get pfopen /pfro exch def
        /pfrd pfub pfhalf mul pfro mul pfJitter pkjit add def
        /pfpx pfrx pfra 90 add cos pfrd mul add def
        /pfpy pfry pfra 90 add sin pfrd mul add def
        pfri pfrstart gt {
            /pflen pflen
                pfpx pfppx sub dup mul pfpy pfppy sub dup mul add sqrt
            add def
        } if
        /pfppx pfpx def /pfppy pfpy def
        pfpx pfpy pfri pfrstart eq { moveto } { lineto } ifelse
    } for
    % The centerline handed to pkribbon is *not* the source path: each
    % sample is displaced sideways by the splay and by /Jitter before
    % pkribbon resamples it, so its length is unrelated to the stop
    % count the outer Bristles*stops budget guards. Two separate ways to
    % inflate it were found in review -- a huge /Jitter, then a huge
    % /Width at /Splay 1, where the offset moves O(Width) between
    % consecutive stops with no jitter at all.
    %
    % Rather than bound the displacement analytically (which means
    % assuming the worst case everywhere, and coarsens ordinary strokes
    % where the splay barely moves between samples), measure the run
    % that was actually built and floor the pitch so pkribbon takes at
    % most 8 samples per source stop. For a normal run the length is
    % about stops*Pitch, so the floor works out to Pitch/8 and never
    % binds; it only engages on the pathological shapes.
    /pfnpts pfrend pfrstart sub 1 add def
    /pfrp pflen 8 pfnpts mul div def
    pfrp pfPitch lt { /pfrp pfPitch def } if
    pfColorRec null ne { pfrstart pfcolor } if
    << /Width pfrw /Pitch pfrp /StartCap /pointed /EndCap /pointed
       /EndTaper pflast { pfFlick } { 0 } ifelse >> pkribbon
} bind def

% i pfray -   (the current bristle's ray of a *pressed* fan, at the
% degenerate stop `i`)
%
% Emitted per bristle from inside the bristle loop rather than as a
% whole-path special case, so that a path mixing ordinary subpaths with
% bare `moveto`s gets a radiating dab for each of the latter. Checking
% only whether the *entire* path was one stop missed exactly that
% combination, and walkpath reports angle 0 for a degenerate stop, so
% the generic branch laid those bristles out along one straight line
% instead (Codex review).
% @internal
/pfray {
    /pfrayi exch def
    pfraw pfrayi get dup 0 get /pfdx exch def 1 get /pfdy exch def
    /pfda pfub 70 mul 90 add def
    /pfdl pfhalf 1.15 mul 1 pfRagged frnd mul sub mul def
    % The outer safety budget counts source-path stops, and a degenerate
    % subpath contributes exactly one however fine /Pitch is -- so the
    % ray's own resampling is the one place nested cost is *not* bounded
    % by that budget, and `/Pitch 0.001` would sample a ray millions of
    % times while passing the guard. The ray is a fixed length, so
    % bounding its pitch from below bounds the work outright (Codex
    % review).
    /pfrp pfPitch dup pfdl pfraysamples div lt { pop pfdl pfraysamples div } if def
    pfrp 0 le { /pfrp pfPitch def } if
    newpath
    pfdx pfdy moveto
    pfdx pfda cos pfdl mul add  pfdy pfda sin pfdl mul add  lineto
    pfColorRec null ne { pfrayi pfcolor } if
    << /Width pfrw /Pitch pfrp /StartCap /pointed /EndCap /pointed
       /EndTaper pfFlick >> pkribbon
} bind def

%%SelfTest: pkfan-colors-rejects-malformed-loads
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors 5 >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (a number as /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors { [ 0 0 0 ] } >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (a procedure as /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ ] >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (no colours) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ 9 { [ 0 0 0 ] } repeat ] >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (nine colours) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 ] ] >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays
%   (a 2-element entry) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 1.5 ] ] >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colors-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a component of 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColorMix 1.5 >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colormix-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/ColorMix 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkfan }
%   (1 colour) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] >> pkfan }
%   (2 colours) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ 8 { [ 0.2 0.4 1 ] } repeat ] /ColorMix 0 /Light -45 >> pkfan }
%   (8 colours, the cap) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkfan-colors-spelling-and-companions
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colours [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/Colours) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColourMix 0.5 >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/ColourMix) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /HighlightColour [ 1 1 1 ] >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/HighlightColour) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /ColorMix 0.5 >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-colormix-needs-colors
%   (/ColorMix without /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Light 90 >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-light-needs-colors
%   (/Light without /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /Light { 90 } >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-light-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (a procedure as /Light) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /Light 90 >> pkfan }
%   (/Light with /Colors) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest

%%SelfTest: pkfan-touch-guards
%   % The no-canvas cases first: nothing in this block has set a surface yet.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 0.5 >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch 0.5 before any pkcanvas) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 1 >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch 1 before any pkcanvas: any /Touch needs a canvas) mustguard
%
%   << >> pkcanvas
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 1.5 >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-touch-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Touch 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch { } >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-touch-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Touch { }) mustguard
%
%   % A coarse /Pitch and two bristles keep the deposit budget out of the way.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto
%     << /Touch 0.5 /Pitch 1000 /Bristles 2 >> pkfan }
%   /pkfan-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a 1e5pt stroke at /Touch 0.5) mustguard
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 0.5 >> pkfan grestore }
%   (/Touch 0.5 with a canvas) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Touch 0.5 /Colors [ [ 1 0 0 ] [ 0 0 1 ] ] /Light 90 >> pkfan grestore }
%   (a touched stroke with a colour load) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto << /Touch 0 >> pkfan grestore }
%   (/Touch 0 on a pressed point) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A fan brush: a bundle of bristles that leaves a flattened ferrule and splays apart as the stroke travels, for foliage, evergreen branches, grasses and soft feathering. Distinct from pkdry, whose bristles stay parallel -- /Splay is the whole difference. With /Colors it spreads up to eight loaded colours across the bristles, blended by /ColorMix and lit from /Light. Many thin bristles at high Load give a soft feathered mark; fewer at low Load give separated bristle marks. A single-point subpath is a pressed fan, its bristles radiating through a 140-degree arc opening toward +y; rotate the space around it to aim it elsewhere. Deterministic under the caller's own srand; bounded by Bristles * stops.
% @example: newpath 40 120 moveto 100 150 160 160 210 145 curveto << /Width 60 /Splay 0.6 >> pkfan
% @param: /Width [points] Fan width at full spread; must be > 0 (default 30)
% @param: /Bristles [count] Bristle count; must be a whole number, hard safety cap 1..60 (default 24)
% @param: /Spread [fraction of /Width] 0..1 fraction of Width the bristles occupy once fully opened (default 0.9)
% @param: /Splay [0..1] how much narrower the ferrule is than the tip; 0 is parallel bristles, 1 narrows the start to a quarter of the tip's spread and never further (default 0.6)
% @param: /BristleWidth [points] Base width of each bristle's own mark; must be > 0 (default Width*0.035)
% @param: /WidthJitter [fraction of /BristleWidth] 0..1 fraction of BristleWidth each bristle's width randomly varies by (default 0.35)
% @param: /Load [per /Width] 0..1 resume-contact rate per one Width of travel (default 0.92)
% @param: /Dropout [per /Width] 0..1 lose-contact rate per one Width of travel; raise it with a low Load for separated bristle marks (default 0.08)
% @param: /Ragged [0..1] how much each bristle's length varies, so the tips feather instead of ending on one straight edge (default 0.25)
% @param: /Flick [0..1] taper toward each bristle's tip, applied only to the run that reaches it (default 0.5)
% @param: /Jitter [points] Seeded lateral displacement of each bristle's trace (default 0)
% @param: /Pitch [points] walkpath sampling pitch for the shared centerline pass; must be > 0 (default BristleWidth*0.6, capped at 6)
% @param: /ColorJitter [0..1] per-bristle color variation around the caller's current color (default 0.06)
% @param: /Colors [array of rgb arrays] 1 to 8 loaded colors, listed from the fan's left edge (looking along the travel) to its right, evenly spaced across the bristles; when set, the current color is not used for the mark, though /ColorJitter still varies it; a pressed fan paints them across its arc, first color on the left of its axis. An entry may be a bare pigment name such as /sapgreen (see PigmentNames), which stands for that pigment; a pigmix spec goes through pigcolors (default unset)
% @param: /ColorMix [0..1] with /Colors, how far neighbouring colors have blended on the brush: 0 is hard bands, 1 a smooth gradient (default 0.3)
% @param: /Light [degrees] with /Colors, the direction the light comes from, counterclockwise from +x in user space: each subpath puts the first color on the side facing the light, whichever way it travels; a pressed fan is never flipped (default unset: the first color stays on the left of the travel)
% @param: /Touch [0..1] How hard the fan presses into the shared canvas set by pkcanvas, which must be called first: below 1 the bristles land only on the weave's knuckles that stand proud enough for that pressure to reach, lower is lighter and 0 deposits nothing; 1 is full contact, identical to leaving it unset. Composes with /Load and /Dropout and takes no rand draws (default unset)
/pkfan {
    /pfopts exch def
    /pfWidth pfopts /Width 30 pkgetdef def
    /pfWidth load xcheck { pkfan-width-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfWidth 0 le { pkfan-width-must-be-positive } if
    /pfBristles pfopts /Bristles 24 pkgetdef def
    /pfBristles load xcheck { pkfan-bristles-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfBristles 1 lt pfBristles 60 gt or pfBristles truncate pfBristles ne or
        { pkfan-bristles-must-be-1-to-60 } if
    /pfSpread pfopts /Spread 0.9 pkgetdef def
    /pfSpread load xcheck { pkfan-spread-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfSpread 0 lt pfSpread 1 gt or { pkfan-spread-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pfSplay pfopts /Splay 0.6 pkgetdef def
    /pfSplay load xcheck { pkfan-splay-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfSplay 0 lt pfSplay 1 gt or { pkfan-splay-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pfrwbase pfopts /BristleWidth pfWidth 0.035 mul pkgetdef def
    /pfrwbase load xcheck { pkfan-bristlewidth-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfrwbase 0 le { pkfan-bristlewidth-must-be-positive } if
    /pfWidthJitter pfopts /WidthJitter 0.35 pkgetdef def
    /pfWidthJitter load xcheck { pkfan-widthjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfWidthJitter 0 lt pfWidthJitter 1 gt or { pkfan-widthjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pfLoad pfopts /Load 0.92 pkgetdef def
    /pfLoad load xcheck { pkfan-load-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfLoad 0 lt pfLoad 1 gt or { pkfan-load-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pfDropout pfopts /Dropout 0.08 pkgetdef def
    /pfDropout load xcheck { pkfan-dropout-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfDropout 0 lt pfDropout 1 gt or { pkfan-dropout-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pfRagged pfopts /Ragged 0.25 pkgetdef def
    /pfRagged load xcheck { pkfan-ragged-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfRagged 0 lt pfRagged 1 gt or { pkfan-ragged-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pfFlick pfopts /Flick 0.5 pkgetdef def
    /pfFlick load xcheck { pkfan-flick-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfFlick 0 lt pfFlick 1 gt or { pkfan-flick-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pfJitter pfopts /Jitter 0 pkgetdef def
    /pfJitter load xcheck { pkfan-jitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /pfPitch pfopts /Pitch pfrwbase 0.6 mul dup 6 gt { pop 6 } if pkgetdef def
    /pfPitch load xcheck { pkfan-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfPitch 0 le { pkfan-pitch-must-be-positive } if
    /pfColorJitter pfopts /ColorJitter 0.06 pkgetdef def
    /pfColorJitter load xcheck { pkfan-colorjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfColorJitter 0 lt pfColorJitter 1 gt or { pkfan-colorjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    % /Touch (issue #225, CANVAS_SURFACE.md 2.2): any /Touch, even 1, needs
    % a canvas. Only a value below 1 builds a catch, so unset and 1 take
    % exactly the path they took before /Touch existed.
    /pfTouch pfopts /Touch 1 pkgetdef def
    /pfTouch load xcheck { pkfan-touch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pfTouch 0 lt pfTouch 1 gt or { pkfan-touch-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    pfopts /Touch known {
        userdict /pjCTM known not { pkfan-touch-needs-a-canvas } if
    } if
    /pfTouched pfTouch 1 lt def
    % The load: null when /Colors is unset, so every new branch below is
    % `pfColorRec null ne` and an unset key never reaches it.
    pfopts (pkfan) puparse /pfColorRec exch def
    pfopts (pkfan) pulightneedscolors
    % Counting pass with the budget checked inside the walk, so a
    % pathological path is rejected before any allocation (pkdry's
    % placement argument).
    % A degenerate subpath costs far more than one stop: it becomes one
    % pkribbon call per bristle, each resampling a fixed-length ray up to
    % pfraysamples times. Counting it as a single stop let a path batching
    % many bare movetos run ~6 million nested samples while the counter
    % saw a few thousand -- 100 such subpaths at 60 bristles took ~24s
    % (Codex review). Charging it at its real relative cost is what makes
    % the advertised ceiling mean what it says.
    % pfcount is the *weighted* cost used for the budget; pfstops is the
    % actual number of stops, which is what the record array is sized
    % by. Conflating the two sized the array by the inflated cost and
    % then read past the filled entries.
    /pfcount 0 def
    /pfstops 0 def
    pfPitch { /pfcae exch def pop pop pop pop pop
              /pfstops pfstops 1 add def
              /pfcount pfcount
                  pfcae 3 and 3 eq { pfraycharge } { 1 } ifelse
              add def
              pfBristles pfcount mul 150000 gt
                  { pkfan-deposit-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if } walkpath
    /pfn pfstops def
    /pfraw pfn array def
    /pfci 0 def
    pfPitch { 6 array astore pfraw pfci 3 -1 roll put /pfci pfci 1 add def } walkpath
    % The catch's box (CANVAS_SURFACE.md 3.2): every stop grown by the
    % farthest any emitter puts paint from it, checked against the cell
    % budget before anything is drawn. With half = Width/2 * Spread:
    %   runs   a bristle's centerline sits at |ub| * half * pfopen(t) from
    %          the stop (|ub| < 1, pfopen <= 1) plus pkjit's |Jitter|;
    %   rays   a pressed fan's ray is at most 1.15 * half long, from the
    %          stop, at /Ragged 0 (Ragged only shortens it);
    % and either way each mark is a pkribbon of half-width at most
    % BristleWidth * (1 + WidthJitter) / 2 (pkribbon is called without a
    % /Jitter, its caps are /pointed, and it resamples a polyline, so
    % nothing sits farther than that half-width from a vertex). The larger
    % of the two reaches is used for every stop.
    pfTouched pfn 0 gt and {
        /pfreach
            pfWidth 0.5 mul pfSpread mul dup pfJitter abs add
            exch 1.15 mul 2 copy lt { exch } if pop
            pfrwbase 1 pfWidthJitter add mul 0.5 mul add
        def
        [ pfraw { pop pfreach } pjstopbox ] /pfTouchBox exch def
        pfTouchBox aload pop pjcells pjbudget gt
            { pkfan-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit } if
    } if
    pfn 0 gt {
        gsave
        pfTouched { pfTouchBox aload pop pfTouch pjcatch } if
        % One flip per stop, so a whole subpath mirrors together. No draws.
        pfColorRec null ne {
            pfColorRec 2 get {
                /pfflips pfraw pfn pfPitch 0 pfColorRec 3 get puflips def
            } if
        } if
        /pfbasecolor [ currentrgbcolor ] def
        /pfferrule 1 pfSplay 0.75 mul sub def
        /pfhalf pfWidth 0.5 mul pfSpread mul def
        1 1 pfBristles {
                /pfb exch def
                /pfub pfb 0.5 sub pfBristles div 2 mul 1 sub def
                /pfrw pfrwbase pfWidthJitter pfrwbase mul pkjit add def
                pfrw pfrwbase 0.05 mul lt { /pfrw pfrwbase 0.05 mul def } if
                % Each bristle keeps its own length, so the tips feather
                % rather than stopping on one straight edge.
                /pfstop 1 pfRagged frnd mul sub def
                % The jitter draw is taken here either way. Unloaded, it
                % colours the bristle now; loaded, it is stored and applied
                % per run (pfcolor), because the load's colour depends on
                % the run's subpath under /Light.
                pfColorRec null ne {
                    /pfcj pfColorJitter pkjit def
                } {
                    pfbasecolor 1 pfColorJitter pkjit add shade setrgbcolor
                } ifelse
                /pfstate pfLoad pfroll def
                /pfrunning false def
                /pfstart 0 def
                0 1 pfn 1 sub {
                    /pfi exch def
                    /pfrec pfraw pfi get def
                    /pfae pfrec 5 get def
                    /pfin pfrec 3 get pfstop le def
                    pfae 1 and 1 eq {
                        % A new subpath opens its own fan.
                        pfrunning { pfstart pfi 1 sub false pfrun /pfrunning false def } if
                        /pfstate pfLoad pfroll def
                    } {
                        /pfstep pfrec 4 get pfWidth div def
                        pfstate {
                            pfDropout pfstep mul pfroll { /pfstate false def } if
                        } {
                            pfLoad pfstep mul pfroll { /pfstate true def } if
                        } ifelse
                    } ifelse
                    pfae 3 and 3 eq {
                        % A degenerate subpath (a bare moveto): a pressed
                        % fan radiating about the point. It honors the
                        % contact state like any other stop, so /Load 0
                        % leaves no dab rather than a full one.
                        pfstate pfin and { pfi pfray } if
                    } {
                        pfstate pfin and {
                            pfrunning not { /pfstart pfi def /pfrunning true def } if
                        } {
                            pfrunning {
                                % The flick belongs to the run that
                                % actually reaches this bristle's end,
                                % not to every dash along the way.
                                pfstart pfi 1 sub pfin not pfstate and pfrun
                                /pfrunning false def
                            } if
                        } ifelse
                        pfae 2 and 2 eq {
                            pfrunning { pfstart pfi true pfrun /pfrunning false def } if
                        } if
                    } ifelse
                } for
                pfrunning { pfstart pfn 1 sub true pfrun /pfrunning false def } if
        } for
        pfbasecolor aload pop setrgbcolor
        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% --- broad flat brush (issue #115) ------------------------------------
% opts pkbroad -
%
% The block-in tool: sky and water bands, reflections, broad masses.
%
% Three presets in this file already make a wide mark, so the first
% question was what makes this one its own tool rather than a preset of
% one of them.
%
%   pkribbon   a band of variable width, but a *geometric* one -- no
%              tool character at all
%   pknib      width driven by the angle between the path and a fixed
%              nib angle: a calligraphic pmn, which swells and narrows
%              as a curve turns
%   pktrowel   a rigid blade: hard scraped edges, lengthwise streaks,
%              no base pass, paint pushed into ridges
%
% What separates a flat *brush* from a palette *knife* is that a brush
% holds its paint in bristles, and so **runs out**. A loaded flat brush
% lays solid colour where it touches down and goes progressively drier
% as it travels; that fading is how a block-in is actually made, and it
% is the thing none of the three above model. Issue #111 deliberately
% left depletion out of pktrowel for this reason -- a blade carries a
% smear of paint, not a reservoir.
%
% So depletion is the whole idea here. The band is divided into /Grain
% striations across its width, and each striation is given its own fixed
% affinity for the surface. The brush's charge falls along the stroke:
%
%     charge(t) = Charge * (1 - Depletion*t)
%
% and a striation deposits for as long as the charge stays above its own
% affinity. Striations therefore drop out one by one as the stroke
% travels, in a seeded but fixed order, and the band thins from solid to
% broken without any per-stop speckle -- which is what makes the marks
% read as *streaks along the direction of travel* rather than as noise.
% A striation whose affinity is above /Charge from the outset never
% deposits at all, so a low /Charge gives the skipping, gappy mark of a
% brush that was barely loaded.
%
% /Depletion 0 turns the fade off entirely and gives an even, fully
% loaded block-in -- the flat side of the tool's range.
%
% Deliberately *not* named /Load. /Load in this file is a contact rate
% per /Width of travel (pkdry, pkfan, pkoil) -- pktrowel's deposit-width
% /Load was renamed /Cover in issue #141 to keep it that way -- and a
% paint reservoir draining along the stroke is a different quantity.
% /Charge is what a brush is charged with; /Depletion is how fast it
% runs out.
%
% /Angle follows **pktrowel's** reading, not pknib's: it rotates the
% brush relative to the direction of travel, so it stays constant along
% a curve. pknib's /Angle is a fixed angle in world space, which is what
% makes a pmn stroke swell and narrow as it turns -- correct for a pmn,
% wrong here, and adopting it would make this preset pknib with
% striations bolted on. Footprint thickness goes as cos(Angle), so the
% range is capped at +-80 for the same reason pktrowel's is.
%
% Edges are crisp by default, which is the other half of "flat brush":
% the outermost striations sit exactly at +-Width/2 and the band has
% straight parallel sides and square ends. /Edge roughens them when a
% less mechanical mark is wanted.
%
% The charge is per *subpath*, not per call: walkpath's `t` restarts at
% each subpath, so three parallel strokes in one call are three brush
% loads, each fading independently. That is what an artist blocking in a
% sky actually does, and `broad_each_subpath_gets_its_own_charge` pins it
% rather than leaving it to be discovered.
%
% Determinism: the caller's own `N srand`. Each striation's affinity and
% colour draws are taken per striation, whether or not it ends up
% depositing, so those alone do not shift when /Charge or /Depletion
% change. But the edge jitter is drawn per *deposited point*, so total
% consumption does depend on how much gets laid down -- this preset
% deliberately makes no fixed-draw-count claim of the kind pktrowel
% documents, for the same reason pkfan doesn't. Reproducibility under a
% fixed seed is claimed and tested; stream invariance is not, because it
% would not be true.
%
% Safety: /Grain is capped 1..40 and Grain * stopCount is checked
% against 120000 inside the counting walk, pktrowel's bound and
% placement.
%
% /Jitter is deliberately absent. An earlier version parsed, validated
% and advertised it, and never read it -- a catalogued no-op that
% shipped because its only test asserted it rejected a *procedure*.
% /Edge already applies pkjit to every striation boundary, which is
% exactly what /Jitter claimed to do, so the fix was to delete the
% duplicate rather than add a second knob for one effect.
% `broad_every_documented_parameter_changes_the_render` now walks the
% advertised list and fails on any parameter that does not move a pixel.
%
% Scratch prefix pm-, chosen after scanning *every* file under lib/ for
% any /name usage rather than just this one. pl- looked free in
% paintkit.ps and is used by graph.ps, scifi.ps and steampunk.ps; the
% next candidate, pe-, is used in ten files including pagekit.ps (peW,
% peX, peTitle...). Nothing collided by exact name there, but "no exact
% collision today" is one peWidth-vs-peW away from the pkflat incident,
% so pm- -- which has no /name usage anywhere under lib/ -- is the one
% to keep.

% i u pmpt -> x y   (brush-space to device space at stop i)
% @internal
/pmpt {
    /pmpu exch def
    pmraw exch get
    dup 0 get /pmpx exch def
    dup 1 get /pmpy exch def
    2 get /pmpa exch def
    /pmpb pmpa 90 add pmAngle add def
    pmpx pmpb cos pmpu mul add
    pmpy pmpb sin pmpu mul add
} bind def

% i pmcolor -   (set the loaded color of the current striation for a run
% that starts at stop i; only called with /Colors set)
% The head position is the striation's centre pmnc, before any narrowing.
% Runs never cross a subpath, so the flip of the run's first stop is the
% run's. /ColorJitter is the striation's own draw, taken up front.
% @internal
/pmcolor {
    /pmcli exch def
    pmColorRec pmnc
    pmColorRec 2 get { pmflips pmcli get } { false } ifelse
    puat puwet 3 array astore 1 pmcj add shade setrgbcolor
} bind def

% i na nb back fwd pmstamp -   (the brush's footprint at a single stop)
%
% A degenerate subpath -- a bare `moveto`, or a `moveto closepath` --
% gives walkpath exactly one stop, and a strip needs two stops to have
% any area, so pkbroad drew nothing at all there while pkribbon,
% pktrowel and pkfan all leave a mark (review of PR #140). A flat brush
% set down and lifted stamps its own footprint, so that is what this is:
% the full band width, square ended like the brush itself.
%
% `back` and `fwd` are how far the footprint reaches either side of the
% stop along the direction of travel, and they are separate because the
% two callers want different shapes. A degenerate stop has no travel at
% all, so the brush is simply set down: half a pitch each way. A run
% that is one sample long *does* have a direction -- the paint went on
% from that stop forwards -- so it takes back 0 and reaches forward only
% as far as the stroke actually travels. Passing the degenerate shape
% for both put a short stroke's mark in the wrong place entirely: a 3pt
% stroke at x 50..53 inked x 48..51, starting before the stroke began
% and never reaching its end, and since /Pitch is user-supplied the
% error is unbounded -- /Pitch 200 on a 30pt path inked 200pt of band
% starting 50pt early (review of PR #140).
% @internal
/pmstamp {
    /pmsfwd exch def /pmsback exch def
    /pmsnb exch def /pmsna exch def /pmsi exch def
    pmColorRec null ne { pmsi pmcolor } if
    % The footprint's long axis is the stop's own travel direction
    % turned by /Angle -- the same rotation pmpt applies across the
    % band. walkpath reports angle 0 at a degenerate stop, so there this
    % reduces to /Angle alone; hard-coding the extent along +x instead
    % (as this did) left the footprint a sheared parallelogram at
    % nonzero /Angle rather than the rotated rectangle a flat brush
    % actually presses.
    /pmsang pmraw pmsi get 2 get pmAngle add def
    /pmscos pmsang cos def
    /pmssin pmsang sin def
    pmsi pmsna pmhalf mul pmpt /pmsay exch def /pmsax exch def
    pmsi pmsnb pmhalf mul pmpt /pmsby exch def /pmsbx exch def
    newpath
    pmsax pmsback pmscos mul sub  pmsay pmsback pmssin mul sub moveto
    pmsax pmsfwd  pmscos mul add  pmsay pmsfwd  pmssin mul add lineto
    pmsbx pmsfwd  pmscos mul add  pmsby pmsfwd  pmssin mul add lineto
    pmsbx pmsback pmscos mul sub  pmsby pmsback pmssin mul sub lineto
    closepath fill
} bind def

% i pmgap -> length   (how far the stroke travels on from stop i)
%
% walkpath reports each stop's `sp`, the arc-length from the previous
% stop, so the gap *after* stop i is the next stop's sp -- provided
% there is a next stop and it is not the start of a new subpath. At the
% last stop of a subpath there is nothing ahead, so the brush's own
% half-pitch footprint is the honest fallback.
% @internal
/pmgap {
    dup 1 add pmn lt {
        1 add pmraw exch get
        dup 5 get 1 and 1 eq
            { pop pmPitch 0.5 mul }
            { 4 get }
        ifelse
    } { pop pmPitch 0.5 mul } ifelse
    dup 0 le { pop pmPitch 0.5 mul } if
} bind def

% start end na nb ranout pmemit -   (one striation run, as a filled strip)
%
% `ranout` says whether this run ended because the charge fell below the
% striation's affinity, as opposed to simply reaching the end of the
% subpath. Only the former is tapered: a striation that ran out of paint
% thins away over its last stretch, while one still carrying paint when
% the stroke stops must keep the brush's square end. Without that
% distinction the depletion fade came out as a staircase of hard
% vertical cutoffs -- seen in the first render, not predicted.
% @internal
/pmemit {
    /pmran exch def
    /pmnbv exch def /pmnav exch def /pmend exch def /pmstart exch def
    pmend pmstart gt {
        /pmspan pmend pmstart sub def
        pmColorRec null ne { pmstart pmcolor } if
        newpath
        pmstart 1 pmend {
            /pmei exch def
            pmei pmnav pmtaper pmhalf mul pmEdge pmedge mul pkjit add pmpt
            pmei pmstart eq { moveto } { lineto } ifelse
        } for
        pmend -1 pmstart {
            /pmei exch def
            pmei pmnbv pmtaper pmhalf mul pmEdge pmedge mul pkjit add pmpt lineto
        } for
        closepath fill
    } {
        % A run one sample long has no strip -- a strip needs two stops
        % to have any area -- but it is a real deposit and has to leave a
        % real mark. The common case is a subpath shorter than /Pitch: a
        % fully loaded brush deposits at the start stop and is dry at the
        % end one, so every striation's run collapses to a single sample
        % and `pkbroad` painted nothing at all (Codex review of PR #140).
        % It also catches any striation whose affinity happens to fall
        % between two consecutive stops' charges, which was silently
        % dropping that striation mid-band. The footprint starts *at*
        % the stop and reaches forward only as far as the stroke
        % actually travels -- unlike the degenerate case, a one-sample
        % run knows which way the brush was going.
        pmstart pmnav pmnbv 0 pmstart pmgap pmstamp
    } ifelse
} bind def

% charge affinity pmwets -> bool   (does this striation deposit here?)
%
% Clamped at both ends rather than compared directly, following ptroll
% and pfroll above. `frnd` really can return exactly 1.0 -- seed 51463
% does -- so a plain `charge gt affinity` rejected a striation of
% affinity 1.0 at /Charge 1, and a full-charge single-striation brush
% painted nothing at all (Codex review of PR #140). /Charge 1 is
% documented as fully loaded, so it means certain contact; /Charge 0
% means none, which the strict comparison already gave.
% @internal
/pmwets {
    exch dup 1 ge
        { pop pop true }
        { dup 0 le { pop pop false } { lt } ifelse }
    ifelse
} bind def

% v pmtaper -> tapered   (narrows a boundary toward the striation center)
%
% The taper has to shrink the striation around *its own* center pmnc,
% not scale its absolute offset from the band centerline: multiplying
% both boundaries by pmtap translates the whole striation inward, so an
% outer one sweeps diagonally across its neighbors as it fades instead
% of thinning in place (Codex review of PR #140).
% @internal
/pmtaper {
    pmnc sub pmtap mul pmnc add
} bind def

% -> multiplier   (reads pmei/pmstart/pmspan/pmran; the run-end taper)
% Written as its own proc so pmemit's two point loops can't drift apart.
% @internal
/pmtap {
    pmran pmspan 0 gt and {
        pmei pmstart sub pmspan div
        dup 0.78 gt
            { 0.78 sub 0.22 div 0.85 mul 1 exch sub }
            { pop 1 }
        ifelse
    } { 1 } ifelse
} bind def

%%SelfTest: pkbroad-colors-rejects-malformed-loads
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors 5 >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (a number as /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors { [ 0 0 0 ] } >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (a procedure as /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ ] >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (no colours) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ 9 { [ 0 0 0 ] } repeat ] >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (nine colours) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 ] ] >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays
%   (a 2-element entry) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 1.5 ] ] >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colors-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a component of 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColorMix 1.5 >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colormix-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/ColorMix 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkbroad }
%   (1 colour) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] >> pkbroad }
%   (2 colours) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ 8 { [ 0.2 0.4 1 ] } repeat ] /ColorMix 0 /Light -45 >> pkbroad }
%   (8 colours, the cap) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkbroad-colors-spelling-and-companions
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colours [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/Colours) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColourMix 0.5 >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/ColourMix) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /HighlightColour [ 1 1 1 ] >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/HighlightColour) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /ColorMix 0.5 >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-colormix-needs-colors
%   (/ColorMix without /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Light 90 >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-light-needs-colors
%   (/Light without /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /Light { 90 } >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-light-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (a procedure as /Light) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /Light 90 >> pkbroad }
%   (/Light with /Colors) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest

%%SelfTest: pkbroad-touch-guards
%   % The no-canvas cases first: nothing in this block has set a surface yet.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 0.5 >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch 0.5 before any pkcanvas) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 1 >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch 1 before any pkcanvas: any /Touch needs a canvas) mustguard
%
%   << >> pkcanvas
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 1.5 >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-touch-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Touch 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch { } >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-touch-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Touch { }) mustguard
%
%   % A coarse /Pitch and one striation keep the deposit budget out of the way.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto
%     << /Touch 0.5 /Pitch 1000 /Grain 1 >> pkbroad }
%   /pkbroad-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a 1e5pt stroke at /Touch 0.5) mustguard
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 0.5 >> pkbroad grestore }
%   (/Touch 0.5 with a canvas) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Touch 0.5 /Colors [ [ 1 0 0 ] [ 0 0 1 ] ] /Light 90 >> pkbroad grestore }
%   (a touched stroke with a colour load) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto << /Touch 0 >> pkbroad grestore }
%   (/Touch 0 on a pressed point) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A broad flat brush for block-ins, sky and water bands, reflections and broad masses. What separates it from pktrowel's blade and pknib's pmn is that a brush runs out of paint: the band is divided into striations that drop out one by one as the charge falls along the stroke, so the mark fades from solid to broken along its own direction. With /Colors it lays up to eight loaded colours across the band, blended by /ColorMix and lit from /Light. Square ends, and parallel edges that are crisp at /Edge 0 -- the default /Edge 0.15 roughens them by about a pixel. Deterministic under the caller's own srand; bounded by Grain * stops.
% @example: newpath 40 120 moveto 300 130 lineto << /Width 46 /Charge 0.95 /Depletion 0.6 >> pkbroad
% @param: /Width [points] Band width; must be > 0 (default 40)
% @param: /Angle [degrees] Brush rotation in degrees relative to the direction of travel, -80..80; constant along a curve, unlike pknib's fixed world angle (default 0)
% @param: /Charge [0..1] how much paint the brush is loaded with; striations whose affinity exceeds it never deposit, so a low charge skips and gaps (default 0.95)
% @param: /Depletion [0..1] how fast the charge runs out along each subpath; 0 is an even fully loaded block-in (default 0.5)
% @param: /Grain [count] Striations across the band; must be a whole number, hard safety cap 1..40 (default 14)
% @param: /Edge [0..1] roughness applied to every striation boundary, not only the band's outer sides -- so above about 0.3 it also breaks holes through the band's interior (8% of interior pixels at 0.9); 0 is a crisp flat-brush edge (default 0.15)
% @param: /Pitch [points] walkpath sampling pitch for the shared centerline pass; must be > 0 (default Width*0.3, capped at 4)
% @param: /ColorJitter [0..1] per-striation color variation around the caller's current color (default 0.05)
% @param: /Colors [array of rgb arrays] 1 to 8 loaded colors, listed from the brush's left edge (looking along the travel) to its right, evenly spaced across the band; when set, the current color is not used for the mark, though /ColorJitter still varies it. An entry may be a bare pigment name such as /sapgreen (see PigmentNames), which stands for that pigment; a pigmix spec goes through pigcolors (default unset)
% @param: /ColorMix [0..1] with /Colors, how far neighbouring colors have blended on the brush: 0 is hard bands, 1 a smooth gradient (default 0.3)
% @param: /Light [degrees] with /Colors, the direction the light comes from, counterclockwise from +x in user space: each subpath puts the first color on the side facing the light, whichever way it travels (default unset: the first color stays on the left of the travel)
% @param: /Touch [0..1] How hard the brush presses into the shared canvas set by pkcanvas, which must be called first: below 1 the band lands only on the weave's knuckles that stand proud enough for that pressure to reach, lower is lighter and 0 deposits nothing; 1 is full contact, identical to leaving it unset. Composes with /Charge and /Depletion and takes no rand draws (default unset)
/pkbroad {
    /pmopts exch def
    /pmWidth pmopts /Width 40 pkgetdef def
    /pmWidth load xcheck { pkbroad-width-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pmWidth 0 le { pkbroad-width-must-be-positive } if
    /pmAngle pmopts /Angle 0 pkgetdef def
    /pmAngle load xcheck { pkbroad-angle-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pmAngle -80 lt pmAngle 80 gt or { pkbroad-angle-must-be-minus80-to-80 } if
    /pmCharge pmopts /Charge 0.95 pkgetdef def
    /pmCharge load xcheck { pkbroad-charge-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pmCharge 0 lt pmCharge 1 gt or { pkbroad-charge-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pmDepletion pmopts /Depletion 0.5 pkgetdef def
    /pmDepletion load xcheck { pkbroad-depletion-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pmDepletion 0 lt pmDepletion 1 gt or { pkbroad-depletion-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pmGrain pmopts /Grain 14 pkgetdef def
    /pmGrain load xcheck { pkbroad-grain-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pmGrain 1 lt pmGrain 40 gt or pmGrain truncate pmGrain ne or
        { pkbroad-grain-must-be-1-to-40 } if
    /pmGrain pmGrain cvi def
    /pmEdge pmopts /Edge 0.15 pkgetdef def
    /pmEdge load xcheck { pkbroad-edge-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pmEdge 0 lt pmEdge 1 gt or { pkbroad-edge-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pmPitch pmopts /Pitch pmWidth 0.3 mul dup 4 gt { pop 4 } if pkgetdef def
    /pmPitch load xcheck { pkbroad-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pmPitch 0 le { pkbroad-pitch-must-be-positive } if
    /pmColorJitter pmopts /ColorJitter 0.05 pkgetdef def
    /pmColorJitter load xcheck { pkbroad-colorjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pmColorJitter 0 lt pmColorJitter 1 gt or { pkbroad-colorjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    % /Touch (issue #225, CANVAS_SURFACE.md 2.2): any /Touch, even 1, needs
    % a canvas. Only a value below 1 builds a catch, so unset and 1 take
    % exactly the path they took before /Touch existed.
    /pmTouch pmopts /Touch 1 pkgetdef def
    /pmTouch load xcheck { pkbroad-touch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pmTouch 0 lt pmTouch 1 gt or { pkbroad-touch-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    pmopts /Touch known {
        userdict /pjCTM known not { pkbroad-touch-needs-a-canvas } if
    } if
    /pmTouched pmTouch 1 lt def
    % The load: null when /Colors is unset, so every new branch below is
    % `pmColorRec null ne` and an unset key never reaches it.
    pmopts (pkbroad) puparse /pmColorRec exch def
    pmopts (pkbroad) pulightneedscolors
    /pmcount 0 def
    pmPitch { pop pop pop pop pop pop /pmcount pmcount 1 add def
              pmGrain pmcount mul 120000 gt
                  { pkbroad-deposit-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if } walkpath
    /pmn pmcount def
    /pmraw pmn array def
    /pmci 0 def
    pmPitch { 6 array astore pmraw pmci 3 -1 roll put /pmci pmci 1 add def } walkpath
    % The catch's box (CANVAS_SURFACE.md 3.2): every stop grown by the
    % farthest any emitter puts paint from it, checked against the cell
    % budget before anything is drawn. pmpt puts every boundary point at
    % |u| across the band from its stop, whatever /Angle is, so:
    %   strips   |u| <= Width/2 + Edge * 0.06*Width (the taper only pulls
    %            a boundary toward its striation's centre, and the lane
    %            overlap is clamped to the band);
    %   stamps   a single-stop run or a bare moveto is a rectangle whose
    %            corners are those band points moved along the travel
    %            (turned by /Angle) by back or fwd, each at most /Pitch
    %            (pmgap is the next stop's gap, or Pitch/2).
    % The larger, a stamp's Width/2 + Pitch, is used for every stop, with
    % the strip's edge roughness added since the two are never combined.
    pmTouched pmn 0 gt and {
        /pmreach pmWidth 0.5 mul pmWidth 0.06 mul pmEdge mul add pmPitch add def
        [ pmraw { pop pmreach } pjstopbox ] /pmTouchBox exch def
        pmTouchBox aload pop pjcells pjbudget gt
            { pkbroad-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit } if
    } if
    pmn 0 gt {
        gsave
        pmTouched { pmTouchBox aload pop pmTouch pjcatch } if
        /pmbase [ currentrgbcolor ] def
        /pmhalf pmWidth 0.5 mul def
        /pmedge pmWidth 0.06 mul def
        % Draw every striation's affinity and color *before* any
        % geometry, the way pkwet plans all its passes before running
        % any, and for the same reason. pmemit calls pkjit once per
        % boundary point -- unconditionally, even at /Edge 0, to keep
        % the stream fixed -- so with the two interleaved, the number of
        % points a striation deposits shifts the stream for the next
        % one. How many points it deposits is exactly what /Charge and
        % /Depletion control, so turning either re-rolled the rest of
        % the band into a *different* mark rather than re-shaping the
        % same one: at seed 3, /Grain 2 /Depletion 0, the second
        % striation's affinity moved from 0.316 to 0.517 for no reason
        % but raising /Charge from 0.4 to 0.8 (Codex review of PR #140).
        %
        % What this fixes is which lanes deposit and what color they
        % are. The per-point edge jitter still runs off the shared
        % stream, so a striation's edge noise can shift with how much
        % was laid down before it -- the same variable consumption the
        % header already declines to make a fixed-draw-count claim
        % about, and re-shaping rather than re-rolling.
        % One flip per stop, so a whole subpath mirrors together. No draws.
        pmColorRec null ne {
            pmColorRec 2 get {
                /pmflips pmraw pmn pmPitch pmAngle pmColorRec 3 get puflips def
            } if
        } if
        pmGrain array /pmaffs exch def
        pmGrain array /pmcjs exch def
        0 1 pmGrain 1 sub {
            /pmg exch def
            pmaffs pmg frnd put
            pmcjs pmg pmColorJitter pkjit put
        } for
        1 1 pmGrain {
            /pmg exch def
            /pmaff pmaffs pmg 1 sub get def
            /pmcj pmcjs pmg 1 sub get def
            pmbase 1 pmcj add shade setrgbcolor
            % The striations abut, so they need the same overlap
            % pktrowel's lanes do, or every boundary leaves an
            % anti-aliased hairline (see that preset's own note).
            /pmna pmg 1 sub pmGrain div 2 mul 1 sub def
            /pmnb pmg pmGrain div 2 mul 1 sub def
            /pmnc pmna pmnb add 0.5 mul def
            % 15% of a lane, but never less than 0.2pt of actual
            % overlap. The fraction alone is sub-pixel once the lanes
            % get thin -- at /Width 60 /Grain 40 it is 0.11pt, and a
            % nominally solid band read 75% coverage on alternating
            % rows, which is exactly the hairline this exists to
            % prevent (review of PR #140). The floor sits just below
            % the default's own overlap (0.21pt at /Width 40
            % /Grain 14), so it only ever raises a lane too thin to
            % tile cleanly and leaves every coarser band exactly as it
            % was.
            %
            % It bounds the hairline rather than removing it outright at
            % the extreme: /Width 60 /Grain 40 measures max luma 64 of
            % 255 inside the band before this, 16 after. Closing it
            % completely needs about 0.45pt, which is more overlap than
            % the *default* lane has, so every ordinary band would have
            % been re-tuned to fix the thinnest one -- and since
            % adjacent striations carry different /ColorJitter colours,
            % widening the overlap changes how the whole preset reads.
            % Not worth it for 6% lightness on one row at maximum
            % /Grain.
            /pmlane pmhalf pmGrain div def
            /pmover 0.15 def
            pmlane 0 gt {
                0.2 pmlane div dup pmover gt { /pmover exch def } { pop } ifelse
            } if
            pmover 1 gt { /pmover 1 def } if
            /pmna pmnc pmna pmnc sub 1 pmover add mul add def
            /pmnb pmnc pmnb pmnc sub 1 pmover add mul add def
            pmna -1 lt { /pmna -1 def } if
            pmnb 1 gt { /pmnb 1 def } if
            /pmrun false def
            /pmstart 0 def
            0 1 pmn 1 sub {
                /pmi exch def
                /pmrec pmraw pmi get def
                /pmae pmrec 5 get def
                pmae 1 and 1 eq {
                    pmrun { pmstart pmi 1 sub pmna pmnb false pmemit /pmrun false def } if
                } if
                /pmch pmCharge 1 pmDepletion pmrec 3 get mul sub mul def
                pmch pmaff pmwets {
                    pmrun not { /pmstart pmi def /pmrun true def } if
                } {
                    pmrun { pmstart pmi 1 sub pmna pmnb true pmemit /pmrun false def } if
                } ifelse
                pmae 3 and 3 eq {
                    % A degenerate subpath: press the footprint rather
                    % than trying to draw a zero-area strip through one
                    % stop. It honors the charge like any other stop.
                    pmch pmaff pmwets
                        { pmi pmna pmnb pmPitch 0.5 mul dup pmstamp } if
                    /pmrun false def
                } {
                    pmae 2 and 2 eq {
                        pmrun { pmstart pmi pmna pmnb false pmemit /pmrun false def } if
                    } if
                } ifelse
            } for
            pmrun { pmstart pmn 1 sub pmna pmnb false pmemit /pmrun false def } if
        } for
        pmbase aload pop setrgbcolor
        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% --- liner / detail brush (issue #116) --------------------------------
% opts pkliner -
%
% The fine-detail tool: bare branches, trunks, grass stems, rigging,
% shoreline accents, whiskers -- the marks made last, over everything
% else, with the tip of a long soft brush.
%
% The issue asks for the complement of `pknib`, and that is the whole
% design brief: `pknib` is a *rigid* nib whose width is a function of the
% angle between the path and a fixed world angle, while a liner (a
% rigger) is a long, soft, heavily loaded brush drawn with its tip. What
% falls out of that difference:
%
%   flexible   a loaded soft tip does not track the intended line. It
%              wanders off it, and more so the finer and faster the
%              stroke gets.
%   loaded     it carries far more paint than its width suggests, and
%              when that runs out the line does not stop cleanly -- it
%              breaks into shorter and shorter dashes.
%   tip-drawn  width comes from how much of the tip is in contact, so
%              the natural mark starts full and whips to a point.
%
% The first of those is a capability nothing else in this file has.
% Every preset here varies width or coverage *around* the path the caller
% drew -- `pkribbon`'s /Jitter perturbs the ribbon's own edges, `pkdry`'s
% bristles ride at fixed parallel offsets -- but none of them leaves it.
% For a wide mark that hardly matters; for a 2pt tapered line it is the
% whole difference between a painted branch and a ruled one, because at
% that width the centerline *is* the mark. So `pkliner` displaces the
% centerline itself, and `liner_waver_leaves_the_ruled_centerline` pins
% that it does.
%
% Which is also why this is not `pkribbon` with a taper preset, and not
% built on `pkribbon` at all. `pkribbon`'s /Pressure is a function of the
% subpath's own `t`, so the tapered mark it makes is exact, mechanical
% and centred on the drawn path; and the dry break-up here has to run
% *across* the gaps it makes, which one `pkribbon` call per dash cannot
% do -- each call would restart the taper at every gap, so a branch would
% read as a row of identical commas rather than one thinning line.
%
% Two fades, deliberately independent:
%
%   /Taper       geometry. The artist lifting the brush. Width only.
%   /Charge      paint. The reservoir. Coverage only, never width.
%   /Depletion
%
% A drier brush does lay a slightly thinner line, so coupling them would
% be defensible physically -- but it would make the two inseparable both
% in the tests and in the artist's hands, and "thinner" and "broken" are
% the two things this tool is steered by. /Taper 1 whips to a true point;
% /Taper 0 is a constant-width line. /Depletion 0 never runs out.
%
% /Charge and /Depletion mean exactly what they mean in `pkbroad`, and
% for the reason that preset's header gives: /Load in this file is a
% contact rate (pkdry, pkfan, pkoil), and one paint-reservoir vocabulary
% across the file beats a second meaning for it.
% What differs is what running out *looks like* on a mark one bristle
% wide: `pkbroad` drops striations out across its band, while here there
% is no band to thin, so the line breaks along its own length instead.
%
% The break-up is a seeded "tooth" -- how well the surface catches a
% passing tip -- sampled from a fixed pool of 64 knots laid down at
% *absolute* spacing (a multiple of /Width) and interpolated smoothly, so
% the line deposits wherever charge(t) still exceeds the tooth beneath
% it. Smooth, so the mark breaks into runs and gaps that lengthen as the
% charge falls, rather than into per-stop speckle. Absolute rather than
% per-stroke spacing because break-up scale is a property of the tip on the
% paper, not of how long the stroke happens to be: knots spread
% over each subpath would give a 300pt trunk 19pt teeth and a 20pt twig
% 1.2pt teeth -- the whole drawing self-similar, and the twigs' gaps
% sub-pixel. The tooth is the tip's, drawn fresh for every subpath. Break-up
% that two strokes share, the canvas itself, is /Touch (see pkcanvas).
%
% Determinism: the caller's own `N srand`. Because the pool is fixed and
% emission consumes no randomness at all, the draw count per subpath is
% exactly constant -- 3 waver phases + 1 tint + 64 knots -- so turning
% /Charge, /Depletion, /Taper or /Waver re-*shapes* the same mark instead
% of re-rolling it into a different one. None of the other seeded presets
% here can make that claim (`pkbroad` and `pkfan` both explicitly
% decline it), so it is worth having, and `liner_turning_the_knobs_does_
% not_reroll_the_stroke` pins it. It is also fragile in one specific
% direction: it holds *only* while nothing in emission draws. A future
% /Edge-style per-point knob would silently make it false, which is
% exactly how `pkbroad`'s /Charge came to re-roll its striations.
%
% Two properties of these knobs worth stating rather than leaving to be
% discovered, both found by measuring rather than by reasoning:
%
%   /Charge responds fastest in the lower half of its range. The tooth
%   pool is squared, so its median sits near 0.25 and a half-charged
%   brush still covers about three quarters of the stroke -- which is
%   the right behaviour (a half-loaded brush does still mostly touch),
%   but it means the skipping, gappy marks live below about 0.3.
%
%   /ColorJitter cannot show a negative tint on pure black paint. It
%   shades through artkit's `shade`, which multiplies toward black below
%   1 and interpolates toward white above it, so black paint moves only
%   when the draw comes out positive. Shared with pkbroad and pkdry,
%   which jitter the same way.
%
% /Waver is in multiples of /Width, and its cap (0..10) is far past where
% the mark still reads as the caller's path -- deliberately, the way
% `pkribbon` documents /Jitter past Width/2. Past about 2 the line is
% somewhere in the neighbourhood of the path rather than on it; the
% defaults sit near 0.8, which at /Width 2.4 is a couple of points of
% wander over a whole stroke. The amplitude grows toward the end of the
% stroke, since it is the thin whipping tip that wanders, not the loaded
% base -- rendered flat first, and a constant-amplitude waver reads as a
% wobbly *rope*, not a brush.
%
% The ribbon's normals are finite-differenced from the *displaced*
% centerline, not taken from each stop's own reported angle: once the
% waver has turned the line, the source path's tangent is the wrong one,
% and using it pinches the band on the outside of every wander.
%
% A degenerate single-point subpath presses the tip: a filled dot of
% diameter Width. That is the detail end of the tool -- specks, stamens,
% distant birds, sparkle on water -- one dab per `moveto`. Unlike
% `pkbroad`'s pressed footprint it does *not* consult the tooth, only
% whether there is any paint at all (/Charge 0): a pressed tip makes
% contact by pressure, not by the surface catching a moving tip, and a
% detail dab that randomly fails to land is a bad primitive.
%
% Safety: the stop count is checked inside the counting walk against
% 120000, `pkbroad`'s bound and placement. Total work is O(stops) -- one
% filled polygon per wet run, and runs are bounded by stops.
%
% Scratch prefix pq-, chosen the way `pkbroad`'s pm- was: scanned every
% .ps file under lib/, examples/, gallery/, site/ and tests/ for any
% /name usage, not just this file. pq- has none anywhere.

% s pqaff -> affinity   (the surface's tooth at arc length s)
%
% Value noise: the knot pool read at absolute spacing and smoothstepped
% between knots, wrapping by index. The wrap makes the tooth repeat every
% pqKnots*pqKnotSpan (about 900pt at the default width, longer than a
% page), which is far enough that no stroke sees it twice.
% @internal
/pqaff {
    /pqas exch def
    /pqau pqas pqKnotSpan div def
    /pqak pqau truncate cvi def
    /pqaf pqau pqak sub def
    /pqa0 pqknots pqak pqKnots mod get def
    /pqa1 pqknots pqak 1 add pqKnots mod get def
    % f*f*(3-2f): a linear ramp between knots leaves a visible kink at
    % every knot where the line's coverage changes slope, which reads as
    % regular beading along a long stroke.
    /pqaf pqaf dup mul 3 pqaf 2 mul sub mul def
    pqa0 pqa1 pqa0 sub pqaf mul add
} bind def

% charge affinity pqwets -> bool   (is the tip depositing here?)
%
% Clamped at both ends rather than compared directly, exactly as
% pkbroad's pmwets is and for the same measured reason: `frnd` really can
% return 1.0, so a plain `charge gt affinity` makes a fully loaded liner
% skip wherever the tooth happens to peak. /Charge 1 is documented as
% fully loaded, so it means certain contact.
% @internal
/pqwets {
    exch dup 1 ge
        { pop pop true }
        { dup 0 le { pop pop false } { lt } ifelse }
    ifelse
} bind def

% t pqhw -> half width   (the artist's lift, before any run-end taper)
% @internal
/pqhw {
    /pqht exch def
    pqWidth 0.5 mul 1 pqTaper pqht 1.25 exp mul sub mul
    dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if
} bind def

% s t pqoff -> lateral offset   (how far the tip has wandered)
%
% Three sines rather than one: a single wavelength reads as a sine wave,
% which is a mechanical shape of its own, and two still read as a regular
% beat over a long stroke. The long one is the arm's drift, the short one
% the tip's tremor. The amplitude term grows toward the tip -- see the
% header.
% @internal
/pqoff {
    /pqot exch def /pqos exch def
    pqWaver 0 eq
        { 0 }
        {
            /pqoamp pqWaver pqWidth mul 0.35 0.65 pqot mul add mul def
            0.58 pqos 360 mul pqLam1 div pqPh1 add sin mul
            0.30 pqos 360 mul pqLam2 div pqPh2 add sin mul
            0.12 pqos 360 mul pqLam3 div pqPh3 add sin mul
            add add pqoamp mul
        }
    ifelse
} bind def

% i pqnrm -> nx ny   (unit normal of the displaced centerline at stop i)
%
% Finite-differenced from the neighbouring *displaced* stops, clamped to
% the current subpath (pqlo/pqhi) so a subpath boundary can't difference
% across a `moveto` jump. Where the neighbourhood is degenerate -- two
% coincident stops -- the source stop's own reported angle is the only
% direction information there is, so it stands in.
% @internal
/pqnrm {
    /pqni exch def
    /pqna pqni 1 sub def pqna pqlo lt { /pqna pqlo def } if
    /pqnb pqni 1 add def pqnb pqhi gt { /pqnb pqhi def } if
    /pqndx pqcx pqnb get pqcx pqna get sub def
    /pqndy pqcy pqnb get pqcy pqna get sub def
    /pqnl pqndx dup mul pqndy dup mul add sqrt def
    pqnl 0 gt
        { pqndy neg pqnl div  pqndx pqnl div }
        { pqraw pqni get 2 get 90 add dup cos exch sin }
    ifelse
} bind def

% i pqrunw -> half width   (the lift, plus this run's own lift-on/off)
%
% A run that begins or ends mid-stroke is the tip lifting on or off the
% surface, so it thins over its own first/last third; a run that simply
% starts where the subpath starts, or ends where it ends, keeps the
% stroke's own width there. Without that, a broken tail came out as a row
% of blunt dashes with square ends -- seen in the first render.
% @internal
/pqrunw {
    /pqri exch def
    pqraw pqri get 3 get pqhw
    pqrspan 0 gt {
        /pqrf pqri pqrstart sub pqrspan div def
        % ...but a *brief* touch has no room to thin, so how deeply the
        % lift is applied scales with how long the run actually is. The
        % ramps are fractions of the run, so without this a short run
        % takes the full lift at both of its ends at once and renders at
        % a quarter of its width and under. Measured across 30 seeds and
        % three widths at coarse pitches: the median mark loses 7% of its
        % ink and the worst a third of it. Nothing vanishes either way --
        % a run of three or more stops still reaches full width in its
        % middle, so the dash *count* is unchanged; what was wrong is how
        % much paint a brief touch leaves. Six stops is where the
        % fractional ramps have room to read as a taper at all.
        /pqrdep pqrspan 6 div dup 1 gt { pop 1 } if def
        pqrton {
            pqrf 0.3 lt
                { 0.25 0.75 pqrf 0.3 div mul add
                  1 sub pqrdep mul 1 add mul } if
        } if
        pqrtoff {
            pqrf 0.7 gt
                { 1 pqrf 0.7 sub 0.3 div 0.85 mul sub
                  1 sub pqrdep mul 1 add mul } if
        } if
    } if
} bind def

% i side pqside -> x y   (a boundary point of the ribbon at stop i)
%
% pqsdi, not pqsi: the caller's own run loop uses pqsi for the stop it is
% examining, and this proc runs inside that loop. Nothing reads pqsi
% after the emit today, so the clash would be invisible until someone
% moved one line -- which is how this file's prefix collisions have gone
% before (see pkflat in pkbroad's notes).
% @internal
/pqside {
    /pqsdd exch def /pqsdi exch def
    pqsdi pqnrm /pqsny exch def /pqsnx exch def
    /pqsw pqsdi pqrunw pqsdd mul def
    pqcx pqsdi get pqsnx pqsw mul add
    pqcy pqsdi get pqsny pqsw mul add
} bind def

% start end pqemit -   (one wet run, as a filled ribbon)
% @internal
/pqemit {
    /pqrend exch def /pqrstart exch def
    /pqrspan pqrend pqrstart sub def
    % Geometric span, not index span. walkpath emits a guaranteed final
    % stop for every subpath, so a subpath whose length is an exact
    % multiple of /Pitch ends with *two* stops at the same coordinates.
    % A run consisting only of those has two distinct indexes and no
    % extent at all, and the ribbon branch filled a zero-area polygon:
    % the deposit vanished silently. Seed 21 on a 300pt line at /Pitch
    % 100 painted nothing whatsoever (Codex review of PR #143, round 2).
    pqrspan 0 gt
    pqarc pqrend get pqarc pqrstart get sub 0 gt
    and {
        newpath
        pqrstart 1 pqrend {
            /pqei exch def
            pqei 1 pqside
            pqei pqrstart eq { moveto } { lineto } ifelse
        } for
        pqrend -1 pqrstart {
            /pqei exch def
            pqei -1 pqside lineto
        } for
        closepath fill
    } {
        % A run with no extent has no ribbon -- a ribbon needs two
        % separated stops to have any area -- but it is a real deposit
        % and has to leave a real mark. The common case is a whole
        % subpath shorter than /Pitch, which is most of a twig or a grass
        % stem. pkbroad drew nothing at all in exactly this case until
        % PR #140's review.
        %
        % What the mark occupies is the travel either side of the stop,
        % taken from the *displaced* centerline: the next stop that is
        % actually somewhere else, or failing that the previous one.
        % Three separate defects come from getting this wrong, all
        % measured rather than reasoned about:
        %
        %   - Extending by half a pitch at a subpath's last stop put the
        %     whole mark outside the path -- x=370..419 on a line ending
        %     at 370 at /Pitch 100 (round 1).
        %   - Extending along the stop's own *reported* angle, which at a
        %     corner is the direction the brush arrived from, by the
        %     distance to the next stop, which lies along the direction it
        %     leaves in, shot 100pt past the corner of a 40,40 -> 140,40
        %     -> 140,140 path (round 2).
        %   - Taking the direction from pqraw at all ignores the waver,
        %     so an isolated dash sat across the displaced line rather
        %     than along it.
        %
        % Skipping stops that are in the same place, rather than taking
        % the immediate neighbour, is what makes the duplicate-endpoint
        % case above resolve to real geometry instead of a zero-length
        % direction.
        /pqmok false def
        /pqmj pqrstart 1 add def
        {
            pqmj pqhi gt { exit } if
            pqarc pqmj get pqarc pqrstart get gt { /pqmok true def exit } if
            /pqmj pqmj 1 add def
        } loop
        pqmok {
            /pqmxa pqcx pqrstart get def /pqmya pqcy pqrstart get def
            /pqmxb pqcx pqmj get def    /pqmyb pqcy pqmj get def
        } {
            /pqmk pqrstart 1 sub def
            {
                pqmk pqlo lt { exit } if
                pqarc pqmk get pqarc pqrstart get lt
                    { /pqmok true def exit } if
                /pqmk pqmk 1 sub def
            } loop
            pqmok {
                /pqmxa pqcx pqmk get def /pqmya pqcy pqmk get def
                /pqmxb pqcx pqrstart get def /pqmyb pqcy pqrstart get def
            } if
        } ifelse
        /pqmw pqrstart pqrunw def
        pqmok {
            /pqmdx pqmxb pqmxa sub def
            /pqmdy pqmyb pqmya sub def
            /pqmlen pqmdx dup mul pqmdy dup mul add sqrt def
        } { /pqmlen 0 def } ifelse
        pqmlen 0 gt {
            /pqmnx pqmdy neg pqmlen div def
            /pqmny pqmdx pqmlen div def
            newpath
            pqmxa pqmnx pqmw mul add pqmya pqmny pqmw mul add moveto
            pqmxb pqmnx pqmw mul add pqmyb pqmny pqmw mul add lineto
            pqmxb pqmnx pqmw mul sub pqmyb pqmny pqmw mul sub lineto
            pqmxa pqmnx pqmw mul sub pqmya pqmny pqmw mul sub lineto
            closepath fill
        } {
            % A subpath that goes nowhere at all (every stop in one
            % place) has no direction to occupy, so the brush is simply
            % set down -- bounded by the tip's own width, never by
            % /Pitch, which is where every unbounded case above came
            % from.
            pqmw 0 gt {
                newpath
                pqcx pqrstart get pqcy pqrstart get pqmw 0 360 arc fill
            } if
        } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def

% start end lifton liftoff pqrun -   (emit one run with its end shapes)
% @internal
/pqrun {
    /pqrtoff exch def /pqrton exch def
    pqemit
} bind def

%%SelfTest: pkliner-touch-guards
%   % The no-canvas cases first: nothing in this block has set a surface yet.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 0.5 >> pkliner }
%   /pkliner-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch 0.5 before any pkcanvas) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 1 >> pkliner }
%   /pkliner-touch-needs-a-canvas
%   (/Touch 1 before any pkcanvas: any /Touch needs a canvas) mustguard
%
%   << >> pkcanvas
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 1.5 >> pkliner }
%   /pkliner-touch-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Touch 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch { } >> pkliner }
%   /pkliner-touch-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Touch { }) mustguard
%
%   % A coarse /Pitch keeps the deposit budget out of the way.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto
%     << /Touch 0.5 /Pitch 1000 >> pkliner }
%   /pkliner-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a 1e5pt stroke at /Touch 0.5) mustguard
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Touch 0.5 >> pkliner grestore }
%   (/Touch 0.5 with a canvas) mustpass
%
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto << /Touch 0 >> pkliner grestore }
%   (/Touch 0 on a pressed point) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A liner/detail brush: the long soft tip that draws branches, trunks, grass stems and other fine marks. The complement of pknib's rigid nib -- a flexible loaded brush, so the line wanders off the drawn centerline (no other preset here leaves it), whips from full width to a point, and breaks into shortening dashes as the paint runs out. A single-point subpath presses a detail dot. Deterministic under the caller's own srand, with a fixed number of draws per subpath.
% @example: newpath 60 40 moveto 92 150 118 214 150 300 curveto << /Width 3 /Taper 0.9 /Waver 1.2 >> pkliner
% @param: /Width [points] Width at the loaded end of the stroke, and the diameter of a pressed dot; must be > 0 (default 2.4)
% @param: /Taper [0..1] how far the line narrows toward the end of each subpath -- the artist's lift, width only; 1 whips to a true point, 0 is constant width (default 0.85)
% @param: /Charge [0..1] how much paint the brush is loaded with -- coverage only, never width; pkbroad's meaning (default 0.95)
% @param: /Depletion [0..1] how fast the charge runs out along each subpath; 0 never runs out (default 0.35)
% @param: /Waver [multiple of /Width] Lateral wander of the loaded tip, in multiples of Width, 0..10; grows toward the tip; 0 is a mechanically straight line, and past about 2 the mark reads as near the caller's path rather than on it (default 0.8)
% @param: /Pitch [points] walkpath sampling pitch for the centerline pass; must be > 0 (default Width*0.8, capped at 3 and floored at 0.5)
% @param: /ColorJitter [0..1] one small tint shift per subpath -- one dip of the brush is one tint (default 0.05)
% @param: /Touch [0..1] How hard the tip presses into the shared canvas set by pkcanvas, which must be called first: below 1 the line lands only on the weave's knuckles that stand proud enough for that pressure to reach, lower is lighter and 0 deposits nothing; 1 is full contact, identical to leaving it unset. Composes with /Charge and /Depletion and takes no rand draws (default unset)
/pkliner {
    /pqopts exch def
    /pqWidth pqopts /Width 2.4 pkgetdef def
    /pqWidth load xcheck { pkliner-width-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqWidth 0 le { pkliner-width-must-be-positive } if
    /pqTaper pqopts /Taper 0.85 pkgetdef def
    /pqTaper load xcheck { pkliner-taper-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqTaper 0 lt pqTaper 1 gt or { pkliner-taper-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pqCharge pqopts /Charge 0.95 pkgetdef def
    /pqCharge load xcheck { pkliner-charge-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqCharge 0 lt pqCharge 1 gt or { pkliner-charge-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pqDepletion pqopts /Depletion 0.35 pkgetdef def
    /pqDepletion load xcheck { pkliner-depletion-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqDepletion 0 lt pqDepletion 1 gt or
        { pkliner-depletion-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pqWaver pqopts /Waver 0.8 pkgetdef def
    /pqWaver load xcheck { pkliner-waver-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqWaver 0 lt pqWaver 10 gt or { pkliner-waver-must-be-0-to-10 } if
    % Floored as well as capped, unlike the siblings' pitch defaults: a
    % liner is a *thin* brush, so Width*0.8 alone would sample a hair
    % stroke at a fifth of a point and multiply a long path's stop count
    % up toward the safety budget for a finer centerline than anything
    % 2pt wide can show.
    /pqPitch
        pqopts /Pitch
        pqWidth 0.8 mul dup 3 gt { pop 3 } if dup 0.5 lt { pop 0.5 } if
        pkgetdef
    def
    /pqPitch load xcheck { pkliner-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqPitch 0 le { pkliner-pitch-must-be-positive } if
    /pqColorJitter pqopts /ColorJitter 0.05 pkgetdef def
    /pqColorJitter load xcheck
        { pkliner-colorjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqColorJitter 0 lt pqColorJitter 1 gt or
        { pkliner-colorjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    % /Touch (issue #225, CANVAS_SURFACE.md 2.2): any /Touch, even 1, needs
    % a canvas. Only a value below 1 builds a catch, so unset and 1 take
    % exactly the path they took before /Touch existed.
    /pqTouch pqopts /Touch 1 pkgetdef def
    /pqTouch load xcheck { pkliner-touch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqTouch 0 lt pqTouch 1 gt or { pkliner-touch-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    pqopts /Touch known {
        userdict /pjCTM known not { pkliner-touch-needs-a-canvas } if
    } if
    /pqTouched pqTouch 1 lt def

    % Two walkpath passes over the centerline -- count (checking the
    % budget on every stop, before any drawing starts), then collect --
    % the same shape pkdry, pktrowel and pkbroad use.
    /pqcount 0 def
    pqPitch {
        pop pop pop pop pop pop
        /pqcount pqcount 1 add def
        pqcount 120000 gt
            { pkliner-deposit-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
    } walkpath
    /pqn pqcount def
    /pqraw pqn array def
    /pqci 0 def
    pqPitch {
        6 array astore pqraw pqci 3 -1 roll put
        /pqci pqci 1 add def
    } walkpath

    % The catch's box (CANVAS_SURFACE.md 3.2): every stop grown by the
    % farthest any emitter puts paint from it, checked against the cell
    % budget before anything is drawn. The displaced centerline does not
    % exist yet, so the bound is a constant:
    %   ribbon   the tip wanders at most Waver*Width from its stop (the
    %            three waver sines' weights sum to 1 and the amplitude
    %            grows from 0.35 to 1 of Waver*Width), and the ribbon's
    %            half-width is at most Width/2 (taper only narrows it);
    %   dots     a pressed dot (a degenerate stop) is not displaced and is
    %            Width/2 in radius, so it takes the smaller reach;
    %   stamps   the no-extent fallback stays within Width/2 of the
    %            displaced centerline, so inside the ribbon's reach.
    pqTouched pqn 0 gt and {
        /pqreach pqWidth 0.5 mul pqWaver pqWidth mul add def
        [ pqraw { 5 get 3 and 3 eq { pqWidth 0.5 mul } { pqreach } ifelse }
          pjstopbox ] /pqTouchBox exch def
        pqTouchBox aload pop pjcells pjbudget gt
            { pkliner-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit } if
    } if

    pqn 0 gt {
        gsave
        pqTouched { pqTouchBox aload pop pqTouch pjcatch } if
        /pqbase [ currentrgbcolor ] def
        /pqcx pqn array def
        /pqcy pqn array def
        /pqarc pqn array def
        /pqKnots 64 def
        % Floored: the tooth is a property of the tip on the paper, and below half a
        % point there is nothing left for a tip to catch on. It also
        % keeps the knot coordinate pqaff hands to `cvi` bounded by twice
        % the path length (itself bounded by the deposit budget) rather
        % than by 1/Width, which /Width 1e-9 sends past the integer range
        % `cvi` is defined over.
        /pqKnotSpan pqWidth 5 mul dup 0.5 lt { pop 0.5 } if def
        /pqknots pqKnots array def
        /pqLam1 pqWidth 26 mul def
        /pqLam2 pqWidth 9 mul def
        /pqLam3 pqWidth 3.5 mul def
        /pqi 0 def
        {
            pqi pqn ge { exit } if
            % This subpath runs pqlo..pqhi. walkpath flags the last stop
            % of every subpath (bit 2), and a degenerate one carries both
            % bits, so the scan terminates on either.
            /pqlo pqi def
            /pqhi pqlo def
            {
                pqraw pqhi get 5 get 2 and 2 eq { exit } if
                pqhi pqn 1 sub ge { exit } if
                /pqhi pqhi 1 add def
            } loop

            % The whole plan for this subpath is drawn before any of its
            % geometry is computed, and nothing below draws again. That
            % is what makes the per-subpath draw count fixed -- see the
            % header -- so /Charge and /Depletion re-shape this mark
            % rather than re-rolling the next one.
            /pqPh1 frnd 360 mul def
            /pqPh2 frnd 360 mul def
            /pqPh3 frnd 360 mul def
            /pqtint pqColorJitter pkjit def
            % Squared, not uniform: the tooth is what the surface does to
            % a passing tip, and a surface is mostly smooth with
            % occasional high spots. Rendered flat first -- a uniform
            % pool put gaps along the whole length of a *fully charged*
            % line, since a twelfth of it sat above /Charge 0.95 from the
            % outset, which is the mark of a nearly empty brush, not a
            % full one. Squaring leaves the loaded end near-continuous
            % and still hands the depleting end plenty to catch on.
            0 1 pqKnots 1 sub { pqknots exch frnd dup mul put } for

            % Arc length within the subpath, then the displaced
            % centerline. Both are needed before any normal can be taken,
            % since the normals are differenced from the displaced points
            % rather than from the source path's own angles.
            pqarc pqlo 0 put
            pqlo 1 add 1 pqhi {
                /pqai exch def
                pqarc pqai pqarc pqai 1 sub get pqraw pqai get 4 get add put
            } for
            pqlo 1 pqhi {
                /pqpi exch def
                /pqprec pqraw pqpi get def
                /pqpn pqprec 2 get 90 add def
                % A degenerate stop is a *pressed* tip, and a press has no
                % direction of travel to wander along -- the waver models
                % a moving tip. Displacing it anyway moved the dab off the
                % `moveto` the caller asked for, by up to Waver*Width:
                % /Width 10 /Waver 10 put a dot asked for at y=80 at
                % y=97 (Codex review of PR #143), against this preset's
                % own documented promise. Costs no randomness either way,
                % since pqoff draws nothing.
                pqprec 5 get 3 and 3 eq
                    { 0 }
                    { pqarc pqpi get pqprec 3 get pqoff }
                ifelse /pqpo exch def
                pqcx pqpi pqprec 0 get pqpn cos pqpo mul add put
                pqcy pqpi pqprec 1 get pqpn sin pqpo mul add put
            } for

            pqbase 1 pqtint add shade setrgbcolor

            pqraw pqlo get 5 get 3 and 3 eq {
                % A pressed tip: the dot lands on any charge at all --
                % contact is by pressure here, not by the surface
                % catching a moving tip (see the header).
                pqCharge 0 gt {
                    newpath
                    pqcx pqlo get pqcy pqlo get
                    pqraw pqlo get 3 get pqhw
                    0 360 arc fill
                } if
            } {
                /pqwet false def
                /pqstart pqlo def
                pqlo 1 pqhi {
                    /pqsi exch def
                    /pqch
                        pqCharge
                        1 pqDepletion pqraw pqsi get 3 get mul sub mul
                    def
                    pqch pqarc pqsi get pqaff pqwets {
                        pqwet not {
                            /pqstart pqsi def /pqwet true def
                        } if
                    } {
                        pqwet {
                            % Ended by running dry: the tip lifts off, so
                            % this run's tail thins. It lifted *on* only
                            % if it didn't start where the stroke did.
                            pqstart pqsi 1 sub
                            pqstart pqlo ne true pqrun
                            /pqwet false def
                        } if
                    } ifelse
                } for
                pqwet {
                    % Still carrying paint when the stroke ended: the
                    % stroke's own end, so no lift-off taper on top of
                    % the /Taper the whole line already has.
                    pqstart pqhi pqstart pqlo ne false pqrun
                    /pqwet false def
                } if
            } ifelse

            /pqi pqhi 1 add def
        } loop
        pqbase aload pop setrgbcolor
        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% --- wet interaction: soft layered paint (issue #113) -----------------
% proc opts pkwet -
%
% Softens *any* mark so it settles into the layer beneath instead of
% sitting on top of it — the wet-on-wet vocabulary blended skies,
% clouds, mist and distant forms are made of.
%
% Unlike every other entry point in this file, `pkwet` does not draw a
% mark of its own: it takes the caller's own mark-drawing procedure and
% re-runs it. That is deliberate. The alternative — a /Wet key bolted
% onto each of pkribbon/pknib/pkdry/pkspray/pkoil/pktrowel — is six
% copies of one idea with six chances to drift apart, and it would still
% miss whatever preset is written next. As a wrapper this works with all
% six today and with anything added later, for one implementation:
%
%   { newpath 40 400 moveto 200 430 380 390 560 420 curveto
%     << /Width 60 >> pkribbon }
%   << /Soft 0.7 /Under [0.62 0.72 0.86] >> pkwet
%
% Why there is no alpha here
% --------------------------
% The obvious implementation is a translucent pass: set an alpha, draw,
% let what's underneath show through. It was built and rendered, and it
% is wrong for this library, for two measured reasons.
%
% Alpha applies to *every fill the wrapped proc makes*, and half these
% presets are built from many deliberately overlapping fills — pkoil
% lays a base ribbon plus a dozen ridges, and pktrowel's lanes overlap
% by 18% precisely so their shared edges don't leave anti-aliased seams.
% Under a single translucent pass each of those internal overlaps
% composites against itself, so pkoil's ridges blotch against their own
% base and pktrowel's seam-closing overlap comes back as a dark stripe
% at every lane boundary — reintroducing, from the other direction, the
% exact striped-rectangle artifact issue #111 removed. Stacking several
% offset translucent passes compounds it into visible ghosting.
%
% The second reason is portability: Ghostscript has no PostScript-
% callable alpha operator at all (see pkwash's header), so an
% alpha-based pkwet would need pkwash's degraded flattening fallback,
% where overlaps stop mixing. Grading opaque passes needs no alpha at
% all, so pkwet has no reduced-fidelity path to fall back to and no
% `pkalphaok` branch: it does the same thing under Ghostscript that it
% does here. (Its seeded displacement angles come from `rand`, so the
% precise scatter still lands differently under gs's own generator --
% exactly as it does for every other seeded preset in this file, and
% for the wrapped preset's own texture. "No fallback" is the claim, not
% "pixel-identical".)
%
% What it does instead
% --------------------
% The proc is run /Layers times, outermost pass first, each one
% displaced a little further from the mark's true position and mixed a
% little further toward /Under — the colour the artist declares is
% already on the canvas there. The outermost pass is nearly /Under
% itself, so it dissolves invisibly into the backdrop; the last pass
% sits exactly on the path in the caller's own colour. The result is a
% mark whose core is solid and whose edge grades away, which is what
% wet paint worked into a wet ground actually looks like.
%
% Pickup is *declared*, not sampled. Really picking colour up off the
% canvas means reading back pixels, and PostScript has no operator for
% that (it is precisely the gap tracked as issue #134). Since the artist
% knows what they just laid down, /Under asks them rather than guessing
% — and for the skies, mists and distant planes this is for, the
% backdrop is a known near-flat tone anyway.
%
% Displacement directions are spaced by the golden angle (137.5°) plus
% a seeded wobble, so however few passes there are they distribute
% around the mark instead of piling onto one side and reading as a drop
% shadow.
%
% Every pass's displacement and colour is computed *before* the first
% pass runs, into one plan array. Interleaving pkwet's own draws with
% the wrapped proc's would make this mark's geometry depend on how much
% randomness the caller's proc happens to consume — so swapping
% pkribbon for pkspray inside the braces would move the halo, not just
% change the brush. Drawing the plan up front decouples them, and it
% means no caller code has run yet when the plan is fixed.
%
% One consequence of the grading being between an outermost pass and a
% core: a single-layer call has no such span, so at /Layers 1 there is
% only the core pass, sitting exactly on the path, and /Offset has
% nothing to act on and is inert. That is why /Soft 0 (which resolves to
% Layers 1) lays the same marks as a plain call whatever /Offset says.
%
% /Offset was named /Spread before issue #141. Everywhere else in this
% file /Spread is a 0..1 fraction of /Width, and pkwet's is a distance in
% points, so the same name read as two different quantities. /Spread
% still works here as a deprecated alias for /Offset; when both are
% given, /Offset wins and /Spread is ignored without being validated.
%
% /Soft is the one knob that matters: it supplies /Layers and /Offset,
% and every other key is a refinement. At /Soft 0 pkwet draws a single
% pass, at the caller's exact colour, with no displacement and without
% touching the random stream, so it *marks the page* exactly as calling
% the proc directly would -- pinned by
% `wet_soft_zero_is_identical_to_calling_the_proc`.
%
% That is a claim about the marks, and it used to be written as
% "byte-identical to calling the proc directly", which is stronger than
% pkwet delivers at any /Soft including 0 (review of PR #138). Every
% pass runs inside its own gsave and its own `stopped`, and both are
% required -- the gsave is what displaces a pass and what pays itself
% back when a pass is abandoned; the `stopped` is what keeps a caught
% error from leaking that gsave and pkwet's own depth counter. Two
% consequences a caller can see, at every /Soft:
%
%   * The graphics state the procedure leaves behind is discarded --
%     including the current path. `{ newpath ... lineto } pkwet stroke`
%     strokes nothing (measured: 640 ink pixels direct, 0 through
%     pkwet), and a `setrgbcolor` inside the procedure does not outlive
%     it. Marks the procedure *makes* are on the page and stay there;
%     state it hands back does not. A mark-drawing procedure is the
%     documented input, and those paint rather than hand a path out.
%   * A `stopped` context is an `exit` boundary, per the PLRM. See the
%     pass loop for what that means and why it is the honest choice.
%
% Revealing a *textured* underlayer still works, and does not need
% translucency: reach for a broken brush. A pkdry or pktrowel mark at
% low /Coverage is full of gaps, so mist laid on with one lets a
% treeline read straight through it. The specimen shows this.
%
% Cost: the wrapped proc runs up to /Layers times, so total work is up
% to 6x whatever that proc costs, including its own deposit budget.
% pkwet adds no budget of its own beyond the 1..6 cap — the same shape
% of nested-cost gap pkdry documents for its own bristle dashes (#79).
% Directional calls are the exception, below.
%
% Directional mode (issue #181)
% -----------------------------
% Several core wet-on-wet moves are blends *along a direction*: a
% reflection pulled straight down with a dry brush, a mountain's foot
% tapped and lifted into haze, a sky softened with long horizontal
% sweeps. /Direction (degrees) lines the passes up along that axis
% instead of scattering them round the mark:
%
%   { ... } << /Direction 270 /OneSided true /Offset 22
%              /Under [0.44 0.54 0.66] >> pkwet      % pull down
%
% Each non-core pass sits f*Offset along the direction -- exactly, so
% the outermost pass reaches the full /Offset and a pull reads as a
% pull -- plus a seeded wobble across it, /Stretch times narrower.
% /OneSided true puts every pass on the +Direction side (down only, up
% only); otherwise passes alternate sides by depth, outermost on the
% + side, so a sweep goes both ways. The grading toward /Under, the
% frames, the unwinding and the draws are exactly the isotropic ones:
% one draw per non-core pass either way, so *at a fixed /Layers*,
% switching /Direction on or turning /Direction, /Stretch, /OneSided or
% /Offset never re-rolls a later mark. With /Layers left to default,
% though, a directional call's depth follows /Offset (see Depth, below),
% and a different depth is a different number of passes -- of pkwet's
% draws and of the wrapped brush's own. Pin /Layers while tuning
% /Offset. A call that never names /Direction takes the old path
% unchanged.
%
% This is the pull the #118 notes assumed would need pixel readback
% (#134). For a procedural scene it doesn't: the wrapper already re-runs
% the mark, so it can re-run it displaced along a line.
%
% Three things a directional call needs that an isotropic one gets away
% without:
%
%   * The procedure must paint in the color it inherits, *or* in loaded
%     colors (`/Colors` on a preset that takes them), which pkwet grades
%     for you. Grading works by setting a mixed color *before* the
%     procedure runs; one that sets its own color (as every element of
%     a gallery scene does) draws every pass at full strength, and a
%     line of full-strength copies is a hard echo, not a pull. A loaded
%     preset mixes each of its colors by the running pass's factor
%     instead (`puwet`), so a two-color stroke fades like a one-color
%     one. Wrap a multi-colored scene
%     element by element, each with the /Under that is really beneath
%     it -- under a reflected treeline that is the reflected range, not
%     the open water.
%   * /Direction is in the user space pkwet is *called* in, since each
%     pass is a `translate` applied before the procedure runs. Put a
%     reflection's mirror inside the procedure, `{ gsave 0 2*WL
%     translate 1 -1 scale ... grestore }`, and 270 is down the page;
%     call pkwet from inside the `1 -1 scale` and 270 points up.
%   * The opaque core is laid last, so a pull streaks *past* each mark's
%     edge -- below a reflected peak, over the water -- rather than
%     smearing detail under a later solid fill. Wrapping element by
%     element is also what lets the snow's own pull land over the range.
%
% Depth. A procedure that reseeds draws identical marks on every pass,
% and identical copies lined up along one axis read as stepped echoes
% once they sit more than about 2pt apart (measured: a pulled treeline
% at 6 layers over 22pt repeated its crest as distinct bands). So a
% directional call's default /Layers is whatever keeps the step between
% same-side neighbours at or under 2pt -- ceil(Offset/2)+1 one-sided,
% ceil(Offset)+1 two-sided, since alternating sides doubles each side's
% step -- capped at 16, and its /Layers cap is 32 rather than 6. An
% explicit /Layers is honored up to that cap, and /Soft 0 is still the one
% plain pass.
%
% 2pt is where the echoes start to show, not where they stop. A pull that
% fades over a long /Offset against a flat ground, as a gradient and not as
% a texture, still reads as stepped at 2pt (at 2x each step is 4 device
% pixels of one flat colour). Ask for about 1pt: /Layers /Offset + 1
% one-sided, 2 x /Offset + 1 two-sided. That is why the cap is 32 and
% the default is not: the default stays at 16, so a call that leaves
% /Layers alone draws what it always did. The canvas specimen's pull
% (examples/paintkit_canvas_demo.ps) is /Offset 28 /Layers 29 (issue #417;
% /Layers 16 over the same 28pt, or /Offset 20 at 16 layers, still bands;
% /Offset 15 at 16 layers is better but leaves faint steps and a stubby pull).
%
% Cost, directional. A pull is up to 32x its procedure, and pulling a
% reflection -- itself a second copy of the scene -- then grazing it
% across is a nest by design. Nesting multiplies, so any chain with a
% directional call in it may multiply its innermost procedure at most
% 64 times (a full 16-layer pull grazed four ways, a 32-layer one grazed
% twice) -- a bound on the
% product down one nesting chain, not on total work: a procedure that
% loops over several inner calls costs each of them. Checked before any
% of the rejected call's draws: past that, pkwet-too-many-passes, with
% the depth rolled back like the nesting guard. Purely isotropic chains
% keep exactly the bounds they always had. The specimen,
% examples/paintkit_wet_pull_demo.ps, pulls three reflected elements
% and grazes them: about 20s for the page in a release build.
%
% A backdrop that varies (issue #179)
% ------------------------------------
% /Under is one colour, and a mark rarely sits on one: a cloud spans a
% graded sky, mist drifts over the foot of a range that fades into it.
% With one colour the outer passes are right at one end and leave a pale
% or dark rim at the other (#118 hand-matched it until the rim went).
% /Under may also be a procedure `x y -> r g b`, the backdrop as a
% function, and /At [x y] says where the mark sits:
%
%   { ... } << /Soft 1 /Offset 30
%              /At [ 135 70 ] /Under { exch pop skyat } >> pkwet
%
% Each softening pass asks the procedure for the backdrop at /At plus that
% pass's own displacement, and mixes toward the answer instead of toward a
% fixed colour; the core pass has no backdrop and asks nothing. So the
% question is per pass, not per pixel: pkwet cannot see where the wrapped
% mark paints (it is a procedure; see below), and one colour has to serve
% a whole pass. What the procedure fixes is the shift the displacement
% causes, the larger share of the rim at a generous /Offset. A mark that
% is tall against the gradient still has the rest, so split it into
% several pkwet calls, each with its own /At, or give /Box and /Bands
% instead (below). /At is required with a procedure unless /Box is given
% (named error otherwise) and rejected with an [r g b], where it would mean
% nothing. The coordinates are those of the user space pkwet is
% called in, as /Direction's are: inside a mirror or a translate the
% procedure sees shifted ones. A literal [r g b] takes exactly the code it
% always did, with the same draws.
%
% The procedure runs inside pkwet's frame, once per softening pass, before
% the first pass and after the plan is fixed. Keep it a pure function: it
% must not paint, must not call pkwet, and must not rely on what else is
% on the operand stack (it is called with nothing of pkwet's there, so
% one that counts the stack sees only the caller's items). It must leave
% exactly three numbers in 0..1. Anything else raises
% pkwet-under-procedure-must-return-3-numbers, -results-must-be-numbers or
% -results-must-be-0-to-1, with the depth counter and the operand stack
% put back first. Cost: at most Layers - 1 calls per pkwet call.
%
% A backdrop per band (issue #359)
% --------------------------------
% One colour per pass leaves the rim a tall mark's own height makes: over a
% sky that changes down the mark, the top of a pass is right and the bottom
% is not. pkwet still cannot see where the wrapped
% procedure paints, so it cannot ask per fill or per pixel; what it can do is
% cut the page into cells and paint each pass once per cell:
%
%   { ... } << /Soft 1 /Offset 30 /Layers 5
%              /Box [ 120 20 60 110 ] /Bands [ 1 6 ]
%              /Under { exch pop skyat } >> pkwet
%
% /Box says where the mark sits (x y w h, in the user space pkwet is called
% in); /Bands is the grid, columns by rows. The grid covers the box grown by
% /Offset on every side, because a pass is displaced by up to /Offset and its
% rim lands there; the outer cells' clips run on outward, so none of it is
% cut off. The procedure is asked once per cell, at its centre (not per pass,
% and not displaced: a cell is a fixed piece of page, and the question is what
% is under it), and each softening pass runs once per cell, clipped to the
% cell, in the caller's colour mixed toward that cell's answer. The core is
% laid once, whole, in the caller's own colour. /Box and /At are alternatives
% (a named error for both, or for one of /Box and /Bands without the other,
% or for either with a literal /Under).
%
% The wrapped procedure runs once per cell, and every run has to lay the same
% marks or the pieces would not fit. So the random stream is saved when a
% pass starts and put back (`rrand`, `srand`) before every cell after the
% first: each cell draws the same mark and the clips cut it up, and when the
% pass is done the stream stands where one unbanded run would have left it,
% so no mark drawn after the call moves, and the number of cells never
% re-rolls a thing. A procedure that reseeds itself replays its own seed in
% every cell. The same replay is why a banded call over a backdrop that never
% changes draws what the literal does, to the rounding of a clip's coverage
% (`wet_bands_over_a_constant_backdrop_match_the_literal`); the cell edges
% between cells are snapped to the device pixel grid, so neighbours share
% one edge exactly and leave no seam (exact for an axis-aligned or quarter-turn
% CTM; a page turned by another angle can show a faint hairline).
%
% Measured (tests/paintkit.rs, a 110pt column over a strong gradient,
% /Offset 34): the residual rim is 186.7 with one backdrop per pass, 112.5
% with 4 rows and 82.9 with 8, 2.25x less. On a short cloud per pass is
% already near the answer, and 3 rows are worse than it (146.9 against 119.8)
% where 6 are better (98.4). Pick rows so a cell is no taller than about half
% /Offset; for a mark about as tall as /Offset, /At is as good and cheaper.
%
% Cost: the wrapped procedure runs (Layers - 1) x cells + 1 times, so it is
% counted against the same 64-run budget as a directional call
% (pkwet-too-many-passes, checked before any draw): 12 cells at 6 layers, 21
% at 4. An unclipped procedure still does all its work in every cell, so a
% spray at the specimen's density costs that many times as much. Not done,
% and still open: asking the backdrop per fill inside a preset (a hook into
% each preset's colour set), which would take no clipping at all.
%
% pkwash is deliberately *not* wrappable: it resets alpha and blend mode
% itself, by design, so it will not honor a surrounding context. pkwet
% is for the stroke families.
%
% Determinism: the caller's own `N srand`, matching the presets it
% wraps rather than pkwash's private generator — one call should not
% mix two randomness doctrines.
%
% Scratch prefix pq- (pkwet), distinct from pk-/pn-/pb-/pz-/po-/pt-/
% pw-/pv- per this file's header. It has to be: the wrapped proc almost
% always calls a preset that redefines its own prefix.
%
% Where this preset's per-call state lives took three tries, because
% pkwet is the only thing here that re-enters caller code *in a loop*
% and both obvious answers are wrong:
%
%   plain names       a wrapped proc that itself calls pkwet overwrites
%                     the outer call's plan mid-loop, and the outer call
%                     then resumes against the inner one's -- running 1
%                     of its 4 passes, measured.
%   the operand stack recursion-safe, but the state sits *underneath*
%                     the callee, so a stack-balanced proc containing
%                     `count 0 eq { ... } if` behaves differently under
%                     pkwet than called directly, and one containing
%                     `clear` breaks the loop outright. That contradicts
%                     the /Soft 0 promise just as badly.
%
% Both found by review. What works is a frame indexed by nesting depth:
% `pqframes pqdepth get` holds this invocation's plan and procedure, an
% inner call takes depth+1 and restores the counter on the way out, and
% nothing of ours is on the operand stack or the dict stack while the
% caller's procedure runs. Reads happen immediately before the call and
% again on the next iteration, never across it.
%
% Depth is capped (pkwet-nesting-too-deep past 8) rather than growing a
% frame array without bound, and that guard rolls the counter back
% before it signals, so the rejection itself cannot poison later calls.
%
% The procedure has to construct its own path, and that is a real
% precondition rather than a stylistic one. Each pass displaces itself
% with `translate`, and a PostScript path is in device space from the
% moment it is built -- so a path constructed *before* the call cannot
% be moved by any transform set afterwards. Every pass would then walk
% the identical geometry and the mark would come out with no softening
% at all, silently: measured at 2044 ink pixels against 4676 for the
% same call with the path built inside (Codex review of PR #138).
% Rebuilding a caller's path under each transform would mean capturing
% it with `pathforall` and replaying it, which is a second mechanism
% for a usage every example, every test and the @example tag already
% writes the other way -- so this is documented and pinned instead.
%
% An error raised inside a wrapped procedure is handled, and it took
% three review findings to get there -- all of them, it turned out, the
% same missing unwind-protect. Left uncaught, an error skipped this
% procedure's remaining work, so one graphics-state frame leaked per
% abandoned pass (the caller resumed one gsave deep, translated, in
% this preset's mixed color) *and* pqdepth stayed elevated. The second
% of those is the dangerous one: with pqdepth naming the inner frame,
% an outer invocation's next pass read that frame and executed the
% *inner* call's procedure, turning an error the caller had already
% caught into an uncaught one.
%
% Each pass therefore runs under `stopped`, restores its own gsave and
% its own single depth increment, and re-raises. Every level unwinds
% exactly its own increment on the way past, so an inner call restores
% 2 -> 1 before re-raising and the outer resumes naming its own frame.
%
% That idiom was previously unavailable, and the reason was an
% interpreter gap rather than anything about this preset: pscat's
% top-level `stop` with no enclosing `stopped` ended execution
% *silently* (exit 0, no message) where Ghostscript reports the pending
% error from `$error`, so re-raising turned a hard error into no error
% at all for every caller that did not itself catch. That is issue
% #142, fixed in this branch -- `stop` now consults `$error /newerror`
% and reports, matching Ghostscript on all three of its cases. The same
% gap is documented in artkit.ps's scatter `/Seed` restore, which can
% adopt the idiom too now.

% Per-invocation state for pkwet, indexed by nesting depth. See the
% header for why neither plain names nor the operand stack will do.
%
% Written value-first with an explicit exch, like pwhasalpha below and
% for the same reason: build.rs's tag scanner models the depth-0 stack
% with a two-slot window and does not know array's arity, so the more
% obvious '/pqframes 9 array def' reads as a def whose key is three
% slots back and fails the build. It stays on one line so the tag block
% is still directly above the def.
% @internal
9 array /pqframes exch def
% @internal
/pqdepth 0 def

% pkwet's counter and frames are reached through userdict explicitly,
% never by a bare name. `def` writes to the *current* dict, and
% `stopped` does not restore the dict stack -- so a wrapped procedure
% that raises with a dict still `begin`-ned had this procedure's
% decrement land in that dict while userdict's counter stayed high.
% Eight of those and every later top-level call dies with
% pkwet-nesting-too-deep, which is exactly the poisoning the guard
% below claims cannot happen (review of PR #138).
% -> depth
% @internal
/pqdnow { userdict /pqdepth get } bind def
% depth pqdset -
% @internal
/pqdset { userdict /pqdepth 3 -1 roll put } bind def
% -> frame   (this invocation's 9-slot frame; pkwet lists the slots)
% @internal
/pqmyframe { userdict /pqframes get pqdnow get } bind def

% x y -> [r g b]   (issue #179: ask the /Under procedure for the backdrop
% at one point and check its answer)
% It runs with nothing of this call's on the operand stack, so a procedure
% that counts the stack sees only the caller's items, and it is the only
% caller code that runs while the frame is half built: every way out
% below first rolls the depth back, so a caught error or a rejected
% result never leaves pqdepth raised (the nesting guard does the same).
% Reads pqUnderProc. Draws no random number.
% @internal
/pqaskunder {
    count 2 sub /pqc0 exch def
    { pqUnderProc } stopped { pqdnow 1 sub pqdset stop } if
    % Exactly three results, nothing more, nothing fewer. Anything
    % else is dropped back to the entry count (if the procedure ate
    % into the caller's items there is nothing to put back) and
    % named, rather than left to fail inside mix3.
    count pqc0 sub 3 ne {
        count pqc0 sub dup 0 gt { { pop } repeat } { pop } ifelse
        pqdnow 1 sub pqdset pkwet-under-procedure-must-return-3-numbers
    } if
    3 array astore /pqur exch def
    0 1 2 {
        pqur exch get dup type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
            { pop pqdnow 1 sub pqdset pkwet-under-procedure-results-must-be-numbers } if
        dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or
            { pqdnow 1 sub pqdset pkwet-under-procedure-results-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    } for
    pqur
} bind def

% -> -   (procedural /Under, issue #179: evaluate the backdrop procedure
% for every softening pass and re-mix that pass's colour toward the result)
% Reads pqplan, pqks, pqbase, pqPickup, pqLayers and pqAt, and
% leaves the per-pass backdrop arrays in pqunders (null at the core, which
% has none). The procedure gets the pass's position, `At` plus the pass's
% displacement from the plan, in the user space pkwet was called in.
% Nothing here draws a random number.
% @internal
/pqwetunder {
    /pqunders pqLayers array def
    0 1 pqLayers 1 sub {
        /pqi exch def
        pqplan pqi get dup length 2 gt {
            /pqent exch def
            pqAt 0 get pqent 0 get add
            pqAt 1 get pqent 1 get add
            pqaskunder /pqur exch def
            pqunders pqi pqur put
            pqplan pqi
            [
                pqent 0 get pqent 1 get
                pqbase pqur pqks pqi get mix3
            ]
            put
        } { pop } ifelse
    } for
} bind def

% x -> x'   /   y -> y'   (issue #359: a user-space coordinate moved to the
% nearest device pixel edge along its own axis; exact when the CTM is
% axis-aligned or a quarter turn, where one device coordinate is the whole
% story, so both are rounded)
% @internal
/pqsx { 0 transform round exch round exch itransform pop } bind def
% @internal
/pqsy { 0 exch transform round exch round exch itransform exch pop } bind def

% -> -   (banded /Under, issue #359: the grid of cells and what each one
% is mixed toward)
% Reads pqBox, pqCols, pqRows, pqSpread, pqplan, pqks, pqbase and
% pqLayers and leaves pqbrec = [ rects backdrops mixed rng ] for the frame:
%   rects      per cell, the [x y w h] its softening passes are clipped to
%   backdrops  per cell, the colour the procedure answered at its centre
%   mixed      per pass, null at the core, else per cell the pass colour
%              (the caller's colour mixed toward that cell's backdrop)
%   rng        the stream saved at the start of each pass (see pqwetbandpass)
% The grid covers /Box grown by /Offset on every side, because a pass is
% displaced by up to /Offset and its rim lands there; the edge cells' clips
% run on outward without limit, so nothing of a displaced pass is cut off.
% Cells are numbered row by row from the low corner, the order asked in.
% @internal
/pqwetbands {
    /pqbx pqBox 0 get pqSpread sub def
    /pqby pqBox 1 get pqSpread sub def
    /pqbw pqBox 2 get pqSpread 2 mul add pqCols div def
    /pqbh pqBox 3 get pqSpread 2 mul add pqRows div def
    % The clip edges between cells, snapped to the device pixel grid: two
    % clips that abut at a fractional pixel each cover part of the pixel, and
    % two opaque fills at part coverage let the ground show through as a
    % hairline (the seam pktrowel's lane overlap exists to avoid). Snapped, the
    % neighbours share one edge exactly. A snap is exact when the CTM does
    % rotate by other than a quarter turn or skew, which is every page here; a
    % CTM turned by some other angle can show a faint seam. The first and last edges run on outward without limit.
    /pqxe pqCols 1 add array def
    /pqye pqRows 1 add array def
    0 1 pqCols {
        /pqbi exch def
        pqxe pqbi
        pqbi 0 eq { pqbx 100000 sub } {
            pqbi pqCols eq { pqbx pqbw pqCols mul add 100000 add }
                           { pqbx pqbi pqbw mul add pqsx } ifelse } ifelse
        put
    } for
    0 1 pqRows {
        /pqbj exch def
        pqye pqbj
        pqbj 0 eq { pqby 100000 sub } {
            pqbj pqRows eq { pqby pqbh pqRows mul add 100000 add }
                           { pqby pqbj pqbh mul add pqsy } ifelse } ifelse
        put
    } for
    /pqrects pqCols pqRows mul array def
    /pqbks pqCols pqRows mul array def
    0 1 pqRows 1 sub {
        /pqbj exch def
        0 1 pqCols 1 sub {
            /pqbi exch def
            pqbx pqbi pqbw mul add pqbw 2 div add
            pqby pqbj pqbh mul add pqbh 2 div add
            pqaskunder /pqur exch def
            pqbks pqbj pqCols mul pqbi add pqur put
            pqrects pqbj pqCols mul pqbi add
            [ pqxe pqbi get  pqye pqbj get
              pqxe pqbi 1 add get pqxe pqbi get sub
              pqye pqbj 1 add get pqye pqbj get sub ]
            put
        } for
    } for
    /pqmixed pqLayers array def
    0 1 pqLayers 1 sub {
        /pqi exch def
        pqplan pqi get length 2 gt {
            pqmixed pqi pqbks length array put
            0 1 pqbks length 1 sub {
                /pqbn exch def
                pqmixed pqi get pqbn
                [ pqbase pqbks pqbn get pqks pqi get mix3 ]
                put
            } for
        } if
    } for
    /pqbrec [ pqrects pqbks pqmixed null ] def
} bind def

% -> -   (one banded softening pass, issue #359: the pass named by frame
% slot 6, once per cell)
% Each cell is its own gsave, clipped to the cell's rectangle in the user
% space pkwet was called in, painting in that cell's mix, translated by the
% pass's displacement, and running the wrapped procedure under `stopped`
% exactly as an unbanded pass does. Slot 5 is pointed at the cell's backdrop
% so a loaded colour (`puwet`) grades toward the same colour.
%
% The wrapped procedure runs once per cell, and each run must lay the same
% marks, or the cells would not fit together. So the random stream is saved
% when the pass starts and put back before every cell after the first
% (`rrand`/`srand`, the convention hatchkit and artkit use): every cell
% draws the same mark and the clips cut it into pieces, and when the pass is
% done the stream is where one unbanded run would have left it, so no mark
% after the call moves. A procedure that reseeds itself replays its own seed
% in every cell anyway.
%
% Everything this reads is read from the frame, never from a global: the
% wrapped procedure may itself call pkwet, which would overwrite them.
% @internal
/pqwetbandpass {
    pqmyframe 8 get 3 rrand put
    0 1 pqmyframe 8 get 0 get length 1 sub {
        /pqwc exch def
        pqmyframe 8 get /pqwr exch def
        pqwr 0 get length 1 gt pqwc 0 gt and { pqwr 3 get srand } if
        gsave
            pqwr 0 get length 1 gt { pqwr 0 get pqwc get aload pop rectclip } if
            pqwr 2 get pqmyframe 6 get get pqwc get aload pop setrgbcolor
            pqmyframe 5 pqwr 1 get pqwc get put
            pqmyframe 0 get pqmyframe 6 get get dup 0 get exch 1 get translate
            { pqmyframe 1 get exec } stopped
        {
            grestore
            pqdnow 1 sub pqdset
            stop
        } if
        grestore
    } for
} bind def

%%SelfTest: pkwet-rejects-malformed-directional-options
%   { { } << /Direction (down) >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-direction-must-be-a-number
%   (/Direction as a string) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Direction { 270 } >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-direction-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Direction { 270 }) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Direction 270 /Stretch 0.5 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-stretch-must-be-at-least-1
%   (/Stretch 0.5) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Direction 270 /Stretch (x) >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-stretch-must-be-a-number
%   (/Stretch as a string) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Direction 270 /Stretch { 4 } >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-stretch-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Stretch { 4 }) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Direction 270 /OneSided 1 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-onesided-must-be-a-boolean
%   (/OneSided 1) mustguard
%
%   % Directional keys without a direction are rejected, not ignored.
%   { { } << /Stretch 4 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-stretch-needs-a-direction
%   (/Stretch without /Direction) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /OneSided true >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-onesided-needs-a-direction
%   (/OneSided without /Direction) mustguard
%
%   % The directional cap is 32 (issue #417: it was 16); the isotropic one
%   % is still 6.
%   { { } << /Direction 270 /Layers 33 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-layers-must-be-1-to-32
%   (/Layers 33 with /Direction) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Layers 7 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-layers-must-be-1-to-6
%   (/Layers 7 without /Direction) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Direction 270 /Layers 16 >> pkwet }
%   (/Layers 16 with /Direction) mustpass
%
%   { { } << /Direction 270 /Layers 32 >> pkwet }
%   (/Layers 32 with /Direction) mustpass
%
%   % An absurd /Offset clamps the default depth instead of overflowing cvi.
%   { { } << /Direction 270 /Offset 1e30 >> pkwet }
%   (/Offset 1e30 with /Direction) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkwet-bounds-a-directional-nest
%   % 16 x 5 = 80 runs of the innermost procedure is over the 64 budget.
%   { { { } << /Direction 0 /Layers 16 >> pkwet } << /Direction 270 /Layers 5 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-too-many-passes
%   (a 16-layer pull nested five ways) mustguard
%
%   % ...and the rejection rolls the depth back, so it cannot poison
%   % later calls.
%   { { { } << /Direction 0 /Layers 16 >> pkwet } << /Direction 270 /Layers 5 >> pkwet }
%   stopped pop userdict /pqdepth get 0 eq
%   (the depth after a rejected nest) mustbe
%
%   % A pull grazed four ways is exactly the budget.
%   { { { } << /Direction 270 /Layers 16 >> pkwet } << /Direction 0 /Layers 4 >> pkwet }
%   (a 16-layer pull grazed four ways) mustpass
%
%   % Purely isotropic chains are not budgeted.
%   { { { { } << /Layers 6 >> pkwet } << /Layers 6 >> pkwet } << /Layers 6 >> pkwet }
%   (a 6x6x6 isotropic nest) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkwet-offset-and-its-deprecated-spread-alias
%   % /Spread is the deprecated name for /Offset (issue #141) and is
%   % still read, through the same guard, when /Offset is absent.
%   { { } << /Offset -1 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-offset-must-not-be-negative
%   (/Offset -1) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Spread -1 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-offset-must-not-be-negative
%   (/Spread -1, the deprecated alias) mustguard
%
%   % When both are given /Offset wins, and /Spread is not even validated.
%   { { } << /Offset 5 /Spread -1 >> pkwet }
%   (/Offset 5 with a negative /Spread) mustpass
%
%   { { } << /Offset -1 /Spread 5 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-offset-must-not-be-negative
%   (/Offset -1 with a valid /Spread) mustguard
%
%   % The value is used, not only validated: a one-sided pull's default
%   % depth is ceil(Offset/2)+1 passes, and the procedure runs once per
%   % pass.
%   userdict /pkwetn 0 put
%   { userdict /pkwetn pkwetn 1 add put }
%   << /Direction 270 /OneSided true /Spread 22 >> pkwet
%   pkwetn 12 eq (/Spread 22 alone draws 12 passes, as /Offset 22 does) mustbe
%
%   userdict /pkwetn 0 put
%   { userdict /pkwetn pkwetn 1 add put }
%   << /Direction 270 /OneSided true /Offset 22 >> pkwet
%   pkwetn 12 eq (/Offset 22 draws 12 passes) mustbe
%
%   userdict /pkwetn 0 put
%   { userdict /pkwetn pkwetn 1 add put }
%   << /Direction 270 /OneSided true /Offset 6 /Spread 22 >> pkwet
%   pkwetn 4 eq (/Offset 6 wins over /Spread 22: 4 passes) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkwet-rejects-malformed-procedural-under
%   % A procedural /Under needs /At, and /At means nothing without one.
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-under-procedure-needs-at
%   (a procedure without /At) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under [ 0 0 0 ] /At [ 0 0 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-at-needs-an-under-procedure
%   (/At with a literal /Under) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /At 5 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-at-must-be-an-x-y-pair
%   (/At as a number) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /At [ 0 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-at-must-be-an-x-y-pair
%   (/At with one element) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /At [ 0 (a) ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-at-must-be-an-x-y-pair
%   (/At with a string) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /At { 0 0 } >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-at-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/At as a procedure) mustguard
%
%   % What the backdrop procedure hands back. Each rejection also puts the
%   % depth counter and the operand stack back: the guard itself is what
%   % must not poison the next call.
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 1 2 } /At [ 0 0 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-under-procedure-must-return-3-numbers
%   (two results) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 1 2 3 4 } /At [ 0 0 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-under-procedure-must-return-3-numbers
%   (four results) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { } /At [ 0 0 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-under-procedure-must-return-3-numbers
%   (a procedure that leaves the position, four values) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop (a) 0 0 } /At [ 0 0 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-under-procedure-results-must-be-numbers
%   (a string result) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 2 0 0 } /At [ 0 0 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-under-procedure-results-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a result of 2) mustguard
%
%   pqdepth 0 eq (every rejection rolled the depth back) mustbe
%
%   % The procedure is asked once per softening pass, at /At plus the
%   % pass's displacement, and never for the core: a one-sided pull of 12
%   % over 3 layers goes down 6 and 12 from /At, so y = 94 then 88 (the
%   % plan is indexed from the core out; the passes are drawn the other way).
%   userdict /pwul [ ] put
%   { } << /Direction 270 /OneSided true /Offset 12 /Layers 3
%          /At [ 5 100 ]
%          /Under { /pwy exch def /pwx exch def
%                   userdict /pwul [ pwul aload pop pwx pwy ] put
%                   0.5 0.5 0.5 } >> pkwet
%   pwul length 4 eq (asked for the 2 softening passes, not the core) mustbe
%   pwul 1 get 94 sub abs 0.01 lt (the inner softening pass is 6 down) mustbe
%   pwul 3 get 88 sub abs 0.01 lt (the outermost pass is 12 down) mustbe
%   pwul 0 get 5 sub abs 3 lt (x stays near /At) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkwet-rejects-malformed-bands
%   % /Box and /Bands go together, instead of /At, with a procedural /Under.
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-box-needs-bands
%   (/Box without /Bands) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Bands [ 2 2 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-bands-need-a-box
%   (/Bands without /Box) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands [ 2 2 ] /At [ 0 0 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-at-and-box-are-exclusive
%   (/At with /Box) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under [ 0 0 0 ] /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands [ 2 2 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-box-needs-an-under-procedure
%   (/Box with a literal /Under) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box 5 /Bands [ 2 2 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-box-must-be-x-y-w-h
%   (a number as /Box) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 ] /Bands [ 2 2 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-box-must-be-x-y-w-h
%   (a 3-element /Box) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 0 9 ] /Bands [ 2 2 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-box-must-be-x-y-w-h
%   (a /Box with no width) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box { 0 0 9 9 } /Bands [ 2 2 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-box-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (a procedure as /Box) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands 4 >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-bands-must-be-cols-rows
%   (a number as /Bands) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands [ 2 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-bands-must-be-cols-rows
%   (a 1-element /Bands) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands [ 2 1.5 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-bands-must-be-cols-rows
%   (a fractional /Bands) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands { 2 2 } >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-bands-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (a procedure as /Bands) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands [ 0 2 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-bands-must-be-1-to-16
%   (0 columns) mustguard
%
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands [ 2 17 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-bands-must-be-1-to-16
%   (17 rows) mustguard
%
%   % A banded call is asked once per cell, for the softening passes only,
%   % and its answers are checked as an /At call's are.
%   { { } << /Under { pop pop 0 0 2 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands [ 1 2 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-under-procedure-results-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a result of 2 from a banded call) mustguard
%
%   pqdepth 0 eq (every rejection rolled the depth back) mustbe
%
%   % The pass budget counts every cell: (Layers - 1) * cells + 1 runs of
%   % the mark. 5 * 12 + 1 = 61 is inside the 64 a chain of this kind may
%   % multiply its mark by; 5 * 16 + 1 = 81 is not.
%   userdict /pwbn 0 put
%   { userdict /pwbn pwbn 1 add put }
%   << /Layers 6 /Offset 0 /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands [ 3 4 ] >> pkwet
%   pwbn 61 eq (3 x 4 cells at 6 layers run the mark 61 times) mustbe
%
%   { { } << /Layers 6 /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands [ 4 4 ] >> pkwet }
%   /pkwet-too-many-passes
%   (4 x 4 cells at 6 layers is 81 runs) mustguard
%
%   % One cell is no banding: the mark runs once per pass.
%   userdict /pwbn 0 put
%   { userdict /pwbn pwbn 1 add put }
%   << /Layers 4 /Under { pop pop 0 0 0 } /Box [ 0 0 9 9 ] /Bands [ 1 1 ] >> pkwet
%   pwbn 4 eq (one cell runs the mark once per pass) mustbe
%
%   % The procedure is asked once per cell, at its centre in the padded
%   % grid: /Box grown by /Offset (here 4) on every side, 2 x 1 cells.
%   userdict /pwul [ ] put
%   { } << /Layers 2 /Offset 4 /Box [ 10 20 8 6 ] /Bands [ 2 1 ]
%          /Under { /pwy exch def /pwx exch def
%                   userdict /pwul [ pwul aload pop pwx pwy ] put
%                   0.5 0.5 0.5 } >> pkwet
%   pwul length 4 eq (asked once per cell, not per pass) mustbe
%   pwul 0 get 10 sub abs 0.01 lt (the first cell's centre x) mustbe
%   pwul 1 get 23 sub abs 0.01 lt (and y) mustbe
%   pwul 2 get 18 sub abs 0.01 lt (the second cell's centre x) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Runs a mark-drawing procedure several times, each pass displaced further and mixed further toward a declared backdrop color, so the mark's core stays solid while its edge grades away into the layer beneath. The wet-on-wet softener for skies, clouds, mist and distant forms; works with every stroke preset in this file. The procedure must build its own path: a path constructed before the call cannot be displaced, because PostScript paths are in device space once built, so every pass would land on the same geometry and nothing would soften. Pair it with a broken or particulate brush (pkspray, pkdry, pktrowel at low Coverage) -- a solid brush keeps its own hard silhouette and only gains a halo. With /Direction the passes line up along one axis instead: a reflection pulled down, a mountain foot lifted into mist, a sky swept across. /Under may be a procedure `x y -> r g b` (with /At, or with /Box and /Bands for a mark that is tall against the backdrop) for a backdrop that varies across the mark, such as a graded sky. Uses no alpha, so unlike pkwash it needs no Ghostscript fallback.
% @example: { newpath 40 120 moveto 260 150 lineto << /Width 40 >> pkribbon } << /Soft 0.7 /Under [0.62 0.72 0.86] >> pkwet
% @param: /Soft [0..1] master softness; supplies the defaults for Layers and Offset. 0 is a single undisplaced pass that marks the page exactly as calling the proc directly would, random stream included; the procedure still runs inside a gsave, so a path or graphics state it leaves behind does not survive the call (default 0.6)
% @param: /Under [rgb array] the color already on the canvas that this mark settles into; the outermost pass is nearly this color. May also be a procedure `x y -> r g b` (three numbers in 0..1, user space), the backdrop as a function: it is asked once per softening pass, at /At plus that pass's displacement, so the rim goes where the backdrop changes across the mark. It must be a pure function: no painting, no pkwet call, no reliance on the operand stack. A mark taller than the backdrop's change is better asked per band: see /Box and /Bands (default [1 1 1])
% @param: /At [points] With a procedural /Under only (required there): [x y] where the mark sits, in the user space pkwet is called in, the point each pass's displacement is added to before the procedure is asked for the backdrop (default absent)
% @param: /Box [array of points] With a procedural /Under and /Bands (instead of /At): [x y w h], where the mark sits, in the user space pkwet is called in. The /Under procedure is then asked once per cell of a grid over the box grown by /Offset on every side, at the cell's centre, and each softening pass is run once per cell, clipped to it and mixed toward that cell's answer, so the backdrop varies across a tall mark and not only from pass to pass. The grid's outer cells run on outward, so a displaced rim is not cut off (default absent)
% @param: /Bands [array of count] With /Box (required with it): [columns rows] of the grid, each a whole number 1 to 16. A cell wants to be no taller than about half /Offset, and a short mark is as well served by /At. The wrapped procedure runs (Layers - 1) x columns x rows + 1 times, which a nest of such calls may multiply by at most 64 (a 6-layer call can have 12 cells, a 4-layer one 21); the random stream is replayed per cell, so every cell lays the same marks and the stream ends where one unbanded run leaves it (default absent)
% @param: /Pickup [0..1] how far the outermost pass mixes toward Under; lower keeps the mark's own color out to its edge (default 0.85)
% @param: /Layers [count] Passes to draw, outermost first; must be a whole number, hard cap 1..6, or 1..32 with /Direction (default 1 + round(Soft*5); with /Direction, raised to keep same-side passes at most 2pt apart, up to 16)
% @param: /Offset [points] How far in points the outermost pass is displaced from the true path; inert at Layers 1, which has only a core pass. Formerly /Spread, still accepted as a deprecated alias; /Offset wins if both are given (default Soft*14)
% @param: /Direction [degrees] Degrees, in the user space pkwet is called in: lines the passes up along this axis instead of scattering them, for a pulled-down reflection (270), a lifted mist (90) or a swept sky (0). The procedure must paint in the color it inherits, or in loaded colors (/Colors), which pkwet grades for you. A chain of nested calls containing a directional one may multiply its innermost procedure at most 64 times. Without an explicit /Layers its depth follows /Offset, so pin /Layers while tuning /Offset (default absent: isotropic)
% @param: /Stretch [ratio] With /Direction only: along-to-across ratio, at least 1; the cross-axis wobble is the along-axis reach divided by this (default 8)
% @param: /OneSided [boolean] With /Direction only: true puts every pass on the +Direction side (a pull down, a lift up); false alternates sides, a sweep both ways (default false)
/pkwet {
    /pqopts exch def
    /pqproc exch def
    /pqproc load xcheck
        /pqproc load type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or
        and not { pkwet-first-operand-must-be-a-procedure } if
    /pqSoft pqopts /Soft 0.6 pkgetdef def
    /pqSoft load xcheck { pkwet-soft-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqSoft 0 lt pqSoft 1 gt or { pkwet-soft-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    % Directional mode (issue #181) is keyed off whether /Direction is
    % *present*, so a call that never names it takes exactly the path it
    % always did: same defaults, same 1..6 cap, same plan loop, same
    % draws. It is decided first because it sets the /Layers cap.
    /pqdir pqopts /Direction known def
    /pqLayers pqopts /Layers pqSoft 5 mul round cvi 1 add pkgetdef def
    /pqLayers load xcheck { pkwet-layers-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqLayers 1 lt pqLayers pqdir { 32 } { 6 } ifelse gt or
    pqLayers truncate pqLayers ne or
        { pqdir { pkwet-layers-must-be-1-to-32 } { pkwet-layers-must-be-1-to-6 } ifelse } if
    % `/Layers 3.0` clears the whole-number check above and is a
    % perfectly reasonable thing for a caller to compute, but it is still
    % a real, and `real array` raises typecheck in both pscat and
    % Ghostscript. Normalize rather than reject: the value passed the
    % contract, so failing later on its representation would be a bug in
    % us, not bad input (Codex review).
    /pqLayers pqLayers cvi def
    % /Offset, or the deprecated /Spread alias when /Offset is absent
    % (issue #141: /Spread everywhere else in this file is a fraction of
    % /Width, not points). The inner pkgetdef resolves the alias, so when
    % both are given /Offset wins and /Spread is never read.
    /pqSpread pqopts /Offset pqopts /Spread pqSoft 14 mul pkgetdef pkgetdef def
    /pqSpread load xcheck { pkwet-offset-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqSpread 0 lt { pkwet-offset-must-not-be-negative } if
    /pqPickup pqopts /Pickup 0.85 pkgetdef def
    /pqPickup load xcheck { pkwet-pickup-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pqPickup 0 lt pqPickup 1 gt or { pkwet-pickup-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pqUnder pqopts /Under [ 1 1 1 ] pkgetdef def
    % /Under is a literal [r g b] or, since issue #179, a backdrop
    % procedure `x y -> r g b` (see "A backdrop that varies" in the header).
    % The array branch is the code that was always here, verbatim, so a
    % literal /Under validates and plans exactly as before; the procedure
    % branch parks a white placeholder in pqUnder so the plan loop below
    % runs unchanged and its random draws are identical, then pqwetunder
    % overwrites the colours.
    /pqUnder load xcheck {
        /pqUnder load type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or not
            { pkwet-under-must-be-a-3-element-array } if
        % Where the mark sits: /At, one point asked per pass (issue #179), or
        % /Box with /Bands, a grid of cells asked once each (issue #359).
        pqopts /At known pqopts /Box known and { pkwet-at-and-box-are-exclusive } if
        pqopts /Box known pqopts /Bands known xor {
            pqopts /Box known { pkwet-box-needs-bands } { pkwet-bands-need-a-box } ifelse
        } if
        pqopts /Box known {
            /pqBox pqopts /Box get def
            /pqBox load type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or not
                { pkwet-box-must-be-x-y-w-h } if
            /pqBox load xcheck { pkwet-box-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
            pqBox length 4 ne { pkwet-box-must-be-x-y-w-h } if
            0 1 3 {
                pqBox exch get dup type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
                    { pkwet-box-must-be-x-y-w-h } if
                pop
            } for
            pqBox 2 get 0 le pqBox 3 get 0 le or { pkwet-box-must-be-x-y-w-h } if
            /pqBands pqopts /Bands get def
            /pqBands load type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or not
                { pkwet-bands-must-be-cols-rows } if
            /pqBands load xcheck { pkwet-bands-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
            pqBands length 2 ne { pkwet-bands-must-be-cols-rows } if
            0 1 1 {
                pqBands exch get dup type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
                    { pkwet-bands-must-be-cols-rows } if
                dup dup truncate ne { pkwet-bands-must-be-cols-rows } if
                dup 1 lt exch 16 gt or { pkwet-bands-must-be-1-to-16 } if
            } for
            /pqCols pqBands 0 get cvi def
            /pqRows pqBands 1 get cvi def
            /pqbanded true def
        } {
            /pqbanded false def
            pqopts /At known not { pkwet-under-procedure-needs-at } if
            /pqAt pqopts /At get def
            /pqAt load type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or not
                { pkwet-at-must-be-an-x-y-pair } if
            /pqAt load xcheck { pkwet-at-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
            pqAt length 2 ne { pkwet-at-must-be-an-x-y-pair } if
            0 1 1 {
                pqAt exch get dup type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
                    { pkwet-at-must-be-an-x-y-pair } if
                pop
            } for
        } ifelse
        /pqUnderProc /pqUnder load def
        /pqUnder [ 1 1 1 ] def
        /pqunderp true def
    } {
        /pqUnder load type /arraytype ne { pkwet-under-must-be-a-3-element-array } if
        pqUnder length 3 ne { pkwet-under-must-be-a-3-element-array } if
        0 1 2 {
            pqUnder exch get dup type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
                { pkwet-under-components-must-be-numbers } if
            dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or { pkwet-under-components-must-be-0-to-1 } if
        } for
        pqopts /At known { pkwet-at-needs-an-under-procedure } if
        pqopts /Box known pqopts /Bands known or { pkwet-box-needs-an-under-procedure } if
        /pqbanded false def
        /pqunderp false def
    } ifelse
    % /Stretch and /OneSided only mean anything along a direction; given
    % without one they are rejected rather than silently ignored, since
    % an artist who wrote them expected a pull and would otherwise get a
    % plain halo with no hint why.
    pqdir {
        /pqDirection pqopts /Direction get def
        /pqDirection load xcheck { pkwet-direction-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
        /pqDirection load type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
            { pkwet-direction-must-be-a-number } if
        /pqStretch pqopts /Stretch 8 pkgetdef def
        /pqStretch load xcheck { pkwet-stretch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
        /pqStretch load type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
            { pkwet-stretch-must-be-a-number } if
        pqStretch 1 lt { pkwet-stretch-must-be-at-least-1 } if
        /pqOneSided pqopts /OneSided false pkgetdef def
        /pqOneSided load type /booleantype ne { pkwet-onesided-must-be-a-boolean } if
        % Every pass is an identical copy of the mark (a procedure that
        % reseeds, as every reflected element must, draws the same marks
        % each time), and lined up along one axis those copies read as
        % stepped echoes once they sit more than about 2pt apart --
        % measured on a pulled treeline at 6 layers over 22pt, where the
        % crest repeated as distinct bands. So a directional call's
        % *default* depth is whatever keeps the step under 2pt, which is
        % why its default tops out at 16 rather than 6 (the explicit cap
        % is 32, issue #417, for a pull that wants 1pt). The step
        % that matters is between neighbours on the *same side*: one-sided, that is
        % Offset/(L-1); two-sided, passes alternate sides, so each side
        % gets every other offset and its step is twice that (Codex
        % review: /Offset 12 at 7 layers put a sweep's copies 4pt apart).
        % Past the cap -- a one-sided Offset over 30, a two-sided one over
        % 15 -- the step grows; pin /Layers higher (up to 32) or /Offset
        % shorter, or accept it. Only the default: an explicit /Layers is honored as
        % given, and a single-layer call (/Soft 0) stays the plain call it
        % is everywhere else.
        pqopts /Layers known not pqLayers 1 gt and {
            % clamped before cvi, which raises rangecheck on a huge real
            /pqLayers pqLayers
                pqSpread pqOneSided { 2 } { 1 } ifelse div ceiling 15 min cvi 1 add
            max def
        } if
    } {
        pqopts /Stretch known { pkwet-stretch-needs-a-direction } if
        pqopts /OneSided known { pkwet-onesided-needs-a-direction } if
    } ifelse
    pqdnow 1 add pqdset
    % Roll the depth back *before* signalling. A caller that catches this
    % with `stopped` would otherwise be left with pqdepth stuck above the
    % limit, so one rejected nested call would poison every later
    % top-level pkwet until the library was reloaded (Codex review).
    pqdnow 8 gt { pqdnow 1 sub pqdset pkwet-nesting-too-deep } if
    % The pass budget (issue #181). Nesting multiplies: every pass of an
    % outer call runs the inner call's every pass, so the wrapped mark's
    % cost is the *product* of /Layers down the chain. Directional mode
    % raised the per-call cap to 16 and its headline use, pulling a
    % reflection down and then grazing it across, is a nest by design --
    % over a reflection that is already a second copy of the scene. So a
    % chain with any directional call in it may multiply its innermost
    % mark at most 64 times (a full pull, 16, grazed 4 ways), checked
    % here, before any of this call's draws. Purely isotropic chains are
    % left as they always were, under the 1..6-per-call and depth-8 caps.
    % Each frame records its own running product and flag, so the parent
    % is read off its frame rather than a counter that would need
    % unwinding -- the depth machinery below already restores which frame
    % is live. Depth 1 has no parent; slot 0 is never written.
    pqdnow 1 gt
        { userdict /pqframes get pqdnow 1 sub get dup 2 get exch 3 get }
        { 1 false }
    ifelse
    pqdir or pqbanded or /pqchaindir exch def
    % A banded call (issue #359) runs the mark once per cell in every
    % softening pass, so that is what it multiplies the chain by.
    pqbanded { pqLayers 1 sub pqCols pqRows mul mul 1 add } { pqLayers } ifelse
    mul /pqchain exch def
    pqchaindir pqchain 64 gt and
        { pqdnow 1 sub pqdset pkwet-too-many-passes } if
    % Nine slots: 0 the plan, 1 the procedure, 2-3 the pass budget's
    % running product and flag, 4-6 the pass mix that loaded colours
    % read (issue #183 slice L3, below), and 7 the per-pass backdrop
    % colours of a procedural /Under (issue #179; null for a literal one,
    % which is what keeps puwet's old path), and 8 a banded call's record
    % (issue #359: the cells' clips, backdrops, pass colours and the saved
    % random stream; null for any other call). The later slots go at the end
    % so 0-3 keep the meaning the budget and the nesting machinery rely on.
    userdict /pqframes get pqdnow 9 array put
    pqmyframe 1 /pqproc load put
    pqmyframe 2 pqchain put
    pqmyframe 3 pqchaindir put
    /pqbase [ currentrgbcolor ] def
    % Plan every pass before running any of them, so that none of pkwet's
    % own randomness is interleaved with the wrapped proc's -- otherwise
    % this mark's halo would shift according to how many draws the
    % caller's brush happens to take.
    /pqplan pqLayers array def
    /pqks pqLayers array def
    0 1 pqLayers 1 sub {
        /pqi exch def
        % 1.0 at the outermost pass, 0.0 at the core. A single-pass call
        % has no outermost/core span to grade across, and the obvious
        % (pqi / (Layers-1)) divides by zero there.
        /pqf pqLayers 1 gt { pqi pqLayers 1 sub div } { 0 } ifelse def
        /pqmag pqf pqSpread mul def
        pqplan pqi
        [
            % Whether a displacement draw happens is keyed off the pass's
            % position in the stack, *not* off whether the displacement
            % comes out non-zero. Keying it off the magnitude looked
            % equivalent and wasn't: /Offset 0 with several layers would
            % then skip every draw while any positive spread takes one
            % per non-core pass, so turning /Offset alone re-rolled the
            % wrapped brush and everything drawn after it -- exactly the
            % contract this preset documents (Codex review). The core
            % pass is never displaced by definition, so skipping its draw
            % is a property of /Layers, which is allowed to change
            % consumption, and is what keeps /Soft 0 free.
            pqf 0 gt {
                pqdir {
                    % Along the direction, deterministically: the
                    % outermost pass reaches the full /Offset, which is
                    % what makes a pull read as a pull rather than a
                    % scatter. Across it, a seeded wobble /Stretch times
                    % narrower -- the one draw, so a directional call
                    % consumes the stream exactly as an isotropic one.
                    % Two-sided passes alternate by distance from the
                    % outermost, which is always on the + side whatever
                    % the depth's parity, so the sweep goes both ways.
                    /pqalong pqOneSided pqLayers 1 sub pqi sub 2 mod 0 eq or
                        { pqmag } { pqmag neg } ifelse def
                    /pqacross pqmag pqStretch div pkjit def
                    pqalong pqDirection cos mul pqacross pqDirection sin mul sub
                    pqalong pqDirection sin mul pqacross pqDirection cos mul add
                } {
                    /pqang pqi 137.5 mul 40 pkjit add def
                    pqang cos pqmag mul  pqang sin pqmag mul
                } ifelse
            } { 0 0 } ifelse
            % The core pass carries no color at all -- a 2-element plan
            % entry rather than a 5-element one. Its pickup mix is the
            % identity (f is 0 there), so setting the color would be a
            % no-op numerically while still switching the graphics state
            % into DeviceRGB, which would make the wrapped procedure run
            % in a different color space than the caller chose: a
            % DeviceGray procedure using `setcolor` raises typecheck
            % under pkwet but works when called directly, so /Soft 0
            % would not have been the plain call it claims to be (Codex
            % review). The softening passes do set an RGB color, because
            % mixing toward /Under is defined in RGB -- each is inside
            % its own gsave/grestore, so that never escapes.
            pqf 0 gt { pqbase pqUnder pqf pqPickup mul mix3 } if
        ]
        put
        % The same factor, published for loaded colours: 0 at the core,
        % exactly the t the line above hands mix3 elsewhere. No draw.
        pqks pqi pqf 0 gt { pqf pqPickup mul } { 0 } ifelse put
    } for
    % A procedural /Under (issue #179) re-colours the softening passes now,
    % after the plan's displacements are fixed and before any pass runs,
    % so the backdrop procedure sees the final geometry and the wrapped
    % procedure has not run yet.
    % (a single-layer call has only the core, which asks nothing, banded or not)
    pqunderp { pqbanded { pqLayers 1 gt { pqwetbands } if } { pqwetunder } ifelse } if
    % Outermost pass first, so each subsequent (less displaced, less
    % mixed) pass is laid over it and the caller's own color lands last,
    % exactly on the path.
    %
    % The plan and the procedure are read out of this invocation's frame
    % (pqframes pqdepth get) immediately before each pass and again on
    % the next, never held across the call. That is what lets a wrapped
    % procedure call pkwet itself -- softening an already-soft composite
    % mark is a reasonable thing to write -- without the inner call
    % overwriting the outer one's plan mid-loop. See the header for why
    % neither plain names nor the operand stack works here.
    pqmyframe 0 pqplan put
    % The pass mix, for a preset painting in *loaded* colours (`/Colors`,
    % issue #183 slice L3, docs/dev/design/PAINT_LOADING.md 3.4). Setting the colour
    % before a pass grades an inherited colour, but a loaded brush paints
    % in its own, so every pass would come out at full strength and a pull
    % would read as a hard block. `puwet` instead mixes each loaded colour
    % toward slot 5 by slot 4's factor for the pass in slot 6, at every
    % depth 1..pqdnow, outermost first -- the order nested calls mix an
    % inherited colour. /Under is copied, so a procedure that edits the
    % caller's array mid-call can't make puwet disagree with the plan.
    % Only puwet reads these slots, and only for depths that are live, so
    % a stale frame left by a finished or unwound call is never read and
    % the error path needs nothing new. Every existing render is unchanged.
    pqmyframe 4 pqks put
    pqmyframe 5 pqUnder aload pop 3 array astore put
    % A procedural /Under's per-pass colours, for puwet; pqwetunder left
    % them in pqunders. Slot 5 is then the unread white placeholder.
    pqunderp pqbanded not and { pqmyframe 7 pqunders put } if
    % A banded call's cells, clips and colours (issue #359); slot 8 stays null
    % for any other call, and the pass loop tests it.
    pqbanded pqLayers 1 gt and { pqmyframe 8 pqbrec put } if
    % Each pass runs under `stopped`, which is what makes this wrapper
    % safe to abandon. Three separate review findings were all the same
    % missing unwind-protect: a caught error leaked one graphics-state
    % frame per abandoned pass; it also left pqdepth naming the inner
    % frame, so an outer invocation's next pass executed the *inner*
    % call's procedure and turned a caught error into an uncaught one;
    % and a caller-owned `exit` was swallowed by this `for`. Catching
    % here closes all three, because every level unwinds exactly its own
    % one increment on the way past: an inner pkwet restores 2 -> 1
    % before re-raising, so the outer resumes with pqdepth naming its
    % own frame.
    %
    % Re-raising with `stop` is only usable because pscat now reports a
    % `stop` that reaches the top level, matching Ghostscript (issue
    % #142, fixed in this branch). Before that it ended execution
    % silently, which is why this was documented as a residual instead.
    %
    % `exit` needs the other branch. An `exit` inside the procedure hits
    % this `stopped` boundary and becomes `invalidexit` -- which is the
    % PLRM's own rule, not a quirk of this preset: `exit` looks for the
    % innermost enclosing loop, and a `stopped` context reached first is
    % an error. So a wrapped procedure cannot `exit` a loop the caller
    % owns through pkwet, and gets a loud `invalidexit` if it tries.
    %
    % An earlier round instead told the two apart by reading `errorname`
    % and re-running the exit outside this `for`. That is removed. It
    % inferred a control operation from a *name that can be stale*: a
    % procedure doing its own cleanup, `{ { exit } stopped { stop } if }`,
    % re-raises a `stop` while `$error /errorname` still reads
    % `/invalidexit`, so the heuristic turned that caller's deliberate
    % `stop` into an exit of the caller's loop and reported `false` to
    % their `stopped` (Codex review of PR #138, round 4). It also left
    % `$error /newerror` set on the forwarded path, so a later
    % control-flow `stop` raised the stale `invalidexit`. Blocking `exit`
    % the way the PLRM says is both simpler and honest; swallowing it
    % silently, which is what this did originally, is the one behavior
    % that would not be.
    pqLayers 1 sub -1 0 {
        % Slot 6 names the pass about to run, written in the same place
        % its colour is set.
        dup pqmyframe 6 3 -1 roll put
        pqmyframe 0 get exch get
        % A banded softening pass (issue #359) goes cell by cell; the core,
        % and every pass of any other call, takes the path that was here.
        dup length 2 gt pqmyframe 8 get null ne and
        { pop pqwetbandpass }
        {
        gsave
            dup length 2 gt
                { aload pop setrgbcolor translate }
                { aload pop translate }
            ifelse
            { pqmyframe 1 get exec } stopped
        {
            grestore
            pqdnow 1 sub pqdset
            stop
        } if
        grestore
        } ifelse
    } for
    pqdnow 1 sub pqdset
    % No trailing setrgbcolor: every pass ran inside its own
    % gsave/grestore, so the caller's color is already restored -- and
    % restoring it *by hand* would force the graphics state into
    % DeviceRGB, losing the caller's color space if they were painting in
    % DeviceGray or DeviceCMYK (Codex review).
} bind def

% --- watercolor wash and paper ground (issue #47) ---------------------
% opts pkwash -
% x y w h opts pkpaper -
%
% The watercolor medium, implementing the architecture issue #46's
% spike selected and recorded in `docs/dev/design/WATERCOLOR.md`: real alpha
% compositing in the renderer (`setalpha`/`setblendmode`, this repo's
% only non-PLRM painting operators) for the translucency and pooling,
% noise-perturbed *vector* geometry for the wet, irregular boundary --
% not a fluid solver, not a raster post-pass, both of which that spike
% weighed and rejected.
%
% `pkwash` takes the current path as the wet region and lays one wash
% into it: /Layers translucent passes, each with its own wobbled
% boundary (so the layers' edges don't coincide and the mark reads as
% pigment settling rather than one flat shape), an optional darker rim
% where the pigment pools as the edge dries, and optional granulation
% inside the wash. `pkpaper` lays the ground the wash sits on: a tone
% fill plus seeded grain and fibers.
%
% Color, as with every other preset in this file, comes from whatever
% the caller already set -- there is no color key.
%
% Wash order. Under the default /Blend /Normal, washes composite
% source-over: a later wash reads as fresher paint over a drier one,
% and the result depends on the order they were laid down. That is
% honest watercolor behavior, and `docs/dev/design/WATERCOLOR.md` flagged it as
% something this issue had to either document or give control over.
% Both: /Blend /Multiply is the order-independent alternative --
% multiplication commutes, so a stack of washes reads the same however
% it was ordered, at the cost of never lightening anything.
%
% Determinism. Every random choice here comes from this section's own
% generator (`pwrand`), not from `rand`/`frnd`. Two reasons, both
% deliberate departures from the sibling presets above:
%   * /Seed makes one wash reproducible on its own without disturbing
%     the caller's `rand` stream, so re-rolling a single wash's texture
%     doesn't change every mark placed after it. The obvious
%     alternative -- `rrand` to save the stream, `srand` to seed
%     locally, `srand` again to restore -- looks equivalent but is not:
%     under `--sweep-seed` (issue #21) an `srand` of a literal seed, as
%     /Seed would be, is intercepted and forced to the sweep's value,
%     so the local seed would no longer be the wash's own and /Seed
%     would stop telling one wash from another. (Since issue #202 the
%     restore itself is safe: a restore of an `rrand` value is left
%     alone under the override, so the caller's stream is not reset and
%     the never-called-srand diagnostic stays truthful. This bullet
%     rests on the local seed, not on the restore.)
%   * `pwrand` is a Schrage-decomposed minimal-standard LCG, whose
%     intermediate products are provably within 32-bit integer range,
%     so it produces the *same* texture under real Ghostscript (where
%     `rand` is a different generator entirely and integer overflow
%     silently promotes to real) as it does here.
% Without /Seed the generator is seeded from a single `rand` draw, so
% a wash still follows the caller's own `srand` the way the rest of
% this file does.
%
% Ghostscript. This is the first preset in this file that does not
% render the same way under plain `gs file.ps`: gs 10.x has no
% PostScript-callable alpha operator at all (tested directly in #46's
% spike -- `.setfillconstantalpha`, `.setopacityalpha`,
% `.setstrokeconstantalpha` and `setalpha` are all `where`-undefined;
% gs's real transparency lives inside its PDF interpreter). Rather
% than error there, `pkwash`/`pkpaper` probe once at load time
% (`pkalphaok`) and fall back to painting each mark in the *flattened*
% color that mark would have had over white paper -- 1-(1-c)*a per
% channel, accumulated across layers so the build-up survives. What
% that fallback cannot reproduce is anything underneath showing
% through: a wash over `pkpaper`'s ground, or two washes overlapping,
% goes flat instead of mixing. It is a legible approximation, not a
% substitute, and it prints a one-line diagnostic the first time it
% engages so a `gs` run says so out loud. Verified output for
% alpha-bearing content goes through `--pdf` (PDF's `ExtGState`
% `ca`/`CA`/`BM`, which gs *does* implement -- see `tests/pdf.rs`),
% not through `gs file.ps`.
%
% Resource limits, in the same spirit as `pkdry`'s and `pkspray`'s
% deposit budgets: /Layers is hard-capped at 1..8, the boundary sample
% count and the grain/fleck counts are each checked against a fixed
% budget before any drawing starts, and raster dimensions and output
% size are already bounded by pscat's own `--page` ceiling, so nothing
% here can turn an unattended render unbounded.
%
% Scratch prefix pw- (pkwash) and pv- (pkpaper) -- deliberately
% distinct from pk-/pn-/pb-/pz-/po-, see the file header.

% Whether the operators *exist*, as opposed to whether this library
% should *use* them -- two questions that look like one until someone
% sets the `pkalphaok` dial below to false by hand. Then they diverge,
% and everything that neutralizes ambient compositing has to key off
% this one: forcing the fallback in pscat is a request to preview what
% Ghostscript does, and gs cannot have an ambient `setalpha` leaking
% into the preview because gs has no `setalpha` at all (Codex review,
% PR #109). Immutable; nothing here ever assigns it again.
%
% Written probe-first with `exch def` rather than the more obvious
% `/name systemdict /setalpha known def`: build.rs's tag scanner models
% the depth-0 stack with a two-slot window, so a `def` whose key sits
% four objects back reads `/setalpha` as the name being defined (a real
% limitation of that parser, of the same family as issue #104's, not a
% quirk of this line). It stays on one line so the tag block is still
% directly above the `def`, which the scanner also requires.
% @internal
systemdict /setalpha known /pwhasalpha exch def

% @kind: Dial
% @summary: Whether pkwash/pkpaper composite for real. Set at load from whether pscat's setalpha operator exists; false under plain Ghostscript, where they fall back to flattening each mark against white. Set it false by hand to preview that fallback here.
% @example: pkalphaok { (real alpha) } { (flattened fallback) } ifelse print
/pkalphaok pwhasalpha def

% Set once the fallback has announced itself, so a piece with fifty
% washes prints the diagnostic once rather than fifty times.
% @internal
/pwwarned false def

% @internal
/pwwarn {
    pkalphaok not pwwarned not and {
        /pwwarned true def
        (paintkit: no setalpha here -- watercolor washes fall back to )
        print
        (flattening each mark against white; overlaps will not mix.\n)
        print
    } if
} bind def

% seed -   (seeds this section's own generator; see the header)
% Reduced mod 2147483646 *before* `abs` so the most negative
% representable integer can't overflow on its way through, and offset
% to 1..2147483646 because 0 is the Lehmer generator's one fixed point.
% @internal
/pwsrand {
    cvi 2147483646 mod abs 1 add
    /pwstate exch def
} bind def

% -> 0.0..1.0
% Schrage's decomposition of the minimal standard LCG (16807, 2^31-1):
% every intermediate product stays inside 32-bit signed range
% (16807*127772 and 2836*16806 are both under 2^31-1), which is what
% makes the sequence identical under Ghostscript's 32-bit integers
% rather than silently promoting to real and drifting.
% @internal
/pwrand {
    /pwq pwstate 127773 idiv def
    /pwstate pwstate 127773 mod 16807 mul pwq 2836 mul sub def
    pwstate 0 lt { /pwstate pwstate 2147483647 add def } if
    pwstate 2147483647 div
} bind def

% The wobble's harmonic ladder. *Integer* multiples of the subpath's
% normalized progress, so the displacement at t=1 equals the one at t=0
% and a closed wash boundary joins seamlessly instead of stepping at the
% seam. Spread roughly geometrically rather than 1,2,3,4,...: a wet edge
% has both slow lobes and fine crenulation, and evenly-spaced harmonics
% spend most of their amplitude in the middle of that range where
% neither reads.
% @internal
/pwfreqs [1 2 3 5 8 13] def

% -   (fresh wobble coefficients for one wash layer)
% Amplitude falls off as f^-0.8 (pink-ish, so the low harmonics carry
% the shape and the high ones only roughen it), each scaled by its own
% random factor, and the whole set normalized to sum to 1 -- which makes
% /Wet an honest bound on the displacement whatever the draws came out
% as, rather than a number whose meaning drifts with the coefficients.
% @internal
/pwwobinit {
    /pwamp pwfreqs length array def
    /pwpha pwfreqs length array def
    /pwsum 0 def
    0 1 pwfreqs length 1 sub {
        /pwk exch def
        /pwav
            1 pwfreqs pwk get 0.8 exp div
            0.55 pwrand 0.9 mul add mul
        def
        pwamp pwk pwav put
        pwpha pwk pwrand 360 mul put
        /pwsum pwsum pwav add def
    } for
    /pwanorm 1 pwsum div def
    % A small whole-layer offset on top of the per-point wobble. Two
    % layers whose boundaries differ only in *shape* still land on top
    % of each other almost everywhere; nudging each one bodily leaves
    % the thin crescents of single-layer coverage that read as the
    % internal contour lines a glazed wash actually has. Deliberately a
    % translation rather than an inward bias: "inward" would depend on
    % the caller's winding direction, which nothing here can know.
    /pwdx pwrand 0.6 mul 0.3 sub def
    /pwdy pwrand 0.6 mul 0.3 sub def
} bind def

% t subphase -> displacement in about -1..1
% @internal
/pwwob {
    /pwwsp exch def
    /pwwt exch def
    /pwacc 0 def
    0 1 pwfreqs length 1 sub {
        /pwk exch def
        /pwacc
            pwacc
            pwamp pwk get
            pwwt pwfreqs pwk get 360 mul mul
            pwpha pwk get add pwwsp add sin
            mul
            add
        def
    } for
    pwacc pwanorm mul
} bind def

% atotal -   (fallback only: the opaque color this mark would have had
% over white paper at total coverage `atotal`)
% @internal
/pwsetflat {
    /pwft exch def
    1 1 pwr sub pwft mul sub
    1 1 pwg sub pwft mul sub
    1 1 pwb sub pwft mul sub
    setrgbcolor
} bind def

% amark -   (paint for one mark of alpha `amark` laid over whatever
% this wash has already put down, tracked in pwcum)
% @internal
/pwmark {
    /pwmk exch def
    pkalphaok
        { pwmk setalpha }
        { 1 1 pwcum sub 1 pwmk sub mul sub pwsetflat }
    ifelse
} bind def

% -   (builds the current layer's wobbled boundary into the current
% path, one closed subpath per subpath of the sampled source)
% @internal
/pwbuild {
    /pwsub 0 def
    /pwstarted false def
    0 1 pwcount 1 sub {
        /pwi exch def
        pwraw pwi get
        dup 0 get /pwx exch def
        dup 1 get /pwy exch def
        dup 2 get /pwang exch def
        dup 3 get /pwt exch def
        5 get /pwae exch def
        /pwfirst pwae 1 and 1 eq def
        pwfirst {
            pwstarted { closepath } if
            /pwstarted true def
            /pwsub pwsub 1 add def
        } if
        % Displace along the local normal. Each subpath gets its own
        % phase offset, or every subpath of a multi-blob wash would
        % wobble in lockstep.
        /pwd pwt pwsub 137 mul pwwob pwwet mul def
        /pwnx pwang 90 add cos pwd mul pwx add pwdx pwwet mul add def
        /pwny pwang 90 add sin pwd mul pwy add pwdy pwwet mul add def
        pwfirst { pwnx pwny moveto } { pwnx pwny lineto } ifelse
    } for
    pwstarted { closepath } if
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Fills the current path as a translucent watercolor wash: layered passes each with their own wobbled wet boundary, an optional darker rim where pigment pools at the drying edge, and optional granulation. Color comes from whatever the caller already set. Needs pscat's setalpha; falls back to flattening against white elsewhere.
% @example: newpath 200 200 90 0 360 arc closepath << /Alpha 0.3 /Wet 6 /Bloom 0.6 >> pkwash
% @param: /Alpha [0..1] Per-layer wash strength, 0..1 (default 0.32)
% @param: /Layers [count] Translucent passes to build up, hard-capped 1..8 (default 2)
% @param: /Wet [points] Peak boundary spread in points -- how far the wet edge wanders off the path (default 2.5)
% @param: /Bloom [0..1] Edge-pooling strength, 0..1, the darker rim a drying wash leaves (default 0.45)
% @param: /BloomWidth [points] Rim width in points (default Wet*1.5, at least 2)
% @param: /Grain [0..1] Granulation inside the wash, 0..1 -- seeded pigment speckle (default 0)
% @param: /Blend [name] /Normal (source-over, order-dependent) or /Multiply (commutative, never lightens) (default /Normal)
% @param: /Pitch [points] Boundary sampling pitch; must be > 0 (default the path's own perimeter/180, clamped to 0.6..4)
% @param: /Seed [integer] Integer seed for this wash alone, leaving the caller's rand stream untouched (default: one rand draw from the caller's stream)
/pkwash {
    /pwopts exch def

    % Validation runs unconditionally, before the path is looked at --
    % same doctrine as pkribbon/pkspray above, and for the same reason
    % (an empty path must still reject a malformed dict). `/key load
    % xcheck` rejects procedure-valued options: no key here takes one,
    % and a name bound to a procedure auto-executes on bare reference.
    /pwalpha pwopts /Alpha 0.32 pkgetdef def
    /pwalpha load xcheck { pkwash-alpha-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pwalpha 0 lt pwalpha 1 gt or { pkwash-alpha-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pwlayers pwopts /Layers 2 pkgetdef def
    /pwlayers load xcheck { pkwash-layers-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /pwlayers pwlayers truncate cvi def
    pwlayers 1 lt pwlayers 8 gt or { pkwash-layers-must-be-1-to-8 } if

    /pwwet pwopts /Wet 2.5 pkgetdef def
    /pwwet load xcheck { pkwash-wet-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pwwet 0 lt { pkwash-wet-must-be-non-negative } if

    /pwbloom pwopts /Bloom 0.45 pkgetdef def
    /pwbloom load xcheck { pkwash-bloom-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pwbloom 0 lt pwbloom 1 gt or { pkwash-bloom-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pwbloomw
        pwopts /BloomWidth
        pwwet 1.5 mul dup 2 lt { pop 2 } if
        pkgetdef
    def
    /pwbloomw load xcheck { pkwash-bloomwidth-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pwbloomw 0 le { pkwash-bloomwidth-must-be-positive } if

    /pwgrain pwopts /Grain 0 pkgetdef def
    /pwgrain load xcheck { pkwash-grain-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pwgrain 0 lt pwgrain 1 gt or { pkwash-grain-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pwblend pwopts /Blend /Normal pkgetdef def
    % The executable-value guard has to come *before* the first bare
    % `pwblend` reference, not after: a name bound to a procedure
    % auto-executes on bare reference (pkgetdef's own header documents
    % the trap), so checking the value first would already have run it.
    /pwblend load xcheck { pkwash-blend-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pwblend /Normal ne pwblend /Multiply ne and
        { pkwash-blend-must-be-Normal-or-Multiply } if

    % Seed this wash's own generator before anything draws, so /Seed
    % and the caller's-stream default reach every later pwrand call
    % identically.
    pwopts /Seed known
        { pwopts /Seed get pwsrand }
        { rand pwsrand }
    ifelse

    pwwarn

    % An explicitly supplied /Pitch is validated *here*, before any
    % traversal: the measurement pass below is O(length/8), and making
    % malformed input pay for it before being rejected is backwards
    % (Codex review, PR #109, round 5). Only the *derived* default
    % needs the measured length, and that one can't be invalid -- it's
    % clamped into 0.6..4 by construction.
    /pwpitchgiven pwopts /Pitch known def
    pwpitchgiven {
        /pwpitch pwopts /Pitch get def
        /pwpitch load xcheck { pkwash-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
        pwpitch 0 le { pkwash-pitch-must-be-positive } if
    } if

    % Pass 0: arc length and subpath count. `sp` sums to each subpath's
    % true length whatever pitch it was walked at, so a coarse walk
    % measures the path as exactly as a fine one would, for a fraction
    % of the calls.
    %
    % Even this pass is O(length/8), so it carries its own abort: a
    % path long enough to blow that bound would take an unbounded time
    % just to *measure*, before any of the budgets below could ever
    % have their say. Raising from inside the callback unwinds the walk
    % immediately -- walkpath has no early-exit of its own (Codex
    % review, PR #109).
    /pwlen 0 def
    /pwsubs 0 def
    /pwmeas 0 def
    8 {
        /pwae exch def /pwsp exch def pop pop pop pop
        /pwlen pwlen pwsp add def
        pwae 1 and 1 eq { /pwsubs pwsubs 1 add def } if
        /pwmeas pwmeas 1 add def
        pwmeas 40000 gt { pkwash-path-too-long-for-safety-limit } if
    } walkpath

    % A default pitch derived from the path's own size: a wash is a
    % region, not a stroke, so its scale is whatever the caller drew --
    % a fixed pitch either polygonalizes a small blob or samples a
    % page-sized one thousands of times.
    pwpitchgiven not {
        /pwpitch
            pwlen 180 div
            dup 0.6 lt { pop 0.6 } if
            dup 4 gt { pop 4 } if
        def
    } if

    % The budget is enforced *arithmetically*, from the length already
    % measured, before any walk at `pwpitch` happens. Counting first and
    % checking after -- the obvious shape, and what this did originally
    % -- doesn't bound anything: at `/Pitch 0.0001` the counting walk
    % itself is the unbounded work, and the check it eventually reaches
    % never runs (Codex review, PR #109).
    %
    % walkpath emits floor(L/pitch)+1 regular stops per subpath plus at
    % most one guaranteed endpoint, so length/pitch + 2 per subpath is a
    % true upper bound (a sum of floors never exceeds the floor of the
    % sum); the slack covers float rounding. Sizing the collection array
    % from that bound rather than from an exact count is what lets this
    % walk the path *twice* rather than three times -- parity with
    % pkribbon and pkspray, which matters because walkpath's own
    % per-segment traversal cost is paid once per pass.
    /pwcap pwlen pwpitch div pwsubs 2 mul add 8 add truncate cvi def
    pwcap 20000 gt
        { pkwash-boundary-sample-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if

    % Collect into that preallocated array -- fill-as-you-walk is the
    % only shape that survives walkpath's cross-subpath stack discipline
    % (same as pkribbon's pkraw and pkspray's pzraw). If the bound above
    % were ever wrong, `put` raises rangecheck rather than corrupting
    % anything.
    /pwraw pwcap array def
    /pwci 0 def
    pwpitch {
        6 array astore pwraw pwci 3 -1 roll put
        /pwci pwci 1 add def
    } walkpath
    /pwcount pwci def

    pwcount 0 gt {
        % The granulation region and its budget are both settled here,
        % before the first fill. Deriving them from the *source* path's
        % bbox inflated by the largest displacement a boundary can take
        % (the wobble is normalized to at most /Wet, the per-layer
        % offset to at most 0.3 of it) rather than from a built
        % boundary is what makes that possible -- and it means a
        % rejected grain count leaves the canvas untouched, instead of
        % aborting on top of layers and bloom already painted (Codex
        % review, PR #109). The scatter is clipped to the wash either
        % way, so a slightly generous region costs nothing but a few
        % rejected candidate points.
        pathbbox
        /pwy1 exch def /pwx1 exch def /pwy0 exch def /pwx0 exch def
        /pwinf pwwet 1.3 mul 1 add def
        /pwx0 pwx0 pwinf sub def
        /pwy0 pwy0 pwinf sub def
        /pwx1 pwx1 pwinf add def
        /pwy1 pwy1 pwinf add def
        % One mark per ~14 square points at /Grain 1 -- dense enough to
        % read as tooth in the paper rather than as scattered dots.
        /pwn
            pwgrain pwx1 pwx0 sub mul pwy1 pwy0 sub mul 14 div
            truncate cvi
        def
        pwn 0 lt { /pwn 0 def } if
        pwn 120000 gt { pkwash-grain-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if

        currentrgbcolor /pwb exch def /pwg exch def /pwr exch def
        gsave
        % Neutralize first, then opt in. Neutralizing keys off
        % `pwhasalpha` (do the operators exist?) rather than
        % `pkalphaok` (should this wash use them?), so a forced-fallback
        % preview is a faithful one: gs has no ambient alpha or blend to
        % inherit, so neither may this. Then, when the wash really is
        % compositing, its own /Blend is set unconditionally rather than
        % only when it's /Multiply -- a caller who left
        % `/Multiply setblendmode` in force would otherwise get a
        % Multiply wash out of `/Blend /Normal`, making a documented
        % option depend on incoming graphics state (Codex review,
        % PR #109, rounds 2 and 4). All inside the gsave, so the
        % caller's own state comes back either way.
        pwhasalpha {
            1 setalpha /Normal setblendmode
            pkalphaok { pwblend setblendmode } if
        } if

        % The layers. pwcum tracks the coverage already laid down, which
        % the alpha path ignores (the renderer is doing the real
        % compositing) and the flattened fallback needs in order to
        % reproduce the build-up.
        /pwcum 0 def
        1 1 pwlayers {
            pop
            pwwobinit
            pwalpha pwmark
            newpath pwbuild fill
            /pwcum 1 1 pwcum sub 1 pwalpha sub mul sub def
        } for

        % Edge pooling. The rim is a stroke of the boundary clipped to
        % the wash itself, so only its inward half survives -- a band
        % that follows the wet edge exactly and cannot leak outside it.
        % Two passes, wide-and-faint under narrow-and-stronger, so the
        % rim grades inward instead of reading as an outline. The
        % boundary is whatever the last layer built: the rim belongs to
        % the edge that is actually visible.
        pwbloom 0 gt {
            newpath pwbuild
            gsave
                clip
                % Cap, join, width *and* dash: an inherited dash pattern
                % would turn the pooled edge into a dashed rim, and the
                % rim is the one mark here whose whole job is to be
                % continuous (Codex review, PR #109).
                1 setlinejoin 1 setlinecap [] 0 setdash
                newpath pwbuild
                pwbloomw 2 mul setlinewidth
                pwalpha pwbloom mul 0.55 mul pwmark
                stroke
                newpath pwbuild
                pwbloomw 0.8 mul setlinewidth
                pwalpha pwbloom mul pwmark
                stroke
            grestore
        } if

        % Granulation: pigment settling into the paper's tooth, as
        % seeded dots clipped to the wash. One accumulated path and one
        % fill, not a fill per dot.
        pwgrain 0 gt {
            newpath pwbuild
            pwn 0 gt {
                gsave
                    clip
                    pwalpha pwgrain mul 0.8 mul pwmark
                    newpath
                    1 1 pwn {
                        pop
                        /pwgx pwx0 pwrand pwx1 pwx0 sub mul add def
                        /pwgy pwy0 pwrand pwy1 pwy0 sub mul add def
                        /pwgr 0.35 pwrand 0.95 mul add def
                        pwgx pwgr add pwgy moveto
                        pwgx pwgy pwgr 0 360 arc
                        closepath
                    } for
                    fill
                grestore
            } if
        } if

        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% -   (pkpaper's own rectangle as the current path; needed twice now,
% once to fill the ground and once to clip the texture to it)
% @internal
/pvrect {
    newpath
    pvx pvy moveto pvw 0 rlineto 0 pvh rlineto pvw neg 0 rlineto
    closepath
} bind def

% tone-component -> the opaque color one grain mark flattens to
% Only reached when there's no real alpha to composite with. The
% backdrop here is not a guess the way `pkwash`'s white-paper
% assumption is -- a grain mark sits directly on a ground this same
% procedure just painted -- so both blend modes have an exact
% closed form, and /Blend has to be honored rather than collapsed
% into the source-over one (Codex review, PR #109, round 5):
%   Normal:   t*(1 - depth*a)
%   Multiply: t*(1 - a + a*t*(1-depth))   -- the darkened source is
%             multiplied by the tone it sits on before compositing
% @internal
/pvflat {
    /pvfcomp exch def
    pvblend /Multiply eq
        {
            pvfcomp
            1 pvalpha sub
            pvalpha pvfcomp mul 1 pvdepth sub mul add
            mul
        }
        { pvfcomp 1 pvdepth pvalpha mul sub mul }
    ifelse
} bind def

% cx cy len wid ang -   (adds one rotated fleck to the current path)
% Built from explicit corner arithmetic rather than translate/rotate,
% so it costs no CTM churn inside a loop that runs thousands of times
% and leaves the caller's coordinate system exactly as it found it.
% @internal
/pvfleck {
    /pvfa exch def
    /pvfw exch 2 div def
    /pvfl exch 2 div def
    /pvfy exch def
    /pvfx exch def
    /pvfc pvfa cos def
    /pvfs pvfa sin def
    pvfx pvfl pvfc mul sub pvfw pvfs mul add
    pvfy pvfl pvfs mul sub pvfw pvfc mul sub moveto
    pvfx pvfl pvfc mul add pvfw pvfs mul add
    pvfy pvfl pvfs mul add pvfw pvfc mul sub lineto
    pvfx pvfl pvfc mul add pvfw pvfs mul sub
    pvfy pvfl pvfs mul add pvfw pvfc mul add lineto
    pvfx pvfl pvfc mul sub pvfw pvfs mul sub
    pvfy pvfl pvfs mul sub pvfw pvfc mul add lineto
    closepath
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Lays a paper ground for watercolor: a tone fill over the given rectangle plus seeded grain speckle and fiber flecks. Paint it first; washes go over it. Uses setalpha where available and falls back to flattened tones elsewhere.
% @example: 0 0 612 792 << /Tone [0.97 0.955 0.92] /Grain 0.6 >> pkpaper
% @param: /Tone [rgb array] Paper color as a 3-element array (default [0.965 0.95 0.915])
% @param: /Grain [0..1] Grain density, 0..1 (default 0.5)
% @param: /Alpha [0..1] Strength of each grain mark, 0..1 (default 0.18)
% @param: /Depth [0..1] How much darker than the tone a grain mark is, 0..1 (default 0.28)
% @param: /Fiber [0..1] Fraction of grain marks drawn as elongated paper fibers rather than specks, 0..1 (default 0.18)
% @param: /Blend [name] /Normal or /Multiply, for the grain marks (default /Normal)
% @param: /Seed [integer] Integer seed for this ground alone, leaving the caller's rand stream untouched (default: one rand draw from the caller's stream)
/pkpaper {
    /pvopts exch def
    /pvh exch def
    /pvw exch def
    /pvy exch def
    /pvx exch def

    /pvtone pvopts /Tone [0.965 0.95 0.915] pkgetdef def
    % Before any bare `pvtone`, and before the type check specifically:
    % a procedure *is* arraytype, so the type check alone would accept
    % one -- after having already executed it on the bare reference.
    /pvtone load xcheck { pkpaper-tone-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pvtone type /arraytype ne { pkpaper-tone-must-be-an-array } if
    pvtone length 3 ne { pkpaper-tone-must-have-three-components } if
    % The guards above cover the array; its *elements* need their own.
    % `/Tone [{ erasepage 0.9 } 0.8 0.7]` passes every check above, and
    % the procedure then runs the moment the name it gets bound to is
    % referenced -- a malformed color option performing arbitrary side
    % effects instead of raising a type error (Codex review, PR #109).
    % `get` retrieves without executing, so the check itself is safe.
    0 1 2 {
        pvtone exch get
        dup xcheck { pkpaper-tone-components-must-not-be-procedures } if
        dup type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
            { pkpaper-tone-components-must-be-numbers } if
        pop
    } for

    /pvgrain pvopts /Grain 0.5 pkgetdef def
    /pvgrain load xcheck { pkpaper-grain-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pvgrain 0 lt pvgrain 1 gt or { pkpaper-grain-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pvalpha pvopts /Alpha 0.18 pkgetdef def
    /pvalpha load xcheck { pkpaper-alpha-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pvalpha 0 lt pvalpha 1 gt or { pkpaper-alpha-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pvdepth pvopts /Depth 0.28 pkgetdef def
    /pvdepth load xcheck { pkpaper-depth-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pvdepth 0 lt pvdepth 1 gt or { pkpaper-depth-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pvfiber pvopts /Fiber 0.18 pkgetdef def
    /pvfiber load xcheck { pkpaper-fiber-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pvfiber 0 lt pvfiber 1 gt or { pkpaper-fiber-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    /pvblend pvopts /Blend /Normal pkgetdef def
    /pvblend load xcheck { pkpaper-blend-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pvblend /Normal ne pvblend /Multiply ne and
        { pkpaper-blend-must-be-Normal-or-Multiply } if

    pvopts /Seed known
        { pvopts /Seed get pwsrand }
        { rand pwsrand }
    ifelse

    pwwarn

    pvw 0 le pvh 0 le or not {
        /pvtr pvtone 0 get def
        /pvtg pvtone 1 get def
        /pvtb pvtone 2 get def

        % Settled before the tone fill, not after it: a budget checked
        % once the ground is already down leaves a half-painted canvas
        % behind on rejection, which is exactly what the pre-draw
        % validation contract this file states is supposed to prevent
        % (Codex review, PR #109).
        /pvn pvgrain pvw pvh mul mul 55 div truncate cvi def
        pvn 0 lt { /pvn 0 def } if
        pvn 60000 gt { pkpaper-grain-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if

        gsave
        % The ground is a ground: opaque and source-over, whatever the
        % caller had set. /Alpha and /Blend are documented as *grain*
        % options, so inheriting an ambient `setalpha`/`setblendmode`
        % here would make the paper itself translucent with nothing in
        % the contract saying so. Keyed off `pwhasalpha`, not
        % `pkalphaok`, so it also holds in a forced-fallback preview --
        % see that dial's own header (Codex review, PR #109, rounds 3
        % and 4).
        pwhasalpha { 1 setalpha /Normal setblendmode } if
        pvrect
        pvtr pvtg pvtb setrgbcolor
        fill

        pvgrain 0 gt pvalpha 0 gt and {
            pvn 0 gt {
                % A grain mark is the tone darkened by /Depth. With real
                % alpha that's painted at /Alpha over the ground; in the
                % fallback the two collapse to one opaque color, which
                % for a mark directly on the ground is exact rather than
                % approximate -- the fallback only loses fidelity where
                % something *underneath* would have shown through.
                pkalphaok {
                    % Explicit, for the same reason as pkwash's above.
                    pvblend setblendmode
                    pvalpha setalpha
                    pvtr 1 pvdepth sub mul
                    pvtg 1 pvdepth sub mul
                    pvtb 1 pvdepth sub mul
                    setrgbcolor
                } {
                    pvtr pvflat pvtg pvflat pvtb pvflat setrgbcolor
                } ifelse
                % Clipped to the same rectangle the ground filled:
                % centers are sampled right up to the edges, and a
                % speck reaches 0.8 points past its center while a
                % fiber reaches several, so an inset or adjacent
                % `pkpaper` would otherwise scatter texture onto its
                % neighbour (Codex review, PR #109, round 5).
                gsave
                pvrect clip
                newpath
                1 1 pvn {
                    pop
                    /pvgx pvx pwrand pvw mul add def
                    /pvgy pvy pwrand pvh mul add def
                    pwrand pvfiber lt {
                        pvgx pvgy
                        1.5 pwrand 4 mul add
                        0.3 pwrand 0.35 mul add
                        pwrand 180 mul
                        pvfleck
                    } {
                        /pvgr 0.3 pwrand 0.5 mul add def
                        pvgx pvgr add pvgy moveto
                        pvgx pvgy pvgr 0 360 arc
                        closepath
                    } ifelse
                } for
                fill
                grestore
            } if
        } if
        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% --- foliage / dab brush (issue #117) ----------------------------------
% opts pkdab -
% Clustered, irregular paint deposits -- bushes, leafy masses, clumps of
% highlight, any vegetation-like texture. The tool is a loaded brush
% pounced onto the canvas rather than dragged, so there is no ribbon:
% the current path is a *spine*, and every stop along it seeds a cluster
% of blobby dabs scattered within /Scatter of it. A subpath that is a
% single point is a standalone clump (/Clump dabs), the usual way to
% paint one bush or one highlight patch. Closed subpaths are treated as
% their outline, not filled -- there is no portable in-fill test, so a
% mass is built by pouncing along a spine through it.
%
% Layering is the point of the tool, and all three layers come from ONE
% set of dab geometry: each dab is generated once (centre, radius,
% outline) and then painted as a darker underlayer (/Shadow, nudged away
% from /Light), the body in the caller's colour, and a smaller lighter
% dab (/Highlight, nudged toward /Light) on the dabs facing the light.
% Drawing fresh positions per layer would scatter highlights off the mass
% they are meant to light. /Light is a fixed angle in user space, like
% pknib's /Angle: foliage is lit by the sun, not by the stroke direction.
%
% Determinism: no library seed; reproducible under the caller's own
% `N srand`. Every dab slot draws a fixed 13 values from the stream
% whether or not it ends up painted, so /Charge, /Shadow, /Highlight,
% /Light and /ColorJitter re-shape the mark without re-rolling it. What
% DOES change the stream is whatever changes the dab count or layout:
% /Density, /Clump, /Size, /Pitch and the path itself.
%
% Density is per one /Size of travel, scaled by each stop's own spacing
% and carried in an accumulator reset per subpath (pkspray's design), so
% /Pitch does not change coverage. A hard budget on the total dab count
% raises pkdab-dab-count-exceeds-safety-limit before anything is painted.
%
% Scratch prefix pd- (pkdab, pddab).

% Draws one smooth blobby dab from the record in pdd at (pdcx, pdcy) with
% mean radius pdr. Seven vertices, each at its own radius factor, joined
% by quadratic-as-cubic arcs through the edge midpoints so the outline
% has no corners (a straight 7-gon reads as a gem at any real size).
% @internal
/pddab {
    0 1 6 {
        /pdk exch def
        /pdang pdk 360 7 div mul pdd 5 get 360 mul add def
        /pdrad pdr pdd pdk 6 add get mul def
        pdpx pdk pdcx pdrad pdang cos mul add put
        pdpy pdk pdcy pdrad pdang sin mul add put
    } for
    pdpx 6 get pdpx 0 get add 2 div
    pdpy 6 get pdpy 0 get add 2 div moveto
    0 1 6 {
        /pdk exch def
        /pdkp pdk 6 add 7 mod def
        /pdkn pdk 1 add 7 mod def
        /pdpkx pdpx pdk get def /pdpky pdpy pdk get def
        /pdm0x pdpx pdkp get pdpkx add 2 div def
        /pdm0y pdpy pdkp get pdpky add 2 div def
        /pdm1x pdpkx pdpx pdkn get add 2 div def
        /pdm1y pdpky pdpy pdkn get add 2 div def
        pdm0x pdpkx pdm0x sub 2 mul 3 div add
        pdm0y pdpky pdm0y sub 2 mul 3 div add
        pdm1x pdpkx pdm1x sub 2 mul 3 div add
        pdm1y pdpky pdm1y sub 2 mul 3 div add
        pdm1x pdm1y curveto
    } for
    closepath
} bind def

%%SelfTest: pkdab-rejects-malformed-options
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Size 0 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-size-must-be-positive
%   (/Size 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Size { 5 } >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-size-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Size { 5 }) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Scatter 0 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-scatter-must-be-positive
%   (/Scatter 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Density -1 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-density-must-be-non-negative
%   (/Density -1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Clump 0 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-clump-must-be-1-to-400
%   (/Clump 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Clump 2.5 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-clump-must-be-1-to-400
%   (/Clump 2.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Irregular 2 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-irregular-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Irregular 2) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Charge 1.5 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-charge-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Charge 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Shadow -0.1 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-shadow-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Shadow -0.1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Highlight 2 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-highlight-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Highlight 2) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Pitch 0 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-pitch-must-be-positive
%   (/Pitch 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ColorJitter 3 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colorjitter-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/ColorJitter 3) mustguard
%
%   % The documented form must still work.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Size 4 >> pkdab }
%   (a plain /Size) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkdab-enforces-its-dab-budget
%   % The budget is checked in the counting pass, before any painting.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto << /Size 0.5 /Density 500 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-dab-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (an uncapped density on a long spine) mustguard
%
%   % Round 2: samples are bounded on their own, not only via dabs.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100 0 lineto << /Density 0 /Pitch 0.0005 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-sample-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a tiny pitch at density 0) mustguard
%
%   % Charge 1 is full coverage even when frnd lands on exactly 1.0
%   % (seed 5659, Clump 1, painted nothing before the clamp).
%   { 0 0 0 setrgbcolor 5659 srand newpath 10 10 moveto
%     << /Clump 1 /Charge 1 >> pkdab }
%   (a full charge) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkdab-scatter-and-its-deprecated-spread-alias
%   % /Spread is the deprecated name for /Scatter (issue #141) and is
%   % still read, through the same guard, when /Scatter is absent.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Spread 0 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-scatter-must-be-positive
%   (/Spread 0, the deprecated alias) mustguard
%
%   % When both are given /Scatter wins, and /Spread is not even validated.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Scatter 5 /Spread 0 >> pkdab }
%   (/Scatter 5 with a zero /Spread) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Scatter 0 /Spread 5 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-scatter-must-be-positive
%   (/Scatter 0 with a valid /Spread) mustguard
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkdab-loaded-colors-and-hue-keys
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor 5 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-highlightcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/HighlightColor 5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor { 0.5 0.5 0.5 } >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-highlightcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/HighlightColor as a procedure) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor [ 0.5 0.5 ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-highlightcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/HighlightColor with two components) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor [ 0.5 0.5 1.5 ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-highlightcolor-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/HighlightColor with a component of 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor [ 0.5 (x) 0.5 ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-highlightcolor-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/HighlightColor with a string component) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColor 5 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-shadowcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/ShadowColor 5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColor { 0.5 0.5 0.5 } >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-shadowcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/ShadowColor as a procedure) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColor [ 0.5 0.5 ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-shadowcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (/ShadowColor with two components) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColor [ 0.5 0.5 1.5 ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-shadowcolor-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/ShadowColor with a component of 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColor [ 0.5 (x) 0.5 ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-shadowcolor-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/ShadowColor with a string component) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColour [ 1 1 0 ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/HighlightColour) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ShadowColour [ 0 0 1 ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/ShadowColour) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor [ 1 1 0 ] /ShadowColor [ 0 0.2 0.6 ] >> pkdab }
%   (both mix targets) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /HighlightColor [ 1 0 0 ] /Highlight 0 >> pkdab }
%   (a mix target with its layer off) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colours [ [ 1 0 0 ] ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/Colours) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colors 5 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (/Colors 5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colors { [ 0 0 0 ] } >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (/Colors as a procedure) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colors [ ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (/Colors with no colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colors [ 9 { [ 0 0 0 ] } repeat ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (/Colors with nine colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 ] ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays
%   (/Colors with a two-component entry) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 1.5 ] ] >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colors-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Colors with a component of 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /ColorMix 0.5 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colormix-needs-colors
%   (/ColorMix without /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColorMix 1.5 >> pkdab }
%   /pkdab-colormix-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/ColorMix 1.5) mustguard
%
%   % 1, 2 and 8 colors are fine, and /Light is pkdab's own: it never needs /Colors.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 1 0 0 ] ] >> pkdab }
%   (1 color) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colors [ [ 1 0 0 ] [ 0 0 1 ] ] /ColorMix 0 >> pkdab }
%   (2 colors) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Colors [ 8 { [ 0.5 0.5 0.5 ] } repeat ] /ColorMix 1 /Light 45 >> pkdab }
%   (8 colors with /Light) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto << /Light 45 >> pkdab }
%   (/Light alone) mustpass
%
%%EndSelfTest
%
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A foliage / dab brush: clustered, irregular blobs of paint pounced along the current path (a spine), for bushes, leafy masses and vegetation texture. Each dab is generated once and painted in up to three layers -- a darker underlayer, the body in the current color (or, with /Colors, one loaded color per dab), and lighter highlight dabs on the lit side -- so highlights always sit on the mass they light. /HighlightColor and /ShadowColor mix the lit and shaded layers toward a hue instead of white and black. A single-point subpath is a standalone clump. Deterministic under the caller's own srand; bounded by a dab-count budget; respects clip like every other preset.
% @example: newpath 80 60 moveto 180 90 280 60 380 70 curveto << /Size 9 /Scatter 26 /Highlight 0.6 >> pkdab
% @param: /Size [points] Mean dab radius in points; must be > 0 (default 6)
% @param: /Scatter [points] Radius in points of the cluster scattered around each stop of the path; must be > 0. Formerly /Spread, still accepted as a deprecated alias; /Scatter wins if both are given (default Size*2.5)
% @param: /Density [count per /Size] Mean dabs per one /Size of travel along the path; must be >= 0 (0 paints nothing along lines); bounded by the dab-count budget (default 3)
% @param: /Clump [count] Dab count for a single-point subpath, whole number 1..400 (default 18)
% @param: /Irregular [0..1] how far each dab's outline departs from a circle (default 0.5)
% @param: /Charge [0..1] how much paint the brush is loaded with -- coverage only: the fraction of dabs that land at all (default 0.9)
% @param: /Shadow [0..1] strength of the darker underlayer pushed away from the light; 0 paints none (default 0.4)
% @param: /Highlight [0..1] strength and reach of the lighter dabs on the lit side; 0 paints none (default 0.4)
% @param: /Light [degrees] Direction the light comes from, in degrees counterclockwise from +x in user space (default 120, up and to the left)
% @param: /Pitch [points] walkpath sampling pitch for the spine; must be > 0 (default Size, capped at 6)
% @param: /ColorJitter [0..1] per-dab tint variation around the current color (default 0.08)
% @param: /Colors [array of rgb arrays] 1 to 8 loaded colors, evenly spaced along the load; each dab takes one, at the position its existing outline-phase draw gives it, so a clump mixes the colors in proportion to their share of the list ([dark dark dark yellow] is mostly dark). When set, the current color is not used for the mark, though /ColorJitter still varies it. Takes no random draw, so it recolors a mark without moving it. An entry may be a bare pigment name such as /sapgreen (see PigmentNames), which stands for that pigment; a pigmix spec goes through pigcolors (default unset)
% @param: /ColorMix [0..1] with /Colors, how far neighbouring colors have blended on the brush: 0 gives each dab one of two neighbouring colors outright, 1 a smooth gradient between them (default 0.3)
% @param: /HighlightColor [rgb array] the color the lighter dabs on the lit side mix toward, instead of white; /Highlight still sets how far; a bare pigment name such as /titaniumwhite stands for that pigment, and a pigmix spec goes through pigcolor (default unset: toward white)
% @param: /ShadowColor [rgb array] the color the darker underlayer mixes toward, instead of black; /Shadow still sets how far; a bare pigment name such as /titaniumwhite stands for that pigment, and a pigmix spec goes through pigcolor (default unset: toward black)
/pkdab {
    /pdopts exch def
    /pdsize pdopts /Size 6 pkgetdef def
    /pdsize load xcheck { pkdab-size-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pdsize 0 le { pkdab-size-must-be-positive } if
    % /Scatter, or the deprecated /Spread alias when /Scatter is absent
    % (issue #141: /Spread elsewhere in this file is a fraction of /Width).
    % The inner pkgetdef resolves the alias, so /Scatter wins.
    /pdspread pdopts /Scatter pdopts /Spread pdsize 2.5 mul pkgetdef pkgetdef def
    /pdspread load xcheck { pkdab-scatter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pdspread 0 le { pkdab-scatter-must-be-positive } if
    /pddens pdopts /Density 3 pkgetdef def
    /pddens load xcheck { pkdab-density-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pddens 0 lt { pkdab-density-must-be-non-negative } if
    /pdclump pdopts /Clump 18 pkgetdef def
    /pdclump load xcheck { pkdab-clump-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pdclump 1 lt pdclump 400 gt or pdclump truncate pdclump ne or
        { pkdab-clump-must-be-1-to-400 } if
    /pdirr pdopts /Irregular 0.5 pkgetdef def
    /pdirr load xcheck { pkdab-irregular-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pdirr 0 lt pdirr 1 gt or { pkdab-irregular-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pdcharge pdopts /Charge 0.9 pkgetdef def
    /pdcharge load xcheck { pkdab-charge-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pdcharge 0 lt pdcharge 1 gt or { pkdab-charge-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pdshadow pdopts /Shadow 0.4 pkgetdef def
    /pdshadow load xcheck { pkdab-shadow-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pdshadow 0 lt pdshadow 1 gt or { pkdab-shadow-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pdhl pdopts /Highlight 0.4 pkgetdef def
    /pdhl load xcheck { pkdab-highlight-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pdhl 0 lt pdhl 1 gt or { pkdab-highlight-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /pdlight pdopts /Light 120 pkgetdef def
    /pdlight load xcheck { pkdab-light-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /pdpitch pdopts /Pitch pdsize dup 6 gt { pop 6 } if pkgetdef def
    /pdpitch load xcheck { pkdab-pitch-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pdpitch 0 le { pkdab-pitch-must-be-positive } if
    /pdcj pdopts /ColorJitter 0.08 pkgetdef def
    /pdcj load xcheck { pkdab-colorjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pdcj 0 lt pdcj 1 gt or { pkdab-colorjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    % The load and the two colours the layers mix toward: each null when its key
    % is unset, so every new branch below is `... null ne` and an unset key never
    % reaches it. `puparse` is safe on the unset path (it checks the spellings and
    % /ColorMix without /Colors, and never reads /Light), which is why it is called
    % always; `pulightneedscolors` is not called, because /Light is pkdab's own.
    pdopts (pkdab) puparse /pdColorRec exch def
    % The two mix targets are absolute colours, so they are graded for the running
    % `pkwet` pass here, once (`puwet` is affine and the identity outside a pull):
    % mixing a graded body toward an ungraded target would leave the lit and shaded
    % fractions solid in the outer passes.
    pdopts /HighlightColor (pkdab-highlightcolor) puhue
        dup null ne { aload pop puwet 3 array astore } if /pdHighlightColor exch def
    pdopts /ShadowColor (pkdab-shadowcolor) puhue
        dup null ne { aload pop puwet 3 array astore } if /pdShadowColor exch def

    % Counting pass with the budget check inside the callback. A single
    % point arrives with sp = 0, so it gets its clump added explicitly.
    /pdcount 0 def /pdest 0 def
    pdpitch {
        /pdae exch def /pdasp exch def pop pop pop pop
        pdae 3 eq {
            /pdest pdest pdclump add def
        } {
            % The accumulator carries its fraction across stops, so a
            % whole subpath can round up by at most one dab (the one
            % guaranteed below), not one per stop.
            /pdest pdest pddens pdasp mul pdsize div add
                pdae 1 and 1 eq { 1 add } if def
        } ifelse
        pdest 20000 gt { pkdab-dab-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
        % The dab budget alone does not bound the stored samples: at
        % /Density 0 and a tiny /Pitch nothing is painted, yet every stop
        % is still recorded below.
        /pdcount pdcount 1 add def
        pdcount 100000 gt { pkdab-sample-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
    } walkpath

    /pdraw pdcount array def
    /pdci 0 def
    pdpitch {
        6 array astore pdraw pdci 3 -1 roll put
        /pdci pdci 1 add def
    } walkpath

    pdcount 0 gt {
        gsave
        /pdbase [ currentrgbcolor ] def
        /pdlx pdlight cos def /pdly pdlight sin def
        /pdpx 7 array def /pdpy 7 array def

        % Generate every dab once.
        /pddabs pdest ceiling cvi 2 add array def
        /pdn 0 def
        /pdacc 0 def
        0 1 pdcount 1 sub {
            pdraw exch get
            dup 0 get /pdx exch def
            dup 1 get /pdy exch def
            dup 4 get /pdsp exch def
            5 get /pdae exch def
            pdae 3 eq {
                /pdnd pdclump def
            } {
                pdae 1 and 1 eq { /pdacc 0 def /pdgot false def } if
                /pdacc pdacc pddens pdsp mul pdsize div add def
                /pdnd pdacc truncate cvi def
                /pdacc pdacc pdnd sub def
                % A stroke shorter than Size/Density accumulates under one
                % dab and would paint nothing at all, which a twig or a
                % tap of the brush must not do: a non-empty subpath always
                % lands at least one dab, at its last stop. Density 0 is
                % the caller asking for no dabs along lines, and stays so.
                pdnd 0 gt { /pdgot true def } if
                pdae 2 and 2 eq pdgot not and pddens 0 gt and
                    { /pdnd 1 def } if
            } ifelse
            1 1 pdnd {
                pop
                /pdd 15 array def
                frnd 360 mul /pdsa exch def
                frnd sqrt pdspread mul /pdsr exch def
                pdd 0 pdx pdsr pdsa cos mul add put
                pdd 1 pdy pdsr pdsa sin mul add put
                pdd 2 frnd 0.8 mul 0.6 add pdsize mul put
                % frnd can return exactly 1.0, so a bare `lt` would reject some
                % slots at /Charge 1; the draw is still consumed either way.
                pdd 3 frnd pdcharge lt pdcharge 1 ge or { 1 } { 0 } ifelse put
                pdd 4 frnd put
                pdd 5 frnd put
                0 1 6 {
                    6 add pdd exch
                    1 frnd 0.5 sub pdirr mul 1.3 mul add dup 0.3 lt { pop 0.3 } if
                    put
                } for
                % Lit-side measure, -1..1: where the dab sits in its
                % cluster relative to the light.
                pdd 13 pdsr pdsa cos mul pdlx mul pdsr pdsa sin mul pdly mul add
                    pdspread div put
                % This dab's colour, resolved once so all three layers read the same
                % value: the caller's colour, or the load at the position the outline
                % phase already drew (slot 5, 0..1 from the first colour to the last;
                % `puat` takes u = 1 - 2p). No new draw. `puwet` grades it for any
                % `pkwet` pass it is painted in.
                pdd 14
                pdColorRec null ne {
                    pdColorRec 1 pdd 5 get 2 mul sub false puat puwet 3 array astore
                } { pdbase } ifelse
                put
                pddabs pdn pdd put
                /pdn pdn 1 add def
            } for
        } for

        % Layer 1: the darker underlayer, nudged away from the light.
        pdshadow 0 gt {
            0 1 pdn 1 sub {
                pddabs exch get /pdd exch def
                pdd 3 get 1 eq {
                    /pdr pdd 2 get 1.12 mul def
                    /pdcx pdd 0 get pdlx pdd 2 get mul 0.35 mul sub def
                    /pdcy pdd 1 get pdly pdd 2 get mul 0.35 mul sub def
                    pdShadowColor null ne {
                        % Toward /ShadowColor by the same strength, then the jitter as
                        % its own shade, so /ColorJitter keeps its meaning.
                        pdd 14 get pdShadowColor pdshadow 0.55 mul mix3
                        3 array astore 1 pdcj 0.5 mul pdd 4 get 2 mul 1 sub mul add shade
                    } {
                        pdd 14 get 1 pdshadow 0.55 mul sub pdcj 0.5 mul pdd 4 get 2 mul 1 sub mul add shade
                    } ifelse
                    setrgbcolor
                    newpath pddab fill
                } if
            } for
        } if

        % Layer 2: the body, in the dab's colour.
        0 1 pdn 1 sub {
            pddabs exch get /pdd exch def
            pdd 3 get 1 eq {
                /pdr pdd 2 get def
                /pdcx pdd 0 get def
                /pdcy pdd 1 get def
                pdd 14 get 1 pdcj 0.5 mul pdd 4 get 2 mul 1 sub mul add shade
                setrgbcolor
                newpath pddab fill
            } if
        } for

        % Layer 3: smaller, lighter dabs on the dabs that face the light.
        pdhl 0 gt {
            0 1 pdn 1 sub {
                pddabs exch get /pdd exch def
                pdd 3 get 1 eq
                pdd 13 get pdd 4 get 0.5 sub 0.4 mul add 1 pdhl 2 mul sub gt and {
                    /pdr pdd 2 get 0.55 mul def
                    /pdcx pdd 0 get pdlx pdd 2 get mul 0.3 mul add def
                    /pdcy pdd 1 get pdly pdd 2 get mul 0.3 mul add def
                    pdHighlightColor null ne {
                        pdd 14 get pdHighlightColor pdhl 0.6 mul mix3
                    } {
                        pdd 14 get 1 pdhl 0.6 mul add shade
                    } ifelse
                    setrgbcolor
                    newpath pddab fill
                } if
            } for
        } if
        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% ============================================================================
% Pigments: a named oil palette that mixes like paint (issue #185)
% ============================================================================
%
% artkit's `mix3` is an RGB lerp, which is wrong for paint in the ways that
% matter: blue and yellow lerp to grey, white only lightens, and a dark mix
% never goes rich. This section is the paint answer. It touches no preset and
% changes no existing render; the presets take its colours through the keys
% they already have (`/Colors`, `/HighlightColor`, `/ShadowColor`), which since
% #356 also accept a bare pigment name (the resolver, `pupig`, reads `Pigments`).
%
%   /name pigment            -> r g b       a pigment as it comes from the tube
%   [ /name w /name w ... ] pigmix -> r g b mix by weight, like paint
%   /name t pigtint          -> r g b       t parts titanium white
%   /name t pigshade         -> r g b       t parts midnight black
%   spec pigcolor            -> [r g b]     one colour as an array
%   [ spec ... ] pigcolors   -> [[r g b]..] a whole /Colors list
%
% The palette is `Pigments`, a dict of name -> [r g b strength], listed in
% tube order by `PigmentNames`: titaniumwhite midnightblack phthaloblue
% prussianblue alizarin sapgreen cadmiumyellow indianyellow yellowochre
% vandykebrown darksienna burntumber brightred. r g b is the pigment's
% colour as it reads on white paper; strength is its tinting strength
% relative to a plain colour (1). Inside a mix every channel is held to
% 0.012-0.985 so a pure 0 or 1 cannot blow up the maths; a lone pigment
% is returned as it is stored.
%
% The model is Kubelka-Munk's two-constant theory, run per channel on r g b
% rather than on a spectrum. Paint absorbs (K) and scatters (S) light, and in
% a mixture the absorption and scattering simply add, weighted by how much of
% each pigment is in the pot. A pigment of reflectance R has K/S =
% (1-R)^2 / 2R; a mixture of pigments i with weights w_i and strengths s_i
% has, per channel,
%
%     a_i = w_i s_i          K/S = sum(a_i (1-R_i)^2 / 2R_i) / sum(a_i)
%     R   = 1 + K/S - sqrt((K/S)^2 + 2 K/S)
%
% That is a few multiplies and three square roots per pigmix, with no
% iteration. What it buys over a lerp: blue and yellow multiply their
% absorption bands, so they leave green; white (strength 12) has almost no
% absorption and a great deal of scatter, so tints are not a straight line
% toward white (how fast each lightens depends on the pigment) and lose
% saturation as they lighten; a strong dark
% pigment (phthalo blue) dominates a mix, as it does in a pot.
%
% What it is not, so nobody expects it:
%   * three bands, not a spectrum. Two pigments that look alike in r g b but
%     differ in spectrum (the two blues, say) mix as their r g b says, and
%     complements (phthalo blue and bright red) fall to a very dark neutral
%     rather than the lively brown a real pot gives. The pigments' r g b and
%     strengths are tuned by eye against reference mixes, not measured.
%   * mixed in the encoded (gamma) rgb the display uses, not linear light.
%     That saves a power per channel; a linear-light mix would give different
%     (usually darker) midtones.
%   * a mix, not a layer. It says what a pot of paint looks like, not what a
%     thin glaze over another colour looks like, and nothing here is wet,
%     opaque or dry. Weights are parts by volume, not by visual share.
%
% Every function validates its input and raises a self-documenting guard
% (`pigmix-unknown-pigment`, ...). Scratch variables all start `pgm`: the
% presets' nested calls clobber `pk*` names, and pigmix is meant to be called
% from inside a preset's own procedure.

% @kind: Dial
% @summary: The oil palette: a dict of 13 named pigments, each [r g b strength]; r g b is the colour as it reads on white paper, strength the tinting strength (titanium white 12, a plain colour 1). Read it with pigment, mix it with pigmix; iterate it in tube order with PigmentNames.
% @example: Pigments /phthaloblue get aload pop pop setrgbcolor
/Pigments <<
    /titaniumwhite [0.95 0.96 0.97 12.0]
    /midnightblack [0.04 0.04 0.05 1.0]
    /phthaloblue   [0.09 0.24 0.55 1.6]
    /prussianblue  [0.05 0.17 0.32 1.4]
    /alizarin      [0.55 0.05 0.24 1.8]
    /sapgreen      [0.16 0.38 0.08 1.2]
    /cadmiumyellow [0.99 0.87 0.04 1.6]
    /indianyellow  [0.94 0.63 0.07 1.1]
    /yellowochre   [0.78 0.56 0.20 1.0]
    /vandykebrown  [0.24 0.14 0.09 0.9]
    /darksienna    [0.46 0.22 0.10 1.0]
    /burntumber    [0.32 0.17 0.10 1.0]
    /brightred     [0.82 0.10 0.07 1.6]
>> def

% @kind: Dial
% @summary: The names of the 13 pigments in tube order (Pigments has no order of its own): white, black, the blues, alizarin, the greens, the yellows and ochre, the browns, bright red.
% @example: PigmentNames { pigment setrgbcolor ... } forall
/PigmentNames [
    /titaniumwhite /midnightblack /phthaloblue /prussianblue /alizarin
    /sapgreen /cadmiumyellow /indianyellow /yellowochre /vandykebrown
    /darksienna /burntumber /brightred
] def

% r -> K/S for one channel. The clamp keeps a pure 0 or 1 channel finite.
% @internal
/pgmks { 0.012 max 0.985 min dup 1 exch sub dup mul exch 2 mul div } bind def

% q -> R   (the Kubelka-Munk reflectance for K/S = q)
% @internal
/pgmr { dup dup mul 1 index 2 mul add sqrt neg exch 1 add add } bind def

% number -> bool
% @internal
/pgmnum { type dup /integertype eq exch /realtype eq or } bind def

% entry -> r g b strength   (a pigment name, or a literal [r g b] array)
% @internal
/pgmentry {
    dup type /nametype eq {
        dup Pigments exch known not { pigmix-unknown-pigment } if
        Pigments exch get aload pop
    } {
        dup xcheck { pigmix-entry-must-be-a-name-or-rgb-array } if
        dup type /arraytype ne { pigmix-entry-must-be-a-name-or-rgb-array } if
        dup length 3 ne { pigmix-entry-must-be-a-name-or-rgb-array } if
        dup {
            dup pgmnum { dup 0 ge exch 1 le and } { pop false } ifelse
            not { pigmix-rgb-components-must-be-0-to-1 } if
        } forall
        aload pop 1
    } ifelse
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Mix pigments like paint. Takes a flat array of name-and-weight pairs, [ /phthaloblue 1 /alizarin 1 ], and returns r g b like mix3. An entry may be a literal [r g b] array in place of a name (a plain colour, strength 1). Weights are parts, not fractions, and a zero weight is left out. Blue and yellow give green, blue and crimson violet; white tints instead of just lightening. A single pigment comes back exactly.
% @example: [ /sapgreen 3 /cadmiumyellow 1 ] pigmix setrgbcolor
/pigmix {
    /pgms exch def
    % `load`, not the name: naming a procedure would run it.
    /pgms load xcheck { pigmix-needs-an-array } if
    /pgms load type /arraytype ne { pigmix-needs-an-array } if
    pgms length 2 mod 0 ne { pigmix-entries-come-in-pairs } if
    /pgmn 0 def /pgmsum 0 def /pgmk0 0 def /pgmk1 0 def /pgmk2 0 def
    0 2 pgms length 2 sub {
        /pgmi exch def
        pgms pgmi 1 add get dup pgmnum not { pigmix-weight-must-be-a-number } if
        dup 0 lt { pigmix-weight-must-not-be-negative } if
        /pgmw exch def
        % The entry is checked even at weight 0, so a typo is never silent.
        pgms pgmi get pgmentry /pgmst exch def 3 array astore /pgmcur exch def
        pgmw 0 gt {
            /pgml pgmcur def
            /pgma pgmw pgmst mul def
            /pgmn pgmn 1 add def
            /pgmsum pgmsum pgma add def
            /pgmk0 pgml 0 get pgmks pgma mul pgmk0 add def
            /pgmk1 pgml 1 get pgmks pgma mul pgmk1 add def
            /pgmk2 pgml 2 get pgmks pgma mul pgmk2 add def
        } if
    } for
    pgmn 0 eq { pigmix-weights-sum-to-zero } if
    pgmn 1 eq {
        pgml aload pop
    } {
        pgmk0 pgmsum div pgmk1 pgmsum div pgmk2 pgmsum div
        pgmr 3 1 roll pgmr 3 1 roll pgmr 3 1 roll
    } ifelse
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: One pigment as it comes from the tube, as r g b. The same as [ /name 1 ] pigmix.
% @example: /alizarin pigment setrgbcolor
/pigment {
    dup type /nametype ne { pigment-needs-a-name } if
    1 2 array astore pigmix
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A tint: the pigment (or [r g b] colour) with t parts titanium white, t from 0 (the pigment) to 1 (white). Not a lerp: how fast a colour lightens depends on the pigment, and the tint loses saturation as it lightens.
% @example: /phthaloblue 0.25 pigtint setrgbcolor
/pigtint {
    /pgmt exch def /pgmp exch def
    /pgmt load xcheck { pigtint-amount-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /pgmp load xcheck { pigtint-pigment-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /pgmt load pgmnum not { pigtint-amount-must-be-a-number } if
    pgmt 0 lt pgmt 1 gt or { pigtint-amount-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    [ pgmp 1 pgmt sub /titaniumwhite pgmt ] pigmix
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A shade: the pigment (or [r g b] colour) with t parts midnight black, t from 0 (the pigment) to 1 (black). Black has strength 1, so t 0.1 already goes deep; shade with a dark brown or blue instead of black for a richer dark.
% @example: /sapgreen 0.1 pigshade setrgbcolor
/pigshade {
    /pgmt exch def /pgmp exch def
    /pgmt load xcheck { pigshade-amount-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /pgmp load xcheck { pigshade-pigment-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /pgmt load pgmnum not { pigshade-amount-must-be-a-number } if
    pgmt 0 lt pgmt 1 gt or { pigshade-amount-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    [ pgmp 1 pgmt sub /midnightblack pgmt ] pigmix
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: One colour as an [r g b] array, the shape /Colors, /HighlightColor and /ShadowColor take. The operand is a pigment name or a pigmix spec: /sapgreen pigcolor, or [ /sapgreen 3 /cadmiumyellow 1 ] pigcolor.
% @example: << /HighlightColor /cadmiumyellow pigcolor >> pkdab
/pigcolor {
    dup type /nametype eq { pigment } { pigmix } ifelse
    3 array astore
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A whole /Colors list from pigments. Takes an array whose entries are pigment names, pigmix specs or plain [r g b] arrays, and returns the array of rgb arrays a preset's /Colors wants. A list of bare names needs no wrapper (/Colors takes names itself); pigcolors is for lists that include pigmix specs.
% @example: << /Colors [ /sapgreen [ /sapgreen 1 /cadmiumyellow 1 ] ] pigcolors >> pkfan
/pigcolors {
    /pgmsrc exch def
    /pgmsrc load xcheck { pigcolors-needs-an-array } if
    /pgmsrc load type /arraytype ne { pigcolors-needs-an-array } if
    pgmsrc length array
    0 1 pgmsrc length 1 sub {
        /pgmj exch def
        dup pgmj
        pgmsrc pgmj get
        % A plain [r g b] array is kept as the colour it is; a spec starts
        % with a name.
        dup type /arraytype eq
            { dup length 0 gt { dup 0 get pgmnum } { false } ifelse } { false } ifelse
        { pgmentry pop 3 array astore } { pigcolor } ifelse
        put
    } for
} bind def

%%SelfTest: pigmix-mixes-like-paint
%   % r g b r' g' b' compared within 0.002 (near3), or exactly (same3).
%   /pgtnear3 { 6 array astore /pgtv exch def
%       true 0 1 2 { /pgti exch def
%           pgtv pgti get pgtv pgti 3 add get sub abs 0.002 lt and } for } bind def
%   /pgtsame3 { 6 array astore /pgtv exch def
%       true 0 1 2 { /pgti exch def
%           pgtv pgti get pgtv pgti 3 add get eq and } for } bind def
%
%   % One pigment comes back exactly, however many parts of it.
%   [ /alizarin 3 ] pigmix 0.55 0.05 0.24 pgtsame3
%   (one pigment is exact) mustbe
%   /alizarin pigment 0.55 0.05 0.24 pgtsame3
%   (pigment is exact) mustbe
%
%   % The mixes the issue asks for, pinned to the model's output.
%   [ /phthaloblue 1 /cadmiumyellow 1 ] pigmix 0.155 0.349 0.073 pgtnear3
%   (phthalo blue and cadmium yellow) mustbe
%   [ /phthaloblue 1 /alizarin 1 ] pigmix 0.157 0.079 0.320 pgtnear3
%   (phthalo blue and alizarin) mustbe
%   /phthaloblue 0.25 pigtint 0.227 0.446 0.723 pgtnear3
%   (a quarter white on phthalo blue) mustbe
%
%   % What the pins mean: blue and yellow green, blue and crimson violet.
%   [ /phthaloblue 1 /cadmiumyellow 1 ] pigmix
%   /pgtb exch def /pgtg exch def /pgtr exch def
%   pgtg pgtr gt pgtg pgtb gt and
%   (blue and yellow: green is the largest channel) mustbe
%   [ /phthaloblue 1 /alizarin 1 ] pigmix
%   /pgtb exch def /pgtg exch def /pgtr exch def
%   pgtb pgtg gt pgtr pgtg gt and
%   (blue and crimson: green is the smallest channel) mustbe
%
%   % Weights are parts: scaling them all changes nothing, and neither does
%   % the order.
%   [ /sapgreen 3 /cadmiumyellow 1 ] pigmix
%   [ /cadmiumyellow 10 /sapgreen 30 ] pigmix pgtnear3
%   (scale and order do not matter) mustbe
%
%   % A zero weight is left out, but its entry is still checked.
%   [ /alizarin 1 /phthaloblue 0 ] pigmix 0.55 0.05 0.24 pgtsame3
%   (a zero weight leaves the entry out) mustbe
%   { [ /alizarin 1 /nothue 0 ] pigmix }
%   /pigmix-unknown-pigment
%   (a zero-weight typo is still caught) mustguard
%
%   % Tints: 0 is the pigment, 1 is white; shades end at black.
%   /sapgreen 0 pigtint 0.16 0.38 0.08 pgtsame3
%   (a tint of 0 is the pigment) mustbe
%   /sapgreen 1 pigtint 0.95 0.96 0.97 pgtsame3
%   (a tint of 1 is white) mustbe
%   /sapgreen 1 pigshade 0.04 0.04 0.05 pgtsame3
%   (a shade of 1 is black) mustbe
%
%   % A plain [r g b] array mixes as a colour of strength 1.
%   [ [ 0.8 0.2 0.4 ] 1 [ 0.8 0.2 0.4 ] 1 ] pigmix 0.8 0.2 0.4 pgtnear3
%   (a colour mixed with itself) mustbe
%
%   % pigcolor and pigcolors give the shape /Colors takes.
%   /alizarin pigcolor dup length 3 eq exch 0 get 0.55 eq and
%   (pigcolor of a name) mustbe
%   [ /alizarin 1 /titaniumwhite 1 ] pigcolor length 3 eq
%   (pigcolor of a spec) mustbe
%   [ /alizarin [ /alizarin 1 /titaniumwhite 1 ] [ 0.1 0.2 0.3 ] ] pigcolors
%   dup length 3 eq exch 2 get 1 get 0.2 eq and
%   (pigcolors keeps a plain rgb array) mustbe
%   { << /Colors [ /sapgreen [ /sapgreen 1 /cadmiumyellow 1 ] ] pigcolors >>
%     (pkfan) puparse pop }
%   (pigcolors output passes the /Colors guards) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pigmix-rejects-malformed-specs
%   { 5 pigmix } /pigmix-needs-an-array (a number) mustguard
%   { { /alizarin 1 } pigmix } /pigmix-needs-an-array (a procedure) mustguard
%   % A procedure that would return a valid spec must be refused, not run.
%   { { [ /alizarin 1 ] } pigmix } /pigmix-needs-an-array (a procedure returning a spec) mustguard
%   { [ /alizarin ] pigmix } /pigmix-entries-come-in-pairs (a missing weight) mustguard
%   { [ /alizarin 1 /sapgreen ] pigmix } /pigmix-entries-come-in-pairs (a trailing name) mustguard
%   { [ /nothue 1 ] pigmix } /pigmix-unknown-pigment (an unknown pigment) mustguard
%   { [ /alizarin (a lot) ] pigmix } /pigmix-weight-must-be-a-number (a string weight) mustguard
%   { [ /alizarin -1 /sapgreen 2 ] pigmix } /pigmix-weight-must-not-be-negative (a negative weight) mustguard
%   { [ /alizarin 0 /sapgreen 0 ] pigmix } /pigmix-weights-sum-to-zero (all weights zero) mustguard
%   { [ ] pigmix } /pigmix-weights-sum-to-zero (an empty mix) mustguard
%   { [ 5 1 ] pigmix } /pigmix-entry-must-be-a-name-or-rgb-array (a number as an entry) mustguard
%   { [ [ 1 0 ] 1 ] pigmix } /pigmix-entry-must-be-a-name-or-rgb-array (a 2-element entry) mustguard
%   { [ { 1 0 0 } 1 ] pigmix } /pigmix-entry-must-be-a-name-or-rgb-array (a procedure as an entry) mustguard
%   { [ [ 0 0 1.5 ] 1 ] pigmix } /pigmix-rgb-components-must-be-0-to-1 (a component of 1.5) mustguard
%   { (alizarin) pigment } /pigment-needs-a-name (a string as a pigment) mustguard
%   { /nothue pigment } /pigmix-unknown-pigment (an unknown pigment name) mustguard
%   { /alizarin 1.5 pigtint } /pigtint-amount-must-be-0-to-1 (a tint of 1.5) mustguard
%   { /alizarin { 0.5 } pigtint } /pigtint-amount-must-not-be-a-procedure (a procedure as the tint) mustguard
%   { { /alizarin } 0.5 pigtint } /pigtint-pigment-must-not-be-a-procedure (a procedure as the pigment) mustguard
%   { /alizarin { 0.5 } pigshade } /pigshade-amount-must-not-be-a-procedure (a procedure as the shade) mustguard
%   { { /alizarin } 0.5 pigshade } /pigshade-pigment-must-not-be-a-procedure (a procedure as the shaded pigment) mustguard
%   { /alizarin (x) pigtint } /pigtint-amount-must-be-a-number (a string tint) mustguard
%   { /alizarin -0.1 pigshade } /pigshade-amount-must-be-0-to-1 (a shade of -0.1) mustguard
%   { 5 pigcolors } /pigcolors-needs-an-array (a number as the list) mustguard
%   { { [ /alizarin ] } pigcolors } /pigcolors-needs-an-array (a procedure as the list) mustguard
%   { [ /nothue ] pigcolors } /pigmix-unknown-pigment (an unknown name in the list) mustguard
%   { [ [ 0.1 0.2 ] ] pigcolors } /pigmix-entry-must-be-a-name-or-rgb-array (a short rgb array in the list) mustguard
%%EndSelfTest
%
% ============================================================================
% Loaded colours: the `pu-` resolver (issue #183, slice L1 of
% docs/dev/design/PAINT_LOADING.md)
% ============================================================================
%
% A brush loaded with more than one colour (`/Colors`, adopted by the presets
% in later slices) has to turn "where on the head am I" into a colour. That is
% this section: the shared, preset-independent half. No preset reads any of it
% yet, so nothing here changes a render.
%
%   opts (pkfan) puparse            -> rec | null   validate, build the load
%   opts (pkfan) puspell            -> -            reject the British spellings
%   opts (pkfan) pulightneedscolors -> -            reject /Light without /Colors
%   rec u flip puat                 -> r g b        colour at head position u
%   r g b puwet                     -> r g b        pkwet's grading of the pass
%
% Why a prefix of its own. The resolver runs inside `pkfan` and `pkoil`, whose
% nested `pkribbon` calls clobber every `pk*` name. `pu` names are scratch
% only: `puparse` returns a record the preset keeps under *its own* prefix, and
% nothing afterwards depends on a `pu` global surviving.
%
% The record is `[colors mix lightset light]`: the validated /Colors array, the
% /ColorMix (default 0.3), whether /Light was given, and /Light (0 when not).
% `puparse` returns `null` when /Colors is unset, so a preset's branch is
% `rec null ne` and an unset key never reaches the new code (the #181
% /Direction rule: branch on presence, never on a default that happens to
% compute the same thing).
%
% Errors are named after the *calling preset* (`pkfan-colors-must-be-an-
% array`), so the preset's name is an operand and `pugrd` builds the guard name
% from it. The one exception to "one name per rule" is the spelling guard: all
% five British spellings raise `<preset>-colours-is-spelled-colors`, which
% says what to do and is all an artist needs.
%
% `/Light` is split from `puparse` on purpose. A preset that already owns
% /Light (`pkdab`) must not be told it needs /Colors, so `pulightneedscolors`
% is a separate call for the presets that don't (`pkfan`, `pktrowel`,
% `pkbroad`). `puparse` still validates a /Light that comes with /Colors.
%
% Head position `u` is -1 at the right edge and +1 at the left, the same as
% every preset's own head position, so the first colour sits at +1. `flip`
% mirrors it (the per-subpath `/Light` flip of slice L2).

% (preset) (suffix) pugrd -   (raise the guard named preset+suffix)
% The suffix carries its own leading hyphen. Built from a concatenated string
% so the one helper serves every preset; the self-tests match the resulting
% names exactly, so a concatenation bug fails them.
% @internal
/pugrd {
    /pugs exch def /pugp exch def
    pugp length pugs length add string
    dup 0 pugp putinterval
    dup pugp length pugs putinterval
    cvn cvx exec
} bind def

% opts (preset) puspell -   (/Colours and friends are errors, not ignored)
% `pkgetdef` ignores keys it doesn't know, so without this a British spelling
% would render a plain single-colour mark and say nothing.
% @internal
/puspell {
    /pusn exch def /puso exch def
    puso /Colours known
    puso /ColourMix known or
    puso /HighlightColour known or
    puso /ShadowColour known or
    puso /TipColour known or
        { pusn (-colours-is-spelled-colors) pugrd } if
} bind def

% (a) (b) pucat -> (ab)   (concatenate two strings; builds a guard prefix)
% @internal
/pucat {
    /pucb exch def /puca exch def
    puca length pucb length add string
    dup 0 puca putinterval
    dup puca length pucb putinterval
} bind def

% obj pujspec -> bool   (a literal array whose first element is a name)
% That is the shape of a `pigmix` spec ([ /sapgreen 1 /cadmiumyellow 1 ]). It
% never fits a colour key, which takes an [r g b] of numbers, so a key that sees
% one says to wrap it in `pigcolors` instead of "not an rgb array". The length
% is tested before the element is read: `[ ]` is not a spec.
% @internal
/pujspec {
    dup type /arraytype eq {
        dup xcheck { pop false } {
            dup length 0 gt { 0 get type /nametype eq } { pop false } ifelse
        } ifelse
    } { pop false } ifelse
} bind def

% (prefix) /name pupig -> [r g b]
% A bare pigment name in a colour key (#356): the same colour `pigcolor` gives,
% read straight from `Pigments` so the resolver does not call into pigmix and
% its `pgm` scratch. A lone pigment is returned as stored, so a name and its
% `pigcolor` are the same bits. The array is a fresh one: it must not alias the
% palette's own entry. An unknown name raises `<prefix>-unknown-pigment-see-
% PigmentNames`, which says where the valid names are listed.
% @internal
/pupig {
    /pujn exch def /pujp exch def
    Pigments pujn known not { pujp (-unknown-pigment-see-PigmentNames) pugrd } if
    Pigments pujn get aload pop pop 3 array astore
} bind def

% opts /Key (preset-key) puhue -> [r g b] | null
% One optional rgb colour key (`/HighlightColor`, `/ShadowColor`): `null` when
% the key is unset, else the validated array. The second operand is the guard
% prefix, preset and key together (`pkdab-highlightcolor`), so the guards read
% `pkdab-highlightcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array` and `...-components-must-be-0-to-1`.
% `xcheck` comes before any type check, as in `puparse`. The caller keeps the
% result under its own prefix.
% @internal
/puhue {
    /puhp exch def /puhk exch def /puho exch def
    puho puhk known not { null } {
        /puhv puho puhk get def
        /puhv load xcheck { puhp (-must-be-an-rgb-array) pugrd } if
        % A literal name is a pigment: resolve it once, then it is checked like
        % any [r g b].
        /puhv load type /nametype eq { /puhv puhp /puhv load pupig def } if
        /puhv load type /arraytype ne { puhp (-must-be-an-rgb-array) pugrd } if
        puhv pujspec { puhp (-pigmix-spec-needs-pigcolors) pugrd } if
        puhv length 3 ne { puhp (-must-be-an-rgb-array) pugrd } if
        puhv {
            dup type dup /integertype eq exch /realtype eq or
                { dup 0 ge exch 1 le and } { pop false } ifelse
            not { puhp (-components-must-be-0-to-1) pugrd } if
        } forall
        puhv
    } ifelse
} bind def

% opts (preset) pulightneedscolors -   (/Light without /Colors is an error)
% For presets that don't already have a /Light of their own: an artist who
% wrote it expected an effect (#181's /Stretch-without-/Direction rule).
% @internal
/pulightneedscolors {
    /pulnn exch def /pulno exch def
    pulno /Light known pulno /Colors known not and
        { pulnn (-light-needs-colors) pugrd } if
} bind def

% opts (preset) puparse -> rec | null
% See the section header for the record. Checks run in a fixed order, the
% spelling guard first, so a misspelled key is reported as that and not as
% whatever its absence causes.
% @internal
/puparse {
    /pupn exch def /pupo exch def
    pupo pupn puspell
    pupo /Colors known not {
        pupo /ColorMix known { pupn (-colormix-needs-colors) pugrd } if
        null
    } {
        /pupc pupo /Colors get def
        % `xcheck` before any type check: a procedure is an `arraytype` too.
        /pupc load xcheck { pupn (-colors-must-be-an-array) pugrd } if
        /pupc load type /arraytype ne { pupn (-colors-must-be-an-array) pugrd } if
        pupc length dup 1 lt exch 8 gt or
            { pupn (-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors) pugrd } if
        % A bare pigment name stands for its colour: resolve each one here, once,
        % so the rest of the checks and the record see only [r g b] arrays. The
        % copy is made only when a name is present, so a load without one is the
        % caller's own array, exactly as before.
        /pujany false def
        pupc { dup xcheck not exch type /nametype eq and { /pujany true def } if } forall
        pujany {
            /pujr pupc length array def
            0 1 pupc length 1 sub {
                /pujk exch def
                pupc pujk get
                dup xcheck not 1 index type /nametype eq and
                    { /pujv exch def pupn (-colors) pucat pujv pupig } if
                pujr exch pujk exch put
            } for
            /pupc pujr def
        } if
        % Each entry is a literal 3-element array, not a procedure that
        % happens to hold three items. A pigmix spec gets its own guard.
        pupc {
            dup pujspec { pupn (-colors-pigmix-spec-needs-pigcolors) pugrd } if
            dup xcheck { pop false } {
                dup type /arraytype eq { length 3 eq } { pop false } ifelse
            } ifelse
            not { pupn (-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays) pugrd } if
        } forall
        pupc {
            {
                dup type dup /integertype eq exch /realtype eq or
                    { dup 0 ge exch 1 le and } { pop false } ifelse
                not { pupn (-colors-components-must-be-0-to-1) pugrd } if
            } forall
        } forall
        /pupm pupo /ColorMix 0.3 pkgetdef def
        /pupm load xcheck { pupn (-colormix-must-not-be-a-procedure) pugrd } if
        /pupm load type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
            { pupn (-colormix-must-be-a-number) pugrd } if
        pupm 0 lt pupm 1 gt or { pupn (-colormix-must-be-0-to-1) pugrd } if
        pupo /Light known {
            /pupl pupo /Light get def
            /pupl load xcheck { pupn (-light-must-not-be-a-procedure) pugrd } if
            /pupl load type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
                { pupn (-light-must-be-a-number) pugrd } if
            /pupls true def
        } {
            /pupl 0 def /pupls false def
        } ifelse
        [ pupc pupm pupls pupl ]
    } ifelse
} bind def

% rec u flip puat -> r g b
% The colour at head position u (section 2.4 of the design). N colours sit at
% p = k/(N-1) with p = (1-u)/2, so u = +1 is the first colour. `k` is clamped
% before the fraction is taken, so p = 1 is the last colour with t = 1 and not
% the second-to-last with t = 0. A blend that comes out at exactly 0 or 1
% returns that colour itself rather than mix3(a, b, 1), which need not equal
% b to the last bit: a load whose colours all equal the current colour then
% paints byte for byte what the plain brush paints (slice L2 pins that).
% N = 1 is the colour. u is clamped, so a head position a hair past +-1 from
% float rounding can't index outside the array.
% @internal
/puat {
    /puaf exch def /puau exch def /puar exch def
    /puac puar 0 get def
    puac length 1 eq {
        puac 0 get aload pop
    } {
        1 puau puaf { neg } if sub 2 div 0 max 1 min
        puac length 1 sub mul
        dup cvi puac length 2 sub min /puak exch def
        puak sub /puatt exch def
        puar 1 get /puaw exch def
        % The ramp is compared before it is divided: a tiny /ColorMix would
        % otherwise overflow the division (undefinedresult), and under gs's
        % 32-bit reals that is anything under about 1e-38. Inside the ramp
        % |d / mix| < 0.5, so the divide is safe. /ColorMix 0 has no ramp,
        % so both comparisons are the midpoint step.
        puatt 0.5 sub /puad exch def
        puad puaw 0.5 mul ge { 1 }
        { puad puaw -0.5 mul le { 0 } { puad puaw div 0.5 add } ifelse }
        ifelse /puatt exch def
        puatt 0 eq
            { puac puak get aload pop }
            { puatt 1 eq
                { puac puak 1 add get aload pop }
                { puac puak get puac puak 1 add get puatt mix3 }
              ifelse }
        ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def

% r g b puwet -> r g b
% A loaded colour graded by the `pkwet` passes it is painted in (slice L3,
% section 3.4 of the design). `pkwet` grades an inherited colour by setting
% mix3(base, Under, k) before each pass; a loaded brush ignores the current
% colour, so it calls this to take the same mix itself. For each live depth
% d = 1 .. pqdnow, outermost first (the order nested calls mix an inherited
% colour), the colour becomes mix3(c, Under_d, k_d), where k_d is frame d's
% slot-4 factor for the pass in slot 6 and Under_d is slot 5. Outside any
% pkwet it is the identity. A core pass's factor is 0 and is skipped rather
% than mixed, so the core, and the whole of a /Soft 0 call, is the identity
% to the last bit. Reads only its operands and the live frames; no draws.
% @internal
/puwet {
    pqdnow 0 gt {
        3 array astore /puwc exch def
        1 1 pqdnow {
            userdict /pqframes get exch get /puwf exch def
            puwf 4 get puwf 6 get get /puwk exch def
            puwk 0 ne {
                % a procedural /Under (issue #179) keeps one backdrop per pass
                /puwc [ puwc
                    puwf 7 get dup null eq { pop puwf 5 get } { puwf 6 get get } ifelse
                    puwk mix3 ] def
            } if
        } for
        puwc aload pop
    } if
} bind def

%%SelfTest: puparse-rejects-malformed-loads
%   { << /Colors 5 >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (a number as /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors (red) >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (a string as /Colors) mustguard
%
%   % A procedure is an arraytype too: xcheck has to come first.
%   { << /Colors { [ 0 0 0 ] } >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (a procedure as /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (no colours) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ 9 { [ 0 0 0 ] } repeat ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (nine colours) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays
%   (a 2-element entry) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 0 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays
%   (a 4-element entry) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ 0.5 ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays
%   (a number as an entry) mustguard
%
%   % An executable entry of three items is not an rgb array.
%   { << /Colors [ { 0 0 0 } ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays
%   (a procedure as an entry) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 1.5 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a component of 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ -0.1 0 0 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a negative component) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 (x) 0 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a string component) mustguard
%
%   % The guard is named after the calling preset.
%   { << /Colors 5 >> (pktrowel) puparse }
%   /pktrowel-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (the preset name comes from the operand) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColorMix 1.5 >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colormix-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/ColorMix 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColorMix -0.1 >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colormix-must-be-0-to-1
%   (a negative /ColorMix) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColorMix (a lot) >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colormix-must-be-a-number
%   (a string as /ColorMix) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColorMix { 0.5 } >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colormix-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (a procedure as /ColorMix) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /Light (north) >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-light-must-be-a-number
%   (a string as /Light) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /Light { 90 } >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-light-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (a procedure as /Light) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse pop }
%   (1 colour) mustpass
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse pop }
%   (2 colours) mustpass
%
%   { << /Colors [ 8 { [ 0.2 0.4 1 ] } repeat ] >> (pkfan) puparse pop }
%   (8 colours, the cap) mustpass
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 1 0 ] ] /ColorMix 0 /Light -45 >> (pkfan) puparse pop }
%   (/ColorMix 0 and a negative /Light) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: puparse-rejects-the-british-spellings
%   { << /Colours [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/Colours) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColourMix 0.5 >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/ColourMix) mustguard
%
%   { << /HighlightColour [ 1 1 1 ] >> (pkoil) puspell }
%   /pkoil-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/HighlightColour) mustguard
%
%   { << /ShadowColour [ 0 0 0 ] >> (pkdab) puspell }
%   /pkdab-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/ShadowColour) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /TipColour [ 1 1 1 ] >> (pkbroad) puparse }
%   /pkbroad-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (/TipColour) mustguard
%
%   % Reported as the misspelling, not as the /Colors the artist meant.
%   { << /Colours 5 >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colours-is-spelled-colors
%   (a bad value behind a misspelled key) mustguard
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /HighlightColor [ 1 1 1 ] >> (pkoil) puspell }
%   (the American spellings) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: puparse-rejects-keys-that-modify-nothing
%   { << /ColorMix 0.5 >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colormix-needs-colors
%   (/ColorMix without /Colors) mustguard
%
%   { << /Light 90 >> (pkbroad) pulightneedscolors }
%   /pkbroad-light-needs-colors
%   (/Light without /Colors) mustguard
%
%   % puparse leaves /Light to the preset: pkdab has its own.
%   { << /Light 90 >> (pkdab) puparse pop }
%   (puparse alone does not reject /Light) mustpass
%
%   { << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /Light 90 >> (pkbroad) pulightneedscolors }
%   (/Light with /Colors) mustpass
%
%   << >> (pkfan) puparse type /nulltype eq
%   (no keys at all gives null) mustbe
%
%   << /Light 90 >> (pkdab) puparse type /nulltype eq
%   (unset /Colors gives null) mustbe
%
%   % The record is [colors mix lightset light].
%   << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse
%   dup length 4 eq
%   1 index 1 get 0.3 eq and
%   1 index 2 get false eq and
%   exch 3 get 0 eq and
%   (the default record) mustbe
%
%   << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] ] /ColorMix 1 /Light 90 >> (pkfan) puparse
%   dup 1 get 1 eq
%   1 index 2 get true eq and
%   exch 3 get 90 eq and
%   (a given /ColorMix and /Light) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: colour-keys-take-bare-pigment-names
%   % #356: a literal pigment name in /Colors, /HighlightColor or /ShadowColor
%   % stands for the colour `pigcolor` gives, bit for bit.
%   /pgeq { /pgqb exch def /pgqa exch def
%       true 0 1 2 { /pgqi exch def pgqa pgqi get pgqb pgqi get eq and } for } bind def
%   << /Colors [ /sapgreen /cadmiumyellow ] >> (pkfan) puparse 0 get /pgcols exch def
%   pgcols length 2 eq
%   pgcols 0 get /sapgreen pigcolor pgeq and
%   pgcols 1 get /cadmiumyellow pigcolor pgeq and
%   (bare names in /Colors are the pigments pigcolor gives) mustbe
%
%   % Names and rgb arrays mix; the caller's array is left alone.
%   /pgin [ /alizarin [ 0 0 1 ] ] def
%   << /Colors pgin >> (pkfan) puparse 0 get /pgcols exch def
%   pgcols 0 get /alizarin pigcolor pgeq
%   pgcols 1 get [ 0 0 1 ] pgeq and
%   pgin 0 get type /nametype eq and
%   (names and rgb arrays mix, and the options array is not changed) mustbe
%
%   % No name: the caller's own array comes back, as before.
%   /pgl [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] def
%   << /Colors pgl >> (pkfan) puparse 0 get pgl eq
%   (a load without names is the same array) mustbe
%
%   % The resolved colour is its own array, not the palette's entry.
%   << /Colors [ /sapgreen ] >> (pkfan) puparse 0 get 0 get 0 0.5 put
%   Pigments /sapgreen get 0 get 0.16 eq
%   (a resolved colour does not alias the palette) mustbe
%
%   << /HighlightColor /titaniumwhite >> /HighlightColor (pkdab-highlightcolor) puhue
%   /titaniumwhite pigcolor pgeq
%   (a bare name as /HighlightColor) mustbe
%   << /ShadowColor /midnightblack >> /ShadowColor (pkoil-shadowcolor) puhue
%   /midnightblack pigcolor pgeq
%   (a bare name as /ShadowColor) mustbe
%
%   { << /Colors [ /sapgreen /nothue ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-unknown-pigment-see-PigmentNames
%   (an unknown name in /Colors) mustguard
%   { << /HighlightColor /nothue >> /HighlightColor (pkdab-highlightcolor) puhue }
%   /pkdab-highlightcolor-unknown-pigment-see-PigmentNames
%   (an unknown name as /HighlightColor) mustguard
%   { << /ShadowColor /nothue >> /ShadowColor (pkoil-shadowcolor) puhue }
%   /pkoil-shadowcolor-unknown-pigment-see-PigmentNames
%   (an unknown name as /ShadowColor) mustguard
%   % A single name is a pigment, not a /Colors list.
%   { << /Colors /sapgreen >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-be-an-array
%   (a name as the whole /Colors) mustguard
%   % The length rule still comes first.
%   { << /Colors [ 9 { /sapgreen } repeat ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-must-have-1-to-8-colors
%   (nine names) mustguard
%   % A pigmix spec belongs in pigcolors, and the guard says so.
%   { << /Colors [ [ /sapgreen 1 /cadmiumyellow 1 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-pigmix-spec-needs-pigcolors
%   (a pigmix spec in /Colors) mustguard
%   { << /Colors [ [ /sapgreen 1 /nothue ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-pigmix-spec-needs-pigcolors
%   (a three-element spec in /Colors) mustguard
%   { << /HighlightColor [ /sapgreen 1 ] >> /HighlightColor (pkdab-highlightcolor) puhue }
%   /pkdab-highlightcolor-pigmix-spec-needs-pigcolors
%   (a pigmix spec as /HighlightColor) mustguard
%   % The old guards are unchanged for what is not a name.
%   { << /Colors [ [ ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays
%   (an empty array entry) mustguard
%   { << /Colors [ { /sapgreen } ] >> (pkfan) puparse }
%   /pkfan-colors-entries-must-be-rgb-arrays
%   (a procedure entry) mustguard
%   { << /HighlightColor [ ] >> /HighlightColor (pkdab-highlightcolor) puhue }
%   /pkdab-highlightcolor-must-be-an-rgb-array
%   (an empty array as /HighlightColor) mustguard
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: puat-resolves-colours-at-head-positions
%   % All values are dyadic so `eq` is exact.
%   % u = +1 is the left edge and the first colour, u = -1 the last.
%   << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 0.5 0.25 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse /purec exch def
%
%   purec 1 false puat
%   0 eq exch 0 eq and exch 0 eq and
%   (u = +1 is the first colour) mustbe
%
%   purec -1 false puat
%   0.25 eq exch 0.5 eq and exch 1 eq and
%   (u = -1 is the last colour, not the one before it) mustbe
%
%   purec 1 true puat
%   0.25 eq exch 0.5 eq and exch 1 eq and
%   (flip mirrors the head) mustbe
%
%   purec -1 true puat
%   0 eq exch 0 eq and exch 0 eq and
%   (flip at u = -1 is the first colour) mustbe
%
%   % A head position a hair past the edge is clamped, not a rangecheck.
%   purec 1.0000001 false puat
%   0 eq exch 0 eq and exch 0 eq and
%   (u just past +1) mustbe
%
%   purec -1.5 false puat
%   0.25 eq exch 0.5 eq and exch 1 eq and
%   (u well past -1) mustbe
%
%   % ColorMix 0 is hard bands, stepping at the midpoint.
%   << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] /ColorMix 0 >> (pkfan) puparse /purec exch def
%   purec 0.25 false puat
%   0 eq exch 0 eq and exch 0 eq and
%   (/ColorMix 0 just left of the midpoint) mustbe
%   purec -0.25 false puat
%   1 eq exch 1 eq and exch 1 eq and
%   (/ColorMix 0 just right of the midpoint) mustbe
%   purec 0 false puat
%   1 eq exch 1 eq and exch 1 eq and
%   (/ColorMix 0 steps up at exactly the midpoint) mustbe
%
%   % A tiny /ColorMix is a step, not an overflow in the division.
%   << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] /ColorMix 1e-300 >> (pkfan) puparse /purec exch def
%   purec 0.25 false puat
%   0 eq exch 0 eq and exch 0 eq and
%   (a tiny /ColorMix, left of the midpoint) mustbe
%   purec -0.25 false puat
%   1 eq exch 1 eq and exch 1 eq and
%   (a tiny /ColorMix, right of the midpoint) mustbe
%
%   % ColorMix 1 is a straight gradient; 0.5 holds the outer quarters.
%   << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] /ColorMix 1 >> (pkfan) puparse /purec exch def
%   purec 0 false puat
%   0.5 eq exch 0.5 eq and exch 0.5 eq and
%   (/ColorMix 1 at the middle) mustbe
%   << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] /ColorMix 0.5 >> (pkfan) puparse /purec exch def
%   purec 0.25 false puat
%   0.25 eq exch 0.25 eq and exch 0.25 eq and
%   (/ColorMix 0.5 a quarter of the way) mustbe
%
%   % Three colours: the middle one at the middle, the last at the far edge.
%   << /Colors [ [ 0 0 0 ] [ 0.5 0.5 0.5 ] [ 1 1 1 ] ] /ColorMix 0 >> (pkfan) puparse
%   /purec exch def
%   purec 0 false puat
%   0.5 eq exch 0.5 eq and exch 0.5 eq and
%   (the middle of three) mustbe
%   purec -1 false puat
%   1 eq exch 1 eq and exch 1 eq and
%   (the last of three is at the far edge) mustbe
%   purec 1 false puat
%   0 eq exch 0 eq and exch 0 eq and
%   (the first of three is at the near edge) mustbe
%
%   % One colour is that colour wherever you ask.
%   << /Colors [ [ 0.25 0.5 0.75 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse /purec exch def
%   purec 1 false puat
%   0.75 eq exch 0.5 eq and exch 0.25 eq and
%   (one colour, u = +1) mustbe
%   purec -1 true puat
%   0.75 eq exch 0.5 eq and exch 0.25 eq and
%   (one colour, u = -1 flipped) mustbe
%
%   % A load of identical colours resolves to exactly that colour at any
%   % blend, which is what lets a uniform load paint the plain brush.
%   << /Colors [ [ 0.1 0.2 0.3 ] [ 0.1 0.2 0.3 ] ] >> (pkfan) puparse /purec exch def
%   purec 0.3 false puat
%   0.3 eq exch 0.2 eq and exch 0.1 eq and
%   (a uniform load is exactly its colour) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: puwet-applies-pkwets-pass-mix
%   0.1 0.2 0.3 puwet
%   0.3 eq exch 0.2 eq and exch 0.1 eq and
%   (outside any pkwet) mustbe
%
%   % /Soft 0 is the plain call, so a loaded colour is not graded there.
%   { 0.1 0.2 0.3 puwet /putb exch def /putg exch def /putr exch def }
%   << /Soft 0 >> pkwet
%   putr 0.1 eq putg 0.2 eq and putb 0.3 eq and
%   (inside /Soft 0) mustbe
%
%   % Two passes, outermost first: the outer pass is mixed all the way to
%   % /Under (Pickup 1), the core not at all.
%   userdict /putl [ ] put
%   { userdict /putl [ putl aload pop 0 0.5 1 puwet ] put }
%   << /Layers 2 /Offset 0 /Pickup 1 /Under [ 1 0.5 0 ] >> pkwet
%   putl length 6 eq
%   putl 0 get 1 eq and putl 1 get 0.5 eq and putl 2 get 0 eq and
%   putl 3 get 0 eq and putl 4 get 0.5 eq and putl 5 get 1 eq and
%   (an outer pass goes to /Under, the core stays put) mustbe
%
%   % Nested: the outer call's mix first, then the inner's. The outer
%   % pass of the outer call with the inner core gives [1 1 1]; with the
%   % inner's outer pass on top, that goes half way to black.
%   userdict /putl [ ] put
%   { { userdict /putl [ putl aload pop 0 0 0 puwet ] put }
%     << /Layers 2 /Offset 0 /Pickup 0.5 /Under [ 0 0 0 ] >> pkwet }
%   << /Layers 2 /Offset 0 /Pickup 1 /Under [ 1 1 1 ] >> pkwet
%   putl length 12 eq
%   putl 0 get 0.5 eq and putl 3 get 1 eq and
%   putl 6 get 0 eq and putl 9 get 0 eq and
%   (nested passes mix outermost call first) mustbe
%
%   % Errors unwind the depth, so a later top-level call is ungraded.
%   { { 1 0 0 puwet pop pop pop nosuchname } << /Layers 3 /Offset 0 >> pkwet } stopped pop
%   0.1 0.2 0.3 puwet
%   0.3 eq exch 0.2 eq and exch 0.1 eq and
%   (after an unwound pkwet) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest

% raw n pitch angle light puflips -> flips
% The per-subpath /Light flip of slice L2 (section 2.3 of the design). `raw` is
% a preset's array of walkpath stops, each starting [x y angle t sp atend ...];
% `n` is how many; `angle` is the preset's /Angle (0 where it has none). The
% result is an array of `n` booleans, one per stop, and every stop of a subpath
% carries that subpath's flip, so a bristle, lane or striation flips whole.
%
% A subpath is flipped when the left normal of its chord (first stop to last
% stop), turned by `angle` the way the preset turns its own blade, points away
% from the light: cos(normal - light) < 0. The first colour sits on the left, so
% the left normal facing the light is "lit side is the first colour" and needs
% no flip. A chord shorter than `pitch` (a closed circle, a pressed point, a
% stroke barely longer than one step) is left alone. Every entry is written, so
% a later `get` never meets a null. No random draws.
% @internal
/puflips {
    /pufl exch def /pufa exch def /pufp exch def /pufn exch def /pufr exch def
    /puff pufn array def
    0 1 pufn 1 sub { puff exch false put } for
    /pufs 0 def
    0 1 pufn 1 sub {
        /pufi exch def
        pufr pufi get 5 get dup 1 and 1 eq { /pufs pufi def } if
        2 and 2 eq {
            pufr pufs get dup 0 get /pufx0 exch def 1 get /pufy0 exch def
            pufr pufi get dup 0 get /pufx1 exch def 1 get /pufy1 exch def
            pufx1 pufx0 sub /pufdx exch def
            pufy1 pufy0 sub /pufdy exch def
            pufdx dup mul pufdy dup mul add sqrt pufp ge {
                pufdy pufdx atan 90 add pufa add pufl sub cos 0 lt {
                    pufs 1 pufi { puff exch true put } for
                } if
            } if
        } if
    } for
    puff
} bind def

%%SelfTest: puflips-mirrors-subpaths-facing-away-from-the-light
%   % Stops are [x y angle t sp atend]: 1 starts a subpath, 2 ends one, 3 is both.
%   % Light 90 comes from above. Left-to-right has its left normal up: no flip.
%   [ [ 0 0 0 0 0 1 ] [ 10 0 0 1 10 2 ] ] 2 1 0 90 puflips
%   dup 0 get false eq exch 1 get false eq and
%   (left to right, lit from above) mustbe
%
%   % Right-to-left has its left normal down: both stops flip together.
%   [ [ 10 0 180 0 0 1 ] [ 0 0 180 1 10 2 ] ] 2 1 0 90 puflips
%   dup 0 get true eq exch 1 get true eq and
%   (right to left, lit from above) mustbe
%
%   % Moving the light underneath swaps the two.
%   [ [ 0 0 0 0 0 1 ] [ 10 0 0 1 10 2 ] ] 2 1 0 270 puflips
%   dup 0 get true eq exch 1 get true eq and
%   (left to right, lit from below) mustbe
%
%   % Travelling up (+y) has its left normal at -x; light from -x (180) lights it.
%   [ [ 0 0 90 0 0 1 ] [ 0 10 90 1 10 2 ] ] 2 1 0 180 puflips
%   dup 0 get false eq exch 1 get false eq and
%   (travelling up, lit from the west) mustbe
%   [ [ 0 0 90 0 0 1 ] [ 0 10 90 1 10 2 ] ] 2 1 0 0 puflips
%   dup 0 get true eq exch 1 get true eq and
%   (travelling up, lit from the east) mustbe
%
%   % The preset's /Angle turns the blade, and the normal with it.
%   [ [ 0 0 0 0 0 1 ] [ 10 0 0 1 10 2 ] ] 2 1 180 90 puflips
%   dup 0 get true eq exch 1 get true eq and
%   (a blade turned 180) mustbe
%
%   % A chord shorter than the pitch is not flipped, however the light sits.
%   [ [ 0 0 0 0 0 1 ] [ 0.5 0 0 1 0.5 2 ] ] 2 1 0 270 puflips
%   dup 0 get false eq exch 1 get false eq and
%   (a chord under one pitch) mustbe
%
%   % A single-stop subpath (a pressed point) and a closed ring are left alone.
%   [ [ 5 5 0 0 0 3 ] ] 1 1 0 270 puflips 0 get false eq
%   (a pressed point) mustbe
%   [ [ 0 0 0 0 0 1 ] [ 10 5 0 0.5 10 0 ] [ 0 0 0 1 10 2 ] ] 3 1 0 270 puflips
%   dup 0 get false eq exch dup 1 get false eq exch 2 get false eq and and
%   (a closed ring) mustbe
%
%   % Subpaths are independent, and all entries are booleans.
%   [ [ 0 0 0 0 0 1 ] [ 10 0 0 1 10 2 ]
%     [ 10 5 180 0 0 1 ] [ 0 5 180 1 10 2 ] ] 4 1 0 90 puflips
%   dup 0 get false eq 1 index 1 get false eq and
%   1 index 2 get true eq and exch 3 get true eq and
%   (two subpaths flip on their own) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest

% ============================================================================
% Brush motions (issue #184)
% ============================================================================
%
% The presets above model brush *heads*; a painting is equally made of brush
% *motions*, and the wet-on-wet vocabulary is mostly named ones. Each
% generator here builds the path a preset takes as input -- from a region or a
% guide line plus a few artistic controls -- and paints nothing:
%
%   region opts  pkcrisscross   criss-cross X strokes over a band (skies)
%   region opts  pkscumblein    small separate circles in a region (clouds)
%   opts         pkscumble      a chain of overlapping loops along a line
%   opts         pktap          taps / pounces along a line (foliage)
%   opts         pkpull         pull-down or push-up from a line (reflections,
%                               grass, a knife's "touch and pull")
%   opts         pkzigzag       a zig-zag along a line (water, sweeps)
%
% They live here rather than in artkit because their vocabulary is painterly
% (the region motions share artkit's regions, and every motion reads artkit's
% frnd and walkpath, but nothing in artkit has a notion of a brush stroke) and
% because they are the input side of the presets in this file.
%
% One contract for the whole family:
%
% * Each ends with an ordinary path -- moveto/lineto/curveto subpaths, so
%   `... pkcrisscross << >> pkbroad` just works with any preset except `pknib`
%   (which wants exactly one open subpath per call).
% * Each *replaces* the current path (it starts with `newpath`), so a guide
%   path or a region's outline left behind by `scpath` is never painted by
%   the brush. The region motions take an artkit region (`screct` or `scpath`)
%   as their first operand; the spine motions read the current path as their
%   guide, flattened, then replace it with the motion path.
% * None paints or touches colour, and all draw only from `frnd`, so they are
%   deterministic under the caller's own `srand`. Every slot draws a fixed
%   number of values, so a knob that only re-shapes a stroke (length, angle,
%   amplitude) does not re-roll where the strokes land.
% * Each is bounded by a work budget, rejected by a self-documenting guard.
%
% A subpath that ends where it starts is still *open* (no closepath) unless it
% is a circle from `pkscumblein`, which is closed.
%
% Scratch prefixes: mh- (shared helpers), mx- (pkcrisscross), mc- (pkscumblein),
% ms- (pkscumble), mt- (pktap), mp- (pkpull), mz- (pkzigzag). None calls a
% preset, so no pk*-prefixed name is clobbered.

% region -> bool   is this a region dict (artkit's screct / scpath)?
% @internal
/mhisregion {
    dup type /dicttype eq
        { dup /BBox known exch /Kind known and }
        { pop false }
    ifelse
} bind def

% region count mhgrid -
% Lays a jittered grid of about `count` kept cells. The cell count is what the
% *bounding box* needs for `count` to land inside the region, i.e.
% count * bboxArea / regionArea, so a non-rectangular region still keeps about
% `count` points. Rows and columns are then rounded together from that target
% (rows from the box's aspect, columns to fit), not each rounded up on its
% own, which nearly doubled the count on a thin band. Sets mhreg mhgx0 mhgy0
% mhcols mhrows mhcw mhch. A region of zero area leaves a 0 x 0 grid, so the
% caller's loops do not run.
% @internal
/mhgrid {
    /mhcount exch def /mhreg exch def
    mhreg /BBox get aload pop
    /mhgy1 exch def /mhgx1 exch def /mhgy0 exch def /mhgx0 exch def
    /mhbw mhgx1 mhgx0 sub def /mhbh mhgy1 mhgy0 sub def
    /mharea mhreg scarea def
    mharea 0 gt mhbw 0 gt and mhbh 0 gt and
    {
        /mhtarget mhcount mhbw mhbh mul mul mharea div def
        /mhrows mhtarget mhbh mul mhbw div sqrt round def
        % A tall, narrow box would otherwise ask for more rows than cells.
        mhrows mhtarget round gt { /mhrows mhtarget round def } if
        mhrows 1 lt { /mhrows 1 def } if
        /mhcols mhtarget mhrows div round def
        mhcols 1 lt { /mhcols 1 def } if
        /mhcw mhbw mhcols div def
        /mhch mhbh mhrows div def
    }
    { /mhcols 0 def /mhrows 0 def /mhcw 1 def /mhch 1 def }
    ifelse
} bind def

% pitch cap {guard} mhcollect -
% Walks the current path (flattened) and stores every stop as
% [x y ang t sp atend] in mhstops, its length in mhn. Counts first, with the
% budget check inside the callback so an absurd pitch fails fast instead of
% after storing millions of stops; then fills a preallocated array (walkpath's
% proc must consume all six operands every call, see its header). `guard` is a
% procedure that raises the caller's own named guard.
% @internal
/mhcollect {
    /mhguard exch def /mhcap exch def /mhpitch exch def
    /mhn 0 def
    mhpitch {
        pop pop pop pop pop pop
        /mhn mhn 1 add def
        mhn mhcap gt { mhguard } if
    } walkpath
    /mhstops mhn array def
    /mhi 0 def
    mhpitch {
        6 array astore mhstops mhi 3 -1 roll put
        /mhi mhi 1 add def
    } walkpath
} bind def

%%SelfTest: motion-region-and-budget-guards
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Count 0 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-count-must-be-1-to-5000
%   (/Count 0) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Count 2.5 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-count-must-be-1-to-5000
%   (/Count 2.5) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Count { 5 } >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-count-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Count { 5 }) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Length 0 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-length-must-be-positive
%   (/Length 0) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Angle 91 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-angle-must-be-0-to-90
%   (/Angle 91) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Lean -1 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-lean-must-be-non-negative
%   (/Lean -1) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /LengthJitter 2 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-lengthjitter-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/LengthJitter 2) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Bow 0.9 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-bow-must-be-0-to-0.5
%   (/Bow 0.9) mustguard
%
%   { 7 << /Count 5 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-needs-a-region
%   (a number as the region) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Count 5 >> pkcrisscross }
%   (the documented form) mustpass
%
%   % The grid is bounded by cells, not just by /Count: a thin diagonal sliver
%   % has a bounding box vastly bigger than its area, so keeping /Count
%   % points in it would take millions of cells.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10000 10000 lineto 10000 10001 lineto closepath scpath
%     << /Count 5000 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-too-many-cells
%   (a thin diagonal sliver) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 10000 10000 lineto 10000 10001 lineto closepath scpath
%     << /Count 5000 >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-too-many-cells
%   (a thin diagonal sliver) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Radius 0 >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-radius-must-be-positive
%   (/Radius 0) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Squash 0 >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-squash-must-be-0-exclusive-to-1
%   (/Squash 0) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Count 9999 >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-count-must-be-1-to-5000
%   (/Count 9999) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /RadiusJitter -1 >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-radiusjitter-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/RadiusJitter -1) mustguard
%
%   { << >> << >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-needs-a-region
%   (a dict as the region) mustguard
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkscumblein-light-guards
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Light { 90 } >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-light-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Light { 90 }) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Light /up >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-light-must-be-a-number
%   (/Light /up) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Light 90 /Reach 0 >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-reach-must-be-0-exclusive-to-1
%   (/Reach 0) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Light 90 /Reach 1.5 >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-reach-must-be-0-exclusive-to-1
%   (/Reach 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Light 90 /Feather -0.1 >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-feather-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Feather -0.1) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Light 90 /Feather { 1 } >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-feather-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Feather { 1 }) mustguard
%
%   % /Reach and /Feather say how the lit side is cut; without /Light there
%   % is no lit side, so they would silently do nothing.
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Reach 0.4 >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-reach-needs-light
%   (/Reach without /Light) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Feather 0 >> pkscumblein }
%   /pkscumblein-feather-needs-light
%   (/Feather without /Light) mustguard
%
%   % /Light 0 is a real angle (light from +x), not "unset".
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Light 0 /Reach 1 /Feather 0 >> pkscumblein }
%   (/Light 0 with the full reach) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkscumblein-light-keeps-the-lit-side
%   % Every circle a lit pass keeps sits within /Reach of the edge facing the
%   % light (its centre is the circle's moveto point less its radius, so test
%   % the start point with the radius as slack), and the lit pass is a subset
%   % of the unlit one under the same seed: same draws, fewer circles.
%   { /lt_n 0 def /lt_bad 0 def
%     7 srand 0 0 200 100 screct << /Count 200 /Radius 3 /RadiusJitter 0 >> pkscumblein
%     { pop pop /lt_n lt_n 1 add def } { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%     /lt_all lt_n def /lt_n 0 def
%     7 srand 0 0 200 100 screct
%     << /Count 200 /Radius 3 /RadiusJitter 0 /Light 90 /Reach 0.25 /Feather 0 >> pkscumblein
%     { /lt_n lt_n 1 add def exch pop 100 25 sub 6 sub lt { /lt_bad lt_bad 1 add def } if }
%     { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%     lt_bad 0 ne { lit-circle-outside-the-band } if
%     lt_n 0 eq lt_n lt_all ge or { lit-pass-is-not-a-proper-subset } if
%     % From below the same rectangle: the band is now along the bottom.
%     /lt_bad 0 def
%     7 srand 0 0 200 100 screct
%     << /Count 200 /Radius 3 /RadiusJitter 0 /Light 270 /Reach 0.25 /Feather 0 >> pkscumblein
%     { exch pop 25 6 add gt { /lt_bad lt_bad 1 add def } if }
%     { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%     lt_bad 0 ne { lit-circle-outside-the-bottom-band } if }
%   (the lit pass keeps only the band facing the light) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkcrisscross-lean-and-its-deprecated-spread-alias
%   % /Spread is the deprecated name for /Lean (issue #141) and is still
%   % read, through the same guard, when /Lean is absent.
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Spread -1 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-lean-must-be-non-negative
%   (/Spread -1, the deprecated alias) mustguard
%
%   % When both are given /Lean wins, and /Spread is not even validated.
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Lean 4 /Spread -1 >> pkcrisscross }
%   (/Lean 4 with a negative /Spread) mustpass
%
%   { 0 0 100 100 screct << /Lean -1 /Spread 4 >> pkcrisscross }
%   /pkcrisscross-lean-must-be-non-negative
%   (/Lean -1 with a valid /Spread) mustguard
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: motion-spine-guards
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Radius 0 >> pkscumble }
%   /pkscumble-radius-must-be-positive
%   (/Radius 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Step 0 >> pkscumble }
%   /pkscumble-step-must-be-positive
%   (/Step 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Squash 2 >> pkscumble }
%   /pkscumble-squash-must-be-0-exclusive-to-1
%   (/Squash 2) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto << /Radius 1 /Step 0.2 >> pkscumble }
%   /pkscumble-sample-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a tiny step on a long line) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Spacing 0 >> pktap }
%   /pktap-spacing-must-be-positive
%   (/Spacing 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Length -1 >> pktap }
%   /pktap-length-must-be-non-negative
%   (/Length -1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Scatter -1 >> pktap }
%   /pktap-scatter-must-be-non-negative
%   (/Scatter -1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Lean { 3 } >> pktap }
%   /pktap-lean-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Lean { 3 }) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto << /Spacing 0.1 >> pktap }
%   /pktap-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a tiny spacing on a long line) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Length 0 >> pkpull }
%   /pkpull-length-must-be-positive
%   (/Length 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Spacing -3 >> pkpull }
%   /pkpull-spacing-must-be-positive
%   (/Spacing -3) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Wander 2 >> pkpull }
%   /pkpull-wander-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Wander 2) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Bow 1 >> pkpull }
%   /pkpull-bow-must-be-0-to-0.5
%   (/Bow 1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Scatter -1 >> pkpull }
%   /pkpull-scatter-must-be-non-negative
%   (/Scatter -1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Scatter { 3 } >> pkpull }
%   /pkpull-scatter-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Scatter { 3 }) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Scatter 0 >> pkpull }
%   (/Scatter 0) mustpass
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto << /Spacing 0.1 >> pkpull }
%   /pkpull-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a tiny spacing on a long line) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Amplitude -1 >> pkzigzag }
%   /pkzigzag-amplitude-must-be-non-negative
%   (/Amplitude -1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Wavelength 0 >> pkzigzag }
%   /pkzigzag-wavelength-must-be-positive
%   (/Wavelength 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /WaveJitter 3 >> pkzigzag }
%   /pkzigzag-wavejitter-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/WaveJitter 3) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto << /Wavelength 0.2 >> pkzigzag }
%   /pkzigzag-point-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a tiny wavelength on a long line) mustguard
%
%   % Every spine motion accepts the documented form and an empty path.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << >> pkscumble
%     newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << >> pktap
%     newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << >> pkpull
%     newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << >> pkzigzag
%     newpath << >> pkzigzag }
%   (every spine motion with defaults, and an empty path) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A criss-cross brush motion: short strokes at two crossing headings scattered evenly over a region (artkit's screct or scpath), the sky block-in of wet-on-wet painting. Builds a path of straight or bowed strokes and paints nothing, so it composes with any preset: `region opts pkcrisscross << >> pkbroad`. Replaces the current path. A jittered grid keeps coverage even; deterministic under the caller's own srand with a fixed number of draws per cell.
% @example: 40 300 520 160 screct << /Count 90 /Length 50 /Angle 25 >> pkcrisscross << /Width 30 /Charge 0.7 >> pkbroad
% @param: /Count [count] About how many strokes to lay down, whole number 1..5000; a non-rectangular region keeps about this many (default 40)
% @param: /Length [points] Mean stroke length in points; must be > 0 (default 30)
% @param: /Angle [degrees] Half-opening of the X: strokes run at +Angle and -Angle from the x axis, in degrees 0..90 (default 35)
% @param: /Lean [degrees] Random scatter of each stroke's heading, in degrees; must be >= 0. Formerly /Spread, still accepted as a deprecated alias; /Lean wins if both are given (default 6)
% @param: /LengthJitter [fraction of /Length] 0..1 how far each stroke's length varies around /Length (default 0.3)
% @param: /Bow [fraction of stroke length] 0..0.5 sideways bend of each stroke as a fraction of its length; 0 is straight (default 0)
/pkcrisscross {
    /mxopts exch def /mxreg exch def
    mxreg mhisregion not { pkcrisscross-needs-a-region } if
    /mxcount mxopts /Count 40 pkgetdef def
    /mxcount load xcheck { pkcrisscross-count-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mxcount 1 lt mxcount 5000 gt or mxcount truncate mxcount ne or
        { pkcrisscross-count-must-be-1-to-5000 } if
    /mxlen mxopts /Length 30 pkgetdef def
    /mxlen load xcheck { pkcrisscross-length-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mxlen 0 le { pkcrisscross-length-must-be-positive } if
    /mxangle mxopts /Angle 35 pkgetdef def
    /mxangle load xcheck { pkcrisscross-angle-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mxangle 0 lt mxangle 90 gt or { pkcrisscross-angle-must-be-0-to-90 } if
    % /Lean, or the deprecated /Spread alias when /Lean is absent (issue
    % #141: /Spread elsewhere in this file is a fraction of /Width). The
    % inner pkgetdef resolves the alias, so /Lean wins.
    /mxspread mxopts /Lean mxopts /Spread 6 pkgetdef pkgetdef def
    /mxspread load xcheck { pkcrisscross-lean-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mxspread 0 lt { pkcrisscross-lean-must-be-non-negative } if
    /mxlj mxopts /LengthJitter 0.3 pkgetdef def
    /mxlj load xcheck { pkcrisscross-lengthjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mxlj 0 lt mxlj 1 gt or { pkcrisscross-lengthjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mxbow mxopts /Bow 0 pkgetdef def
    /mxbow load xcheck { pkcrisscross-bow-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mxbow 0 lt mxbow 0.5 gt or { pkcrisscross-bow-must-be-0-to-0.5 } if

    mxreg mxcount mhgrid
    mhcols mhrows mul 40000 gt { pkcrisscross-too-many-cells } if
    newpath
    0 1 mhrows cvi 1 sub {
        /mxr exch def
        0 1 mhcols cvi 1 sub {
            /mxc exch def
            % Six draws per cell whether or not the cell is kept.
            frnd /mxfx exch def frnd /mxfy exch def
            frnd /mxfam exch def frnd /mxfa exch def
            frnd /mxfl exch def frnd /mxfb exch def
            /mxx mhgx0 mxc mxfx add mhcw mul add def
            /mxy mhgy0 mxr mxfy add mhch mul add def
            mxx mxy mhreg scin {
                /mxang mxangle mxfam 0.5 lt { 1 } { -1 } ifelse mul
                    mxfa 2 mul 1 sub mxspread mul add def
                /mxhl mxlen 1 mxfl 2 mul 1 sub mxlj mul add mul 2 div def
                /mxdx mxang cos mxhl mul def
                /mxdy mxang sin mxhl mul def
                /mxsx mxx mxdx sub def /mxsy mxy mxdy sub def
                /mxex mxx mxdx add def /mxey mxy mxdy add def
                mxbow 0 gt {
                    % Controls a third and two thirds along, pushed to one side.
                    /mxoff mxbow mxlen mul mxfb 0.5 lt { 1 } { -1 } ifelse mul def
                    /mxnx mxang sin neg mxoff mul def
                    /mxny mxang cos mxoff mul def
                    mxsx mxsy moveto
                    mxsx mxdx 2 mul 3 div add mxnx add
                    mxsy mxdy 2 mul 3 div add mxny add
                    mxsx mxdx 4 mul 3 div add mxnx add
                    mxsy mxdy 4 mul 3 div add mxny add
                    mxex mxey curveto
                } {
                    mxsx mxsy moveto mxex mxey lineto
                } ifelse
            } if
        } for
    } for
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A scumbling brush motion in a region (artkit's screct or scpath): many small separate circles scattered evenly, the tiny-circles stroke of clouds and bushes. Builds closed circle (or squashed ellipse) subpaths and paints nothing, so it composes with any preset: `region opts pkscumblein << >> pkoil`. Replaces the current path. For a connected chain of overlapping loops along a line use pkscumble.
% @example: newpath 200 440 moveto 300 470 400 470 520 430 curveto scpath << /Count 80 /Radius 7 /Squash 0.6 >> pkscumblein << /Width 8 >> pkoil
% @param: /Count [count] About how many circles to lay down, whole number 1..5000; a non-rectangular region keeps about this many (default 60)
% @param: /Radius [points] Mean circle radius in points; must be > 0 (default 4)
% @param: /RadiusJitter [fraction of /Radius] 0..1 how far each circle's radius varies around /Radius (default 0.4)
% @param: /Squash [ratio] Vertical radius as a fraction of the horizontal one, > 0 and <= 1; 1 is a circle, smaller flattens a cloud's underside (default 1)
% @param: /Light [degrees] Light-aware massing (issue #345): the direction the light comes from, counterclockwise from +x in user space (270 is from below, 90 from above). Set, it keeps only the circles on the region's lit side, a band that follows the outline's lumps along the edge facing the light, so a second pass in a lighter load over a body pass gives the mass a lit side: `region << /Count n >> pkscumblein body-brush  region << /Count n /Light 120 >> pkscumblein lit-brush`. Same circles, positions and random draws as the unlit call with the same seed (default unset: every circle is kept)
% @param: /Reach [fraction of the region's depth along the light] With /Light, how deep the lit band reaches in from the edge facing the light, > 0 and <= 1; the depth is the length of the line through the region's bounding box centre in the light's direction, so 0.3 lights about the outer 30% of a round mass, and a band stays in proportion when the region is resized (default 0.3)
% @param: /Feather [0..1] With /Light, how much of /Reach is a graded border: circles in the inner /Feather part of the band thin out toward the shadow instead of stopping on a hard line; 0 is a hard edge (default 0.5)
/pkscumblein {
    /mcopts exch def /mcreg exch def
    mcreg mhisregion not { pkscumblein-needs-a-region } if
    /mccount mcopts /Count 60 pkgetdef def
    /mccount load xcheck { pkscumblein-count-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mccount 1 lt mccount 5000 gt or mccount truncate mccount ne or
        { pkscumblein-count-must-be-1-to-5000 } if
    /mcrad mcopts /Radius 4 pkgetdef def
    /mcrad load xcheck { pkscumblein-radius-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mcrad 0 le { pkscumblein-radius-must-be-positive } if
    /mcrj mcopts /RadiusJitter 0.4 pkgetdef def
    /mcrj load xcheck { pkscumblein-radiusjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mcrj 0 lt mcrj 1 gt or { pkscumblein-radiusjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mcsq mcopts /Squash 1 pkgetdef def
    /mcsq load xcheck { pkscumblein-squash-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mcsq 0 le mcsq 1 gt or { pkscumblein-squash-must-be-0-exclusive-to-1 } if
    % Light-aware massing (issue #345). Tested with `known`, not by value:
    % /Light 0 is light from +x. Unset, none of this runs and the motion is
    % byte-identical to before.
    /mclit mcopts /Light known def
    mclit {
        /mclight mcopts /Light get def
        /mclight load xcheck { pkscumblein-light-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
        /mclight load type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
            { pkscumblein-light-must-be-a-number } if
        /mcreach mcopts /Reach 0.3 pkgetdef def
        /mcreach load xcheck { pkscumblein-reach-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
        mcreach 0 le mcreach 1 gt or { pkscumblein-reach-must-be-0-exclusive-to-1 } if
        /mcfeather mcopts /Feather 0.5 pkgetdef def
        /mcfeather load xcheck { pkscumblein-feather-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
        mcfeather 0 lt mcfeather 1 gt or { pkscumblein-feather-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    } {
        mcopts /Reach known { pkscumblein-reach-needs-light } if
        mcopts /Feather known { pkscumblein-feather-needs-light } if
    } ifelse

    mcreg mccount mhgrid
    mclit {
        % The region's depth along the light: the length of the line through
        % its bounding box's centre in the light's direction, i.e. the box's
        % width or height, whichever the line crosses first. /Reach is a
        % fraction of it, so the lit band keeps its proportion when the region
        % is resized. (Not the box projected on the light's axis: for a wide,
        % low bush lit at 45 degrees that is nearly twice the real depth.)
        /mclx mclight cos def /mcly mclight sin def
        /mcdepth 1e30
            mclx abs 1e-6 gt { mhbw mclx abs div min } if
            mcly abs 1e-6 gt { mhbh mcly abs div min } if
        def
    } if
    mhcols mhrows mul 40000 gt { pkscumblein-too-many-cells } if
    newpath
    /mcm matrix currentmatrix def
    0 1 mhrows cvi 1 sub {
        /mcr exch def
        0 1 mhcols cvi 1 sub {
            /mcc exch def
            frnd /mcfx exch def frnd /mcfy exch def
            frnd /mcfr exch def frnd /mcph exch def
            /mcx mhgx0 mcc mcfx add mhcw mul add def
            /mcy mhgy0 mcr mcfy add mhch mul add def
            mcx mcy mcreg scin
            mclit {
                % Lit when a step toward the light leaves the region: the
                % circle sits within that step of the edge facing the light,
                % so every lump of the outline gets its own lit cap. The step
                % shrinks by up to /Feather on the cell's own phase draw
                % (uniform, and independent of where the circle lands), which
                % grades the inner border without a new draw.
                {
                    /mcstep mcreach mcdepth mul 1 mcfeather mcph mul sub mul def
                    mcx mclx mcstep mul add mcy mcly mcstep mul add mcreg scin not
                } { false } ifelse
            } if {
                /mcrr mcrad 1 mcfr 2 mul 1 sub mcrj mul add mul 0.1 mcrad mul max def
                /mcpa mcph 360 mul def
                % Build in a translated, squashed space, then put the matrix
                % back: only the path survives, in device space.
                mcx mcy translate 1 mcsq scale
                mcrr mcpa cos mul mcrr mcpa sin mul moveto
                0 0 mcrr mcpa mcpa 360 add arc closepath
                mcm setmatrix
            } if
        } for
    } for
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A scumbling brush motion along a line: the current path is read as a spine and replaced with one continuous chain of overlapping loops per subpath, the circular scrubbing stroke that builds clouds and bushes. Builds a path and paints nothing, so it composes with any preset: `opts pkscumble << >> pkoil`. A one-point subpath becomes a single circle. Deterministic under the caller's own srand.
% @example: newpath 120 430 moveto 300 470 480 450 600 430 curveto << /Radius 9 /Squash 0.7 >> pkscumble << /Width 8 >> pkoil
% @param: /Radius [points] Mean loop radius in points; must be > 0 (default 4)
% @param: /Step [points] Distance the loop advances along the spine per full turn, in points; must be > 0. Loops overlap while it is under 2*pi*Radius, and open into a wave beyond (default 1.6*Radius)
% @param: /RadiusJitter [fraction of /Radius] 0..1 how far each loop's radius varies around /Radius; blended smoothly between loops (default 0.3)
% @param: /Squash [ratio] Radius across the line as a fraction of the radius along it, > 0 and <= 1 (default 1)
/pkscumble {
    /msopts exch def
    /msrad msopts /Radius 4 pkgetdef def
    /msrad load xcheck { pkscumble-radius-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    msrad 0 le { pkscumble-radius-must-be-positive } if
    /msstep msopts /Step msrad 1.6 mul pkgetdef def
    /msstep load xcheck { pkscumble-step-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    msstep 0 le { pkscumble-step-must-be-positive } if
    /msrj msopts /RadiusJitter 0.3 pkgetdef def
    /msrj load xcheck { pkscumble-radiusjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    msrj 0 lt msrj 1 gt or { pkscumble-radiusjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mssq msopts /Squash 1 pkgetdef def
    /mssq load xcheck { pkscumble-squash-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mssq 0 le mssq 1 gt or { pkscumble-squash-must-be-0-exclusive-to-1 } if

    % 24 samples per loop; the walk pitch is one sample's worth of travel.
    msstep 24 div 100000 { pkscumble-sample-count-exceeds-safety-limit } mhcollect
    newpath
    0 1 mhn 1 sub {
        mhstops exch get aload pop
        /msatend exch def /mssp exch def pop /msang exch def /msy exch def /msx exch def
        % Phase follows the distance actually travelled (a loop per /Step), not
        % the stop count: walkpath's guaranteed end stop can sit on top of the
        % last regular one or a fraction of a pitch past it, and counting it as
        % a whole sample added a hook of terminal geometry (Codex round 3).
        msatend 1 and 1 eq { /mscum 0 def /msloop -1 def } { /mscum mscum mssp add def } ifelse
        % One draw per stop; a new loop radius is taken at each loop start.
        frnd /msd exch def
        /msturn mscum msstep div def
        msturn floor /msnl exch def
        msnl msloop gt {
            /msrprev msloop 0 lt { -1 } { msrnext } ifelse def
            /msrnext msrad 1 msd 2 mul 1 sub msrj mul add mul 0.1 msrad mul max def
            msrprev 0 lt { /msrprev msrnext def } if
            /msloop msnl def
        } if
        msatend 3 eq {
            % A lone point: one full circle (squashed in the line's frame,
            % which for a point is the x axis).
            0 1 24 {
                15 mul /msph exch def
                msx msrnext msph cos mul add
                msy msrnext mssq mul msph sin mul add
                msph 0 eq { moveto } { lineto } ifelse
            } for
            closepath
        } {
            % A stop with no travel since the previous one (the end stop of a
            % guide whose length is a whole number of pitches) is the same
            % point again. Zero travel is judged against the pitch, not a fixed distance: an
            % absolute epsilon dropped every stop of a chain drawn at a tiny
            % user-space scale.
            msatend 1 and 1 eq mssp msstep 24 div 0.001 mul gt or {
                /msr msrprev msrnext msrprev sub msturn msnl sub mul add def
                /msph msturn 360 mul def
                msx msang cos msr mul msph cos mul add msang sin neg msr mssq mul mul msph sin mul add
                msy msang sin msr mul msph cos mul add msang cos msr mssq mul mul msph sin mul add
                msatend 1 and 1 eq { moveto } { lineto } ifelse
            } if
        } ifelse
    } for
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A tapping / pouncing brush motion along a line: the current path is read as a spine and replaced with short stabs scattered along it, the foliage and distant-forest stroke. Each tap is a tiny two-point stroke, so it paints with any preset: `opts pktap << >> pkfan`; at /Length 0 each tap is a zero-length stroke that pkdab reads as one clump. Deterministic under the caller's own srand.
% @example: newpath 40 300 moveto 200 330 400 300 600 320 curveto << /Spacing 5 /Length 8 /Scatter 4 >> pktap << /Width 5 /Bristles 10 >> pkfan
% @param: /Spacing [points] Mean distance between taps along the spine, in points; must be > 0 (default 8)
% @param: /Length [points] Length of each stab in points; must be >= 0 (default 6)
% @param: /Direction [degrees] Heading of each stab in degrees counterclockwise from +x, in user space (default 90, straight up)
% @param: /Lean [degrees] Random lean of each stab around /Direction, in degrees; must be >= 0 (default 15)
% @param: /Scatter [points] Random offset of each tap from the spine, in points; must be >= 0 (default 3)
% @param: /LengthJitter [fraction of /Length] 0..1 how far each stab's length varies around /Length (default 0.5)
/pktap {
    /mtopts exch def
    /mtspacing mtopts /Spacing 8 pkgetdef def
    /mtspacing load xcheck { pktap-spacing-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mtspacing 0 le { pktap-spacing-must-be-positive } if
    /mtlen mtopts /Length 6 pkgetdef def
    /mtlen load xcheck { pktap-length-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mtlen 0 lt { pktap-length-must-be-non-negative } if
    /mtdir mtopts /Direction 90 pkgetdef def
    /mtdir load xcheck { pktap-direction-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /mtlean mtopts /Lean 15 pkgetdef def
    /mtlean load xcheck { pktap-lean-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mtlean 0 lt { pktap-lean-must-be-non-negative } if
    /mtscat mtopts /Scatter 3 pkgetdef def
    /mtscat load xcheck { pktap-scatter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mtscat 0 lt { pktap-scatter-must-be-non-negative } if
    /mtlj mtopts /LengthJitter 0.5 pkgetdef def
    /mtlj load xcheck { pktap-lengthjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mtlj 0 lt mtlj 1 gt or { pktap-lengthjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    mtspacing 50000 { pktap-count-exceeds-safety-limit } mhcollect
    newpath
    0 1 mhn 1 sub {
        mhstops exch get aload pop
        /mtatend exch def /mtsp exch def pop pop /mty exch def /mtx exch def
        frnd /mtfx exch def frnd /mtfy exch def
        frnd /mtfl exch def frnd /mtfj exch def
        % The walk's guaranteed final stop can sit right on top of the
        % previous one; a tap there would just double it up.
        mtatend 3 and 2 eq mtsp mtspacing 0.5 mul lt and not {
            /mtpx mtx mtfx 2 mul 1 sub mtscat mul add def
            /mtpy mty mtfy 2 mul 1 sub mtscat mul add def
            /mta mtdir mtfl 2 mul 1 sub mtlean mul add def
            /mtl mtlen 1 mtfj 2 mul 1 sub mtlj mul add mul def
            mtpx mtpy moveto
            mtpx mta cos mtl mul add mtpy mta sin mtl mul add lineto
        } if
    } for
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A pull brush motion from a line: the current path is read as a spine and replaced with strokes that start on it (or within /Scatter of it) and are pulled along a fixed direction -- down from a horizon for reflections, up from a ground line for grass, down a ridge for a knife's "touch and pull" highlights. Builds a path and paints nothing, so it composes with any preset: `opts pkpull << >> pkliner`. Deterministic under the caller's own srand.
% @example: newpath 0 300 moveto 800 300 lineto << /Direction -90 /Length 60 /Spacing 6 >> pkpull << /Width 5 /Charge 0.6 >> pkbroad
% @param: /Length [points] Mean stroke length in points; must be > 0 (default 30)
% @param: /LengthJitter [fraction of /Length] 0..1 how far each stroke's length varies around /Length (default 0.5)
% @param: /Spacing [points] Mean distance between stroke starts along the spine, in points; must be > 0 (default 4)
% @param: /Wander [fraction of /Spacing] 0..1 how far a start slides along the spine from its even slot, as a fraction of /Spacing (default 0.5)
% @param: /Direction [degrees] Heading of every pull in degrees counterclockwise from +x, in user space: -90 pulls down, 90 pushes up (default -90)
% @param: /Lean [degrees] Random lean of each pull around /Direction, in degrees; must be >= 0 (default 6)
% @param: /Bow [fraction of stroke length] 0..0.5 sideways bend of each pull as a fraction of its length, to either side at random; 0 is straight (default 0)
% @param: /Scatter [points] Random offset of each stroke's start from the spine, in points, to either side across it (along its normal; /Wander already slides starts along it); must be >= 0. Unset, every start lies exactly on the spine; set (even to 0) it draws one more random value per stroke (default unset)
/pkpull {
    /mpopts exch def
    /mplen mpopts /Length 30 pkgetdef def
    /mplen load xcheck { pkpull-length-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mplen 0 le { pkpull-length-must-be-positive } if
    /mplj mpopts /LengthJitter 0.5 pkgetdef def
    /mplj load xcheck { pkpull-lengthjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mplj 0 lt mplj 1 gt or { pkpull-lengthjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mpspacing mpopts /Spacing 4 pkgetdef def
    /mpspacing load xcheck { pkpull-spacing-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mpspacing 0 le { pkpull-spacing-must-be-positive } if
    /mpwander mpopts /Wander 0.5 pkgetdef def
    /mpwander load xcheck { pkpull-wander-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mpwander 0 lt mpwander 1 gt or { pkpull-wander-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mpdir mpopts /Direction -90 pkgetdef def
    /mpdir load xcheck { pkpull-direction-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /mplean mpopts /Lean 6 pkgetdef def
    /mplean load xcheck { pkpull-lean-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mplean 0 lt { pkpull-lean-must-be-non-negative } if
    /mpbow mpopts /Bow 0 pkgetdef def
    /mpbow load xcheck { pkpull-bow-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mpbow 0 lt mpbow 0.5 gt or { pkpull-bow-must-be-0-to-0.5 } if
    % /Scatter has no default: unset draws no extra value per stroke, so a
    % render that never names it keeps the random stream it always had.
    /mpscatset mpopts /Scatter known def
    mpscatset {
        /mpscat mpopts /Scatter get def
        /mpscat load xcheck { pkpull-scatter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
        mpscat 0 lt { pkpull-scatter-must-be-non-negative } if
    } if

    % The guide is walked at a sixth of the spacing and every stroke starts
    % on one of those samples. A sample always lies on the flattened guide,
    % so a start can slide along it (by whole samples, clamped to the
    % subpath) without ever cutting a corner or leaving a curve, which
    % interpolating between the even slots did. 120000 samples bound 20000
    % strokes.
    /mpn 6 def
    mpspacing mpn div 120000 { pkpull-count-exceeds-safety-limit } mhcollect
    newpath
    0 1 mhn 1 sub {
        /mpi exch def
        mhstops mpi get 5 get /mpatend exch def
        mpatend 1 and 1 eq {
            % A new subpath: remember where it starts and where it ends.
            /mpbase mpi def
            /mpend mpi def
            {
                mpend mhn 1 sub ge { exit } if
                mhstops mpend get 5 get 2 and 2 eq { exit } if
                /mpend mpend 1 add def
            } loop
        } if
        % A slot is every sixth sample from the start, plus the subpath's end
        % when it is at least half a spacing past the last slot (the end stop
        % a bare walk adds can sit right on top of the previous one).
        /mpr mpend mpbase sub mpn mod def
        mpi mpbase sub mpn mod 0 eq
        mpi mpend eq mpr 0 gt and
            { mpr 1 sub mpspacing mpn div mul mhstops mpend get 4 get add
              mpspacing 0.5 mul ge }
            { false }
        ifelse
        or {
            frnd /mpfw exch def frnd /mpfl exch def
            frnd /mpfj exch def frnd /mpfb exch def
            /mpj mpi mpfw 2 mul 1 sub mpwander mul mpn 2 div mul round cvi add def
            mpj mpbase lt { /mpj mpbase def } if
            mpj mpend gt { /mpj mpend def } if
            mhstops mpj get /mpst exch def
            /mpsx mpst 0 get def /mpsy mpst 1 get def
            mpscatset {
                % Across the spine: along the normal of the stop the start
                % slid to (its tangent angle is element 2, in degrees).
                frnd 2 mul 1 sub mpscat mul /mpsoff exch def
                /mpsx mpsx mpst 2 get sin mpsoff mul sub def
                /mpsy mpsy mpst 2 get cos mpsoff mul add def
            } if
            /mpa mpdir mpfl 2 mul 1 sub mplean mul add def
            /mpl mplen 1 mpfj 2 mul 1 sub mplj mul add mul def
            /mpdx mpa cos mpl mul def /mpdy mpa sin mpl mul def
            mpsx mpsy moveto
            mpbow 0 gt {
                /mpoff mpbow mpl mul mpfb 0.5 lt { 1 } { -1 } ifelse mul def
                /mpnx mpa sin neg mpoff mul def
                /mpny mpa cos mpoff mul def
                % mpdx/mpdy are the whole stroke, so the controls sit at a third
                % and two thirds of it, pushed to one side.
                mpsx mpdx 3 div add mpnx add
                mpsy mpdy 3 div add mpny add
                mpsx mpdx 2 mul 3 div add mpnx add
                mpsy mpdy 2 mul 3 div add mpny add
                mpsx mpdx add mpsy mpdy add curveto
            } {
                mpsx mpdx add mpsy mpdy add lineto
            } ifelse
        } if
    } for
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A zig-zag brush motion along a line: the current path is read as a spine and replaced with one polyline per subpath that alternates to either side of it, the back-and-forth stroke of water and sweeps. It starts and ends on the spine. Builds a path and paints nothing, so it composes with any preset: `opts pkzigzag << >> pkbroad`. Deterministic under the caller's own srand.
% @example: newpath 40 200 moveto 760 200 lineto << /Amplitude 5 /Wavelength 24 >> pkzigzag << /Width 3 /Charge 0.8 >> pkliner
% @param: /Amplitude [points] Distance of each peak from the spine, in points; must be >= 0 (default 6)
% @param: /Wavelength [points] Distance along the spine for one full back-and-forth, in points; must be > 0 (default 18)
% @param: /AmpJitter [fraction of /Amplitude] 0..1 how far each peak's height varies around /Amplitude (default 0.3)
% @param: /WaveJitter [0..1] how far each peak slides along the spine from its even slot (default 0.2)
/pkzigzag {
    /mzopts exch def
    /mzamp mzopts /Amplitude 6 pkgetdef def
    /mzamp load xcheck { pkzigzag-amplitude-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mzamp 0 lt { pkzigzag-amplitude-must-be-non-negative } if
    /mzwave mzopts /Wavelength 18 pkgetdef def
    /mzwave load xcheck { pkzigzag-wavelength-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mzwave 0 le { pkzigzag-wavelength-must-be-positive } if
    /mzaj mzopts /AmpJitter 0.3 pkgetdef def
    /mzaj load xcheck { pkzigzag-ampjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mzaj 0 lt mzaj 1 gt or { pkzigzag-ampjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mzwj mzopts /WaveJitter 0.2 pkgetdef def
    /mzwj load xcheck { pkzigzag-wavejitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mzwj 0 lt mzwj 1 gt or { pkzigzag-wavejitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    % The guide is walked at a sixth of a half wavelength and every vertex is
    % one of those samples, so sliding a vertex (/WaveJitter) moves it along
    % the guide by whole samples and never off it: sliding along the tangent
    % left the guide at every corner (Codex round 4). 300000 samples bound
    % 50000 vertices.
    /mzn 6 def
    mzwave 2 div mzn div 300000 { pkzigzag-point-count-exceeds-safety-limit } mhcollect
    newpath
    /mzk 0 def
    0 1 mhn 1 sub {
        /mzi exch def
        mhstops mzi get /mzst exch def
        mzst 5 get /mzatend exch def
        mzatend 1 and 1 eq {
            % A new subpath: remember where it starts and where it ends.
            /mzbase mzi def /mzk 0 def
            /mzend mzi def
            {
                mzend mhn 1 sub ge { exit } if
                mhstops mzend get 5 get 2 and 2 eq { exit } if
                /mzend mzend 1 add def
            } loop
            mzst 0 get mzst 1 get moveto
            % A lone point is a degenerate stroke, so it still paints.
            mzatend 2 and 2 eq { mzst 0 get mzst 1 get lineto } if
        } {
            mzatend 2 and 2 eq {
                % The line ends on the spine, not on a peak.
                mzst 0 get mzst 1 get lineto
            } {
                % Every sixth sample is a vertex, unless the end is less than
                % half a leg ahead (the guaranteed end stop can sit on top of
                % it or just behind it): that one is dropped, so the line does
                % not end on a peak followed by a jog back to the spine.
                mzi mzbase sub mzn mod 0 eq
                mzend mzi sub 1 sub mzwave 2 div mzn div mul
                    mhstops mzend get 4 get add mzwave 2 div 0.5 mul ge and {
                    frnd /mzfa exch def frnd /mzfw exch def
                    /mzk mzk 1 add def
                    /mzside mzk 2 mod 1 eq { 1 } { -1 } ifelse def
                    /mzoff mzamp 1 mzfa 2 mul 1 sub mzaj mul add mul mzside mul def
                    % At most 2 samples either way, a third of a leg, so
                    % neighbouring vertices keep their order.
                    mhstops mzi mzfw 2 mul 1 sub mzwj mul 2.7 mul truncate cvi add get
                    /mzst exch def
                    mzst 0 get mzst 2 get sin neg mzoff mul add
                    mzst 1 get mzst 2 get cos mzoff mul add
                    lineto
                } if
            } ifelse
        } ifelse
    } for
} bind def

% ============================================================================
% Brushed edges (issue #347)
% ============================================================================
%
% Every big mass in a landscape (a meadow, a near bank, the water between two
% banks) starts as a filled path, and a filled path has a ruled vector edge.
% Paint never has one. A painter breaks it by feathering: short strokes
% dragged *across* the boundary, the mass's own colour pulled out over what is
% behind it and the colour behind cut back into the mass, so the two
% interlock. The edge stays where it was, but it reads as brushed. Near
% things keep a firmer edge than far ones, and an edge is rarely broken all
% the way along: stretches that dissolve and stretches left crisp ("lost and
% found") lead the eye along it.
%
%   opts pkfeather     the motion: strokes across the current path's edge,
%                      along its local normal (paints nothing)
%   opts pkbrushedge   the one-call edge: the backdrop (/Under) cut back in,
%                      then the mass colour (the current colour, or /Mass)
%                      pulled out, each through a brush
%
% A colour key (/Under, /Mass) is an [r g b] array, a pigment name, a
% procedure x y -> r g b, or a region list (issue #456):
%
%   [ [ region colour ] [ region colour ] ... default ]
%
% asked at each stroke's start: the first region holding it gives the colour,
% else the default. A region is artkit's (`screct`, `scpath`, tested with
% `siinside`, nonzero like `fill`) or a procedure that builds a path, made
% into a region once per call. It is a convenience: a procedure that tests a
% region built beforehand with `scin` does the same stroke for stroke.
%
% `pkfeather` follows the motion family's contract (see "Brush motions"
% above): it replaces the current path with the strokes, paints nothing, and
% draws only from frnd: one draw per call (the /CrispSeed default) and a
% fixed four per stroke slot. Which way is
% "out" is decided once per subpath:
%
% * A subpath closed with `closepath` is part of a mass. The mass is the whole
%   current path filled with the nonzero rule, exactly as `fill` would paint
%   it (artkit's `scpath` region), and a few probe points just to the left of
%   the walk ask it which side the paint is on. So a hole, wound either way,
%   is brushed out into the hole, and a mirrored space (`1 -1 scale`) needs
%   nothing special.
% * With two or more closed subpaths (a cloud bank of overlapping blobs) an
%   edge can lie inside the others' mass. A pair of points either side of each
%   stroke's anchor asks the same region, and when both are in the mass the
%   stroke is skipped: the edge is hidden, so brushing it would nick the
%   middle of the mass (issue #381). Its random draws are still taken, so the
%   strokes that remain are the ones the edge would have had. The subpath's
%   side is voted on wherever its edge shows. One closed subpath never asks.
% * An open subpath is an edge with no mass attached (the visible crest of a
%   bank, the far edge of a meadow): its out side is the one that faces
%   /Outward (default 90, up), whichever way the guide was drawn.
%
% `pkbrushedge` keeps the caller's path and graphics state (it works inside
% gsave/grestore, like the presets), so the usual shape is
%
%   MASS setrgbcolor  bankpath fill
%   bankpath << /Under WATER >> pkbrushedge
%
% Scratch prefixes: mf- (pkfeather), pe- (pkbrushedge). pkbrushedge calls
% pkfeather (mf-, and the motions' shared mh- helpers), artkit's scpath, and
% the caller's /Brush, which is usually a preset and so rewrites pk*, po-,
% pb- and the other preset prefixes; pe- is none of those.

%%SelfTest: pkfeather-and-pkbrushedge-guards
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Toward /up >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-toward-must-be-out-in-or-both
%   (/Toward /up) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Toward (out) >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-toward-must-be-out-in-or-both
%   (/Toward as a string) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Length 0 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-length-must-be-positive
%   (/Length 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /LengthJitter 1.5 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-lengthjitter-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/LengthJitter 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Overlap -0.1 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-overlap-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Overlap -0.1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Spacing 0 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-spacing-must-be-positive
%   (/Spacing 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Wander 2 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-wander-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Wander 2) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Lean -1 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-lean-must-be-non-negative
%   (/Lean -1) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Slant 85 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-slant-must-be-minus-80-to-80
%   (/Slant 85) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Bow 0.6 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-bow-must-be-0-to-0.5
%   (/Bow 0.6) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Crisp 1.2 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-crisp-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Crisp 1.2) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /CrispRun 0 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-crisprun-must-be-positive
%   (/CrispRun 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /CrispSeed 2 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-crispseed-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/CrispSeed 2) mustguard
%
%   % The wrapper scales and mirrors keys for its inward pass only when they
%   % are numbers, so a bad one is still named by pkfeather's own guard.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Under [ 1 1 1 ] /Length { 5 } >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkfeather-length-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Length { 5 } with /Under) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Under [ 1 1 1 ] /Overlap { 0.3 } >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkfeather-overlap-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Overlap { 0.3 } with /Under) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Outward { 90 } >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-outward-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Outward { 90 }) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 100000 0 lineto << /Spacing 0.1 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a tiny spacing on a long line) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 100 0 lineto << /Crisp 0.5 /CrispRun 1e-12 >> pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-crisp-stretch-count-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a vanishing /CrispRun) mustguard
%
%   % A closed square whose length is a whole number of strokes gets one
%   % stroke per corner, not a second one where the walk closes.
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 3 0 lineto 3 3 lineto 0 3 lineto closepath
%   << /Spacing 3 /Wander 0 >> pkfeather
%   /zzn 0 def { pop pop /zzn zzn 1 add def } { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%   newpath zzn 4 eq (a closed square gets no doubled seam stroke) mustbe
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 7 pkfeather }
%   /pkfeather-needs-an-options-dict
%   (a number as the options) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << >> pkfeather
%     newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 50 50 lineto closepath << /Toward /both /Crisp 0.5 >> pkfeather
%     newpath << >> pkfeather }
%   (pkfeather with defaults, a closed mass, and an empty path) mustpass
%
%   % Which side is out. Straight strokes starting on the edge (/Overlap 0,
%   % no lean, wander or jitter) must all end outside the mass: a ring's outer
%   % edge outward, its hole's edge into the hole, whichever way each is
%   % wound, and the same under a mirrored CTM.
%   /zzfring { newpath 50 0 moveto 0 0 50 0 360 arc closepath
%              20 0 moveto 0 0 20 360 0 arcn closepath } def
%   /zzfringr { newpath 50 0 moveto 0 0 50 360 0 arcn closepath
%               20 0 moveto 0 0 20 0 360 arc closepath } def
%   /zzfout {   % {ring} -> bool : every stroke end lies outside the mass
%     exec scpath /zzreg exch def
%     << /Overlap 0 /Lean 0 /Wander 0 /LengthJitter 0 /Length 5 >> pkfeather
%     /zzok true def
%     { pop pop } { zzreg scin { /zzok false def } if } { 6 { pop } repeat } { }
%     pathforall newpath zzok } def
%   { zzfring } zzfout (a ring with its hole wound the other way) mustbe
%   { zzfringr } zzfout (the same ring wound the other way round) mustbe
%   gsave 1 -1 scale { zzfring } zzfout grestore (a ring under 1 -1 scale) mustbe
%   % A mass far thinner than the probe step still finds its out side (the
%   % strokes are shorter than the mass is thick, so a wrong side ends in it).
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 100 0 lineto 100 0.1 lineto 0 0.1 lineto closepath
%   scpath /zzreg exch def
%   << /Overlap 0 /Lean 0 /Wander 0 /LengthJitter 0 /Length 0.02 >> pkfeather
%   /zzok true def
%   { pop pop } { zzreg scin { /zzok false def } if } { 6 { pop } repeat } { }
%   pathforall newpath zzok (a 100 x 0.1 sliver) mustbe
%   % Overlapping closed blobs (issue #381): an edge inside the union is
%   % hidden and gets no stroke, so every stroke end of the union's outline
%   % lies outside the union. Two squares that cross; and a small circle
%   % mostly swallowed by a big one, whose visible arc must still brush out.
%   /zzfblobs {   % {blobs} -> bool : every stroke end lies outside the union
%     exec scpath /zzreg exch def
%     << /Overlap 0 /Lean 0 /Wander 0 /LengthJitter 0 /Length 5 >> pkfeather
%     /zzok true def
%     { pop pop } { zzreg scin { /zzok false def } if } { 6 { pop } repeat } { }
%     pathforall newpath zzok } def
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto 40 40 lineto 0 40 lineto closepath
%     20 10 moveto 60 10 lineto 60 50 lineto 20 50 lineto closepath } zzfblobs
%   (two overlapping squares: no stroke on an edge inside the other) mustbe
%   { newpath 50 0 moveto 0 0 50 0 360 arc closepath
%     57 0 moveto 45 0 12 0 360 arc closepath } zzfblobs
%   (a blob mostly swallowed by another still brushes its visible arc out) mustbe
%   % ... down to a bar inside the circle with only its end sticking out: the
%   % subpath's own seven first probes all fall on hidden edge (it starts
%   % where the end falls between them), so it needs the wider search.
%   { newpath 50 0 moveto 0 0 50 0 360 arc closepath
%     3.7 -5 moveto 51 -5 lineto 51 5 lineto 0 5 lineto 0 -5 lineto closepath } zzfblobs
%   (a bar all but swallowed brushes its visible end out) mustbe
%   { newpath 50 0 moveto 0 0 50 0 360 arc closepath
%     57 0 moveto 45 0 12 0 360 arc closepath
%     30 0 moveto 10 0 20 0 360 arc closepath } zzfblobs
%   (three overlapping circles) mustbe
%   gsave 1 -1 scale
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto 40 40 lineto 0 40 lineto closepath
%     20 10 moveto 60 10 lineto 60 50 lineto 20 50 lineto closepath } zzfblobs
%   grestore (two overlapping squares under 1 -1 scale) mustbe
%   % Hidden strokes still draw their four numbers, so the visible strokes
%   % are the ones the blobs would have had; and the hidden edge is simply
%   % not painted: fewer strokes than the two outlines alone would give.
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto 40 40 lineto 0 40 lineto closepath
%   20 10 moveto 60 10 lineto 60 50 lineto 20 50 lineto closepath
%   << /Wander 0 >> pkfeather
%   /zzn 0 def { pop pop /zzn zzn 1 add def } { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%   newpath
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto 40 40 lineto 0 40 lineto closepath << /Wander 0 >> pkfeather
%   /zzm 0 def { pop pop /zzm zzm 1 add def } { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%   newpath zzn zzm 2 mul lt (overlapping squares: strokes only on the visible edge) mustbe
%   % Blobs that do not touch lose nothing: two far-apart squares get exactly
%   % the strokes of one square, twice.
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto 40 40 lineto 0 40 lineto closepath
%   200 0 moveto 240 0 lineto 240 40 lineto 200 40 lineto closepath
%   << /Wander 0 >> pkfeather
%   /zzn 0 def { pop pop /zzn zzn 1 add def } { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 40 0 lineto 40 40 lineto 0 40 lineto closepath << /Wander 0 >> pkfeather
%   /zzm 0 def { pop pop /zzm zzm 1 add def } { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%   newpath zzn zzm 2 mul eq (blobs that do not touch keep every stroke) mustbe
%   % A hole thinner than the probe step is not an edge hidden inside the
%   % mass: its boundary is still brushed (Codex review, round 1). A square
%   % with a hairline slit wound the other way gets the strokes of both.
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 100 0 lineto 100 100 lineto 0 100 lineto closepath
%   << /Wander 0 >> pkfeather
%   /zzsq 0 def { pop pop /zzsq zzsq 1 add def } { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 100 0 lineto 100 100 lineto 0 100 lineto closepath
%   10 50 moveto 10 50.1 lineto 90 50.1 lineto 90 50 lineto closepath
%   << /Wander 0 >> pkfeather
%   /zzsl 0 def { pop pop /zzsl zzsl 1 add def } { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%   newpath zzsl zzsq 40 add gt (a hairline hole in a mass keeps its brushed edge) mustbe
%   % ... and it is brushed into the hole, not into the mass (strokes shorter
%   % than the hole is thick, so a wrong side ends in the mass).
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 100 0 lineto 100 100 lineto 0 100 lineto closepath
%   10 50 moveto 10 50.1 lineto 90 50.1 lineto 90 50 lineto closepath
%   scpath /zzreg exch def
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 100 0 lineto 100 100 lineto 0 100 lineto closepath
%   10 50 moveto 10 50.1 lineto 90 50.1 lineto 90 50 lineto closepath
%   << /Overlap 0 /Lean 0 /Wander 0 /LengthJitter 0 /Length 0.04 >> pkfeather
%   /zzok true def
%   { pop pop } { zzreg scin { /zzok false def } if } { 6 { pop } repeat } { }
%   pathforall newpath zzok (a hairline hole's strokes end outside the mass) mustbe
%   % An open crest drawn right to left still brushes up (/Outward 90).
%   newpath 100 0 moveto 0 0 lineto
%   << /Overlap 0 /Lean 0 /Wander 0 /LengthJitter 0 /Length 5 >> pkfeather
%   /zzok true def
%   { pop pop } { exch pop 5 sub abs 0.001 gt { /zzok false def } if } { 6 { pop } repeat } { }
%   pathforall newpath zzok (an open crest drawn right to left brushes up) mustbe
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /InSpacing 0 >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-inspacing-must-be-positive
%   (/InSpacing 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 7 pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-needs-an-options-dict
%   (a number as the options) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Brush 3 >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-brush-must-be-a-procedure
%   (/Brush 3) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Toward /in >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-sets-toward-itself
%   (/Toward given to the wrapper) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Under [ 0 0 ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-must-be-a-3-element-array
%   (/Under [ 0 0 ]) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Under [ 0 2 0 ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Under [ 0 2 0 ]) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Under /nosuchpaint >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-unknown-pigment-see-PigmentNames
%   (/Under /nosuchpaint) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Under { pop pop 1 2 } >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-procedure-must-return-3-numbers
%   (/Under returning two numbers) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Under { pop pop 0 0 5 } >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-procedure-results-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Under returning 5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /InLength 0 >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-inlength-must-be-positive
%   (/InLength 0) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 50 50 lineto closepath
%     << /Under [ 1 1 1 ] >> pkbrushedge
%     << /Under /titaniumwhite >> pkbrushedge
%     << /Under { pop pop 1 1 1 } >> pkbrushedge
%     << >> pkbrushedge }
%   (pkbrushedge with each form of /Under, and without one) mustpass
%
%   % The wrapper leaves the caller's path and the operand stack as they were.
%   count newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 50 50 lineto closepath
%   << /Under { pop pop 0.5 0.5 0.5 } >> pkbrushedge
%   count 1 sub eq currentpoint 0 eq exch 0 eq and and newpath
%   (the caller's path and operand stack survive) mustbe
%
%   % /Mass (issue #426): the pulled-out colour, in /Under's three forms.
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Mass [ 0 0 ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-mass-must-be-a-3-element-array
%   (/Mass [ 0 0 ]) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Mass [ 0 2 0 ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-mass-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Mass [ 0 2 0 ]) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Mass [ 0 (a) 0 ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-mass-components-must-be-numbers
%   (/Mass [ 0 (a) 0 ]) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Mass /nosuchpaint >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-mass-unknown-pigment-see-PigmentNames
%   (/Mass /nosuchpaint) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Mass { pop pop 1 2 } >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-mass-procedure-must-return-3-numbers
%   (/Mass returning two numbers) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Mass { pop pop 0 0 5 } >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-mass-procedure-results-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Mass returning 5) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Mass { pop pop 0 (a) 0 } >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-mass-procedure-results-must-be-numbers
%   (/Mass returning a string) mustguard
%
%   % A bad /Mass answer mid-call, after a good one has set a stroke's
%   % colour, leaves no gsave open: the caller's colour is back.
%   gsave 0.125 0 0 setrgbcolor /zzq 0 def mark
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 50 50 lineto closepath
%     << /Mass { pop pop /zzq zzq 1 add def zzq 1 eq { 1 1 1 } { 1 2 } ifelse } >>
%     pkbrushedge } stopped /zzst exch def cleartomark
%   zzst currentrgbcolor pop pop 0.125 sub abs 0.01 lt and zzq 2 eq and grestore
%   (a bad /Mass answer mid-call unwinds every gsave) mustbe
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 50 50 lineto closepath
%     << /Mass [ 0.2 0.3 0.1 ] >> pkbrushedge
%     << /Mass /sapgreen /Under /titaniumwhite >> pkbrushedge
%     << /Mass { pop pop 0.2 0.3 0.1 } /Under { pop pop 1 1 1 } >> pkbrushedge }
%   (pkbrushedge with each form of /Mass) mustpass
%
%   % An array /Mass is the colour the outward strokes are painted in.
%   /zzmok true def
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 50 50 lineto closepath
%   << /Mass [ 0.2 0.4 0.6 ] /Brush { currentrgbcolor 0.6 sub abs 0.01 gt
%        exch 0.4 sub abs 0.01 gt or exch 0.2 sub abs 0.01 gt or
%        { /zzmok false def } if newpath } >> pkbrushedge
%   newpath zzmok (an array /Mass colours the outward strokes) mustbe
%
%   % A procedural /Mass is asked once per outward stroke, at its start:
%   % each /Brush call paints one stroke, in the colour the procedure gives
%   % at that stroke's first point (a gradient along x here).
%   /zzmf { 100 div 0 max 1 min } def
%   /zzmc 0 def /zzmb 0 def /zzmok true def
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 100 0 lineto 100 40 lineto 0 40 lineto closepath
%   << /Mass { /zzmc zzmc 1 add def pop zzmf 0 0.5 }
%      /Brush { /zzmb zzmb 1 add def /zzm1 null def /zzmm 0 def
%        { /zzmm zzmm 1 add def zzm1 null eq { 2 array astore /zzm1 exch def } { pop pop } ifelse }
%        { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%        zzmm 1 ne { /zzmok false def } if
%        currentrgbcolor pop pop zzm1 0 get zzmf sub abs 0.01 gt { /zzmok false def } if
%        newpath } >> pkbrushedge
%   newpath zzmok zzmb 20 gt and zzmc zzmb eq and
%   (a procedural /Mass colours each outward stroke where it starts) mustbe
%
%   % A region list (issue #456): [ [ region colour ] ... default ], for
%   % /Under and /Mass alike.
%   /zzrp { 0 0 moveto 10 0 lineto 10 10 lineto closepath } def
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Under [ [ /zzrp load [ 1 1 1 ] ] ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-regions-need-a-region-and-a-default-colour
%   (/Under region list without a default) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Under [ [ /zzrp load [ 1 1 1 ] ] [ /zzrp load [ 0 0 0 ] ] ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-regions-must-end-with-a-default-colour
%   (/Under region list ending in a pair) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Under [ [ /zzrp load [ 1 1 1 ] 3 ] [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-region-entries-must-be-region-colour-pairs
%   (/Under region entry of three elements) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Under [ [ /zzrp load [ 1 1 1 ] ] 7 [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-region-entries-must-be-region-colour-pairs
%   (/Under region entry that is a number) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Under [ [ (box) [ 1 1 1 ] ] [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-region-must-be-a-region-or-a-path-procedure
%   (/Under region that is a string) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Under [ [ /zzrp load [ 0 2 0 ] ] [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-components-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Under region colour out of range) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Under [ [ /zzrp load [ 1 1 1 ] ] /nosuchpaint ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-unknown-pigment-see-PigmentNames
%   (/Under region default an unknown pigment) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Under [ [ /zzrp load [ [ /zzrp load [ 1 1 1 ] ] [ 0 0 0 ] ] ] [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-under-region-colours-must-not-be-region-lists
%   (/Under region list nested in a region colour) mustguard
%
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 50 50 lineto closepath
%     << /Mass [ [ /zzrp load { pop pop 1 2 } ] [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   /pkbrushedge-mass-procedure-must-return-3-numbers
%   (/Mass region colour procedure returning two numbers) mustguard
%
%   % A {path} region that fails while being built unwinds its gsave, and
%   % a good one leaves the caller's path and operand stack alone.
%   % (The error leaves peregions' own `[` mark, so the stack is cut back
%   % by count, not cleartomark.)
%   gsave 0.125 0 0 setrgbcolor count /zzc0 exch def
%   { newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%     << /Under [ [ { 0.9 0.9 0.9 setrgbcolor 0 0 moveto zznosuchop } [ 1 1 1 ] ] [ 0 0 0 ] ] >> pkbrushedge }
%   stopped /zzst exch def count zzc0 sub { pop } repeat
%   zzst currentrgbcolor pop pop 0.125 sub abs 0.01 lt and grestore
%   (a failing region path unwinds its gsave) mustbe
%
%   count newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto 50 50 lineto closepath
%   << /Under [ [ { 5 5 moveto 9 5 lineto 9 9 lineto closepath } /titaniumwhite ] [ 0 0 1 ] ]
%      /Mass [ [ 0 0 10 10 screct { pop pop 0 1 0 } ] [ 1 0 0 ] ] >> pkbrushedge
%   count 1 sub eq currentpoint 0 eq exch 0 eq and and newpath
%   (a region list leaves the caller's path and operand stack alone) mustbe
%
%   % Each stroke is painted in the colour of the first region its start
%   % lies in, else the default (a procedure, asked at the start). The
%   % mass is a closed box, so pkfeather builds its own scpath region too.
%   % Region 1, a {path}: x < 50, colour A. Region 2, a screct: y < 20,
%   % colour C (it overlaps region 1, which comes first). Elsewhere B, red
%   % rising with x. Starts within a point of a boundary are not judged.
%   /zzrA [ 0.1 0.9 0.1 ] def /zzrC [ 0.9 0.9 0.1 ] def
%   /zzrB { pop 200 div 0 max 1 min 0 0.5 } def
%   /zzrL [ [ { -60 -60 moveto 50 -60 lineto 50 100 lineto -60 100 lineto closepath } zzrA ]
%           [ -60 -60 300 80 screct zzrC ] /zzrB load ] def
%   /zzrok true def /zzrn 0 def /zzra 0 def /zzrb 0 def /zzrc 0 def
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 100 0 lineto 100 40 lineto 0 40 lineto closepath
%   << /Under zzrL /Mass zzrL
%      /Brush { /zzrn zzrn 1 add def /zzr1 null def
%        { zzr1 null eq { 2 array astore /zzr1 exch def } { pop pop } ifelse }
%        { pop pop } { 6 { pop } repeat } { } pathforall
%        zzr1 aload pop /zzry exch def /zzrx exch def
%        zzrx 50 sub abs 1 gt zzry 20 sub abs 1 gt and {
%          zzrx 50 lt { zzrA aload pop /zzra zzra 1 add def }
%            { zzry 20 lt { zzrC aload pop /zzrc zzrc 1 add def }
%              { zzrx zzry zzrB /zzrb zzrb 1 add def } ifelse } ifelse
%          3 array astore /zzre exch def
%          currentrgbcolor zzre 2 get sub abs 0.01 gt
%          exch zzre 1 get sub abs 0.01 gt or
%          exch zzre 0 get sub abs 0.01 gt or { /zzrok false def } if
%        } if
%        newpath } >> pkbrushedge
%   newpath zzrok zzra 0 gt and zzrb 0 gt and zzrc 0 gt and
%   (a region list colours each stroke by the first region it starts in) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest

% x y -> bool   is the point inside the mass (mfreg)?
% @internal
/mfinmass { mfreg siinside } bind def

% n -> u   a number in [0, 1) that depends only on n and mfseed: the crisp
% stretches' own generator, so they do not move with the frnd stream.
% @internal
/mfu { 12.9898 mul mfseed 36000 mul add sin 43758.5453 mul abs dup floor sub } bind def

% -> len   the length of crisp/brushed stretch number mfiv of subpath mfsub,
% in the state mfbrushing says: /CrispRun * (1 - /Crisp) brushed or
% /CrispRun * /Crisp crisp on average, each 0.4 to 1.6 times that.
% @internal
/mfivlen {
    mfrun mfbrushing { 1 mfcrisp sub } { mfcrisp } ifelse mul
    mfsub 7919 mul mfiv 2 mul add 2 add mfu 1.2 mul 0.4 add mul
} bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: A feathering brush motion across a mass's edge: the current path is read as the edge of a filled mass and replaced with short strokes that cross it along the local normal, so a ruled vector edge can be brushed instead. /Toward /out starts each stroke inside the mass and pulls it out (paint it in the mass's colour); /in starts outside and pulls in (paint it in the colour behind); /both alternates. A subpath closed with closepath is a mass edge, and the mass itself (the whole path filled nonzero, as fill would) decides which side is out, holes included, and with two or more closed subpaths an edge lying inside the others' mass (overlapping blobs) gets no stroke; an open subpath is a bare edge whose out side faces /Outward. Builds a path and paints nothing, so it composes with any preset: `opts pkfeather << >> pkbroad`. pkbrushedge does both directions and both colours in one call. Deterministic under the caller's own srand.
% @example: newpath 0 120 moveto 120 150 240 110 360 135 curveto << /Length 12 /Spacing 3 /Slant 15 >> pkfeather << /Width 2 /Charge 0.8 /Depletion 0.8 >> pkbroad
% @param: /Toward [name] /out, /in or /both: which way each stroke is pulled across the edge; /out starts in the mass and ends outside it, /in the reverse, /both alternates stroke by stroke (default /out)
% @param: /Length [points] Mean stroke length in points; must be > 0 (default 8)
% @param: /LengthJitter [fraction of /Length] 0..1 how far each stroke's length varies around /Length (default 0.6)
% @param: /Overlap [fraction of stroke length] 0..1 how much of each stroke lies on the side it starts from, before it crosses the edge; 0 starts on the edge (default 0.35)
% @param: /Spacing [points] Mean distance between strokes along the edge, in points; must be > 0 (default 3)
% @param: /Wander [fraction of /Spacing] 0..1 how far a stroke slides along the edge from its even slot (default 0.5)
% @param: /Lean [degrees] Random lean of each stroke around its heading, in degrees; must be >= 0 (default 30)
% @param: /Slant [degrees] A fixed tilt of every stroke away from the edge's normal, counterclockwise, -80..80: a meadow's grass bent by the wind, a bank dragged one way (default 0)
% @param: /Bow [fraction of stroke length] 0..0.5 sideways bend of each stroke as a fraction of its length, to either side at random; 0 is straight (default 0)
% @param: /Crisp [0..1] the share of the edge left crisp, in stretches: brushed (lost) stretches alternate with untouched (found) ones, as a painter's edges do, rather than the edge being broken evenly all along; 0 brushes all of it (default 0)
% @param: /CrispRun [points] Mean length of one crisp stretch plus the brushed stretch after it, in points; must be > 0. Only matters with /Crisp above 0 (default 60)
% @param: /CrispSeed [0..1] where the crisp stretches fall: they depend only on this seed and the arc length along each subpath, so two calls with one seed leave the same stretches crisp at any spacing; pkbrushedge gives both its passes one seed this way (default one draw from frnd, taken whether or not the key is given)
% @param: /Outward [degrees] For an open subpath only: the direction that is out of the mass, counterclockwise from +x in user space; each open subpath brushes out on whichever side of it faces this way. Closed subpaths ignore it (default 90, up)
/pkfeather {
    /mfopts exch def
    /mfopts load type /dicttype ne { pkfeather-needs-an-options-dict } if
    /mftoward mfopts /Toward /out pkgetdef def
    /mftoward load type /nametype ne { pkfeather-toward-must-be-out-in-or-both } if
    /mftoward load /out ne /mftoward load /in ne and /mftoward load /both ne and
        { pkfeather-toward-must-be-out-in-or-both } if
    /mflen mfopts /Length 8 pkgetdef def
    /mflen load xcheck { pkfeather-length-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mflen 0 le { pkfeather-length-must-be-positive } if
    /mflj mfopts /LengthJitter 0.6 pkgetdef def
    /mflj load xcheck { pkfeather-lengthjitter-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mflj 0 lt mflj 1 gt or { pkfeather-lengthjitter-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mfover mfopts /Overlap 0.35 pkgetdef def
    /mfover load xcheck { pkfeather-overlap-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mfover 0 lt mfover 1 gt or { pkfeather-overlap-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mfspacing mfopts /Spacing 3 pkgetdef def
    /mfspacing load xcheck { pkfeather-spacing-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mfspacing 0 le { pkfeather-spacing-must-be-positive } if
    /mfwander mfopts /Wander 0.5 pkgetdef def
    /mfwander load xcheck { pkfeather-wander-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mfwander 0 lt mfwander 1 gt or { pkfeather-wander-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mflean mfopts /Lean 30 pkgetdef def
    /mflean load xcheck { pkfeather-lean-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mflean 0 lt { pkfeather-lean-must-be-non-negative } if
    /mfslant mfopts /Slant 0 pkgetdef def
    /mfslant load xcheck { pkfeather-slant-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mfslant -80 lt mfslant 80 gt or { pkfeather-slant-must-be-minus-80-to-80 } if
    /mfbow mfopts /Bow 0 pkgetdef def
    /mfbow load xcheck { pkfeather-bow-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mfbow 0 lt mfbow 0.5 gt or { pkfeather-bow-must-be-0-to-0.5 } if
    /mfcrisp mfopts /Crisp 0 pkgetdef def
    /mfcrisp load xcheck { pkfeather-crisp-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mfcrisp 0 lt mfcrisp 1 gt or { pkfeather-crisp-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mfrun mfopts /CrispRun 60 pkgetdef def
    /mfrun load xcheck { pkfeather-crisprun-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mfrun 0 le { pkfeather-crisprun-must-be-positive } if
    % The seed is drawn whether or not it is given, so giving it moves
    % nothing else.
    frnd /mfseed0 exch def
    /mfseed mfopts /CrispSeed mfseed0 pkgetdef def
    /mfseed load xcheck { pkfeather-crispseed-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    mfseed 0 lt mfseed 1 gt or { pkfeather-crispseed-must-be-0-to-1 } if
    /mfoutward mfopts /Outward 90 pkgetdef def
    /mfoutward load xcheck { pkfeather-outward-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    /mfoutward load type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
        { pkfeather-outward-must-be-a-number } if

    % Which subpaths were closed with closepath, and, if any was, the mass
    % they bound: the whole path as fill would paint it. Both read the path
    % before mhcollect walks it (walkpath leaves it in place, flattened).
    pkscanclosed /mfclosed exch def
    /mfanyclosed false def
    mfclosed { { /mfanyclosed true def } if } forall
    mfanyclosed { scpath /mfreg exch def } if
    % Two or more closed subpaths may overlap (a cloud bank built from
    % several blobs): an edge lying inside the others' mass is hidden, and
    % its strokes are skipped (issue #381). One closed subpath is its own
    % whole mass, so it never pays for the test.
    /mfncl 0 def
    mfclosed { { /mfncl mfncl 1 add def } if } forall
    /mfmulti mfncl 2 ge def

    % The edge is walked at a sixth of the spacing and every stroke sits on
    % one of those samples, as in pkpull: a stroke can slide along the edge
    % by whole samples without leaving it. 120000 samples bound 20000 strokes.
    /mfn 6 def
    mfspacing mfn div 120000 { pkfeather-count-exceeds-safety-limit } mhcollect

    % The crisp stretches are walked along the whole edge, so their count is
    % bounded too: a tiny /CrispRun on a long edge would otherwise loop for
    % ever (or stall, once a stretch is below the arc length's precision).
    % The shortest stretch is 0.4 of the shorter mean.
    mfcrisp 0 gt mfcrisp 1 lt and {
        0 0 1 mhn 1 sub { mhstops exch get 4 get add } for
        mfrun mfcrisp 1 mfcrisp sub min mul 0.4 mul 100000 mul gt
            { pkfeather-crisp-stretch-count-exceeds-safety-limit } if
    } if

    % Each subpath's out side, once for the whole subpath: +1 when out is to
    % the left of the walk, -1 to the right. A one-sided rule per stop would
    % flip at every wiggle of an open guide and at every corner of a mass.
    [ 0 1 mhn 1 sub {
        dup mhstops exch get 5 get 1 and 1 ne { pop } if
    } for ] /mfstarts exch def
    /mfsigns mfstarts length array def
    0 1 mfstarts length 1 sub {
        /mfs exch def
        /mfb mfstarts mfs get def
        /mfe mfs mfstarts length 1 sub lt { mfstarts mfs 1 add get 1 sub } { mhn 1 sub } ifelse def
        mfs mfclosed length lt { mfclosed mfs get } { false } ifelse {
            % Seven probes (more with overlapping blobs, below) spread along the
            % subpath, each a pair of points a small step to either side of the walk; the majority says which
            % side the paint is on. A probe only counts when exactly one of
            % its points is in the mass. Otherwise the step jumped right
            % across a thin mass (or landed on a corner), so it is halved
            % and tried again: the answer depends on the mass, never on the
            % stroke spacing (Codex review, round 1).
            /mfleft 0 def /mfvotes 0 def
            % With overlapping blobs much of the subpath can be hidden inside
            % the others, and a hidden probe does not vote, so it is probed at
            % 28 places, all of them counted: a blob all but swallowed still
            % gets its vote from the arc that shows.
            /mfnp mfmulti { 28 } { 7 } ifelse def
            0 1 mfnp 1 sub {
                mfe mfb sub mfnp div exch 0.5 add mul round cvi mfb add
                mhstops exch get /mfst exch def
                mfst 5 get 3 ne {
                    /mfnx mfst 2 get 90 add cos def
                    /mfny mfst 2 get 90 add sin def
                    /mfeps mfspacing mfn div 0.5 mul def
                    /mfeps0 mfeps def
                    40 {
                        mfst 0 get mfnx mfeps mul add mfst 1 get mfny mfeps mul add
                        mfinmass /mfpl exch def
                        mfst 0 get mfnx mfeps mul sub mfst 1 get mfny mfeps mul sub
                        mfinmass /mfpr exch def
                        mfpl mfpr ne {
                            /mfvotes mfvotes 1 add def
                            mfpl { /mfleft mfleft 1 add def } if
                            exit
                        } if
                        % Both in the mass, with overlapping blobs: a hidden
                        % edge, or a hole thinner than the step. One try at a
                        % sixty-fourth of the step tells them apart; halving
                        % 40 times over a hidden edge would not.
                        mfpl mfpr and mfmulti and {
                            mfeps mfeps0 lt { exit } if
                            /mfeps mfeps0 64 div def
                        } {
                            /mfeps mfeps 0.5 mul def
                        } ifelse
                    } repeat
                } if
            } for
            mfsigns mfs mfleft mfvotes 2 div gt { -1 } { 1 } ifelse put
        } {
            % An open edge: out is the side whose normal, summed along the
            % walk, faces /Outward.
            /mfdot 0 def
            mfb 1 mfe {
                mhstops exch get 2 get 90 add mfoutward sub cos
                /mfdot exch mfdot add def
            } for
            mfsigns mfs mfdot 0 ge { 1 } { -1 } ifelse put
        } ifelse
    } for

    newpath
    /mfsub -1 def /mfk 0 def
    0 1 mhn 1 sub {
        /mfi exch def
        mhstops mfi get 5 get /mfatend exch def
        mfatend 1 and 1 eq {
            /mfsub mfsub 1 add def
            /mfbase mfi def
            /mfend mfi def
            {
                mfend mhn 1 sub ge { exit } if
                mhstops mfend get 5 get 2 and 2 eq { exit } if
                /mfend mfend 1 add def
            } loop
            /mfisclosed mfsub mfclosed length lt { mfclosed mfsub get } { false } ifelse def
            /mfsgn mfsigns mfsub get def
            % The crisp stretches of this subpath: a function of the arc
            % length from its start and the seed alone, so two calls with one
            % seed (pkbrushedge's two passes) leave the same stretches crisp
            % whatever their spacing.
            /mfarc 0 def /mfiv 0 def
            /mftot 0 mfbase 1 add 1 mfend { mhstops exch get 4 get add } for def
            /mfbrushing mfsub 7919 mul mfu mfcrisp ge def
            % Start partway into the first stretch, so subpaths do not all
            % begin on a stretch boundary.
            /mfnext mfivlen mfsub 7919 mul 1 add mfu mul def
        } {
            /mfarc mfarc mhstops mfi get 4 get add def
        } ifelse
        mfcrisp 0 gt mfcrisp 1 lt and {
            { mfarc mfnext le { exit } if
              /mfbrushing mfbrushing not def
              /mfiv mfiv 1 add def
              /mfnext mfnext mfivlen add def } loop
        } if
        % A slot is every sixth sample, plus an open subpath's end when it is
        % at least half a spacing past the last slot. A closed subpath ends
        % where it started, so no slot falls within half a spacing of its
        % end: one there doubled the stroke at the start (Codex round 2; the
        % walk's own end stop can repeat the last regular one, so "the end
        % stop" alone was not enough).
        /mfr mfend mfbase sub mfn mod def
        mfi mfbase sub mfn mod 0 eq
        mfisclosed mfi mfbase gt and { mftot mfarc sub mfspacing 0.5 mul ge and } if
        mfi mfend eq mfr 0 gt and mfisclosed not and
            { mfr 1 sub mfspacing mfn div mul mhstops mfend get 4 get add
              mfspacing 0.5 mul ge }
            { false }
        ifelse
        or {
            % Four draws per slot, brushed or crisp.
            frnd /mffw exch def frnd /mffl exch def
            frnd /mffj exch def frnd /mffb exch def
            mfbrushing {
                /mfj mfi mffw 2 mul 1 sub mfwander mul mfn 2 div mul round cvi add def
                mfj mfbase lt { /mfj mfbase def } if
                mfj mfend gt { /mfj mfend def } if
                mhstops mfj get /mfst exch def
                % An edge hidden inside the other blobs is not brushed: both
                % points of a pair just either side of the stroke's anchor
                % are in the mass. They are asked at the probe step and, if
                % both are in, once more at a sixty-fourth of it: a hole
                % thinner than the step has its far side within that
                % (Codex review, round 1), while a genuinely hidden edge
                % stays inside however small the step.
                /mfhide false def
                mfmulti mfisclosed and mfst 5 get 3 ne and {
                    /mfhide true def
                    [ 1 0.015625 ] {
                        mfspacing mfn div 0.5 mul mul /mfhe exch def
                        mfst 0 get mfst 2 get 90 add cos mfhe mul add
                        mfst 1 get mfst 2 get 90 add sin mfhe mul add mfinmass
                        mfst 0 get mfst 2 get 90 add cos mfhe mul sub
                        mfst 1 get mfst 2 get 90 add sin mfhe mul sub mfinmass
                        and not { /mfhide false def exit } if
                    } forall
                } if
                mfhide not {
                % A lone point has no edge direction: it is pulled along
                % /Outward itself.
                /mfnorm mfst 5 get 3 eq
                    { mfoutward }
                    { mfst 2 get 90 mfsgn mul add }
                ifelse def
                /mfa mfnorm mfslant add mffl 2 mul 1 sub mflean mul add def
                /mftoward load /in eq
                /mftoward load /both eq mfk 2 mod 1 eq and
                or { /mfa mfa 180 add def } if
                /mfl mflen 1 mffj 2 mul 1 sub mflj mul add mul def
                /mfdx mfa cos mfl mul def /mfdy mfa sin mfl mul def
                /mfsx mfst 0 get mfdx mfover mul sub def
                /mfsy mfst 1 get mfdy mfover mul sub def
                mfsx mfsy moveto
                mfbow 0 gt {
                    /mfoff mfbow mfl mul mffb 0.5 lt { 1 } { -1 } ifelse mul def
                    /mfnx mfa sin neg mfoff mul def
                    /mfny mfa cos mfoff mul def
                    mfsx mfdx 3 div add mfnx add
                    mfsy mfdy 3 div add mfny add
                    mfsx mfdx 2 mul 3 div add mfnx add
                    mfsy mfdy 2 mul 3 div add mfny add
                    mfsx mfdx add mfsy mfdy add curveto
                } {
                    mfsx mfdx add mfsy mfdy add lineto
                } ifelse
                } if
            } if
            /mfk mfk 1 add def
        } if
    } for
} bind def

% x y -> -   (a colour procedure, /Under or /Mass: ask pecolp for the colour
% at x y, check the answer, and set it as the colour). The procedure runs
% with nothing of pkbrushedge's on the operand stack; a bad answer is named
% under the key's guard prefix pecolx, such as (pkbrushedge-under).
% @internal
/pecolat {
    count 2 sub /pec0 exch def
    pecolp
    count pec0 sub 3 ne {
        count pec0 sub dup 0 gt { { pop } repeat } { pop } ifelse
        pecolx (-procedure-must-return-3-numbers) pugrd
    } if
    3 array astore /peur exch def
    0 1 2 {
        peur exch get dup type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
            { pop pecolx (-procedure-results-must-be-numbers) pugrd } if
        dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or
            { pecolx (-procedure-results-must-be-0-to-1) pugrd } if
    } for
    peur aload pop setrgbcolor
} bind def

% opts /Key (prefix) -> value mode   (one colour key, /Under or /Mass:
% mode 0 unset (value null), 1 an [r g b] array (a pigment name resolved),
% 2 a procedure x y -> r g b. Guards are named with the prefix, such as
% pkbrushedge-under-must-be-a-3-element-array.)
% @internal
/pecolkey {
    /pekp exch def /pekk exch def /peko exch def
    peko pekk known not { null 0 } {
        /pekv peko pekk get def
        /pekv load peislist {
            % A region list (issue #456): handed whole to peregions, which
            % needs nothing of this call's scratch afterwards.
            pekv pekp peregions 3
        } {
        /pekv load xcheck {
            /pekv load type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or not
                { pekp (-must-be-a-3-element-array) pugrd } if
            /pekv load 2
        } {
            /pekv load type /nametype eq { /pekv pekp pekv pupig def } if
            /pekv load type /arraytype ne { pekp (-must-be-a-3-element-array) pugrd } if
            pekv length 3 ne { pekp (-must-be-a-3-element-array) pugrd } if
            0 1 2 {
                pekv exch get dup type dup /integertype ne exch /realtype ne and
                    { pekp (-components-must-be-numbers) pugrd } if
                dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or { pekp (-components-must-be-0-to-1) pugrd } if
            } for
            pekv 1
        } ifelse
        } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def

% any -> bool   (a region list's shape: a literal array whose first element
% is not a number, as `[ [ region colour ] ... colour ]` is and `[ r g b ]`
% is not; issue #456)
% @internal
/peislist {
    dup xcheck { pop false } {
        dup type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or {
            dup length 0 gt { 0 get penum not } { pop false } ifelse
        } { pop false } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def

% list (prefix) -> entries   (a region list checked and made ready: each
% [ region colour ] pair becomes [ region mode value ] and the default
% colour at the end [ null mode value ], in pecolkey's colour modes 1 and 2.
% A {path} region is built here, once, in the call's user space, inside its
% own gsave so the caller's path survives; one that fails unwinds that
% gsave and passes its error on.)
% @internal
/peregions {
    /perp exch def /perl exch def
    perl length 2 lt { perp (-regions-need-a-region-and-a-default-colour) pugrd } if
    perl dup length 1 sub get peislist
        { perp (-regions-must-end-with-a-default-colour) pugrd } if
    [
    0 1 perl length 1 sub {
        /peri exch def
        peri perl length 1 sub lt {
            perl peri get /pere exch def
            /pere load xcheck { perp (-region-entries-must-be-region-colour-pairs) pugrd } if
            /pere load type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or not
                { perp (-region-entries-must-be-region-colour-pairs) pugrd } if
            pere length 2 ne { perp (-region-entries-must-be-region-colour-pairs) pugrd } if
            pere 0 get dup mhisregion {
                % a region dict (artkit's screct or scpath), used as is
            } {
                dup xcheck
                1 index type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or and {
                    gsave
                    { newpath exec scpath } stopped { grestore stop } if
                    grestore
                } {
                    pop perp (-region-must-be-a-region-or-a-path-procedure) pugrd
                } ifelse
            } ifelse
            pere 1 get
        } {
            null perl peri get
        } ifelse
        % region colour -> [ region mode value ]
        /pecv exch def
        /pecv load peislist
            { perp (-region-colours-must-not-be-region-lists) pugrd } if
        << /C /pecv load >> /C perp pecolkey exch 3 array astore
    } for
    ]
} bind def

% x y entries -> r g b   (the colour of the first entry whose region holds
% x y, or the default's: an array's components, or a procedure's answer at
% x y; pecolat checks it)
% @internal
/pereglook {
    /perl exch def /pery exch def /perx exch def
    perl {
        dup 0 get dup null eq { pop true } { perx pery 3 -1 roll siinside } ifelse
        { exit } if
        pop
    } forall
    dup 1 get 1 eq
        { 2 get aload pop }
        { perx pery 3 -1 roll 2 get exec }
    ifelse
} bind def

% -> -   (the current path is pkfeather's strokes: paint each with one
% /Brush call, in the colour pecolp gives at the stroke's start; see pecolat)
% The strokes are read back from the path first:
% [ /m x y /l x y ] or [ /m x y /c x1 y1 x2 y2 x3 y3 ] each.
% @internal
/pestrokes {
    [ { /m 3 1 roll } { /l 3 1 roll } { /c 7 1 roll } { } pathforall ]
    /pesegs exch def
    /pei 0 def
    {
        pei pesegs length ge { exit } if
        pesegs pei get /m eq {
            pesegs pei 1 add get pesegs pei 2 add get pecolat
            newpath pesegs pei 1 add get pesegs pei 2 add get moveto
            /pei pei 3 add def
            pei pesegs length lt {
                pesegs pei get /l eq {
                    pesegs pei 1 add get pesegs pei 2 add get lineto
                    /pei pei 3 add def
                } {
                    pesegs pei get /c eq {
                        pesegs pei 1 add 6 getinterval aload pop curveto
                        /pei pei 7 add def
                    } if
                } ifelse
            } if
            pebrush
        } {
            /pei pei 1 add def
        } ifelse
    } loop
} bind def

% any -> bool   an integer or a real?
% @internal
/penum { type dup /integertype eq exch /realtype eq or } bind def

% /Toward -> dict   (a copy of the caller's options with /Toward set, and
% /Length scaled by peinlen for the inward pass; the caller's dict is never
% written)
% @internal
/peopts {
    /petow exch def
    peopts0 length 2 add dict
    peopts0 { 2 index 3 1 roll put } forall
    dup /Toward petow put
    % The inward pass mirrors the outward one: /Overlap counts from the side
    % a stroke starts on, so 1 - /Overlap puts an inward stroke over the
    % same stretch of the normal an outward one covers. Fewer of them, so
    % the backdrop nicks the edge rather than ruling a pale band inside it.
    % One seed for both passes, so their crisp stretches line up.
    dup /CrispSeed known not { dup /CrispSeed peseed put } if
    % A key that is not a number is passed on untouched, so pkfeather's
    % own guard names it.
    petow /in eq {
        dup /Length 8 pkgetdef dup penum
            { peinlen mul 1 index exch /Length exch put } { pop } ifelse
        dup /Spacing 3 pkgetdef dup penum
            { peinspace mul 1 index exch /Spacing exch put } { pop } ifelse
        dup /Overlap 0.35 pkgetdef dup penum
            { 1 exch sub 1 index exch /Overlap exch put } { pop } ifelse
    } if
} bind def

% -   gsave and grestore, counted in pedepth so pkbrushedge can unwind
% to the caller's level when an error stops it halfway.
% @internal
/pegsave { gsave /pedepth pedepth 1 add def } bind def
% @internal
/pegrestore { grestore /pedepth pedepth 1 sub def } bind def

% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Brushes the edge of a filled mass in one call: short strokes in the mass's colour (the current colour, or /Mass) pulled out across the current path's edge, then, with /Under, strokes in the backdrop colour cut back into the mass, so the two interlock and the ruled vector edge reads as painted. Fill the mass first; the path is the mass's outline (closed with closepath: holes and mirrored spaces work) or just the visible edge left open, whose out side is /Outward. Every pkfeather key is accepted and passed on (except /Toward, which this sets), and each pass is painted by /Brush. /Under takes what pkwet's /Under takes, plus a pigment name; a procedural /Under is asked once per inward stroke, at the stroke's start, so a graded sky or water cuts back in its own colour all along. /Mass takes the same forms for the pulled-out colour, so a mass lit along its length (a meadow brighter toward the glow) carries its own gradient to its edge. Either key also takes a region list, `[ [ region colour ] ... default ]`, so the backdrop follows another path's fill exactly (water behind a bank, a meadow beside it). The caller's path, colour and graphics state survive. A mass built from several overlapping closed blobs (a cloud bank, a bush of ellipses) can go in as one path: an edge lying inside the others is hidden, and is not brushed. Deterministic under the caller's own srand.
% @example: newpath 0 120 moveto 120 150 240 110 360 135 curveto 360 0 lineto 0 0 lineto closepath gsave 0.18 0.30 0.12 setrgbcolor fill grestore 0.18 0.30 0.12 setrgbcolor << /Under [ 0.62 0.72 0.86 ] /Slant 10 >> pkbrushedge
% @param: /Under [rgb array or name] the colour behind the mass, cut back into it by the inward strokes; an [r g b] array, a pigment name such as /titaniumwhite (see PigmentNames), or a procedure `x y -> r g b` (three numbers in 0..1, user space; a pure function, as pkwet's) asked at each inward stroke's start; or a region list `[ [ region colour ] ... default ]`: each stroke takes the colour of the first region its start lies in (artkit's nonzero inside test, as `fill` paints), else the default at the end. A region is an artkit region (`screct`, `scpath`) or a procedure that builds a path, turned into one region per call in the call's user space (a region dict keeps the user space it was built in, so under another CTM, such as a reflection's `1 -1 scale`, give the procedure); each colour is an array, a pigment name or a procedure, as above. The region list is pkbrushedge's own, not pkwet's. Unset paints the outward strokes only (default unset)
% @param: /Mass [rgb array or name] the mass's colour, pulled out over the backdrop by the outward strokes; an [r g b] array, a pigment name, or a procedure `x y -> r g b` (three numbers in 0..1, user space; a pure function) asked at each outward stroke's start, so a graded mass keeps its gradient along the edge; or a region list, as /Under's. A procedure or a region list costs one /Brush call per outward stroke, so even a constant one paints differently from the array form. Unset uses the current colour (default unset)
% @param: /Brush [proc] the brush both passes are painted with: a procedure that paints the current path in the current colour, normally a preset with its options, such as `{ << /Width 2 >> pkdry }` (default `{ << /Width 5 /Charge 0.8 /Depletion 0.9 /Edge 0.6 /Grain 10 >> pkbroad }`, a small flat brush that runs dry as it crosses, so each stroke breaks up at its far end)
% @param: /InLength [fraction of /Length] the inward strokes' length as a fraction of the outward ones'; must be > 0. Each inward stroke covers the stretch of the normal an outward one does (its /Overlap is mirrored), so most of it lies outside the mass and only its tip cuts in (default 1)
% @param: /InSpacing [multiple of /Spacing] the inward strokes' spacing as a multiple of the outward ones'; must be > 0. Sparser cuts nick the edge; as dense as the outward strokes, they rule a pale band just inside it (default 2)
/pkbrushedge {
    /peopts0 exch def
    /peopts0 load type /dicttype ne { pkbrushedge-needs-an-options-dict } if
    peopts0 /Toward known { pkbrushedge-sets-toward-itself } if
    /pebrush peopts0 /Brush
        { << /Width 5 /Charge 0.8 /Depletion 0.9 /Edge 0.6 /Grain 10 >> pkbroad }
        pkgetdef def
    /pebrush load xcheck not { pkbrushedge-brush-must-be-a-procedure } if
    /peinlen peopts0 /InLength 1 pkgetdef def
    /peinlen load xcheck { pkbrushedge-inlength-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    peinlen 0 le { pkbrushedge-inlength-must-be-positive } if
    /peinspace peopts0 /InSpacing 2 pkgetdef def
    /peinspace load xcheck { pkbrushedge-inspacing-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    peinspace 0 le { pkbrushedge-inspacing-must-be-positive } if
    % /Under (the backdrop) and /Mass (the pulled-out colour): each none, a
    % colour (array or pigment name), or a procedure x y -> r g b.
    peopts0 /Under (pkbrushedge-under) pecolkey /pemode exch def /peUnder exch def
    peopts0 /Mass (pkbrushedge-mass) pecolkey /pemmode exch def /peMass exch def

    % Every gsave below goes through pegsave, which counts it, so an error
    % raised in the middle (a bad pkfeather key, a backdrop procedure's bad
    % answer, the brush's own guard) can be unwound to the caller's level
    % and passed on: the caller's graphics state is never left open.
    frnd /peseed exch def
    /pedepth 0 def
    {
    pegsave
        % In first: the backdrop cut back into the mass.
        pemode 1 eq {
            pegsave peUnder aload pop setrgbcolor /in peopts pkfeather pebrush pegrestore
        } if
        pemode 2 eq {
            % One brush call per stroke, each in the backdrop where it
            % starts.
            pegsave
                /pecolp /peUnder load def /pecolx (pkbrushedge-under) def
                /in peopts pkfeather pestrokes
            pegrestore
        } if
        pemode 3 eq {
            % A region list (issue #456): the same, each stroke in the
            % colour of the region it starts in.
            pegsave
                /pecolp { peUnder pereglook } def /pecolx (pkbrushedge-under) def
                /in peopts pkfeather pestrokes
            pegrestore
        } if
        % Out, last: the mass's own colour pulled over what is behind it, on
        % top of the nicks, so the two interlock. That is the current colour,
        % or /Mass: one colour, or (a procedure) one brush call per stroke,
        % each in the mass's colour where it starts (issue #426).
        pemmode 0 eq { pegsave /out peopts pkfeather pebrush pegrestore } if
        pemmode 1 eq {
            pegsave peMass aload pop setrgbcolor /out peopts pkfeather pebrush pegrestore
        } if
        pemmode 2 eq {
            pegsave
                /pecolp /peMass load def /pecolx (pkbrushedge-mass) def
                /out peopts pkfeather pestrokes
            pegrestore
        } if
        pemmode 3 eq {
            pegsave
                /pecolp { peMass pereglook } def /pecolx (pkbrushedge-mass) def
                /out peopts pkfeather pestrokes
            pegrestore
        } if
    pegrestore
    } stopped { pedepth { grestore } repeat stop } if
} bind def

% ============================================================================
% The canvas surface (issue #182, slice S1 of docs/dev/design/CANVAS_SURFACE.md)
% ============================================================================
%
% A painter's lightest strokes catch only on the raised weave of the canvas,
% and every stroke laid over that canvas catches on the same weave. Every
% other break-up in this file belongs to one stroke (a contact chain, a
% reservoir, a blade's nicks), so nothing is shared between strokes. This
% section is the one break-up that belongs to the canvas:
%
%   opts pkcanvas -            set up the shared surface (paints nothing)
%
% The surface is a seeded height field over *knuckles*, one raised crossing of
% the weave per /Weave x /Weave cell, defined in *canvas space*: the user space
% in force when `pkcanvas` was called, captured as a matrix. A later mark is
% sampled there whatever its own CTM, so a `pkwet` pass, a reflection drawn
% under `1 -1 scale` and the scene above it all catch on the same knuckles, and
% `--dpi` changes only raster fineness (CANVAS_SURFACE.md 3.5).
%
% A preset that adopts the light-touch key `/Touch` (later slices; no preset
% reads the surface yet) calls the one internal `pjcatch` right after the
% `gsave` that opens its drawing block:
%
%   llx lly urx ury touch pjcatch -
%
% It clips to every knuckle the paint reaches at that touch. Before building
% anything the preset checks `llx lly urx ury pjcells` against `pjbudget`
% (60000 cells, about a 600pt square at the default weave once the
% knuckle margin is added) and raises its own
% `pkX-touch-area-exceeds-safety-limit`; `pjcatch` repeats the check as a
% backstop under its own name. Both read one range computation (`pjrange`),
% so the pre-check and the catch it guards can never disagree.
%
% Determinism. The surface takes no `rand` and no `srand`: `pkcanvas` shuffles
% its permutation table with a private Schrage-form minimal-standard LCG
% (`pjrand`, the same generator as `pwrand` under its own state), and sampling
% is table lookups and arithmetic. So `pkcanvas`, `pjcatch` and any /Touch
% value leave the caller's stream exactly where they found it, and adding
% `pkcanvas` to a finished piece moves no mark. `--sweep-seed` varies the marks
% and keeps the canvas. The same /Seed gives the same table under Ghostscript:
% every intermediate stays inside 32-bit integers.
%
% State. `pkcanvas` writes its state into `userdict` explicitly, never with a
% bare `def`, so a call inside the artist's own `begin ... end` still leaves a
% surface behind after the `end` (pkwet's `pqdset` learned this). A preset
% tests `userdict /pjCTM known` for "is a surface set":
%
%   pjCTM            canvas space (`matrix currentmatrix` at the call)
%   pjP              a 256-entry permutation of 0..255, shuffled from /Seed
%   pjW pjM pjR      /Weave, /Mottle, /Relief
%   pjmemo           knuckle heights already computed (a speed cache only)
%
% The height of the knuckle in cell (i, j) is a pure function of the seed:
%
%   hash(i, j, s) = P[(P[(i + s) and 255] + j) and 255] / 255
%   mottle(x, y)  = smoothstepped bilinear value noise over hash(., ., 101),
%                   lattice spacing /Mottle
%   height(i, j)  = (1 - Relief) * hash(i, j, 0) + Relief * mottle(cell centre)
%
% `pjmemo` keys a height by (i + 4096) * 8192 + (j + 4096) for the half-open
% range -4096 <= i, j < 4096 (one-to-one there, and below 2^26); cells outside
% it are computed but not stored, and the memo stops growing at 250000
% entries. A height is a pure function, so the memo changes speed, never
% output.
%
% The knuckles are ellipses, not diamonds (CANVAS_SURFACE.md Q9). A diamond's
% `|x|/a + |y|/b` reaches a cell corner later than an ellipse does, so at
% /Touch 0.99 the diamonds left a hole at every cell corner, short of the
% near-solid catch the design promises there (docs/archive/NOTES.md, issue #220).
%
% The lattice is not ruled (issue #298). On a regular lattice the knuckles
% read as a square pixel grid at 2x, more halftone than canvas. So each
% column (warp) thread wanders sideways and each row (weft) thread up and
% down, slowly along its length (`pjwander`), each knuckle's size varies
% 0.8..1.2 by hash(i, j, 4), and the knuckles are rounder (0.65 x 0.55 of
% a cell where they were 0.8 x 0.42). The rounder, wandering knuckles leave
% more gaps near full touch, so the top-fifth growth is 0.45 where it was
% 0.25: a 200pt square is 96.5% / 99.1% / 99.8% covered at /Touch 0.9 /
% 0.95 / 0.99, against 95.9% / 98.7% / 99.7% before.
%
% Scratch prefix pj-. Distinct from every pk*-prefixed name because the
% adopting presets (pkdry, pkfan) call pkribbon, which clobbers those.

% Cells one catch may build. Past it the preset rejects (CANVAS_SURFACE.md 3.3).
% @internal
/pjbudget 60000 def
% Entries the height memo may hold before later cells are computed fresh.
% @internal
/pjmemocap 250000 def

% any -> bool   (an integer or a real)
% @internal
/pjisnum {
    type dup /integertype eq exch /realtype eq or
} bind def

% seed -   (seeds the surface's own generator)
% Reduced mod 2147483646 *before* `abs`, and offset to 1..2147483646 because 0
% is the Lehmer generator's one fixed point; same shape as pwsrand.
% @internal
/pjsrand {
    cvi 2147483646 mod abs 1 add
    /pjstate exch def
} bind def

% -> 0.0..1.0   (exclusive at both ends)
% Schrage's decomposition of the minimal standard LCG (16807, 2^31-1), so the
% sequence is identical under Ghostscript's 32-bit integers.
% @internal
/pjrand {
    /pjq pjstate 127773 idiv def
    /pjstate pjstate 127773 mod 16807 mul pjq 2836 mul sub def
    pjstate 0 lt { /pjstate pjstate 2147483647 add def } if
    pjstate 2147483647 div
} bind def

% i j s -> 0..1   (reads the permutation table from `pjp`)
% `and 255` rather than `mod`, so a negative index stays in range. i, j and s
% must be integers: `and` rejects a real.
% @internal
/pjhash {
    3 -1 roll add 255 and pjp exch get
    add 255 and pjp exch get 255 div
} bind def

% a b t -> a + (b - a) t
% @internal
/pjlerp {
    /pjlt exch def
    pjlt mul exch 1 pjlt sub mul add
} bind def

% real -> integer 0..255   (floor, reduced mod 256 as a real first)
% The hash depends only on its indices mod 256, so reducing before `cvi`
% changes nothing, and it means a tiny /Mottle far from the origin can't
% overflow `cvi`.
% @internal
/pjwrap {
    floor dup 256 div floor 256 mul sub cvi 255 and
} bind def

% x y -> 0..1   (the slow mottle at a canvas-space point)
% @internal
/pjmottle {
    userdict /pjM get div dup pjwrap /pjmy exch def dup floor sub /pjfy exch def
    userdict /pjM get div dup pjwrap /pjmx exch def dup floor sub /pjfx exch def
    /pjux pjfx dup mul 3 2 pjfx mul sub mul def
    /pjuy pjfy dup mul 3 2 pjfy mul sub mul def
    pjmx pjmy 101 pjhash pjmx 1 add pjmy 101 pjhash pjux pjlerp
    pjmx pjmy 1 add 101 pjhash pjmx 1 add pjmy 1 add 101 pjhash pjux pjlerp
    pjuy pjlerp
} bind def

% i j -> height 0..1   (the knuckle in cell (i, j); i and j integers)
% @internal
/pjheight {
    /pjhj exch def /pjhi exch def
    /pjp userdict /pjP get def
    pjhi -4096 ge pjhi 4096 lt and pjhj -4096 ge and pjhj 4096 lt and
        { /pjhk pjhi 4096 add 8192 mul pjhj 4096 add add def }
        { /pjhk -1 def }
    ifelse
    /pjmd userdict /pjmemo get def
    pjhk 0 ge { pjmd pjhk known } { false } ifelse
        { pjmd pjhk get }
        {
            /pjhr userdict /pjR get def
            /pjhw userdict /pjW get def
            1 pjhr sub pjhi pjhj 0 pjhash mul
            pjhr pjhi 0.5 add pjhw mul pjhj 0.5 add pjhw mul pjmottle mul
            add
            pjhk 0 ge { pjmd length pjmemocap lt { pjmd pjhk 2 index put } if } if
        }
    ifelse
} bind def

% x y -   (widen the canvas-space box pjbx0..pjby1 to a user-space point)
% @internal
/pjrgrow {
    transform pjrcm itransform
    dup pjby0 lt { dup /pjby0 exch def } if
    dup pjby1 gt { dup /pjby1 exch def } if
    pop
    dup pjbx0 lt { dup /pjbx0 exch def } if
    dup pjbx1 gt { dup /pjbx1 exch def } if
    pop
} bind def

% t k s -> offset   (the wander of thread t at crossing k, in cells)
% A thread of the weave is not ruled: it drifts a little from its place,
% slowly, as it runs. The offset is smoothstepped 1D value noise along the
% thread, a lattice point every 3 crossings, from hash(t, floor(k/3), s),
% in -0.35..0.35 of a cell. pjcatch calls it as `i j 5 pjwander` for the
% column (warp) thread's x and `j i 6 pjwander` for the row (weft) thread's
% y (separate slots, so the field is not mirrored about the diagonal), so a
% column of knuckles shifts together and the lattice stops reading as a
% grid. t and k must be integers; a pure function of them, no rand.
% @internal
/pjwander {
    /pjws exch def /pjwk exch def /pjwt exch def
    /pjwq pjwk 3 div floor cvi def
    /pjwf pjwk pjwq 3 mul sub 3 div def
    /pjwf pjwf dup mul 3 2 pjwf mul sub mul def
    pjwt pjwq pjws pjhash pjwt pjwq 1 add pjws pjhash pjwf pjlerp
    0.5 sub 0.7 mul
} bind def

% llx lly urx ury -   (sets pji0 pjj0 pji1 pjj1 and pjhuge)
% The cells whose knuckle can reach a user-space box: its corners taken to
% canvas space, bounded, and grown by the farthest a knuckle reaches from its
% own cell's centre -- a long semi-axis of 0.65W at the largest scale
% 1.1 x 1.45 x 1.2 (catch, top-fifth growth, size variation), about 1.24W,
% plus the 0.15W nudge and the 0.35W thread wander, or 1.75W. Without that margin a knuckle centred in a
% neighbouring cell and overhanging the mark would be missing, and two marks
% with different boxes would catch on different pixels where they overlap.
% pjhuge is true when a cell index is past 1e9, where `cvi` could overflow; the
% callers count that as over budget.
% @internal
/pjrange {
    /pjry1 exch def /pjrx1 exch def /pjry0 exch def /pjrx0 exch def
    /pjrcm userdict /pjCTM get def
    pjrx0 pjry0 transform pjrcm itransform /pjby0 exch def /pjbx0 exch def
    /pjbx1 pjbx0 def /pjby1 pjby0 def
    pjrx1 pjry0 pjrgrow
    pjrx0 pjry1 pjrgrow
    pjrx1 pjry1 pjrgrow
    /pjrw userdict /pjW get def
    /pjrm pjrw 1.75 mul def
    /pjhuge false def
    [ pjbx0 pjrm sub pjby0 pjrm sub pjbx1 pjrm add pjby1 pjrm add ] {
        pjrw div floor
        dup abs 1e9 gt { pop 0 /pjhuge true def } if
        cvi
    } forall
    /pjj1 exch def /pji1 exch def /pjj0 exch def /pji0 exch def
} bind def

% llx lly urx ury -> cells   (how many cells a catch over this box builds)
% @internal
/pjcells {
    pjrange
    pjhuge
        { pjbudget 1 add }
        { pji1 pji0 sub 1 add cvr pjj1 pjj0 sub 1 add cvr mul }
    ifelse
} bind def

% raw reach pjstopbox -> llx lly urx ury
% The user-space box an adopting preset passes to pjcells and pjcatch: the
% bounding box of every stop record in raw (each starts [x y ...]), each
% grown by its own reach. reach is a procedure, rec -> points, so a preset
% can use a value it already resolved per stop (pktrowel's half-extent)
% without re-running the caller's code. raw must not be empty.
% @internal
/pjstopbox {
    /pjsbr exch def
    /pjsbx0 1e30 def /pjsby0 1e30 def
    /pjsbx1 -1e30 def /pjsby1 -1e30 def
    {
        dup pjsbr /pjsbd exch def
        dup 0 get /pjsbx exch def 1 get /pjsby exch def
        pjsbx pjsbd sub dup pjsbx0 lt { /pjsbx0 exch def } { pop } ifelse
        pjsby pjsbd sub dup pjsby0 lt { /pjsby0 exch def } { pop } ifelse
        pjsbx pjsbd add dup pjsbx1 gt { /pjsbx1 exch def } { pop } ifelse
        pjsby pjsbd add dup pjsby1 gt { /pjsby1 exch def } { pop } ifelse
    } forall
    pjsbx0 pjsby0 pjsbx1 pjsby1
} bind def

% cx cy rx ry -   (appends one axis-aligned ellipse as four curveto's)
% Explicit corner arithmetic, with no CTM change per knuckle, as pvfleck does.
% @internal
/pjoval {
    /pjey exch def /pjex exch def /pjoy exch def /pjox exch def
    /pjkx pjex 0.5523 mul def /pjky pjey 0.5523 mul def
    pjox pjex add pjoy moveto
    pjox pjex add pjoy pjky add  pjox pjkx add pjoy pjey add  pjox pjoy pjey add curveto
    pjox pjkx sub pjoy pjey add  pjox pjex sub pjoy pjky add  pjox pjex sub pjoy curveto
    pjox pjex sub pjoy pjky sub  pjox pjkx sub pjoy pjey sub  pjox pjoy pjey sub curveto
    pjox pjkx add pjoy pjey sub  pjox pjex add pjoy pjky sub  pjox pjex add pjoy curveto
    closepath
} bind def

% llx lly urx ury touch -   (intersects the clip with the surface's catch)
% The box is in the current user space. Every knuckle with
% d = height + touch - 1 > 0 joins the clip, scaled by
%   (0.6 + 0.5 min(1, d/0.3)) * (1 + 0.45 max(0, (touch - 0.8)/0.2))
%   * (0.8 + 0.4 hash(i, j, 4))
% and centred off the lattice by the 0.15W nudge and the thread wander
% (pjwander), so a knuckle the paint barely reaches catches a speck, a well-covered one a
% full knuckle that merges with its neighbours, and every knuckle grows over
% the top fifth of the range so coverage reaches the whole mark continuously.
% At touch >= 1 it does nothing. With no knuckle passing, the clip becomes
% empty (a one-point path), not unchanged. Replaces the current path, and
% leaves the CTM as it found it: the knuckles are built under pjCTM, and the
% clip is in device space, so it survives the matrix change. Every guard runs
% before the matrix is touched.
% @internal
/pjcatch {
    /pjt exch def
    pjt 1 ge {
        pop pop pop pop
    } {
        userdict /pjCTM known not { pjcatch-needs-a-canvas } if
        % Test-only (CANVAS_SURFACE.md 6, test 7): with userdict /pjWholePage
        % set, the box becomes the whole current clip, so a test can check
        % that a preset's own reach box clipped no paint. No library code
        % sets it.
        userdict /pjWholePage known {
            pop pop pop pop clippath pathbbox newpath
        } if
        pjcells pjbudget gt { pjcatch-area-exceeds-safety-limit } if
        /pjcw userdict /pjW get def
        /pjp userdict /pjP get def
        /pjgrow pjt 0.8 sub 0.2 div dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if 0.45 mul 1 add def
        /pjsaved matrix currentmatrix def
        userdict /pjCTM get setmatrix
        newpath
        /pjn 0 def
        pji0 1 pji1 {
            /pjci exch def
            pjj0 1 pjj1 {
                /pjcj exch def
                /pjd pjci pjcj pjheight pjt add 1 sub def
                pjd 0 gt {
                    /pjs pjd 0.3 div dup 1 gt { pop 1 } if 0.5 mul 0.6 add
                        pjgrow mul pjcw mul def
                    /pjs pjs pjci pjcj 4 pjhash 0.4 mul 0.8 add mul def
                    pjci 0.5 add pjci pjcj 2 pjhash 0.5 sub 0.3 mul add
                        pjci pjcj 5 pjwander add pjcw mul
                    pjcj 0.5 add pjci pjcj 3 pjhash 0.5 sub 0.3 mul add
                        pjcj pjci 6 pjwander add pjcw mul
                    pjci pjcj add 1 and 1 eq
                        { pjs 0.65 mul pjs 0.55 mul }
                        { pjs 0.55 mul pjs 0.65 mul }
                    ifelse
                    pjoval
                    /pjn pjn 1 add def
                } if
            } for
        } for
        pjn 0 eq { 0 0 moveto 0 0 lineto } if
        clip newpath
        pjsaved setmatrix
    } ifelse
} bind def

%%SelfTest: pkcanvas-rejects-malformed-options
%   { << /Weave 0 >> pkcanvas } /pkcanvas-weave-must-be-positive
%   (/Weave 0) mustguard
%
%   { << /Weave { 3 } >> pkcanvas } /pkcanvas-weave-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Weave { 3 }) mustguard
%
%   { << /Mottle -1 >> pkcanvas } /pkcanvas-mottle-must-be-positive
%   (/Mottle -1) mustguard
%
%   { << /Relief 1.5 >> pkcanvas } /pkcanvas-relief-must-be-0-to-1
%   (/Relief 1.5) mustguard
%
%   { << /Seed (x) >> pkcanvas } /pkcanvas-seed-must-be-a-number
%   (/Seed as a string) mustguard
%
%   { << /Seed { 3 } >> pkcanvas } /pkcanvas-seed-must-not-be-a-procedure
%   (/Seed { 3 }) mustguard
%
%   { << /Seed 1e30 >> pkcanvas } /pkcanvas-seed-must-be-within-integer-range
%   (/Seed 1e30) mustguard
%
%   { << >> pkcanvas } (an empty dict) mustpass
%
%   % A real seed is accepted (`--sweep NAME=` can feed one) and truncated.
%   { << /Seed 7.0 >> pkcanvas } (/Seed 7.0) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkcanvas-takes-no-random-draws
%   1 srand rand /pjtesta exch def
%   1 srand << /Seed 9 >> pkcanvas rand pjtesta eq
%   (pkcanvas leaves the caller's stream untouched) mustbe
%
%   << >> pkcanvas
%   1 srand rand /pjtesta exch def
%   1 srand newpath 0 0 50 50 0.5 pjcatch rand pjtesta eq
%   (pjcatch leaves the caller's stream untouched) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkcanvas-heights-are-pure
%   << /Seed 5 >> pkcanvas
%   3 -7 pjheight /pjtestb exch def
%   3 -7 pjheight pjtestb eq
%   (the same cell twice, the second from the memo) mustbe
%
%   userdict /pjmemo 10 dict put
%   3 -7 pjheight pjtestb eq
%   (the same cell again with the memo cleared) mustbe
%
%   % The memo covers -4096 <= i, j < 4096 only. In a closed range (1, -4096)
%   % and (0, 4096) would share a key; (0, 4096) must not be stored at all.
%   userdict /pjmemo 10 dict put
%   1 -4096 pjheight pop
%   0 4096 pjheight /pjtesta exch def
%   userdict /pjmemo get length 1 eq
%   (a cell past the memo's range is not stored) mustbe
%
%   userdict /pjmemo 10 dict put
%   0 4096 pjheight pjtesta eq
%   (and its height does not depend on what was sampled first) mustbe
%
%   % Calling pkcanvas again with the same seed rebuilds the same surface.
%   << /Seed 5 >> pkcanvas
%   3 -7 pjheight pjtestb eq
%   (the same /Seed rebuilds the same surface) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pkcanvas-state-survives-the-callers-dict
%   % State goes to userdict explicitly, so an artist's own begin ... end
%   % doesn't take the surface with it.
%   5 dict begin << >> pkcanvas end
%   userdict /pjCTM known
%   (a surface set inside begin ... end is still set after end) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pjcatch-guards
%   % touch >= 1 does nothing, even with no canvas set.
%   { gsave 0 0 1e30 1e30 1 pjcatch grestore } (touch 1 over any box) mustpass
%
%   { 0 0 10 10 0.5 pjcatch } /pjcatch-needs-a-canvas
%   (a catch before any pkcanvas) mustguard
%
%   << >> pkcanvas
%   { 0 0 100000 100000 0.5 pjcatch } /pjcatch-area-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a 1e5pt square) mustguard
%
%   { 0 0 1e30 10 0.5 pjcatch } /pjcatch-area-exceeds-safety-limit
%   (a box past cvi's range) mustguard
%
%   0 0 600 600 pjcells pjbudget le
%   (a 600pt square fits the budget at the default weave) mustbe
%
%   { gsave 0 0 600 600 0.5 pjcatch grestore } (a 600pt catch) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: pjcatch-leaves-the-ctm-as-it-found-it
%   % The knuckles are built under pjCTM; every preset draws after the
%   % catch, so the caller's matrix must come back exactly.
%   << >> pkcanvas
%   gsave 7.5 0 translate 2 2 scale 15 rotate
%   matrix currentmatrix /pjtestm exch def
%   0 0 40 40 0.5 pjcatch
%   matrix currentmatrix /pjtestn exch def
%   grestore
%   true 0 1 5 { dup pjtestm exch get exch pjtestn exch get eq and } for
%   (the CTM after pjcatch is the CTM before it) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Sets up the shared canvas surface: a seeded weave of raised knuckles in the current coordinate system, which marks from presets that accept /Touch (pktrowel, pkdry, pkfan, pkbroad and pkliner, issue #182) catch on at /Touch below 1, so light strokes break up on the same canvas rather than each on its own. Call it once, early, in the space the piece is painted in; calling it again replaces the surface. Paints nothing and takes no rand draws, so adding it to a finished piece moves no mark.
% @example: << /Seed 3 /Weave 2.5 >> pkcanvas
% @param: /Seed [integer] Integer seed for this surface alone, leaving the caller's rand stream untouched (default 1, a constant rather than a draw from the caller's stream)
% @param: /Weave [points] Thread spacing of the weave in canvas-space points, one knuckle per Weave x Weave cell; must be > 0 (default 2.5)
% @param: /Mottle [points] Scale of the slow variation in how proud the weave stands, in canvas-space points; must be > 0. Larger gives broader patches of catch and miss (default Weave*8)
% @param: /Relief [0..1] How much the slow mottle, rather than each knuckle's own random height, decides which knuckles stand proud: 0 is an even speckled weave, 1 is all patches (default 0.7)
/pkcanvas {
    /pjopts exch def

    % Every guard runs before any state is written, so a rejected call
    % leaves the previous surface (or none) in place.
    /pjsseed pjopts /Seed 1 pkgetdef def
    /pjsseed load xcheck { pkcanvas-seed-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pjsseed pjisnum not { pkcanvas-seed-must-be-a-number } if
    pjsseed abs 2147483647 ge { pkcanvas-seed-must-be-within-integer-range } if

    /pjsweave pjopts /Weave 2.5 pkgetdef def
    /pjsweave load xcheck { pkcanvas-weave-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pjsweave pjisnum not { pkcanvas-weave-must-be-a-number } if
    pjsweave 0 le { pkcanvas-weave-must-be-positive } if

    /pjsmottle pjopts /Mottle pjsweave 8 mul pkgetdef def
    /pjsmottle load xcheck { pkcanvas-mottle-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pjsmottle pjisnum not { pkcanvas-mottle-must-be-a-number } if
    pjsmottle 0 le { pkcanvas-mottle-must-be-positive } if

    /pjsrelief pjopts /Relief 0.7 pkgetdef def
    /pjsrelief load xcheck { pkcanvas-relief-must-not-be-a-procedure } if
    pjsrelief pjisnum not { pkcanvas-relief-must-be-a-number } if
    pjsrelief 0 lt pjsrelief 1 gt or { pkcanvas-relief-must-be-0-to-1 } if

    % Fisher-Yates over 0..255 from the private generator.
    pjsseed pjsrand
    /pjstab 256 array def
    0 1 255 { pjstab exch dup put } for
    255 -1 1 {
        /pjsk exch def
        /pjsr pjrand pjsk 1 add mul floor cvi def
        pjsr pjsk gt { /pjsr pjsk def } if
        pjstab pjsk get
        pjstab pjsr get
        pjstab pjsk 3 -1 roll put
        pjstab pjsr 3 -1 roll put
    } for

    userdict /pjCTM matrix currentmatrix put
    userdict /pjP pjstab put
    userdict /pjW pjsweave put
    userdict /pjM pjsmottle put
    userdict /pjR pjsrelief put
    userdict /pjmemo 1024 dict put
} bind def
